r3.03.04 External pressure forces in large displacements r3.03.04 External pressure forces in large displacements Summary: A pressure loading in large displacements is a follower loading. By employing skin elements, one is led to compute, on the one hand, a right-hand side whose computation is close to that in small displacements and, on the other hand, an additional stiffness term which is, in general, not symmetric. 1 Introduction Taking into account loadings of pressure type (keyword PRES_REP in the command AFFE_CHAR_MECA) raises a certain number of difficulties in the absence of the small-displacement assumption. Indeed, unlike the dead loads mentioned in R5.03.01, the pressure depends on the displacements, since it is a force whose direction is normal to the domain; one then speaks of follower forces, activated by the keyword TYPE_CHARGE='SUIV' in the command STAT_NON_LINE. Nevertheless, the choice of the current configuration as the reference configuration (updated Lagrangian) leads to simple expressions (given a few notions of differential geometry) of the work of the pressure forces and of its first variation with respect to the displacement, the latter being a non-symmetric bilinear form. 2 Continuous statement of the problem 2.1 Elements of kinematics in large transformations One considers a solid \domain subjected to large strains (see figure). Let \gradTransfor be the gradient tensor of the transformation, \phi taking the initial configuration \domainRefe into the current deformed configuration \domainCurr . One denotes by \posiRefe the position of a point in \domainRefe and by \posiCurr the position of this same point after deformation in \domainCurr . \disp is then the displacement between the two configurations. One therefore has: \posiCurr = \posiRefe + \disp The gradient tensor of the transformation is written: \gradTransfor =  rac{\partial \posiCurr}{\partial \posiRefe} = 	ensTwoUnit + \gradVector{\disp} Solid in large transformations 2.2 Virtual work of the external pressure forces One considers a pressure \presRefe normal to the surface in the reference configuration. This pressure is written \presCurr in the current configuration. Reference configuration and current configuration In the current configuration, the virtual work of the external pressure forces \work{\pres} is written simply (see r3_03_04_Configurations): \work{\pres}(\disp) = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} {-\pres \, 
ormalCurr \cdot \dispVirt \measBound } Furthermore, one assumes henceforth that the value of the pressure does not depend explicitly on the displacement but only on the material point of application: \presCurr(\posiCurr) = \presCurr( uncTransfor (\posiRefe)) = \presRefe(\posiRefe) The follower character of the force comes from the dependence of the normal on the displacement. In this case, one may then express the virtual work of the pressure forces in the reference configuration (change of variable in the integral): \work{\pres}(\disp) = {\int}_{ oundNRefe(\disp)} {-\presRefe \, \det \gradTransfor \, \left[ \inverseTranspose{\gradTransfor} \cdot 
ormalRefe ight] \cdot \dispVirt \left( uncTransfor \left(\posiRefeight)ight)\measBound } On the practical level, formula will be used to compute the work of the pressure forces. However, formula is the best adapted to a derivation with respect to the displacement, the necessity of which will be seen in the following paragraph. 2.3 Variation of the virtual work of the external pressure forces With a view to solving the equilibrium problem of the structure by a Newton method, one is led to express the variation of the virtual work of the external pressure forces with respect to the displacement, in a manner similar to what was done for the virtual work of the internal forces in R5.03.01. The domain of integration being fixed in the expression , differentiation under the summation sign is legitimate (cf. ):  rac{\partial \work{\pres}\left(\dispight)}{\partial \disp} = {\int}_{ oundNRefe(\disp)} {-\presRefe \,  rac{\partial}{\partial \disp} \left[\det \gradTransfor \, \inverseTranspose{\gradTransfor} ight] \cdot \delta \disp \cdot 
ormalRefe \cdot \dispVirt\measBound} We decide to choose as reference configuration the current configuration, for which \gradTransfor=	ensTwoUnit . This choice leads to a simple expression of the derivative of the term in brackets:  rac{\partial}{\partial \disp}\left[ \det \gradTransfor \, \inverseTranspose{\gradTransfor} ight] \cdot \delta \disp = \divVector{\delta \disp} \, 	ensTwoUnit - \gradVector{ \delta \disp} Finally, the variation of the virtual work of the external pressure forces is written in the current configuration:  rac{\partial \work{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} {-\presCurr \, \left[ \divVector{\delta \disp} \, 	ensTwoUnit - \gradVector{ \delta \disp} ight] \cdot 
ormalCurr \cdot \dispVirt \measBound} In the expression a difficulty remains. Indeed, one expects to obtain an essentially surface quantity whereas the integrand brings out terms of differentiation normal to the surface. In other words, one must know the expression of the virtual displacements not only on the surface of the domain but also in its interior (in a neighbourhood of the surface, in order to be able to express the normal derivatives). This drawback is not trivial, since in order to compute the elementary terms due to the surface forces, one employs skin elements for which a normal variation has no meaning. 2.4 Adoption of a curvilinear parametrisation of the surface In order to remedy the problem mentioned previously, one must seek to express the relation by means of surface quantities only. For this, one has recourse to elements of differential geometry, , whose notation one adopts (in particular, one adopts the summation convention on repeated indices, where the Greek indices take the values 1 and 2 while the Latin indices take the values 1 to 3 ). Curvilinear parametrisation of the neighbourhood of the surface subjected to the pressure Let ({	heta}^{1},{	heta}^{2}) be an admissible parametrisation of the surface. In order to describe the volume constituted by a neighbourhood of this surface, one adjoins to it a third variable, {	heta}^{3} , which measures the progression along the unit normal n at ({	heta}^{1},{	heta}^{2}) . One thus has (see r3_03_04_ParametrageCurviligne): \vector{OM}\left({	heta}^{1},{	heta}^{2},{	heta}^{3}ight) = \vector{OS}\left({	heta}^{1},{	heta}^{2})+{	heta}^{3} \,
ormalCurr({	heta}^{1},{	heta}^{2}ight) With this choice of parametrisation, the covariant natural basis (\vectorBaseCV{1},\vectorBaseCV{2},\vectorBaseCV{3}) is written: \vectorBaseCV{i} =  rac{\partial \vector{OM}}{\partial {	heta}^{i}} While the metric tensor \metric is equal to: \metric = \vectorBaseCV{i} 	imes \vectorBaseCV{j} = \left[  egin{array}{ccc} 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{1}{1}& 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{1}{2}& 0\ 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{2}{1}& 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{2}{2}& 0\ 0& 0& 1\end{array}ight] In this curvilinear parametrisation, the integrand has the expression: {-\pres \, 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{i}{j} \, \vectorCmpCT{
ormal}{i} \, \left[ \derivee{\vectorCmpCT{\delta \disp}{k}}{k} \, \vectorCmpCT{\dispVirt}{j} - \derivee{\vectorCmpCT{\delta \disp}{j}}{k} \, \vectorCmpCT{\dispVirt}{k} ight] } This term simplifies considerably. Indeed, one may already note that when j=k , the term in brackets is zero. Furthermore, in the curvilinear system adopted, the contravariant components of 
ormal are: \vectorCmpCT{
ormal}{1}=0, \quad \vectorCmpCT{
ormal}{2}=0 \quad \vectorCmpCT{
ormal}{3}=1 Finally, taking into account the particular form of \metric (that is 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{1}{3}=0 , 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{2}{3}=0 and 	ensTwoCmpCO{\metric}{3}{3}=1 ), the variation of the work is written simply:  rac{\partial \work{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp \cdot \dispVirt = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} { -\presCurr \, \left[ \derivee{\vectorCmpCT{\delta \disp}{\alpha}}{\alpha} \, \vectorCmpCT{ \dispVirt}{3} - \derivee{\vectorCmpCT{\delta \disp}{3}}{\alpha} \, \vectorCmpCT{\dispVirt}{\alpha} ight] \measBound} On this expression, one observes that only surface differential operators intervene (covariant differentiation with respect to {	heta}^{1} and {	heta}^{2} only), which is indeed the aim sought. Introducing the contravariant basis (\vectorBaseCT{1},\vectorBaseCT{2},\vectorBaseCT{3}=
ormal) , also called the dual basis, which is expressed from the covariant basis by: vectorBaseCT{i} = {\left[\metric^{ij}ight]}^{-1} \, vectorBaseCV{j} One may dispense with the curvilinear components:  rac{\partial \work{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} { -\presCurr \, \left[ \left(  rac{\partial \delta \disp}{\partial {	heta}^{\alpha}} \cdot \vectorBaseCT{\alpha} ight) \, \left(\dispVirt \cdot 
ormal ight) - \left(  rac{\partial \delta \disp}{\partial {	heta}^{\alpha}} \cdot 
ormal ight) \, \left( \dispVirt \cdot \vectorBaseCT{\alpha} ight) ight] \measBound} It is henceforth the expression which will be used to compute the variation of the virtual work of the pressure forces. 2.5 Particular case of a structure subjected to a constant internal or external pressure In the particular case of a constant pressure in a cavity (see r3_03_04_Pression), one shows that the pressure forces derive from a potential \Xi which is none other than the product of the pressure by the volume of the cavity. This result extends to the case of a structure immersed in a fluid at constant pressure. Structure under constant internal or external pressure One writes this potential: \Xi = \pres \, {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} { \measDomain } = \pres \, {\int}_{ oundNRefe(\disp)} { \det \gradTransfor \measDomain } Once again, one chooses as reference configuration the current configuration. The variation of \Xi then indeed leads to the virtual work of the external pressure forces:  rac{\partial \Xi }{\partial \disp}\cdot\dispVirt = \pres \, {\int}_{\domainCurr(\disp)} { \divVector(\dispVirt) \, \measDomain } = - {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} { \pres \, \dispVirt \cdot 
ormal \measBound} = \work{\pres} \, \dispVirt In this particular case, the variation of the virtual work is also the second variation of the potential \Xi , that is a symmetric bilinear form:  rac{\partial {W}^{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt= rac{{\partial}^{2}\Xi (\disp)}{\partial {\disp}^{2}}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt 3 Discretisation 3.1 Options computed Skin finite elements (surface elements immersed in a three-dimensional space) are employed to discretise the real and virtual displacements intervening in surface expressions such as and . The latter make it possible to express respectively the right-hand-side vector and the stiffness matrix due to the pressure, whose use by the algorithm of STAT_NON_LINE is specified in R5.03.01 One therefore develops four options: 1. RIGI_MECA_PRSU_R : stiffness matrix for a follower pressure as a real constant 2. RIGI_MECA_PRSU_F : stiffness matrix for a follower pressure as a real function 3. CHAR_MECA_PRSU_R : right-hand-side vector for a follower pressure as a real constant 4. CHAR_MECA_PRSU_F : right-hand-side vector for a follower pressure as a real function These options are developed for the elements 3D, D_PLAN and AXIS. One may apply a normal follower pressure but not a follower tangential shear. The pressure may be a real function of time or a real constant. 3.2 Discretisation of the differential-geometry terms One denotes by \discVect{G_{\alpha}} the discretised version of the vectors of the covariant basis with {\alpha={1,2}} . The first two vectors of the covariant basis \discVect{G_{\alpha={1,2}}} are computed from the vector of the displacements discretised at the nodes \discVect{U} and from the derivatives of the shape functions \discMatr{B_{\alpha}} : \discVect{G_{\alpha}} = \discMatr{B_{\alpha}} \, \discVect{U} The normal 
ormal is computed as the vector product of these first two vectors \vector{g_{\alpha}} : 
ormal =  rac{{\vector{g}}_{1} {\wedge } {\vector{g}}_{2}}{\Vert {\vector{g}}_{1} {\wedge }{\vector{g}}_{2}\Vert} One may also compute the discretised metric tensor in covariant components \discMatr{G_{\alpha  eta}} : \discMatr{G_{\alpha  eta}} = \discVect{G_\alpha} \discVectLigne{{G}_{ eta}} And its Jacobian: \jacobTransfor = \det \discMatr{G_{\alpha  eta}} One may compute the contravariant metric matrix, which will be denoted \discMatr{G^{\delta \gamma}} \discMatr{G^{\delta \gamma}} = \inverse{\discMatr{ G_{\alpha  eta}}} And finally extract the contravariant basis \discVectCmp{G}{\delta} : \discVect{G^\delta} = \discMatr{G^{\delta \gamma}} \, \discVect{G_\alpha} 3.3 Vector of the follower forces The computation of the virtual work of the pressure forces is in fact identical to that carried out in small displacements, subject to a prior updating of the geometry. One starts from the expression of the virtual works: \work{\pres}(\disp) = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} {-\pres \, 
ormalCurr \cdot \dispVirt \measBound } In discretised form: \work{\pres}(\disp) = \discVectLigne{\delta V} \discVect{{F}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} The variation of the displacements is written from the shape functions: \dispVirt = \discMatr{ \shapeFunc } \, \left\lbrace \delta Vightbrace All the differential-geometry terms have been discretised in the preceding paragraph; it only remains to discretise the integral by using a Gauss scheme with the weights \quadWeight{\quadPointIndex} . On an arbitrary quantity A , one will have {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} {A \measBound } = \sum_{\quadPointIndex} {\onQuadPoint{A}{\quadPointIndex} \, \quadWeight{\quadPointIndex} } The pressure \pres given by AFFE_CHAR_MECA is located at the nodes, one must therefore interpolate the pressure \pres from its values on the nodes, which one denotes \onNode{\pres}{
odeIndex} towards the Gauss point of coordinates \onQuadPoint{\xi}{\quadPointIndex} : \onQuadPoint{\pres}{\quadPointIndex} = \sum_{
odeIndex}{\onNode{\shapeFunc(\onQuadPoint{\xi}{\quadPointIndex})}{
odeIndex} \, \onNode{\pres}{
odeIndex} } One denotes by \onNode{\shapeFunc(\onQuadPoint{\xi}{\quadPointIndex})}{
odeIndex} the value of the shape function of the node 
odeIndex at the Gauss point of coordinates \onQuadPoint{\xi}{\quadPointIndex} . Finally: \discVect{{F}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} = - \sum_{{\quadPointIndex}}{ \onQuadPoint{\pres}{\quadPointIndex} \, \quadWeight{\quadPointIndex} \, \onQuadPoint{\jacobTransfor}{\quadPointIndex} \, 	ranspose{\discMatr{\onQuadPoint{ \shapeFunc} {\quadPointIndex} }} \, \discVect{\onQuadPoint{
ormal} {\quadPointIndex} } } 3.4 Matrix of the follower forces The variation of the virtual work of the pressure forces is expressed by the expression  rac{\partial \work{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt = {\int}_{ oundNCurr(\disp)} { -\presCurr \, \left[ \left(  rac{\partial \delta \disp}{\partial {	heta}^{\alpha}} \cdot \vectorBaseCT{\alpha} ight) \, \left(\dispVirt \cdot 
ormal ight) - \left(  rac{\partial \delta \disp}{\partial {	heta}^{\alpha}} \cdot 
ormal ight) \, \left( \dispVirt \cdot \vectorBaseCT{\alpha} ight) ight] \measBound} It makes it possible to extract the tangent matrix \discMatr{{K}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} of the follower forces:  rac{\partial \work{\pres}(\disp)}{\partial \disp}\cdot\delta \disp\cdot\dispVirt = \discVectLigne{\delta V} \discMatr{{-K}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} \discVect{\delta U} The minus sign comes from the fact that the contribution of the matrix is on the left-hand side. Finally: \discMatr{{K}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} = \sum_{{\quadPointIndex}}{ \onQuadPoint{\pres}{\quadPointIndex} \, \quadWeight{\quadPointIndex} \, \onQuadPoint{\jacobTransfor}{\quadPointIndex} \, 	ranspose{\discMatr{\onQuadPoint{ \shapeDFunc} {\quadPointIndex} }} \left( \discVect{\onQuadPoint{G^\delta}{\quadPointIndex} } \, \discVectLigne{\onQuadPoint{
ormal} {\quadPointIndex} } - \discVect{\onQuadPoint{
ormal} {\quadPointIndex} } \, \discVectLigne{\onQuadPoint{G^\delta}{\quadPointIndex} } ight ) \, {\discMatr{\onQuadPoint{ \shapeFunc} {\quadPointIndex} }} } with \discMatr{\onQuadPoint{ \shapeDFunc} {\quadPointIndex} } the matrix of the derivatives of the shape functions. 3.5 Choice of the matrix In general, the matrix \discMatr{{K}^{\pres}\left(\dispight)} is not symmetric (except for the particular case of a structure subjected to a constant internal or external pressure). One also observes in practice that, for strong variations of the geometry (using a hyperelastic constitutive behaviour in large strains such as ELAS_HYPER), symmetrising this matrix is not a good strategy (failure of convergence). One therefore decides to keep this non-symmetric matrix, despite the (slight) extra cost induced by the factorisation of such a matrix. 4 Bibliography Foundations of solid mechanics, Fung Y. C., Prentice Hall. 1965, pp 31-57. Computation of the derivative of a quantity with respect to a crack front by the theta method, Mialon P., EDF - Bulletin de la Direction des Etudes et Recherches - SérieC - n° 3. 1988, pp1-28.

r3.06.11 Hexahedral element with one integration point, stabilised by the “Assumed Strain” method r3.06.11 Hexahedral element with one integration point, stabilised by the “Assumed Strain” method Summary: The standard under-integrated 8-node hexahedral element with 1 integration point introduces spurious modes associated with zero energy (hourglass modes) and can lead to a singularity of the global stiffness matrix for certain boundary conditions. The rank deficiency of the stiffness matrix, due to under-integration, must therefore be made good by adding to the elementary stiffness a so-called stabilisation matrix. This is the purpose of the ASM (Assumed Strain Method) developed here. The main characteristic of this method is that the discretised gradient operator B does not necessarily derive from the displacement field and from the classical relations linking strain to displacement. Indeed, this ASM method consists in projecting the discretised gradient operator onto an appropriate subspace in order to avoid the various types of locking. > Contents 1 Introduction In finite-element analyses, the use of under-integration methods makes it possible to reduce analysis times significantly, which explains their success. The other objective of these methods is to avoid the various lockings encountered in the numerical implementation of finite elements. However, this under-integration does not have only advantages: it unfortunately introduces spurious modes associated with zero energy, which lead to hourglass modes, which deform the mesh in an unrealistic way and end up by making the solution explode. This is due to a rank deficiency of the stiffness matrix caused by under-integration. It is remedied by adding to the elementary stiffness a so-called stabilisation matrix. The kernel of the new stiffness obtained by this means must reduce to the modes corresponding to rigid-body motions only. In recent years, some authors have developed various elements based on the ASM (Assumed Strain Method) technique. The main characteristic of this method is that the discretised gradient operator B does not necessarily derive from the displacement field and from the classical relations linking strain to displacement. Indeed, this ASM method consists in projecting the discretised gradient operator onto an appropriate subspace in order to avoid the various types of locking. This technique has been widely used recently and has led to several stabilised finite elements of the 4-node quadrilateral or 8-node hexahedron type [1], [2], [3]. It is the 8-node hexahedral element under-integrated with 1 integration point and stabilised by the ASM method, due to Belytschko and Bindeman [2], that is described in this document. 2 Formulation of the HEXA8 element with one integration point 2.1 Displacement field and discretised gradient operator In the hexahedral element, the spatial coordinates {x}_{i} are related to the nodal coordinates {x}_{	ext{iI}} by means of the isoparametric shape functions {N}_{I} by the formulae: > {x}_{i}={x}_{	ext{iI}}{N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta )=\sum_{I=1}^{8}{N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ){x}_{	ext{iI}} eq 1.1-1 The summation convention for repeated indices will be used in what follows. The lower-case indices i vary from 1 to 3 and represent the spatial directions. The upper-case indices I vary from 1 to 8 and correspond to the nodes of the element. The same shape functions are used to define the displacement field of the element {u}_{i} as a function of the nodal displacements {u}_{	ext{iI}} : > {u}_{i}={u}_{	ext{iI}}{N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ) Since the same shape functions apply to the coordinates and to the displacements, their material derivative vanishes and the velocity field can be given by: > {v}_{i}={v}_{	ext{iI}}{N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ) eq 1.1-2 The previous interpolation of the velocity field will make it possible to define the strain rate and to write the relations linking the strains to the nodal velocities. The gradient {v}_{i,j} of the velocity field is written: > {v}_{i,j}={v}_{	ext{iI}}{N}_{I,j} By convention, a comma preceding a lower-case index represents differentiation with respect to the spatial coordinates. The strain-rate tensor {D}_{ij} is then given by the symmetric part of the velocity gradient: {D}_{ij}= rac{1}{2}({v}_{i,j}+{v}_{j,i}) Trilinear isoparametric shape functions {N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ) are given by: > {N}_{I}(\xi ,\eta ,\zeta )= rac{1}{8}(1+{\xi}_{I}\xi )(1+{\eta}_{I}\eta )(1+{\zeta}_{I}\zeta ) > \xi ,\eta ,\zeta \in [-1,1],I=1,....,8 with {\xi}_{I},{\eta}_{I},{\zeta}_{I} equal to 1 or –1 eq 1.1-3 These shape functions transform a unit cube in the space (\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ) into an arbitrary hexahedron in the space ({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3})=(x,y,z) . Combining equations 1.1-1, 1.1-2, 1.1-3 , one arrives at the development of the velocity field as the sum of a constant term, of linear terms in {x}_{i} , and of terms involving the functions {h}_{\alpha} : > {v}_{i}={a}_{\mathrm{0i}}+{a}_{\mathrm{1i}}{x}_{1}+{a}_{\mathrm{2i}}{x}_{2}+{a}_{\mathrm{3i}}{x}_{3}+{c}_{\mathrm{1i}}{h}_{1}+{c}_{\mathrm{2i}}{h}_{2}+{c}_{\mathrm{3i}}{h}_{3}+{c}_{\mathrm{4i}}{h}_{4} i = 1,2,3 eq 1.1-4 > {h}_{1}=\eta \zeta {h}_{2}=\xi \zeta {h}_{3}=\eta \xi {h}_{4}=\xi \eta \zeta Indeed, equation 1.1-1 makes it possible to write \xi ,\eta ,\zeta as a function only of the {x}_{i} , of the {h}_{\alpha} and of a constant parameter. Substituting these last relations into equation 1.1-2 , one recovers the sought expressions 1.1-4 . Evaluating equation 1.1-4 at the nodes of the element, one arrives at the following 3 systems of 8 equations: > {\dot{d}}_{i}={a}_{\mathrm{0i}}s+{a}_{\mathrm{1i}}{x}_{1}+{a}_{\mathrm{2i}}{x}_{2}+{a}_{\mathrm{3i}}{x}_{3}+{c}_{\mathrm{1i}}{h}_{1}+{c}_{\mathrm{2i}}{h}_{2}+{c}_{\mathrm{3i}}{h}_{3}+{c}_{\mathrm{4i}}{h}_{4} i=1,2,3 eq 1.1-5 In the previous equation and in what follows, bold characters denote tensors of order at least 1. Thus, the vectors {\dot{d}}_{i} and {x}_{i} represent, respectively, the nodal velocities and coordinates and are given by: > {\dot{d}}_{i}^{t}=({v}_{\mathrm{i1}},{v}_{\mathrm{i2}},{v}_{\mathrm{i3}},...,{v}_{\mathrm{i8}}) > {x}_{i}^{t}=({x}_{\mathrm{i1}},{x}_{\mathrm{i2}},{x}_{\mathrm{i3}},...,{x}_{\mathrm{i8}}) The vectors s and {h}_{\alpha}(\alpha =1,....,4) are given for their part by > {s}^{t}=(1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1) > {h}_{1}^{t}=(1,1,-1,-1,-1,-1,1,1) > {h}_{2}^{t}=(1,-1,-1,1,-1,1,1,-1) > {h}_{3}^{t}=(1,-1,1,-1,1,-1,1,-1) > {h}_{4}^{t}=(-1,1,-1,1,1,-1,1,-1) In order to arrive at an advantageous writing of the discretised gradient operator \mathrm{B} , the 3 vectors {b}_{i} defined by: {b}_{i}^{T}={N}_{,i}(0)= rac{\partial N}{\partial {x}_{i}}{\mid}_{\xi =\eta =\zeta =0} i=1,2,3 eq 1.1-6 are introduced where N represents ({N}_{1},{N}_{2},...,{N}_{8}) . These vectors {b}_{i} represent the simplest form of the under-integrated discretised gradient operator introduced by Hallquist and which is based on the evaluation of the derivatives of the isoparametric shape functions at the origin of the reference frame (\xi ,\eta ,\zeta ) . They can be determined explicitly. Moreover, the following orthogonality conditions can be verified: > {b}_{i}^{T}.{h}_{\alpha}=0 , {b}_{i}^{T}.s=0 , {b}_{i}^{T}.{x}_{j}={\delta}_{ij} > {h}_{\alpha}^{T}.s=0 , {h}_{\alpha}^{T}.{h}_{ eta}=8{\delta}_{\alpha  eta } eq 1.1-7 > i=1à3 , \alpha , eta =1à4 where \delta denotes the Kronecker symbol. At this stage, the unknown constants which appear in the writing of the velocity field ( eq 1.1-4) can be determined by taking the scalar product of equation ( eq 1.1-5) by {b}_{j}^{T} and {\mathrm{h}}_{\alpha}^{\mathrm{T}} , respectively, and using the orthogonality relations above. One obtains: > {a}_{ij}={b}_{j}^{T}.{\dot{d}}_{i} , {c}_{\alpha i}={\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}.{\dot{d}}_{i} with {\gamma}_{\alpha}= rac{1}{8}\left[{h}_{\alpha}-\sum_{j=1}^{3}({h}_{\alpha}^{T}.{x}_{j}){b}_{j}ight] The velocity field is finally put in the following very practical form: > {v}_{i}={a}_{\mathrm{0i}}+({x}_{1}{b}_{1}^{T}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}^{T}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}^{T}+{h}_{1}{\gamma}_{1}^{T}+{h}_{2}{\gamma}_{2}^{T}+{h}_{3}{\gamma}_{3}^{T}+{h}_{4}{\gamma}_{4}^{T}).{\dot{d}}_{i} i=1,2,3 eq 1.1-8 Differentiating the formula above with respect to {x}_{j} , one obtains the velocity gradient: > {v}_{i,j}=({b}_{j}^{T}+\sum_{\alpha =1}^{4}{h}_{\alpha ,j}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}).{\dot{d}}_{i}=({b}_{j}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,j}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}).{\dot{d}}_{i} The discretised gradient operator relating the strain-rate tensor to the vector of nodal velocities by: > {
abla}_{s}v=B.\dot{d} where: {
abla}_{s}v=\left[ egin{array}{c}{v}_{x,x}\ {v}_{y,y}\ {v}_{z,z}\ {v}_{x,y}+{v}_{y,x}\ {v}_{x,z}+{v}_{z,x}\ {v}_{y,x}+{v}_{z,y}\end{array}ight] , \dot{d}=\left[ egin{array}{c}{\dot{d}}_{x}\ {\dot{d}}_{y}\ {\dot{d}}_{z}\end{array}ight] then takes the practical matrix form: B=\left[ egin{array}{ccc}{b}_{x}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,x}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& 0& 0\ 0& {b}_{y}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,y}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& 0\ 0& 0& {b}_{z}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,z}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}\ {b}_{y}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,y}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& {b}_{x}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,x}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& 0\ {b}_{z}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,z}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& 0& {b}_{x}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,x}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}\ 0& {b}_{z}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,z}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}& {b}_{y}^{T}+{h}_{\alpha ,y}{\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}\end{array}ight] eq 1.1-9 The vectors {\gamma}_{\alpha} which appear in B satisfy the following orthogonality conditions: > {\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}.{x}_{j}=0 , {\gamma}_{\alpha}^{T}{h}_{ eta}={\delta}_{\alpha  eta } An element based on this formulation is convergent when it is evaluated exactly. However, the evaluation of this operator B at each of the integration points makes this element too costly for practical applications, and a simplified form of this element is required. 2.2 Variational formulation of the problem The extension of the weak form of the Hu–Washizu variational principle to the case of the mechanics of non-linear solids is written for a single element {\Omega}_{e} 	ext{δπ}(v, ar{\dot{\epsilon}}, ar{\sigma})={\int}_{{\Omega}_{e}}{\delta  ar{\dot{\epsilon}}}^{T}.\sigma d\Omega +\delta {\int}_{{\Omega}_{e}}{ ar{\sigma}}^{T}.(
abla {}_{s}	ext{}v- ar{\dot{\epsilon}})d\Omega -\delta {\dot{d}}^{T}.{f}^{	ext{ext}}=0 eq 1.2-1 where \delta represents a variation, v the velocity field,  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}} the assumed strain rate,  ar{\sigma} the assumed stress, \sigma the stress evaluated by the constitutive law, \dot{d} the nodal velocities, {f}^{	ext{ext}} the external nodal forces and {
abla}_{s}v the symmetric part of the gradient of the velocity field. The “Assumed strain” formulation retained in what follows to build the element is based on a simplified form of the Hu–Washizu variational principle which relies on the fact that the assumed stress is chosen orthogonal to the difference between the symmetric part of the velocity gradient and the assumed strain rate. Thus the second term of equation 1.2-1 is eliminated and one obtains: > \delta \pi ( ar{\dot{\varepsilon}})={\int}_{{\Omega}_{e}}{\delta  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}}}^{T}.\sigma d\Omega -\delta {\dot{d}}^{T}.{f}^{	ext{ext}}=0 eq 1.2-2 In this form, the variational principle is independent of the interpolation of the stress, since the assumed stress no longer appears and therefore does not need to be defined. The discretised equations therefore require only the interpolation of the velocity v and of the assumed strain rate  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}} in the element. If {N}_{I} denotes the isoparametric shape functions of the element, one has: 
u (x,t)=\sum_{I=1}^{m}{N}_{I}(x){\dot{d}}_{I}(t) where m denotes the number of nodes. One deduces: {
abla}_{s}v(x,t)=B(x).\dot{d}(t) The assumed strain rate  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}} is defined for its part by:  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}}(x,t)= ar{B}(x).\dot{d}(t) eq 1.2-3 Substituting equation 1.2-3 into the variational principle 1.2-2 , one obtains: \delta {\dot{d}}^{T}.{\int}_{{\Omega}_{e}}{ ar{B}}^{T}.\sigma d\Omega -\delta {\dot{d}}^{T}.{f}^{	ext{ext}}=0 Since \delta {\dot{d}}^{T} can be chosen arbitrarily, the previous equation leads to: > {f}^{int}={f}^{	ext{ext}} with {f}^{int}={\int}_{{\Omega}_{e}}{ ar{B}}^{T}.\sigma ( ar{\dot{\varepsilon}})d\Omega In the equation above, it is clearly specified that the stress \sigma is computed by the constitutive law from the assumed strain rate  ar{\dot{\varepsilon}} . For non-linear problems, \sigma may also be a function of the integral of the assumed strain rate and of other internal variables. The formulation thus obtained is valid for problems including both types of non-linearity: geometric and material. In the case of linear problems, one has: \sigma =C. ar{\varepsilon}=C. ar{B}.d The internal forces of the element are written: > {f}^{int}={K}_{e}.d with {K}_{e}=\underset{{\Omega}_{e}}{\int}{ ar{B}}^{T}.C. ar{B}d\Omega \stackrel{}{} In a standard displacement approach, the assumed strain rate is identified with the symmetric part of the velocity gradient, which amounts to replacing \stackrel{}{ ar{B}} by \stackrel{}{B} in the previous expressions. One obtains: > {K}_{e}=\underset{{\Omega}_{e}}{\int}{\stackrel{}{B}}^{T}.C.\stackrel{}{B}d\Omega \stackrel{}{} 2.3 “Hourglass” modes associated with zero energy The matrix writing eq 1.1-9 of the discretised gradient operator will make it possible to understand the origin of the hourglass modes, or hourglass modes. As will be seen, these kinematic modes are due to under-integration and are associated with zero energy although they induce a non-zero strain. This anomaly is explained by the difference which under-integration induces between the kernel of the discretised stiffness operator and the kernel of the continuous stiffness operator. Note first that the under-integrated discretised gradient operator (i.e. associated with a single integration point located at the centre of the element) reduces to: > B=\left[ egin{array}{ccc}{b}_{x}^{T}& 0& 0\ 0& {b}_{y}^{T}& 0\ 0& 0& {b}_{z}^{T}\ {b}_{y}^{T}& {b}_{x}^{T}& 0\ {b}_{z}^{T}& 0& {b}_{x}^{T}\ 0& {b}_{z}^{T}& {b}_{y}^{T}\end{array}ight] eq 1.3-1 Indeed, the terms {h}_{\alpha ,i} of equation eq 1.1-9 vanish at the integration point \mid \xi =\eta =\zeta =0 . Let us now analyse the kernel of the stiffness matrix obtained by integration. In the linear case, this elementary matrix is written: {K}_{e}=V{B}^{T}.C.B where V denotes the volume of the element. Examination of the kernel of the under-integrated stiffness amounts to the study of the rank of the matrix B . One must therefore seek the velocity modes \dot{d} with zero strain, that is to say satisfying: > {
abla}_{s}v=B.\dot{d}=0 eq 1.3-2 One must recover in the kernel of {K}_{e} the modes associated with rigid-body motions, that is, in 3D, 3 translations and 3 rotations. The kernel of the continuous operator is therefore of dimension 6 and reduces to the 6 vectors: ( egin{array}{ccc}s& 0& 0\ 0& s& 0\ 0& 0& s\end{array})( egin{array}{ccc}y& z& 0\ -x& 0& z\ 0& -x& -y\end{array}) The first 3 column vectors satisfy 1.3-2 because of {b}_{i}^{T}.s=0 , the last 3 because of {b}_{i}^{T}.{x}_{j}={\delta}_{ij} . But besides the previous rigid modes, the discretised gradient operator given in 1.3-1 annihilates the following 12 other vectors: ( egin{array}{ccc}{h}_{1}& 0& 0\ 0& {h}_{1}& 0\ 0& 0& {h}_{1}\end{array})( egin{array}{ccc}{h}_{2}& 0& 0\ 0& {h}_{2}& 0\ 0& 0& {h}_{2}\end{array}) ( egin{array}{ccc}{h}_{3}& 0& 0\ 0& {h}_{3}& 0\ 0& 0& {h}_{3}\end{array})( egin{array}{ccc}{h}_{4}& 0& 0\ 0& {h}_{4}& 0\ 0& 0& {h}_{4}\end{array}) because {b}_{i}^{T}.{h}_{\alpha}=0 The column vectors above are called hourglass modes or hourglass modes. The 4 modes along \mathit{Ox} are illustrated below: {h}_{1}	ext{warping} {h}_{1}	ext{warping} {h}_{2}	ext{bending} {h}_{1}	ext{warping} z y x {h}_{3}	ext{bending} {h}_{1}	ext{warping} {h}_{4}	ext{nonphysique} {h}_{1}	ext{warping} Hourglass modes along \mathit{Ox} after Belytschko and Bindeman The discretised gradient operator given in 1.3-1 and the exactly integrated one given in 1.1-9 correctly compute the gradients of linear fields and give identical results when they are applied to the 6 rigid-body modes. On the other hand, the exact formula for B properly computes the other strain modes {h}_{\alpha} , whereas the under-integrated form gives erroneous results. The deficiency in the rank of the under-integrated operator B (rank 6 only instead of 18 for exactly integrated B ) results in unrealistic oscillations of the mesh leading to solutions which explode for certain boundary conditions. These under-integrated elements therefore require so-call