u}	o 1 , the sum of the first three terms in each column vector of  ar{{B}_{n}} is zero. 3.3 Choice of the finite elements The two finite elements ADS and ASQBI have been compared on a certain number of tests. It emerges that ASQBI gives better results than ADS in elasticity whereas ADS gives better results in plasticity, its behaviour being a little too soft in the elastic cases. The test case of the cantilever beam in bending V6.04.196 is a good illustration of this. These observations are in good agreement with those of Belytschko and Bindeman [2]. The ASQBI element in elasticity and the ASD element in plasticity have therefore been chosen for implementation in Aster in order to benefit in each case from the best element. Moreover, the user is spared having to choose the finite element. It is recalled that the only difference between the two elements is the matrix  ar{{B}_{n}} . 4 Integration of the element in Code_Aster 4.1 Description and use This element relies on the 3D volume mesh cells HEXA8. 4.1.1 Modelling The modelling 3D_SI is assigned to the designated HEXA8 mesh cells. The usual face elements of the 3D modelling are assigned to the QUAD4 mesh cells. 4.1.2 Material All the material coefficients relating to the constitutive laws valid in small strains for the 3D modellings can be used. 4.1.3 Boundary conditions and loading All the loadings and boundary conditions available on the 3D elements and the face elements are available. 4.1.4 Post-processing options All the post-processing options usually available for the 3D modellings can be used. 4.1.5 Linear buckling analysis The option RIGI_MECA_GE being activated in the catalogue of the element, it is possible to carry out a classical buckling analysis after assembly of the elastic and geometric stiffness matrices. 4.1.6 Non-linear analyses All the constitutive behaviours available for the usual 3D modellings can be used, in small strains. As regards large strains, the only usable option is the approximation PETIT_REAC. Numerical integration is carried out with one Gauss point, just as in material non-linearity. 4.2 Implementation The options are activated in the catalogue meca_hexs8.cata. 4.3 Validation The tests validating this element are, in version 9 of Code_Aster : - SSNV196: 3D beam in bending in elasticity and in plasticity - HSNV125G, PERFE01A, SDNV103, SSND105 use a single element to validate non-linear constitutive behaviours, thus allowing savings in analysis time (1 single Gauss point) 5 Conclusion The 8-node hexahedral element under-integrated with one integration point and stabilised by the ASM method gives correct results on problems where the standard HEXA8 locks (bending, shear), as shown by the test case SSNV196. Moreover it gives significant gains in analysis time. It nevertheless has a drawback in statics (the same as the analogous 2D element, the stabilised under-integrated QUAD4): when the elements are not parallelepiped, the results are affected, or even wrong if the elements have angles far from 90°. The recommended solution is then to resort, on these mesh cells, to the standard HEXA8 (with 8 Gauss points). In explicit dynamics, on the other hand, this drawback does not remain. But the present implementation concerns statics only. 6 Bibliography 1. BELYTSCHKO and L.P. BINDEMAN. Assumed strain stabilization of the 4-node quadrilateral with 1-point quadrature for nonlinear problems. Comput. Methods Appl. Mech. Eng., 88:311‑340, 1991. 1. BELYTSCHKO and L.P. BINDEMAN. Assumed strain stabilization of the 8-node hexahedral element. Comput. Methods Appl. Mech. Eng., 105:225‑260, 1993. 3. F.ABED-MERAÏM and A.COMBESCURE. Stabilisation of under-integrated finite elements. Internal report no. 247 LMT-Cachan, January 2001. 7 Description of the versions of the document: | Aster version | Author(s), Organisation(s) | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | | 9.5 | X Desroches EDF R&D AMA | Initial version |

r3.07.02 Numerical modelling of thin structures: axisymmetric thermo-elasto-plastic shells r3.07.02 Numerical modelling of thin structures: axisymmetric thermo-elasto-plastic shells Summary: A numerical formulation is presented for the modelling of structures whose middle surface has a particular geometry: shells with symmetry of revolution about the axis \mathit{Oy} (modelling COQUE_AXIS). The isotropic thermo-elasto-plastic case is described completely, within the framework of the theories of LOVE‑KIRCHHOFF and of HENCKY-MINDLIN-REISSNER, as well as the various loadings studied, for the isoparametric finite element chosen. 1 Introduction One is concerned in what follows with the mechanical modelling of thin structures whose middle surface has a particular geometry: shells with symmetry of revolution about the axis \mathit{Oy} More particularly, one restricts oneself to the case in which the mechanical parameters (materials, loadings) are independent of one spatial direction (the circumference for the shells of revolution). For the solution of chained thermomechanical problems, one must beforehand use the thermal shell finite element described in R3.11.01 in its axisymmetric version, according to the case. One first reviews below the description of the mechanical model: kinematics, thermo-elasto-plastic constitutive law. The finite element chosen, the interpolation and the integration method are then presented. Finally, a few numerical application results are given, by comparison with analytical solutions. 2 Continuous problem The geometry is defined in a one-dimensional manner by the meridian in the plane (Oxy) for a shell of revolution. 2.1 Description of the geometry, of the kinematics One considers a shell of revolution of axis \mathit{Oy} . For this shell, the middle surface is defined by the curve \omega =	ext{AB} in the plane Oxz : \omega is a meridian for the shell of revolution. Figure 2.1-1: Shell of revolution The curve \omega =	ext{AB} is parametrised b