e condition {\gamma}_{s}=0 ), for example {10}^{6}h/R , where h is the thickness and R a characteristic radius of curvature or a characteristic distance of the loadings: (cf. [bib 2]). In practice the user can supply the value of \kappa under the keyword A_CIS of the command AFFE_CARA_ELEM U4.42.01. 3 Formulation of the finite element and discretisation 3.1 Description of the finite element chosen 3.1.1 Motivations The choice of the HENCKY-MINDLIN-NAGHDI framework to describe the shell kinematics, presented in paragraph [§ 2 ], leads to expressions of the strains in which the derivatives are limited to order 1, unlike the LOVE-KIRCHHOFF model. This offers the advantage of being able to use a finite element of limited order while ensuring conformity. The natural choice is the LAGRANGE element \mathit{P2} , isoparametric, which makes it possible to have a fine representation of a curved geometry and good estimates of the stresses. The degrees of freedom are of course the displacements and the rotations. As stated previously, the LOVE-KIRCHHOFF model can be recovered by penalisation for a very large parameter \kappa affecting the transverse-shear energy. This formulation joins the category of shell finite elements known as “degenerated”, that is to say built by injecting the shell kinematics into elements of three-dimensional continuous media: cf. [bib10]. As for all shell finite elements, particular aspects must be analysed: the taking into account of the rigid-body modes and the risks of membrane or shear locking. In the case of the axisymmetric shell of revolution, there is only one rigid-body mode: the translation along the axis of symmetry \mathit{Oy} . For the finite element to be efficient, it is necessary that the approximations retained for the description of the displacement ensure an exact representation of the state of zero strains (rigid-body mode). In practice, as the notion of rigid-body mode is defined with respect to the global frame, one therefore decides to describe the displacements in the global basis ({e}_{x},{e}_{y}) , in which the rigid-body modes (affine functions) are represented by the interpolation chosen. As regards the risks of locking in membrane and in transverse shear, the usual treatment consists in a selective numerical integration (cf. [bib2]), but practice reveals that these phenomena rarely appear for shells of revolution. 3.1.2 General presentation of the element The reference element chosen is quadratic, isoparametric, with three nodes and three degrees of freedom per node. These degrees of freedom are (see figure ): | {u}_{x},{u}_{y} : | components of the displacement U in the global frame, | | --- | --- | | { eta}_{s} : | the rotation about {e}_{z} of the normal n . | This element is a generalisation of the curved plane-beam element. It is well suited to the discretisation of shells with variable meridional curvature R , cf. [bib2]. Figure 3.1.2-1: Reference element The shape functions (basis functions) are the LAGRANGE polynomials: {\widehat{N}}_{1}\left(\xi ight)=\xi  rac{-1+\xi }{2} {\widehat{N}}_{2}\left(\xi ight)=\xi  rac{1+\xi }{2} 3.1.3 Transformations from the finite element to the reference finite element The geometry is interpolated by means of the coordinates ({x}_{i},{y}_{i}) of the three nodes \mathit{N1} , \mathit{N3} and \mathit{N2} : x\left(\xi ight)=\sum_{i=1}^{3}{x}_{i}{\widehat{N}}_{i}\left(\xi ight) Likewise, by means of the degrees of freedom ({u}_{{x}_{i}},{u}_{{y}_{i}},{ eta}_{{s}_{i}}) at the nodes, one has: {u}_{x}\left(\xi ight)=\sum_{i=1}^{3}{u}_{{x}_{i}}{\widehat{N}}_{i}\left(\xi ight) {u}_{y}\left(\xi ight)=\sum_{i=1}^{3}{u}_{{y}_{i}}{\widehat{N}}_{i}\left(\xi ight) One also needs the Jacobian of the transformation: m(\xi )= rac{	ext{ds}}{d\xi }(\xi )=\sqrt{{({x}_{,\xi })}^{2}+{({y}_{,\xi })}^{2}} and the vectors of the local basis: t\left(\xi ight)= rac{1}{m\left(\xi ight)}\left({x}_{,\xi }{e}_{x}+{y}_{,\xi }{e}_{z}ight) Finally: \cos\alpha = rac{{