n the middle fibre and on the inner and outer skins (with or without curvature correction): option EPSI_ELNO, - the three-dimensional stresses {\sigma}_{\alpha  eta } on the middle fibre and on the inner and outer skins (with or without curvature correction): option SIGM_ELNO, - the generalised forces {N}_{\alpha  eta } and {M}_{\alpha  eta } (with or without curvature correction): option EFGE_ELNO. These values at the nodes are obtained by extrapolation from the values at the GAUSS points of the element, according to the method set out in [bib4] R3.06.03. Finally, one can also have the values {N}_{\alpha  eta } and {M}_{\alpha  eta } at the GAUSS points of the element: option SIEF_ELGA. No post-processing of stresses or of generalised forces is for the moment available for non-linear material behaviours. 4 Conclusion The finite elements which we propose were chosen with a very particular aim: analysis of thin axisymmetric structures with the concern of obtaining a good accuracy on the membrane and flexural solution while having an element that is simple to implement and not too costly. The choice of the degrees of freedom allows a good representation of the boundary conditions. Moreover, this displacement and rotation formulation leads to elements of lower degree: the elements are \mathit{P2} in membrane and \mathit{P2} in bending. It appears that they are easy to handle and that their formulation makes it possible to use a simple pre- and post-processor structure, a non-negligible advantage for producing fairly fine meshes (one-dimensional) and for visualising the results easily (on a simple curve). The kinematics chosen: formulation of HENCKY-MINDLIN-NAGHDI, in displacements and rotations of the middle surface, makes it possible to introduce the transverse-shear energy (of interest for shells of moderate thickness). This energy may be affected by a correction factor k : if one wishes to place oneself in REISSNER theory, it suffices to choose k=5/6 instead of 1 (but of course, the deflection W and the rotations  eta are in this theory only weighted averages through the thickness). Moreover, the LOVE-KIRCHHOFF shell formulation (for very thin structures) can be simulated by penalisation of the condition of vanishing of the transverse distortion, by choosing a factor k={10}^{6}	imes , :math: h being the thickness and L a characteristic distance (radius of curvature, zone of application of the loads…). The non-linear behaviours in plane stress are available for these elements. One points out, however, that the stresses generated by the transverse distortion are treated elastically, for want of a better approach. Indeed, the taking into account of a non-zero constant transverse shear through the thickness and the determination of the associated correction on the shear stiffness relative to a model satisfying the boundary conditions are not possible and therefore make the use of these elements, when the transverse shear is non-zero, rigorously impossible in plasticity. Rigorously, for non-linear behaviours, one should therefore use these elements within the framework of Love–Kirchhoff theory. Elements corresponding to the mechanical elements exist in thermal analysis; the thermomechanical chainings are therefore available with three-node thermal shell finite elements described in R3.11.01 in its axisymmetric version. In the test cases treated, the locking phenomena did not appear. The decomposition of the strain energy will make it possible, if need be, to integrate selectively the terms responsible for the locking, such a modification not being expected to pose any particular difficulty. A more detailed study must of course be carried out on this subject, as regards the numerical methods to be used to avoid this locking when the thickness becomes small. The developments that can be envisaged are: - anisotropy, in order to be able to treat multilayer shells, - buckling problems, - the decomposition into FOURIER series in order to study non-axisymmetric problems of shells of revolution, - the taking into account of a variable thickness … 5 Bibliography 1. ALMROTH - D. BRUSH: Buckling of bars, plates and shells. Mc Graw-Hill 1975. 2. J.L. BATOZ - G. DHATT: Modelling of structures by finite elements. Volume 3 Shells. Hermès 1992. 1. BUI - F. VOLDOIRE: Presentation of a cylindrical shell finite element, P2 in membrane and Morley in bending. Note EDF-DER-MMN, HI 71/6715, of 10.10.90. 1. DESROCHES: Computation of the stresses at the nodes by a local least-squares smoothing method. Note EDF-DER-MMN of 20.01.92 R3.06.03. 1. DHATT - G. TOUZOT: A presentation of the finite-element method. 2nd edition. Maloine SA 1984. 6. GREEN - ZERNA: Theoretical elasticity. Univ. Oxford 1954. 7. TIMOSHENKO and WOINOWSKY-KRIEGER: Plates and shells. Béranger 1961. 1. VOLDOIRE: Formulation and numerical assessment of an enriched axisymmetric elastoplastic shell model. Note EDF-DER-MMN, HI-73/7518, of 04.02.92. 1. BUI: Shear in plates and shells: modelling and computation. Note EDF‑DER-MMN, HI-71/7784, of 20.02.92. 1. ANDRIEUX - F. VOLDOIRE: Shell models. Applications in linear statics. CEA-EDF-INRIA Summer School in Numerical Analysis 1992. 6 Description of the versions of the document | Version Aster | Author(s) Organisation(s) | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | | 4 | F.VOLDOIRE, C.SEVIN EDF-R&D/AMA | Initial text | | 5 | P.MASSIN, EDF-R&D/AMA | Update |

r3.07.05 Volumetric shell elements in geometric non-linearity r3.07.05 Volumetric shell elements in geometric non-linearity Summary: We present in this document the theoretical formulation and the numerical implementation of a volumetric shell finite element for analyses in geometric non-linearity. This approach must make it possible to take into account large displacements and large rotations of thin structures, whose ratio of thickness to characteristic length is less than 1/10 . One will take care that these rotations remain less than 2\pi . This formulation is based on a 3D continuum approach, degenerated by the introduction of the shell kinematics in plane stresses into the weak form of equilibrium. The strain measure that we retain is that of Green–Lagrange, energetically conjugate to the second Piola–Kirchhoff stresses. The formulation of equilibrium is therefore total Lagrangian. The fully geometrically non-linear problem is examined first. The case of linear buckling is treated as a limiting case of the first approach. 1 Introduction The large transformations of a shell are characterised by large displacements of the middle surface and large rotations of the fibres initially normal to this surface. The transformation is therefore represented exactly, at least in the continuous problem. The derivation of the finite-element objects associated with the linearised system of equations arising from the principle of virtual work is carried out without any simplifying assumption on the displacements or the rotations. Moreover, a new selective numerical integration scheme is presented in order to solve the problem of membrane and transverse-shear locking. The rotation degrees of freedom retained are the components of the spatial iterative rotation vector. Between two iterations, it is the vector of the infinitesimal rotation superimposed on the deformed configuration. This choice leads to a tangent stiffness matrix that is not symmetric. This is due to the non-vectorial character of large rotations, which in reality belong to the differential manifold \mathit{SO}(3) . The rotations must remain less than 2\pi because of the choice of update of the large rotations implemented in Code_Aster , for which there is no bijection between the total rotation vector and the orthogonal rotation matrix. An important difference with respect to the linear analysis is to be noted. The finite-element objects are constructed directly in the global frame; the nodal displacements and rotations are measured in the global frame. 2 Formulation In this chapter, we present the various equations governing the problem of deformation of the shell within the framework of a theory of large transformations. 2.1 Geometry of the volumetric shell elements The volumetric shell is represented by the volume \Omega (set of the points Q({\xi}_{3}\mathrm{
