u ALPHA: \alpha .. RHO: ho ..) For a thermo-elastic isotropic behaviour homogeneous through the thickness one uses the keyword ELAS in DEFI_MATERIAU where one defines the coefficients E , Young's modulus, 
u , Poisson's ratio, \alpha , the thermal expansion coefficient and ho the density. AFFE_CHAR_MECA ( DDL_IMPO : ( DX:.. DY:.. DZ:.. DRX:.. DRY:.. DRZ:.. degré de liberté de plaque dans le repère global. FORCE_COQUE: (FX:.. FY:.. FZ:.. MX:.. MY:.. MZ:.. ) These are surface forces on plate elements. These forces can be given in the global frame or in the user frame defined by ANGL_REP. FORCE_NODALE: (FX:.. FY:.. FZ:.. MX:.. MY:.. MZ:.. ) These are shell forces in the global frame. 7.3 Analysis in geometrically non-linear “elasticity” The analysis imposes the following user instructions: COMPORTEMENT : (RELATION : 'elas' COQUE_NCOU : 1 (ou plus) DEFORMATION : 'GROT_GDEP') The numerical integration through the thickness is based on a multi-layer approach with 3 integration points per layer. This is the approach currently used in material non-linearity R3.07.04. The post-processing options SIGM_ELNO of the stresses and VARI_ELNO of the internal variables (here zero) are by default activated at the convergence of each archived time step. 7.4 Implementation The options FULL_MECA, RIGI_MECA_TANG, and RAPH_MECA are already active in the elementary catalogues mec3qu9h.cata and mec3tr7h.cata for material non-linearity. They direct the computation towards /fort/te0414.f, then towards /fort/vdxnlr.f in order to compute and store, among other things, the symmetric tangent stiffness matrix in the address corresponding to the mode MMATUURPMATUUR. For the geometrically non-linear analysis, the computation of the tangent stiffness matrix is directed towards the new routine VDGNLR. This matrix is not symmetric and must be stored in the address corresponding to the mode MMATUNS PMATUNS. One defines the two local modes, symmetric and non-symmetric at the same time, as output of the elementary catalogues. The non-symmetric tangent stiffness matrix in geometric non-linearity is stored at the address reserved for a non-symmetric matrix. On the other hand, if it is a matter of material non-linearity in small strains, the whole tangent stiffness matrix is stored at the address corresponding to the non-symmetric mode. The lower triangular part is duplicated from the upper triangular part. The material non-linear analysis in small strains therefore also proceeds in non-symmetric form. 7.4.1 Modification of TE0414 The computation is directed towards /fort/vdgnlr.f when the type of behaviour COMPORTEMENT is verified, that is to say when the problem is geometrically non-linear. 7.4.2 Addition of a routine VDGNLR According to the option, the routine /fort/vdgnlr.f has the role of: Options: FULL_MECA and RAPH_MECA: Computing the 6 components of the state of the local Cauchy stresses (confused with the state of the second Piola–Kirchhoff stresses) at the normal numerical integration points and the nodal vector of the internal forces. They are stored in the local modes ECONTPG PCONTPR and MVECTUR PVECTUR respectively. As an additional note, one will note that SIEF_ELGA/SIGM_ELGA after the solution is of type PK2 whereas SIEF_ELNO/SIGM_ELNO in post-processing is of Cauchy type. Options: FULL_MECA and RIGI_MECA_TANG: Computing and storing the non-symmetric tangent stiffness matrix in the mode MMATUNS PMATUNS . 7.5 Analysis in linear buckling The option RIGI_MECA_GE, inactive until now, is activated in the elementary catalogues mec3qu9h.cata and mec3tr7h.cata. The new TE0402 is dedicated to the computation of the geometric stiffness matrix due to the initial stresses for Euler buckling. One retrieves the plane states of the local Cauchy stresses (component {S}_{	ext{nn}} zero) at the normal Gauss numerical integration points. These stress states must be obtained by post-processing with the computation option SIEF_ELGA following a linear analysis (mode ECONTPG PCONTRR). In an Euler buckling analysis, the Cauchy stresses \left[\sigma ight] can be confused with the second Piola–Kirchhoff stresses \left[Sight] . It is for this reason that we shall keep the notation \left[Sight] . The stiffness matrix of the initial stresses can be decomposed into a classical symmetric part and a non-classical non-symmetric part. The first is computed as a function of the global stress tensor 3	imes 3 , unlike the second which, for its part, is computed as a function of the local stress vector 5	imes 1 . Since the current algorithm for solving the eigenvalue problem R5.06.01 considers only symmetric matrices, we force the symmetry of the non-classical part of the geometric matrix before storing the upper triangular part of the whole matrix in the mode MMATUUR PMATUUR. 8 Conclusion The formulation that we have just described applies to the analyses of thin curved structures in large displacements, whose ratio of thickness to characteristic length is less than 1/10 . It comes as a direct complement to the finite elements described in the previous reference documentation R3.07.04 and used within the framework of small displacements and strains. They are supported on the same quadrilateral and triangular mesh cells. Their formulation rests on a 3D continuum approach in which one introduces a shell kinematics of Hencky–Mindlin–Naghdi type, in plane stresses, into the weak formulation of equilibrium. The strain measure retained is that of Green–Lagrange, energetically conjugate to the second Piola–Kirchhoff stresses. The formulation of equilibrium is therefore total Lagrangian. The transverse distortion is treated in the same way as in R3.07.04. The rotations must remain less than 2\pi because of the choice of update of the large rotations implemented in Code_Aster for which there is no bijection between the total rotation vector and the orthogonal rotation matrix. Because of the singularity of the tangent stiffness matrix with respect to the component of the rotation about the transform of the normal, one defines a fictitious strain energy associated with this rotation. With this rotation, one associates a constant torsional stiffness. The internal forces associated with this potential energy are taken into account. This potential energy, non-zero, does not correspond to a physical strain. It must therefore remain negligible, which the user can control by imposing a value of COEF_RIGI_DRZ ranging from {10}^{-3} to {10}^{-5} . For the post-processing of the stresses, one restricts oneself to the framework of small strains. One has then been able to establish the identity between the Piola–Kirchhoff stress tensor observed in the initial geometry and the Cauchy stress tensor in the deformed geometry. Moreover, the stress state being plane for the Piola–Kirchhoff tensor, one recovers this property for the Cauchy stress state. It is to be noted that in more general contexts, this property is not retained. Linear buckling is treated as a particular case of the geometrically non-linear problem. It rests on the assumption of a linear dependence of the displacement, strain and stress fields on the load level, before the critical load. It follows that one can expand the tangent stiffness matrix linearly with respect to the load level. One then recovers the geometric part of the matrices of the general geometric non-linearity obtained by identifying the deformed shape of the normal to the middle surface and the initial normal, which is consistent with the linearity of the strains as a function of the load level. 9 Bibliography 1. Al Mikdad M., “Statics and Dynamics of Beams in Large Rotations and Solution of Non-Linear Instability Problems”, doctoral thesis, Université de Technologie de Compiègne, 1998. 2. Bathe K.J., “Finite Element Proceedings in Engineering Analysis”, Prentice Hall, New Jersey, 1982. 3. Cardonna A., “An Integrated Approach to Mechanism Analysis”, doctoral thesis, Université de Liège, 1989. 4. Crisfield M.A., “Non-linear Finite Element Analysis of Solids and Structures”, Volume 1: Essentials, John Wiley, Chichester, 1994. 5. Crisfield M.A., “Non-linear Finite Element Analysis of Solids and Structures