r4.01.02 Anisotropic elasticity r4.01.02 Anisotropic elasticity Summary This document deals with anisotropic thermoelasticity, used for the modellings of 3D and 2D continuous media (C_PLAN, D_PLAN, AXIS), or the layers of composite shells. The thermoelastic medium may be anisotropic in the 3 directions (one speaks of orthotropic elasticity), or isotropic in two directions (one speaks of transversely isotropic elasticity), or it may present a cubic symmetry. > Contents 1 Introduction The objective of this document is to give the expression for the compliance and Hooke matrices for orthotropic, transversely isotropic and cubic-symmetry thermoelastic materials in the 3D, 2D plane-stress, 2D plane-strain and axisymmetric cases. Strictly speaking, for linear thermoelastic materials, the stresses are linear functions of the strains and of the temperature differential. One writes: {\sigma}_{ij}={H}_{ijkl}\left({\epsilon}_{kl}-{\alpha}_{kl}\left(T-{T}_{\mathit{réf}}ight)ight) We speak of Hooke “matrices” because, for the sake of simplification, we have not adopted the notation of a fourth-order tensor for \mathrm{H} , in favour of the notation of W. Voigt, in which the second-order tensors are represented by vectors with 6 components and the fourth-order tensors by 6	imes 6 matrices. The symmetric nature of \sigma and \epsilon and the adoption for these second-order tensors of a vector form make it possible to write: \left\lbrace \sigma ightbrace =\left[\mathrm{H}ight]\left(\left\lbrace \epsilon ightbrace -\left\lbrace \alpha ightbrace \left(T-{T}_{\mathit{réf}}ight)ight) where \left\lbrace \sigma ightbrace and \left\lbrace \epsilon ightbrace are the vector representation of the second-order tensors \sigma and \epsilon , and where \left[\mathrm{H}ight] , the Hooke matrix, is a 6	imes 6 matrix, necessarily symmetric, that is a priori 21 independent coefficients, in the three-dimensional case: \left[\mathrm{H}ight]=\left\lbrace  egin{array}{cccccc}{H}_{11}& {H}_{12}& {H}_{13}& {H}_{14}& {H}_{15}& {H}_{16}\ & {H}_{22}& {H}_{23}& {H}_{24}& {H}_{25}& {H}_{26}\ & & {H}_{33}& {H}_{34}& {H}_{35}& {H}_{36}\ 	ext{SYM}& & & {H}_{44}& {H}_{45}& {H}_{46}\ & & & & {H}_{55}& {H}_{56}\ & & & & & {H}_{66}\end{array}ightbrace The vector \left\lbrace \alpha ightbrace denotes the vector representation of the second-order thermal-expansion tensor, necessarily symmetric, that is a priori 6 independent coefficients, in the three-dimensional case. 2 Typology of the Hooke matrices 2.1 Orthotropy This is a situation in which the elastic material exhibits two symmetries with respect to two perpendicular planes ( orthorhombic symmetry). The elasticity tensor has a priori 9 independent coefficients, a consequence of the relations obtained with these two symmetries among the 21 coefficients. The thermal-expansion tensor has a priori 3 independent coefficients, a consequence of the relations obtained with these two symmetries. In the orthotropy axes: \left[\mathrm{H}ight]=\left\lbrace  egin{array}{cccccc}{H}_{11}& {H}_{12}& {H}_{13}& 0& 0& 0\ & {H}_{22}& {H}_{23}& 0& 0& 0\ & & {H}_{33}& 0& 0& 0\ 	ext{SYM}& & & {H}_{44}& 0& 0\ & & & & {H}_{55}& 0\ & & & & & {H}_{66}\end{array}ightbrace \left\lbrace \alpha ightbrace =\left\lbrace  egin{array}{c}{\alpha}_{11}\phantom{ule{2em}{0ex}}\ {\alpha}_{22}\phantom{ule{2em}{0ex}}\ {\alpha}_{33}\phantom{ule{2em}{0ex}}\ 0\ 0\ 0\end{array}ightbrace 2.2 Transverse isotropy Transverse (or rotational) isotropy is a restriction of orthotropy in which one has isotropy in one of the two orthogonal planes of elastic symmetry, following an invariance by rotation of 2\pi /3 about the axis orthogonal to the plane of transverse isotropy, for example {x}_{3}=0 . The elasticity tensor has a priori 5 independent coefficients. The matrix \left[\mathrm{H}ight] will have the same form as for orthotropy but with four additional relations between the components. Thus one will have, for transverse isotropy in the plane 