r4.02.05 Absorbing boundary elements r4.02.05 Absorbing boundary elements Summary: This document describes the implementation in code_aster of absorbing boundary elements. These elements, of paraxial type, whose theory is described here, are assigned to boundaries of elastic or fluid domains in order to treat 2D or 3D problems of soil–structure or soil–fluid–structure interaction. They make it possible to satisfy the Sommerfeld condition verifying the anechoic assumption: the elimination of non-physical diffracted elastic or acoustic plane waves coming from infinity. 1 Introduction 1.1 Problem of a semi-infinite medium for SSI Standard problems of seismic response and of soil–structure or soil–fluid–structure interaction lead one to consider domains that are infinite or assumed to be so. For example, in the case of dams subjected to an earthquake, one often deals with reservoirs of large size which allow us to make the anechoic assumption: the waves that travel towards the far end of the reservoir do not "come back". The aim is to reduce the size of the structure to be meshed and to make it possible to run complex analyses with present computing resources. One proposes in r4.02.05-domaine_isfs a diagram that describes the type of situations considered. Domain for soil–fluid–structure interaction. Throughout the document, one considers that the boundary of the finite-element mesh of the soil lies in a domain with elastic constitutive behaviour. The theory of elliptic systems simply ensures the existence and uniqueness of the solution of acoustic or elastoplastic problems in bounded domains, under the assumption of boundary conditions ensuring closure of the problem. It is different for infinite domains. One must resort to a particular condition, called the Sommerfeld condition, formulated in the infinite directions of the problem. This condition ensures in particular, in the case of the diffraction of a plane wave (elastic or acoustic) by a structure, the elimination of the non-physical diffracted waves coming from infinity, which the classical conditions on the edges of the domain at a finite distance are not sufficient to ensure. 1.2 State of the art of numerical approaches The preferred method for treating infinite domains is that of boundary elements (or integral equations). The fundamental solution used automatically satisfies the Sommerfeld condition. However, the use of this method is conditional on knowledge of this fundamental solution, which is impossible in the case of a soil with complex geometry, for example, or when the soil or the structure are non-linear. One must then resort to finite elements. From then on, particular conditions at the boundary of the finite-element mesh are necessary in order to forbid the reflection of the outgoing diffracted waves and thus to reproduce artificially the Sommerfeld condition. Several methods make it possible to identify boundary conditions meeting our requirements. Some lead to an exact solution of the problem: they are called "consistent boundaries". They are based on a precise taking into account of wave propagation in the infinite domain. For example, if this domain can be assumed elastic and with a simple stratigraphy far from the structure, one may consider a finite-element–integral-equation coupling. One of the problems of this solution is that it is not local in space: one must take stock over the whole boundary separating the finite domain from the infinite domain, which necessarily leads us to a substructuring problem. This non-locality in space is characteristic of consistent boundaries. To arrive at boundary terms that are local in space, one may use the theory of infinite elements. These are elements of infinite dimension whose basis functions reproduce as well as possible the propagation of elastic or acoustic waves at infinity. These functions must be close to the solution because the classical mathematical theorems no longer ensure the convergence of the computed result towards the solution with such elements. In fact, one can find an analogy between the search for satisfactory basis functions and that for a fundamental solution for the integral equations. The geometric constraints are fairly similar but, above all, this search has a major drawback: it depends on the frequency. Consequently, such boundaries, whether local in space or not, can only be used in the Fourier domain, which rules out a certain category of problems, with constitutive non-linearities or large displacements for example. One therefore comes to having to find efficient absorbing boundaries that are local in space and in time in order to treat, with finite elements, transient problems posed on infinite domains. In what follows we shall present the theory of paraxial elements, which achieve the desired absorption with an efficiency inversely proportional to their simplicity of implementation, as well as the description of the implementation constraints in code_aster. The developments for treating 3D problems are presented. Those for the 2D cases have been carried out and their theory is deduced simply from the 3D modelling. 2 Theory of paraxial elements This part presents the principle of the paraxial approximation in the case of linear elastodynamics. Two theoretical approaches make it possible to grasp the spirit and the practical implementation of elastic paraxial elements: the first is due to Cohen and Jennings and the second to Modaressi. The application of the theory of paraxial elements to the fluid case will be made in the following part. In all that follows, as shown in r4.02.05-domaine_isfs, one assumes that the boundary of the soil mesh is located in a domain with elastic constitutive behaviour. The approach of Modaressi implemented in code_aster makes it possible both to build absorbing boundaries and to introduce the incident seismic field. 2.1 Spectral impedance of the boundary To obtain the paraxial equation, we must first determine the 