e structure produced statically by the imposed displacement of the supports {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{S}} : {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{e}}=\Psi {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{s}} , - \Psi est la matrice des modes statiques. Les modes statiques représentent la réponse de la structure à un déplacement unitaire imposé à chaque degré de liberté de liaison (les autres étant bloqués), en l’absence de forces extérieures. Ainsi, \mathrm{K}\Psi =0 , that is, {	ext{KX}}_{e}=0 . In the case of the single support (all the supports undergo the same imposed motion), \Psi is a rigid-body mode. Assumption in code_aster: One assumes that the damping dissipated by the structure is of viscous type, that is that the damping force is proportional to the relative velocity of the structure. Thus, \mathrm{C}{\dot{\mathrm{X}}}_{e}=0 . The fundamental equation of dynamics in the relative frame is then written: \mathrm{M}{\ddot{\mathrm{X}}}_{\mathrm{r}}+\mathrm{C}{\dot{\mathrm{X}}}_{\mathrm{r}}+{	ext{KX}}_{\mathrm{r}}=-\mathrm{M}\Psi \ddot{{\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{S}}} The operator CALC_CHAR_SEISME U4.63.01 computes the term -\mathrm{M}\Psi , ou plus exactement , -\mathrm{M}\Psi \mathrm{d} where \mathrm{d} is a unit vector such that {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{s}}=\mathrm{d}.f\left(tight) with f a scalar function of time. One distinguishes two types of seismic loading introduced in code_aster by means of the operator CALC_CHAR_SEISME: 1. The loading of type MONO_APPUI, for which \Psi is the identity matrix (the static modes are rigid-body modes), 2. The loading of type MULTI_APPUI, for which \Psi is arbitrary. According to the method of taking the incident field into account with the paraxial elements presented in the first part, we need to know on the boundary the displacement and the stresses due to the incident field. For the loading of type MULTI_APPUI, only the displacement is directly accessible at every time instant. It therefore seems difficult to allow the use of such a loading mode with paraxial elements in the soil. Moreover, if such a loading models the imposed displacements of the supports, it does not require a modelling of the soil since the whole influence is taken into account by these displacements. The MONO_APPUI case can be perceived differently. It represents an overall acceleration applied to the model. From then on, wave propagation in the soil may have a role to play in the behaviour of the structure, since the motions of the soil–structure interface are not imposed. Furthermore, the paraxial elements can be used with this type of loading because it does not create stresses at the boundary of the mesh (a rigid-body mode does not create strains). From then on, one has all the data necessary for the computation of the absorbing impedance on the boundary. Note 1: In the case of a seismic loading MONO_APPUI, the dynamic computation is done in the relative frame. If one returns to the term to be discretised on the paraxial elements (see the first part), one notes that {u}_{i} corresponds exactly to the entrainment displacement {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathrm{e}} presented above. Thus, \mathrm{u}-{\mathrm{u}}_{i} corresponds to the relative displacement computed during the analysis. From then on, the relation to be taken into account on the paraxial elements in such a configuration is simply: t(u)={A}_{0}( rac{\partial u}{\partial t}) Note 2: In the case of a soil–fluid–structure interaction analysis with an infinite fluid, the pressure to be taken into account for the computation of the anechoic impedance in the fluid is indeed the absolute pressure, if one has no incident field in the fluid (which is often the case). The correction that one could dispense with for the soil must then be made for the fluid paraxial elements. 4.2 Implementation of the elements in transient and harmonic analysis 4.2.1 Implementation in transient analysis The mode of implementation of the elastic paraxial elements in transient analysis is very close to that presented for the fluid elements. The difference comes essentially from the need to decompose the displacement into a component along the normal to the element, corresponding to a wave P , and a component in the plane of the element, corresponding to a wave S . One is then in a position to discretise the impedance relation introduced in the first part: \mathrm{t}\left(\mathrm{u}ight)=ho {C}_{p} rac{\partial {\mathrm{u}}_{3}}{\partial t}+ho {C}_{s} rac{\partial {\mathrm{u}}^{	ext{/}}}{\partial t} One does not return to the time-integration scheme that has already been described in the previous part, knowing that one considers the impedance relation by a damping operator added to the left-hand side. For taking into account the additional term: {\mathrm{t}}_{1}\left(\mathrm{u}ight)= rac{\lambda +2\mu }{L}{u}_{3}{\mathrm{e}}_{3}+ rac{\mu}{L}{\mathrm{u}}^{'} one uses this time a stiffness operator added to the left-hand side. 4.2.2 Implementation in harmonic analysis The fluid acoustic elements of code_aster already offer the possibility of taking into account an impedance imposed at the boundary of the mesh in harmonic analysis. This corresponds to the treatment of a term in {\omega}^{3} in the equations, as mentioned above. It is tempting to introduce the possibility of imposing an absorbing impedance for an elastic problem in harmonic analysis. For a computation of the harmonic response of an infinite structure, taking the absorbing impedance into account as a correction of the right-hand side is obviously not applicable. However, the impedance relation at order 0 expresses the surface terms as a function of the velocity of the nodes of the element. One can therefore build a pseudo-matrix of viscous damping expressing the presence of the infinite domain. In the same way, one builds (cf. 4.1.1) a pseudo-matrix of stiffness completing the role of the damping matrix defined previously. The decomposition of the impedance relation according to the normal or tangential components of the displacement on the element forces us to build the impedance matrix in a local frame on the element. One defines this local frame in the elementary routine as well as the transformation matrix that allows the return to the global basis. 4.3 Seismic loading mode by plane wave In addition to the methods of taking the seismic loading into account that are already available, and because of the unsuitability of the MULTI_APPUI mode with the paraxial elements, it seems interesting to introduce a principle of loading by plane wave. This corresponds to the loadings classically encountered in soil–structure interaction computations by integral equations. 4.3.1 Characterisation of a plane wave in transient analysis In harmonic analysis, an elastic plane wave is characterised by its direction, its angular frequency and its type (wave P for compression waves, waves \mathrm{SV} or \mathrm{SH} for shear waves). In transient analysis, the datum of the angular frequency, corresponding to a wave that is stationary in time, must be replaced by the datum of a displacement profile whose propagation over time in the direction of the wave will be taken into account. Les directions des ondes :math: P , :math: \mathit{SV} et :math: \mathit{SH} sont déterminées à partir du vecteur :math: V renseigné par le paramètre DIRECTION. A savoir : :math: P est colinéaire à :math: V et normé à 1, :math: \mathit{SH} est l'intersection du plan horizontal et du plan normal à :math: V , et normé à 1, :math: \mathit{SV} est le produit vectoriel de :math: \mathit{SH} et de :math: P . Il existe un cas d'indétermination avec cette règle quand le plan horizontal et le plan normal sont confondus. Dans ce cas, si :math: V=Z purement vertical, on impose :math: \mathit{SH}=Y , et :math: \mathit{SV}=X . More precisely, one will consider a plane wave in the form: u(x,t)=f(k.x-{C}_{p}t)k for a wave P (with k a unit vector) \mathrm{u}\left(\mathrm{x},tight)=f\left(k.\mathrm{x}-{C}_{S}tight)\wedge k for a wave S (with again k a uni