 are taken into account. Generally, in soil–structure interaction (SSI) studies, the propagation of a seismic wave in the vertical direction is considered as the standard approach. Indeed, in most cases, one expects a verticalisation of the waves in the sedimentary basin in connection with the variation of the propagation speed of the shear waves ( v_S ). In the case where the structures are founded on rock (thus without the verticalisation effect by Snell's law due to superficial layers with a lower v_S ) and for shallow seismic scenarios, the assumption of propagation in the vertical direction is not very realistic. Modelling with vertical propagation makes it possible to represent wave propagation fairly simply from analytical solutions or simplified models. However, this approach does not make it possible to take into account certain phase shifts due to the passage of an inclined wave. That is why it is important to adapt the modes of computation of the propagation of vertical waves to inclined waves. In this part, we present: - The theoretical elements on elastic waves in isotropic solids - The setting up of a modelling exclusively for homogeneous undamped media. For this specific case, the analytical formulation is known and makes it possible to impose directly the oblique plane-wave loading on the boundaries - The setting up of a methodology generalising the application of inclined plane waves to the boundaries of the models in heterogeneous and/or damped media 4.4.1 Elastic plane waves in isotropic solids: reflection and transmission at interfaces The phenomenon of reflection and transmission of waves at interfaces is treated in this chapter . Subsequently, the waves studied are harmonic of angular frequency \omega and, for the sake of simplification, the time dependence e^{j\omega t} is omitted in the expressions of the displacement fields. One studies the reflection of a harmonic plane wave of angular frequency \omega at the free surface of an isotropic an