r4.03.05 Parametric and non-parametric probabilistic models in dynamics r4.03.05 Parametric and non-parametric probabilistic models in dynamics Summary: This document describes two probabilistic approaches, one parametric and the other non-parametric, in order to take into account model uncertainties and modelling uncertainties for dynamic systems in structural mechanics. The non-parametric approach is specific to the solution on a modal basis of linear systems (in transient or harmonic analysis) and to the solution on a modal basis of systems with localised non-linearities (in transient analysis). The parametric approach is a priori universal, but it is presented more particularly in the case where it is combined with the non-parametric approach. 1 General points Uncertainties, even when they are small, can significantly change the prediction of the vibratory behaviour of the structure ([bib13], [bib16]). It is therefore necessary to take them into account in a quantifiable and explicit manner in order to increase the realism and the robustness of the predictions. In this context, a probabilistic model of the uncertainties contributes to the realism of the approach. The classical probabilistic approach, called parametric, makes it possible to incorporate into the mechanical analysis the uncertainties on the data, for example the parametric uncertainties on the geometry, the boundary conditions or the material properties. In this approach, each parameter identified as a source of random uncertainties is modelled by a random variable. The input parameters of the model being thus characterised, the probabilistic numerical methods seek to characterise in a probabilistic way the result quantity or quantities of the model. For complex structures, for which the vibratory behaviour depends on a large number of parameters, this type of probabilistic analysis is limited by the large quantity of information necessary to characterise the input parameters and by the difficulties of implementing the propagation of the variability. A new approach, called the non-parametric probabilistic approach to random uncertainties in structural dynamics, was recently proposed by C. Soize ([bib20] to [bib24]). This approach makes it possible to take into account model uncertainties (uncertainties on the geometry for example) and modelling uncertainties (uncertainties on the beam or plate kinematics for example). It is based on the construction of random matrices of linear dynamic systems, after projection onto a modal basis. These two probabilistic approaches, one parametric and the other non-parametric, are complementary. Thus a mixed approach, parametric and non-parametric, can be developed (an original method that has given rise to publications ([bib6] and [bib11] ). In particular, this mixed method is well suited to taking uncertainties into account in the analysis of a non-linear dynamic system composed of a linear structure reduced on a modal basis and of localised non-linearities. Indeed, the uncertainties at the level of the linear structure can be treated naturally by the non-parametric approach and the uncertainties on the non-linearities can be treated naturally by the parametric approach. The basic numerical model of the non-linear dynamic system is a finite-element model which will be called the « mean finite-element model ». Whether one is concerned with the parametric or the non-parametric approach, the probability laws must be defined adequately and as objectively as possible from this mean model. A Gaussian model of the random matrices is not suited to low-frequency dynamics (negative eigenfrequencies). In order to construct the corresponding probability laws, one uses Jayne's principle of maximum entropy ([bib14], [bib15], [bib17]) as well as the available information (mean finite-element model, algebraic properties of the matrices, etc.) In this document, we present the non-parametric approach for transient or harmonic solutions of the dynamic system. The parametric approach is presented more particularly in the case where it is combined with the non-parametric method. Readers seeking the fundamental results of stochastic dynamics may refer to [18] and readers seeking the theoretical details of the probabilistic approach presented in this document may refer to [bib19]. Examples of uses of the approach are given in [bib5], and [bib7]. In [bib10], experimental tests made it possible to show the predictive character of the approach. 2 Modellings of the dynamic system 2.1 Mean finite-element model 2.1.1 Transient solution in absolute coordinates In the absolute frame, the mechanical system is modelled by the finite-element method. This basic model (in general the one that would have been used in the deterministic study) is designated under the name «mean finite-element model». All quantities relating to the mean models are underlined. Let t\mapsto \underline{\mathrm{y}}(t) be the transient response in the absolute frame of the «mean finite-element model» defined on the time interval of the study [0,T] and with values in {ℝ}^{k} where k is the number of degrees of freedom. The mass, damping and stiffness matrices are respectively denoted \left[\underline{M}ight] , \left[\underline{D}ight] and \left[\underline{K}ight] . The transient response \underline{y}(t) of the «mean finite-element model» satisfies the following discretised non-linear differential equation: \left[\underline{M}ight]\underline{\ddot{y}}(t)+\left[\underline{D}ight]\underline{\dot{y}}+{f}_{c}(t,\underline{\dot{y}(t)},\underline{y(t)},\underline{w})=f(t) , t , eq 2.1.1-1 with the initial conditions, \underline{y}(0)=\dot{\underline{y}}(0)=0 , eq 2.1.1-2 - f(t)\in {ℝ}^{m} represents the finite-element discretisation of the external forces. - {f}_{c}(t,\underline{y}(t),\underline{\dot{y}}(t),\underline{w})\in {ℝ}^{m} corresponds to the localised non-linearities (for example due to elastic impact stops). The elements {\underline{w}}