by its elastic response spectrum (also called oscillator spectrum in the Code_Aster documentation) which one denotes {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) where \xi and \omega are respectively the associated damping ratio and angular frequency. With reasonable assumptions, in particular on the regularity of the non-linear mapping (t,q,p;w)\mapsto {F}_{c}^{n}(t,q,p;w) , one can show that is a second-order process whose trajectories are almost surely continuous. Consequently, for every \xi fixed in a given interval {J}_{\xi} , \omega 	o {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) is a stochastic process indexed on the analysis band {J}_{\omega} with values in {ℝ}^{+} . One accepts that this process is of second order, that is: E\lbrace {S}_{j}{(\xi ,\omega )}^{2}brace <+\infty , orall \omega \in {J}_{\omega} . eq 3.4.1.2-1 3.4.2 Harmonic case In paragraph [§3.1], random matrices and random parameters have been introduced into the reduced matrix model. The harmonic response t\mapsto {Q}^{n}(\omega ) is therefore a stochastic process indexed on \left[{\omega}_{1},{\omega}_{2}ight] with values in {R}^{n} . Consequently, to the vector of the m free degrees of freedom {\underline{y}}^{n}(\omega ) there corresponds the stochastic process {Y}^{n}(\omega ) indexed on \left[{\omega}_{1},{\omega}_{2}ight] and with values in {R}^{m} such that {Y}^{n}(\omega )=\left[{\underline{\Phi}}_{n}ight]{Q}^{n}(\omega ) eq 3.4.2-1 {Y}_{j}^{n}(\omega ) the j -th component of the vector {Y}^{n}(\omega ) is a random variable that one will accept to be of second order. 3.4.3 Construction of the stochastic response by the Monte Carlo method 3.4.3.1 Choice and implementation of the Monte Carlo method The responses and the response spectra correspond to strongly non-linear transformations of the random matrices and of the random parameters that result from the probabilistic modelling of the uncertainties. Furthermore, one can of course construct only numerical approximations of these responses and of these response spectra. The statistics (first statistical moments, probability of exceeding a threshold, …) are formally written as multiple integrals of very large dimension because the number of random variables of the probabilistic model is high by construction. Finally, the number of observed quantities is very large (several d.o.f. for several frequencies). For all these reasons, the method best suited to computing the probabilistic solution (stochastic response and associated statistics) is the Monte-Carlo numerical simulation method. The Monte-Carlo simulation method has the advantage of giving results whose accuracy one can control (verification of the convergence, cf. [§4.1]), unlike most methods based on approximations. It can be costly in computation time, but the use of variance-reduction techniques can make it possible to reduce the number of simulations required (cf. [bib8] or [bib9]). The implementation of the Monte Carlo method consists, for the problem that concerns us, in generating {n}_{s} realisations of the random matrices \left[{M}_{n}ight] , \left[{K}_{n}ight] and \left[{D}_{n}ight] of the dynamic system and/or {n}_{s} realisations of the vector random variable W . The solutions of the deterministic dynamic system for each of the {n}_{s} realisations of ( \left[{M}_{n}ight] , \left[{K}_{n}ight] , \left[{D}_{n}ight] , W ) produce {n}_{s} realisations of the solution stochastic process t\mapsto {Q}^{n}(t) (resp. t\mapsto {Q}^{n}(\omega ) ) and consequently of t	o {Y}_{n}(t) , of t	o {Z}_{n}(t) and of \omega 	o {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) (resp. \omega \mapsto {Y}_{j}^{n}(\omega ) ). The generation of the random matrices is treated in the following paragraph; the generation of the random variable W is more classical and is not recalled. 3.4.3.2 Generation of the pseudo-random matrices In order to generate the realisations of the random matrix \left[{G}_{A}ight] , one uses the following algebraic representation of the random matrix \left[{G}_{A}ight] whose probability law is