osed of the {n}_{s} realisations of {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) and ({S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{(1)})	ext{, ... ,}{S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{(r)})	ext{, ... ,}{S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{({n}_{s})})) the associated ordered sample. A natural estimator of the quantile {S}_{j,\alpha }(\xi ,\omega ) for \alpha = rac{r}{{n}_{S}} , 1\le r\le {n}_{S} is: {\hat{S}}_{j, rac{r}{{n}_{S}}}(\xi ,\omega )={S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{(r)}) eq 3.4.4.1-2 To obtain a more robust estimator of the quantile, one can "average" the estimator over several series of {n}_{s} realisations. If the desired probability is such that \alpha <1/{n}_{S} , or if one wishes to reduce the number of simulations, it is possible to use more sophisticated estimators, for example by assuming that the cumulative distribution function {F}_{\xi ,\omega } belongs to a given domain of attraction (extreme-value theory) or for example by using a Bayesian regularisation method (cf.[bib12]). 3.4.4.2 Sample extreme values For a sample of {n}_{s} realisations of {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) denoted {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{1})	ext{, ... ,}{S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{{n}_{s}}) one defines the sample extreme values by: \omega \mapsto {	ext{dB}}_{j	ext{,min}}(\xi ,\omega ;{n}_{S})={\log}_{10}(\underset{r=1,...,{n}_{S}}{\min}{S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{r})) eq 3.4.4.2-1 \omega \mapsto {	ext{dB}}_{j	ext{,max}}(\xi ,\omega ;{n}_{S})={\log}_{10}(\underset{r=1,...,{n}_{S}}{\max}{S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{r})) eq 3.4.4.2-2 3.4.4.3 "Confidence domain" established from Chebyshev's inequality For a sample of {n}_{s} realisations of the process \omega \mapsto {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ) denoted \omega \mapsto {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{1}) , …, \omega \mapsto {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;{	heta}_{{n}_{S}}) , one can construct the "confidence domain" of the random variable for every , using Chebyshev's inequality associated with a probability level {P}_{C} : 	ext{Proba}\left\lbrace {	ext{dB}}_{j}^{-}(\xi ,\omega )<{	ext{dB}}_{j}(\xi 