in Code_Aster 4.1 Study of the stochastic convergence of the numerical model 4.1.1 Transient case The convergence of the stochastic solution must be studied with respect to the number n of modes and to the number {n}_{s} of Monte Carlo simulations. As the stochastic solution is a second-order process (by assumption, cf. [§3.4.1.2]), its convergence can be analysed by studying the mappings such that: {\mid \parallel {\ddot{Z}}_{j}^{n}\parallel \mid }^{2}={\int}_{0}^{T}E{\left\lbrace {\ddot{Z}}_{j}^{n}(t)ightbrace }^{2}	ext{dt} , eq 4.1.1-1 where is a second-order stochastic process indexed by and with values in R representing the acceleration of the j -th degree of freedom of the structure. Within the framework of Monte-Carlo simulations, this norm is estimated for n fixed from a set of {n}_{s} random realisations by the approximation with {	ext{conv}}_{j}(n,{n}_{S}{)}^{2}={\int}_{0}^{T}{( rac{1}{{n}_{S}}\sum_{i=1}^{{n}_{S}}{\ddot{Z}}_{j}^{n}(t;{	heta}_{i}))}^{2}	ext{dt} eq 4.1.1-2 The stochastic convergence of the model is thus analysed according to the dimension of the reduced model (that is the number of modes n of the eigenspace of the mean finite-element model onto which the stochastic non-linear dynamic system was projected in paragraph [§2.2]) and the number {n}_{s} of Monte-Carlo simulations by studying the function . 4.1.2 Harmonic case The convergence in the case of a transient solution can be transposed directly to the case of a harmonic solution, with the norm: {\mid \parallel {Z}_{j}^{n}\parallel \mid }^{2}={\int}_{{\omega}_{1}}^{{\omega}_{2}}E{\left\lbrace {Z}_{j}^{n}(\omega )ightbrace }^{2}d\omega , eq 4.1.2-1 4.2 Choice of the dispersion parameters To use the method, the dispersion parameters \delta must be fixed. Two approaches can be used a priori to fix the value of these parameters. The first approach consists in identifying the value of the parameters \delta for a given structure or for a class of structure by means of appropriate methods. For that, one can use experimental results of the dynamic responses of the structure. One can also use numerical simulations constructed using a parametric approach to the uncertainties. In this latter case, it should be noted that only the errors on the data of the model are taken into account, since the modelling errors cannot be taken into account by the parametric approach. The second approach consists in not fixing a priori a fixed value of the parameters \delta but in making them vary within a given range (only 3 scalars to be varied for the mass, stiffness and damping matrices on the non-parametric part, in comparison with the very large number of parameters to be varied simultaneously in a classical parametric study). This approach makes it possible to carry out a global analysis of sensitivity to the uncertainties. In the case of an absence of objective information on the dispersion parameters to be chosen, it is preferable to use such an approach. The non-parametric method proposed then appears as a robust and simple approach to sensitivity analysis with respect to uncertainties. 4.3 Main steps The implementation in Code_Aster is composed of three main steps: the construction of the mean reduced matrix model, the generation of the realisations of the response viewed as a stochastic process, and finally the statistical post-processing of these realisations. The last two steps in fact constitute the direct Monte Carlo numerical simulation method. Step 1: construction of the mean reduced matrix model The mean reduced matrix model is constructed by means of a classical sequence of operators depending on the precise analysis carried out, the main ones of which may be: ASSE_MATRICE, CALC_MODES, MODE_STATIQUE, CALC_CHAR_SEISME, PROJ_BASE, … Step 2: generation of the realisations of the transient response The {n}_{s} realisations of the stochastic transient response are computed in a loop in the Python language composed of: - Generation of the p th realisations of the random generalised matrices of mass, of stiffness and of damping by GENE_MATR_ALEA (doc. U4.36.06). These matrices are not diagonal and therefore require full storage. - Generation of the p th realisations of the random variables of the parameters of the non-linearities by GENE_VARI_ALEA (doc. U4.36.07). - Computation of the p th realisation {Q}^{n}(t;p) or {Q}^{n}(\omega ;p) solution of the stochastic matrix system s . This realisation is the solution of the classical matrix system whose matrices and right-hand sides are the realisations previously generated. The computation is therefore performed by DYNA_VIBRA (with matr_asse_GENE_R and vect_asse_GENE as input). - 1-Extraction of the time observations of the predefined physical degrees of freedom (for example {\ddot{Z}}_{{}_{i}}^{n}(t;p) or {Y}_{j}^{n}(\omega ;p) , but also possibly the displacement, velocity, stress fields, etc.) via recu_fonction (after a REST_GENE_PHYS for {Y}_{j}^{n}(\omega ;p) ). 2-Computation of the corresponding spectra (by calc_fonction(SPEC_OSCI) for \omega 	o {S}_{j}(\xi ,\omega ;p) and calc_fonction(MODULE) for {Y}_{j}^{n}(\omega ;p) ) . - Evaluation, via calc_fonction keywords COMB or PUISSANCE or ENVELOPPE, of the contributions to the estimators of the means, of the second-order moments, of the max. and min. sample extreme values for the normalised spectra: {\hat{m}}_{\mathrm{1j}}(x,w;p)={S}_{j}(x,w;p)+{\hat{m}}_{\mathrm{1j}}(x,w;p-1) , {\hat{m}}_{\mathrm{2j}}(x,w;p)={S}_{j}(x,w;p{)}^{2}+{\hat{m}}_{\mathrm{2j}}(x,w;p-1) , {\hat{S}}_{j	ext{,max}}(x,w;p)=	ext{Max}\left\lbrace {S}_{j}(x,w;p),{\hat{S}}_{j	ext{,max}}(x,w;p-1)ightbrace , {\hat{S}}_{j	ext{,min}}(x,w;p)=	ext{Min}\left\lbrace {S}_{j}(x,w;p),{\hat{S}}_{j	ext{,min}}(x,w;p-1)ightbrace . Step 3: statistical post-processing The means, the standard deviations, the max. and min. sample extreme values for the normalised spectra can be evaluated via calc_fonction(COMB): {m}_{\mathrm{1j}}(x,w)= rac{1}{{n}_{s}}{\hat{m}}_{\mathrm{1