r4.03.06 1 r4.03.06 Recalibration algorithms 1 r4.03.06 Recalibration algorithms Summary: This document presents the recalibration algorithms implemented in MACR_RECAL. These are, first, a bounded Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm and, second, an evolutionary algorithm. For the first, the general method is described before certain elements are specified. The calculation of the functional and of the Jacobian matrix, the determination of the initial regularisation parameter and its evolution, the handling of the bounds and the convergence criterion are detailed. The general principles of genetic algorithms, and in particular of the evolutionary algorithm, as well as the implementation in Code_Aster , are presented in the second part of the document. 1 Introduction Before addressing the recalibration problem proper, it is useful to recall a few points about parameter identification. Suppose that one wishes to identify n parameters from a given mechanical test. Within this identification, the following quantities are defined: - \mathrm{c} , the vector of the n parameters to be identified, belonging to O , a closed convex set of {ℝ}^{n} . - \mathrm{d} , the vector of the quantities computed during a simulation of the test using the parameters c , as opposed to {d}^{\exp} , the vector of the quantities measured during an experimental test. Both belong to the space L of observable quantities. The simulation of the experimental test, parameterised by the vector c , may be carried out by various methods: finite differences, finite elements, boundary elements, .... This is what will be called the direct problem. The aim of the identification is to determine the set of parameters c that reduces the difference between measured and experimental quantities (in the strong hope that the reduction of this discrepancy will be sufficient to obtain the desired set of parameters ...). A cost functional denoted J is therefore introduced, depending on c and measuring the distance between d and {d}^{\exp} . J(c)=\parallel d-{d}^{\exp}\parallel eq 1-1 where \parallel .\parallel denotes a norm on L . The identification is therefore expressed as the following minimisation problem: Determine {c}^{	ext{ }}\in O such that J({c}^{	ext{ }})=\underset{c\in O}{	ext{Min}}J(c) Finally, recalibration is defined as the minimisation of a particular type of functional, known as “least squares”, which is expressed in the form: J(c)=\sum_{n=1}^{N}{j}_{n}^{2}(c) eq 1-2 where {j}_{n}(c) represents component n of the discrepancy between the vector of computed quantities and the vector of experimental quantities. It is commonly accepted that, among deterministic minimisation algorithms, the most efficient for this type of functional is the Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm. This is the algorithm that was historically the first to be implemented in the command MACR_RECAL of Code_Aster , and it is presented below. 2 Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm 2.1 Statement of the problem There are several families of minimisation algorithms [bib1]. For relatively regular problems, the most widely used are descent methods. Their principle is to generate iteratively a sequence {({c}^{k})}_{k\in N} defined by: ({c}^{k+1})={c}^{k}+{\alpha}^{k}{g}^{k} eq 2.1-1 such that, for f(x)=J({c}^{k}+x{g}^{k}),x\in {R}_{+}^{	ext{ }} - f(x) is decreasing in the neighbourhood of {0}^{+} - f({\alpha}^{k})=\underset{x>0}{	ext{Min}}f(x) {g}^{k} is the descent direction at step k . It is the method used to determine {g}^{k} that governs the nature and hence the efficiency of the algorithm used, these techniques being based mainly on approximations of J to order 1 or to order 2. For the Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm, an approximation of the functional to order 2 is used. 2.2 Solution In the context of recalibration, least-squares cost functionals of the following type are used: J(c)=\sum_{n=1}^{N}{j}_{n}^{2}(c) eq 2.2-1 where, for example, {j}_{n}(c)=({F}_{n}^{	ext{calc}}(c)-{F}_{n}^{\exp}) , with obvious notation. The particular feature of these cost functionals is that the form of their first and second derivatives is known: ({
