individuals of the population are represented by vectors of real numbers and no longer in binary coding; - the selection process is different: whereas genetic algorithms select n parents to create n children who replace them entirely in the population, evolutionary programs generate m children from n parents and then select the new population by keeping the n best among the set n+m ; - the probabilities of crossover and of mutation may vary over the generations. The interest of introducing an evolutionary algorithm into Code_Aster lies in its ability to explore the topological space of the parameters in several directions, while avoiding local minima of the functional to be minimised as far as possible. The implementation of this algorithm in Code_Aster is the result of the partnership between the AMA department and the Politecnico di Milano. It is also noted that the most criticised shortcoming of evolutionary algorithms, that of being greedy in CPU time, can easily be eliminated by the many possible variants of parallelisation. 3.2 Operation of the algorithm The algorithm is launched by choosing METHODE= 'GENETIQUE' or 'HYBRIDE' in the options of the command MACR_RECAL. In the second case, the evolutionary algorithm carries out a coarse search followed by an optimisation by the Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm. Figure 3.2-a Logical diagram of the operation of the evolutionary algorithm The overall operation of the algorithm is illustrated in the logical diagram of Figure 3.2-a, and the steps are described below: 1. Initialisation : a population of sets of parameters is generated and all the individuals are initialised with the initial values of the parameters to be recalibrated. The size of this population is given by the value of the parameter NB_PARENTS imposed by the user. This value depends on several factors, such as the uncertainty on the solution: the greater this uncertainty, the larger the population must be. It is therefore advisable to start a recalibration study by taking 10 for this value and to refine it according to the results. 2. Evaluation of the functional : this is the functional presented in equation 3.1-3. Initially, after the initialisation step, a single evaluation is made because all the individuals of the population are identical. Then, in the recalibration loop, as many evaluations of the functional are made during one iteration as the number of children (NB_FILS) imposed by the user. The larger this parameter, which governs the renewal rate of the population, the more greedy the algorithm is in CPU time at this step. By default the number of children is set to 5 (half the population size, which is also imposed by default). 3. Stopping criterion : once the population of n parents has been formed and each individual has its value of the functional, one checks: - the best value of the functional; - the number of iterations already performed by the algorithm If the best value of the functional is lower than the relative residual imposed by the user (1.E-3 by default), or if the maximum number of iterations is reached, the best individual of the population is supplied as the solution. 1. Selection : the best individual of the population is selected and it is the only one (in this implementation) that is allowed to reproduce and to generate the m children. This generation is quasi-random, about the best individual, with a standard deviation imposed by the user (keyword ECART_TYPE). During this step, the algorithm handles the bounds imposed on the parameters by the user. The individuals thus generated are accepted as children only if they lie inside the bounds. 1. Replacement : after the m children have been generated, the overall population at this stage of the iteration is n+m (NB_PARENTS + NB_FILS). The replacement operator here ranks the individuals according to the associated values of the functional and replaces the individuals of the PARENTS population with the n best among the n+m . One particular feature of this implementation of the evolutionary algorithm in Code_Aster is the absence, for the time being, of the mutation and crossover mechanisms. 3.3 Hybrid recalibration technique By choosing METHODE='HYBRIDE' in MACR_RECAL, the user has the possibility of combining the advantages of stochastic algorithms (the evolutionary one here) with those of deterministic algorithms (Levenberg–Marquardt in the present case). This consists in carrying out a first “coarse” search in the topological space of the parameters, which will allow the Levenberg–Marquardt algorithm to start the optimisation from a starting point closer to the global solution of the functional. The balance between stochastic and deterministic is to be set by the user in the light of his or her expertise. A large uncertainty on the optimal values of the parameters to be recalibrated must impose: - more iterations of the evolutionary algorithm; - a larger standard deviation for the random draws; - a larger population size. These are the three levers available to the user in order to carry out a high-quality recalibration with a satisfactory level of performance (CPU time and memory used). 4 Bibliography 1. J.C. CULIOLI: “Introduction à l’optimisation”, Ellipses, 1994 1. BAZARAA, H.D. SHERALI & C.M. SHETTY: “Nonlinear Programming, Theory and Algorithms”, Wiley & Sons, 1979 3. “Discrete formulation of frictional contact”, Code_Aster document R5.03.50 1. KUNISCH & F. RENDL; “An infeasible active set method for quadratic programming with simple bounds”, SIAM Journal on Optimization, Volume 14, Number 1, 2003 5. Z.Michalewicz. “Genetic Algorithms+Data Structures = Evolution Programs”. Springer Verlag, 1992--1996. 5 Description of the versions of the document | Aster version | Author(s) Organisation(s) | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | | 7.1 | N.TARDIEU-R&D/AMA | Initial text | | 10.1 | I.NISTOR - R&D/AMA | |

r4.05.05 Generation of seismic signals r4.05.05 Generation of seismic signals Summary: In "best-estimate" seismic studies, one is often led to carry out transient dynamic analyses. The seismic signal is then modelled by a stochastic process. This process expresses the ground acceleration as a function of time. One calls trajectories the time signals (accelerograms) which are realisations of the stochastic process. These signals may come from databases (ground accelerations measured during earthquakes) or be obtained by numerical simulation (cf. also [bib17] ) . The simulation of artificial seismic signals, as carried out by the operator GENE_ACCE_SEISME, is described in this document. The operator proposes two modellings: - Kanai-Tajimi PSD model (§2) - PSD model compatible with a target response spectrum (§3) In both cases, it is possible to introduce an evolution of the frequency content and of the amplitude (modulation) with the duration of the earthquake. 1 Modelling of the seismic excitation by a stochastic process Gaussian stochastic processes are entirely characterised by their power spectral density (PSD). If the process is stationary, then one can simulate trajectories very easily with the aid of the spectral representation theorem as described in §4. This theorem also applies to non-stationary Gaussian processes under the condition that the process can be expressed in the form of an evolutive PSD with weak oscillation (that is to say that the properties change slowly with time). In a simplified seismic analysis, one models only the strong phase of the seismic signal. The latter is then assumed stationary during this period. This is in particular the case within the framework of linear seismic analyses by modal combination (CQC, SRSS). Nevertheless, studies and statistical analyses have shown that the seismic signal is non-stationary in amplitude and in frequency content. As for the frequency content, its evolution can be estimated from natural signals. Most of the time one observes a decrease of the central frequency with time [bib9]. This drop in the central frequency of the seismic excitation may go together with the drop in the natural frequencies of the structure subjected to the earthquake. This coincidence may have aggravating effects which it is appropriate to take into account in the modellings of non-linear structures. 1.1 Modelling of the earthquake by a non-stationary process with evolutive PSD One models the seismic motion by realisations of a centred Gaussian process described by its evolutive power spectral density [bib8]: {S}_{X}(\omega ,t)=q(t){S}_{Y}(\omega ,t)q(t)\in ℝ (1) where {S}_{Y}(\omega ,t) is the non-separable part of the spectral density which models the evolution of the frequency content of the seismic signal over time. The function q(t) is a deterministic modulation function which expresses the amplitude of the signal. In the case where one has {S}_{Y}(\omega ,t)\equiv {S}_{Y}(\omega ) , that is to say that the PSD does not depend on time, Y is a stationary process and X(t)=q(t)Y(t),t\in T a quasi-stationary process of which only the amplitude varies with time. One then speaks of a separable PSD: {S}_{X}(\omega ,t)={q}^{2}(t){S}_{Y}(\omega ) . In Code_Aster , one considers processes Y(t) normalised so that the standard deviation is unity at each time instant. It is the modulation function which determines the appearance of the signals and their energy (and therefore the amplitude over a fixed duration of the strong phase). This is illustrated in Figure 1a . The amplitude of the signals to be generated is then determined by the datum of the Arias intensity {I}_{a} (energy), of the standard deviation {\sigma}_{X} over the duration of the strong phase, or else of the median maximum {a}_{m} over the duration of the strong phase. If one wishes to generate signals "compatible" with a response spectrum, then the energy of the signal and its amplitude are determined by the datum of the target spectrum. Two temporal modulation functions of the amplitude q(t) are proposed in Code_Aster : the gamma function and the Jennings & Housner function [bib4]. The gamma function is written: q(t)={\alpha}_{1}{t}^{({\alpha}_{2}-1)}\exp(-{\alpha}_{3}t),t\in T (2) The parameters {\alpha}_{2} and {\alpha}_{3} describe respectively the shape and the duration of the strong phase of the signal. The parameter {\alpha}_{1} determines the energy of the signal and can be determined from the datum of the Arias intensity. The Jennings & Housner function [bib4] is written: q(t)=\lbrace  egin{array}{cc}{(t/{t}_{1})}^{2}& \mathrm{si}0\le t {t}_{2} , the natural angular frequency is assumed constant and equal to {\omega}_{2}={\omega}_{1}-\omega '({t}_{2}-{t}_{1}) . The user must ensure that the natural angular frequency given by this relation remains positive over time. - Filtering of the KT PSD by a filter of natural angular frequency {\omega}_{f} and a reduced damping {\xi}_{f}=1.0 according to Clough & Penzien (CP). This filtering makes it possible to remove the very low-frequency content which leads to non-zero drifts (non-zero displacements and velocities at the end of the earthquake). In the Code_Aster implementation, one takes {\omega}_{f}=0.05{\omega}_{0} by default, but the user may supply another value if he wishes. Seismologists call the frequency {\omega}_{f} "Corner Frequency", namely the minimum frequency below which the spectrum must tend towards 0. This leads us to the corrected PSD {S}_{\mathrm{CP}}(\omega ) : > {S}_{\mathrm{CP}}(\omega ,t)=∣h(\omega )∣{}^{2}{S}_{\mathrm{KT}}(\omega ,t) , where the filter is written h(\omega )= rac{{\omega}^{2}}{({\omega}_{f}^{2}-{\omega}^{2}+\mathrm{2i}{\xi}_{f}{\omega}_{f}\omega )} - Two temporal modulation functions of the amplitude q(t) are proposed: the gamma function and the Jennings & Housner function [bib4]. These functions as well as their parameterisation are described in Chapter 1. The evolution of the fundamental angular frequency of the evolutive Kanai-Tajimi model is illustrated in Figure 1a . Figure 2a shows the filtered Kanai-Tajimi PSD (Clough & Penzien PSD) for a given natural frequency. For regular PSD functions, the natural frequency of the filter is close to the fundamental frequency of the PSD. In Figure 1, one also visualises the bandwidth of the PSD, denoted \delta . The bandwidth of the PSD is linked to the damping of the filter as explained in §3.1. Note: A model very similar to that described above is proposed in the reference (Rezaeian & Der Kiureghian, [bib9,bib10]) . The difference between the two approaches lies mainly in the writing of the problem in the time domain by Rezaeian & Der Kiureghian and not in the frequency domain through the spectral density as proposed here. Now, the writing in the time domain has a certain number of disadvantages, such as the necessity of evaluating the convolution integral and of working with the Wiener process (white noise) which is not a second-order process. The formulation of the problem by a filtered white noise described by its evolutive PSD as proposed here is, by contrast, very straightforward and numerically much more efficient. Figure 2a Kanai-Tajimi PSD after filtering of the low frequencies according to Clough & Penzien [bib2]. 2.2 Parameterisation of the modulation functions The parameters which characterise the appearance of the modulation function are the strong-phase duration {T}_{\mathrm{SM}} , the instant of the beginning of the strong phase {t}_{\mathrm{ini}} . The amplitude of the signals to be generated is determined by the datum of the Arias intensity {I}_{a} (energy), the standard deviation {\sigma}_{X} over the duration of the strong phase or else the median maximum {a}_{m} over the duration of the strong phase. One assumes given the means of these parameters, in particular the mean strong-phase duration  ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}} , the mean instant of the beginning of the strong phase  ar{{t}_{\mathrm{ini}}} and the mean Arias intensity { ar{I}}_{a} . In practice, the median maximum {a}_{m} is associated with the datum of the PGA ( Peak Ground Acceleration ). Thus, if the PGA is given, then one considers that it corresponds to the median of the maxima {a}_{m}=\mathrm{Médiane}({\max}_{t\in T}(∣X(t)∣)) of the trajectories of the process (during the strong phase T{}_{\mathrm{SM}} ). This allows us to determine the standard deviation {\sigma}_{X} corresponding to this maximum with the aid of the peak factor {\eta}_{{T}_{\mathrm{SM}}} : {a}_{m}\approx {\eta}_{{T}_{\mathrm{SM}}}{\sigma}_{X} (8) The peak factor is given by the formula {\eta}_{{T}_{\mathrm{SM}},p}^{2}=2\ln({\mathrm{2N}}_{\eta}[1-\exp(-{\delta}^{1.2}\sqrt{\pi \ln({\mathrm{2N}}_{\eta})})]) (9) where {N}_{\eta}=1,4427{T}_{\mathrm{SM}}{
u}_{0}^{+} . The variables {
u}_{0}^{+} (central frequency) and \delta (bandwidth) are computed from the moments of the PSD according to expression (17) of §3.1. The mean Arias intensity { ar{I}}_{a} of a process Y(t)\in ℝ modulated by a function q(t) , described by equation (1), is expressed as:  ar{{I}_{a}}=E( rac{\pi}{2g}{\int}_{0}^{\infty}{X}^{2}(t)\mathrm{dt})= rac{\pi}{2g}{\int}_{0}^{\infty}{q}^{2}(t)E({Y}^{2}(t))\mathrm{dt} (4) knowing that X(t)=q(t)Y(t) . The operator E in the expression above denotes the mathematical expectation and g=\mathrm{9.81m}/{s}^{2} . If one normalises the process Y(t) so that {\sigma}_{Y}(t)=1 , one can write:  ar{{I}_{a}}= rac{\pi}{2g}{\int}_{0}^{\infty}{q}^{2}(t)\mathrm{dt} (5) In practice the integration is carried out up to the end instant T of the seismic signals. The mean strong-phase duration  ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}} is defined from the Arias intensity which expresses the energy contained in the seismic signal. Thus,  ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}} is defined as the duration of time between the time instants {t}_{0.05} and {t}_{0.95} where respectively 5% and 95% of the Arias intensity are realised. The instant {t}_{0.05} consequently denotes the beginning of the mean strong phase,  ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}} , such that:  ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}}: rac{\pi}{2g ar{{I}_{a}}}{\int}_{0}^{{t}_{\mathrm{ini}}}{q}^{2}(t)\mathrm{dt}=0.05 (6) and  ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}}+ ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}} the end of the strong phase:  ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}}+ ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}}: rac{\pi}{2g ar{{I}_{a}}}{\int}_{0}^{ ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}}+ ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}}}{q}^{2}(t)\mathrm{dt}=0.95 (7) Given the duration of the strong phase, the mean Arias intensity { ar{I}}_{a} makes it possible to determine the standard deviation of the process X . Note: The parameters of the modulation functions are determined from the mean strong phase and the mean Arias intensity, as described above. The strong phases, maxima (PGA) and Arias intensities of the seismic signals generated with this modelling will consequently exhibit a certain variability about the means. 2.3 Uncertainties and natural variability of the signals The parameters of the Kanai-Tajimi model are: - The mean duration of the strong phase  ar{T}{}_{\mathrm{SM}} and the instant of the beginning of the mean strong phase  ar{t}{}_{\mathrm{ini}} (modulation function), - The natural angular frequency {\omega}_{1} and the slope \omega ' of the Kanai-Tajimi PSD (or {\omega}_{0} if one considers a constant value), - The reduced damping {\xi}_{0} of the Kanai-Tajimi PSD, - The mean Arias intensity { ar{I}}_{a} (mean energy contained in the seismic signal), the PGA (median maximum {a}_{m} ) or the standard deviation {\sigma}_{X} . In order to represent better the natural variability of the seismic signals and to take into account the uncertainty on the parameters of the model, one can model the latter by random variables. Each simulated seismic signal then corresponds to a particular draw of the parameters of the model. The Latin Hypercube sampling method constitutes an efficient method which makes it possible to sweep well the domain of definition of the parameters for a reduced number of draws. The mean values as well as the distributions of these parameters can be estimated from natural seismic signals corresponding to the scenario sought. They are, for some of them, also available in the literature. One finds a very exhaustive treatment of them in the reference [bib9]. Means, minimum and maximum values and distributions of the parameters, identified from accelerograms recorded on medium to hard soil and D>\mathrm{10km} (taken from the NGA earthquake database [bib14]), are presented in the appendix of this document. These results are taken from the report of Rezaeian & Der Kiureghian [bib9]. 3 PSD model for generating signals "compatible" with a target SRO In the case where the earthquake is modelled by a stationary Gaussian process during the strong phase {T}_{\mathit{SM}} , it is possible to establish an (approximate) relation between the spectral density {S}_{Y}(\omega ) of the process and its oscillator response spectrum (SRO), {S}_{a}(\omega ,\xi ) . The latter is indeed often given by seismologists or prescribed by codes and regulations. 3.1 Identification of a PSD compatible with a target spectrum The first-passage problem makes it possible to link the SRO (for a given angular frequency {\omega}_{n} and a given reduced damping {\xi}_{0} ) to the standard deviation of the process via the peak factor \eta : {S}_{a}({\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0})\approx {\omega}_{n}^{2}{\eta}_{{T}_{\mathrm{SM}},p}{\sigma}_{n} (12) where {\sigma}_{n} is the standard deviation of the response process {\sigma}_{n}^{2}=\int∣{h}_{{\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0}}(\omega )∣{}^{2}{S}_{Y}(\omega )d\omega (13) and: {h}_{{\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0}}(\omega )= rac{1}{{\omega}_{n}^{2}-{\omega}^{2}+\mathrm{2i}{\xi}_{0}{\omega}_{n}\omega } (14) is the transfer function (filter) of natural angular frequency {\omega}_{n} and of reduced damping {\xi}_{0} . The peak factor makes it possible to estimate the p -fractiles of the distribution of the maxima of a Gaussian process from the standard deviation. The peak factor, due to Vanmarcke [bib16], is written: {\eta}_{{T}_{\mathrm{SM}},p}^{2}=2\ln({\mathrm{2N}}_{\eta}[1-\exp(-{\delta}^{1.2}\sqrt{\pi \ln({\mathrm{2N}}_{\eta})})]) . (15) In this expression, \delta is the bandwidth of the process (cf. Figure 2a) and {N}_{\eta} is determined from the central frequency {
u}_{0}^{+} as {N}_{\eta}={T}_{\mathrm{SM}}{
u}_{0}^{+}{(-\lnp)}^{-1} (16) In this expression, p denotes the fractile of the distribution of the maxima considered. In general, one takes p=0.5 which corresponds to the "median of the maxima". The central frequency {
u}_{0}^{+} denotes the frequency where the energy of the process is concentrated. The central frequency and the bandwidth can be expressed by the classical Rice formulae:  egin{array}{}{
u}_{0}^{+}= rac{1}{2\pi }\sqrt{ rac{{\lambda}_{2}}{{\lambda}_{0}}},\ \delta =\sqrt{(1- rac{{\lambda}_{1}^{2}}{{\lambda}_{0}{\lambda}_{2}})}\end{array} (17) where the {\lambda}_{i} are the spectral moments of the PSD of the response process: {\lambda}_{i}={\int}_{-\infty }^{+\infty }∣\omega ∣{}^{i}∣{h}_{{\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0}}(\omega )∣{}^{2}{S}_{Y}(\omega )d\omega , (18) with in particular \sqrt{{\lambda}_{0}} the standard deviation of the process. In order to determine the PSD compatible with the SRO, one must invert expression (12). One can show that one has, as a first approximation, for an excitation by white noise ( {S}_{Y}(\omega )=\mathrm{const} ) and for a filter with natural frequency {\omega}_{n} : {\lambda}_{0}={\int}_{-\infty }^{+\infty }∣{h}_{{\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0}}(\omega )∣{}^{2}{S}_{Y}(\omega )d\omega \approx {S}_{Y}({\omega}_{n}){\int}_{-\infty }^{+\infty }∣{h}_{{\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0}}(\omega )∣{}^{2}d\omega = rac{\pi {S}_{Y}({\omega}_{n})}{2\xi {\omega}_{n}^{3}} (19) This formula was subsequently improved by Vanmarcke [bib15, bib16] in order to take into account the contribution of {S}_{Y}(\omega ) in the range of angular frequencies [-{\omega}_{n},{\omega}_{n}] where {h}_{{\omega}_{n}}(\omega )\approx 1/{\omega}_{n}^{2} : {\lambda}_{0}\approx  rac{\pi {S}_{Y}({\omega}_{n})}{2\xi {\omega}_{n}^{3}}+ rac{2}{{\omega}_{n}^{4}}{\int}_{0}^{{\omega}_{n}}{S}_{Y}({\omega}_{n})d\omega - rac{2}{{\omega}_{n}^{3}}{S}_{Y}({\omega}_{n}) (20) This leads us to the Vanmarcke formula for evaluating a PSD {G}_{S}(\omega ) from an SRO {S}_{a}(\omega ,\xi ) (for a given damping {\xi}_{0} ): {G}_{S}({\omega}_{n})= rac{1}{{\omega}_{n}( rac{\pi}{2{\xi}_{0}}-2)}\left[ rac{{S}_{a}^{2}({\omega}_{n},{\xi}_{0})}{{\eta}_{{T}_{\mathit{SM}},p}^{2}}-2{\int}_{0}^{{\omega}_{n}}{G}_{S}(\omega )d\omega ight],{\omega}_{n}>0 (21) under the condition that these two functions are sufficiently smooth. The original Vanmarcke formula was then used in more or less similar forms by numerous au