r4.07.03 Computation of an added-mass matrix on a modal basis r4.07.03 Computation of an added-mass matrix on a modal basis Summary: This document presents one aspect of fluid/structure coupling: when a vibrating structure is immersed in a fluid assumed to be without flow, incompressible and inviscid, it experiences pressure forces whose resultant is proportional to the acceleration of the structure in the fluid: the proportionality coefficient is homogeneous to a mass: it is called the added mass . The means of building an added-mass matrix for one (or several) structure(s) with several degrees of freedom, on the modal basis of the structure(s) established beforehand in the absence of fluid, is specified here. This added-mass matrix is obtained under the assumption of small motions, both for the structure and for the fluid. 1 Notation | p | the fluctuating pressure in the fluid, | | --- | --- | | {\gamma}_{l}^{\mathit{fs}} | contour of the structure indexed by l immersed in the fluid domain | | {\ddot{x}}_{{s}_{l}} | the displacement field in the structure l , | | {ho}_{f},{ho}_{s} | the density of the fluid, of the structure, | | {\mathrm{X}}_{\mathit{il}} | the eigenmode of order i of the structure l in air | |  egin{array}{}{a}_{\mathrm{il}},{\dot{a}}_{\mathrm{il}},\ {\ddot{a}}_{\mathrm{il}}\end{array} | the generalised coordinates, velocities and accelerations associated with mode i of the structure l in air | | \sigma | the stress tensor in the structure | | \Phi | the fluid flux vector | | H | the fluid stiffness matrix | | v | the fluctuating fluid velocity field | | n | the inward normal of the fluid domain. | 2 Introduction Many industrial components or structures are in contact with fluid media, which may moreover be in flow. These surrounding fluid media disturb the vibratory characteristics of the structures, in particular their modal characteristics. This action of the fluid on the structure results in fluid/structure coupling effects. The surrounding fluid medium is assumed here to be incompressible, perfect and at rest (without flow). It will be shown that, then, a structure which vibrates with a small amplitude while sharing a boundary with this fluid domain modifies the pressure field in the fluid at rest, and therefore experiences a fluctuating pressure force, proportional to its acceleration. The proportionality coefficient is a mass. It describes the inertial effect of the fluid on the structure: this is why this mass is called the added mass of the fluid on the structure. The fluid domain may also have fixed boundaries or free surfaces, but in what follows the action of gravity on the free surface will not be considered, and therefore neither will the sloshing modes. When several structures are in contact with the same fluid domain, when one of the structures starts to vibrate, not only does it experience the inertia of the fluid, but it also modifies the fluctuating pressure field around the interfaces with the fluid of all the other structures. The forces experienced by each of them are proportional to the acceleration of the vibrating structure: here again the proportionality coefficients are masses called coupling added masses . The added masses are established in the basis of the eigenmodes of the structures considered in the absence of fluid. The modal analysis of the structures can then be carried out again taking these added masses into account, and one may possibly return to the finite-element basis (“physical” basis). 3 Recall of the equations of the problem 3.1 Equations in the fluid It is assumed that K vibrating structures are immersed in a perfect (inviscid), incompressible fluid domain at rest (without flow). The effect of gravity is neglected. The Euler equations associated with the fluctuations in the fluid without flow can therefore be written: - conservation of mass:  rac{\partial {ho}_{f}}{\partial t}+div({ho}_{f}\mathrm{v})=0 eq 3.1-1 - conservation of momentum (under the linearis