 added to the generalised mass matrix of the structure using the operator COMB_MATR_ASSE U4.72.01. This makes it possible to compute the coupled fluid/structure modes of the immersed structures (“wet” modes) (operator CALC_MODES U4.52.02). It is advisable to consult the test case FDLV114 — Seismic response of a cylindrical tank V8.01.114. 6 Bibliography 1. CONCA, J. PLANCHARD, B. THOMAS, M. VANNINATHAN: “Mathematical problems in fluid/structure coupling” _ EYROLLES (1994). 1. BEAUD, G. ROUSSEAU: “Inter-software validation of the added-mass computation with Code_Aster and the CALIFE code”, HT-32/95/004/A. 7 Description of the versions of the document | Aster version | Author(s) Organisation(s) | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | | 3 | G.ROUSSEAU (EDF/EP/AMV) | Initial text | | 15.2 | F.VOLDOIRE (EDF/R&D/ERMES) | Explanatory comments |

r4.07.05 Homogenisation of a network of beams immersed in a fluid r4.07.05 Homogenisation of a network of beams immersed in a fluid Summary: This note describes a model obtained by a homogenisation method in order to characterise the vibratory behaviour of a periodic network of tubes immersed in an incompressible fluid. The development of a finite element associated with this homogenised model is then presented. The tubes are modelled by Euler beams and the fluid by a potential model. This modelling is accessible in the command AFFE_MODELE by choosing the modelling 3D_FAISCEAU. > Contents 1 Introduction In the nuclear industry, certain structures consist of quasi-periodic networks of tubes immersed in fluids: the “fuel” assemblies, the steam generators, ... To determine the vibratory behaviour of such structures, the classical approach (each tube is modelled, the volume occupied by the fluid is meshed) is costly and tedious or even impracticable (in particular, the construction of a complicated mesh containing a large number of nodes). The structures studied presenting a quasi-periodic character, it seems interesting to use homogenisation methods. Homogenisation techniques applied to a network of tubes immersed in a fluid have already been developed on various occasions [bib1], [bib5], [bib4]. The models obtained differ by the assumptions made on the fluid (compressibility, initial velocity of the flow, viscosity). Depending on the assumptions adopted, the action of the fluid on the network of tubes corresponds to an added mass (lowering of the vibration frequencies with respect to those determined in the absence of fluid), to an added damping or even to an added stiffness [bib5]. At the beginning, finite elements associated with two-dimensional models (network of masses immersed in a fluid) were developed [bib2]. To study the three-dimensional problems (network of tubes), one solution consisted in projecting the motion onto the first bending mode of the beams [bib4]. Subsequently, three-dimensional finite elements were developed [bib3], [bib8]. 2 Initial physical problem 2.1 Description of the problem One considers a set of identical beams, of axis z , arranged periodically (let \varepsilon be the spatial period). These beams are located inside an enclosure filled with fluid (see [fig 2.1-a]). One wishes to characterise the vibratory behaviour of such a medium, considering for the moment only the added-mass effect of the fluid, which is predominant [bib6]. Figure 2.1-a 2.2 Modelling assumptions One considers that the fluid is a perfect fluid, initially at rest, incompressible. As the assumption of small displacements about the equilibrium position has been made (fluid initially at rest), the displacement field of the fluid particles is irrotational, so that there exists a fluid displacement potential denoted \Phi . There is no fluid flux through the outer surface \Gamma . One considers that the beams are homogeneous and of constant cross-section along z\in \left]0,Light[ . To model the beams, the Euler model is used and only the bending motions are taken into account. The beam cross-section is rigid and the displacement of any point of the cross-section is denoted: {\mathrm{s}}^{l} the bending of beam no. l ({\mathrm{s}}^{l}(z)=({\mathrm{s}}_{x}^{l}(z),{\mathrm{s}}_{y}^{l}(z))) . The beams are clamped at their two ends. The variational form of the vibro-acoustic fluid–structure problem (conservation of mass, dynamic equation of each tube) is written: {\int}_{{\Omega}_{F}}
