al {s}_{x}}{\partial z} , which are linked to the rotational degrees of freedom {	heta}_{x},{	heta}_{y} by the formulae {	heta}_{x}= rac{\partial {s}_{y}}{\partial z},{	heta}_{y}=- rac{\partial {s}_{x}}{\partial z} , must be known. As regards the variations along x,y , one restricts oneself for the moment to shape functions {Q}_{1} . For the potential degree of freedom, shape functions {Q}_{1} or {Q}_{2} along the three directions x,y,z of space are used. The finite element therefore has as unknowns the following degrees of freedom: {s}_{x},{s}_{y},{	heta}_{x},{	heta}_{y},\Phi . Note: The order of the nodes of the support mesh cells is very important. Indeed, the edges parallel to the axis z are not represented in the same way as the edges contained in the planes z=\mathrm{Cte} . The nodes of the mesh cells are therefore arranged in a very precise order: list of the nodes of the lower base, then list of the opposite nodes of the upper base (or vice versa). As regards the geometry, the shape functions making it possible to pass from the reference element to the current element are {Q}_{1} . The finite element is therefore subparametric. Two finite elements have been developed: - one associated with a HEXA8 mesh cell. At each node of the mesh cell, the unknowns are {s}_{x},{s}_{y},{	heta}_{x},{	heta}_{y},\Phi . The shape functions associated with the potential \Phi are {Q}_{1} . - another associated with a HEXA20 mesh cell. At each corner node of the mesh cell, the unknowns are {s}_{x},{s}_{y},{	heta}_{x},{	heta}_{y},\Phi . At each mid-edge node, the unknown is \Phi . The shape functions associated with the potential \Phi are {Q}_{2} . 5.2 Reference finite elements 5.2.1 HEXA8 mesh cell On the HEXA8 reference finite element (see [fig 5.2-a]), the following shape functions are defined: {N}_{\pm 1,\pm 1,\pm 1}^{L}(\xi )={P}_{\pm 1}({\xi}_{1}){P}_{\pm 1}({\xi}_{2}){P}_{\pm 1}^{L}({\xi}_{3})	ext{avec}L=\Phi 	ext{ou}D	ext{ou}R eq 5.2.1-1 The indices \pm 1 represent the coo