he elementary matrices is transformed into an integral on the reference element (one carries out a change of variable) which is then computed by using a quadrature formula of GAUSS type. The Gauss points are chosen so as to integrate exactly the integrals on the reference element. Different families of integration points are used to compute the mass matrices and the stiffness matrices (the degrees of the polynomials to be integrated are different). But here, to compute the various contributions of the mass matrix, different families of Gauss points may still be used. The reference element being a HEXA8 or a HEXA20, the integral over the volume can be separated into a product of three integrals which each correspond to one direction of the reference space. The number of integration points necessary is determined by direction. Depending on the reference mesh cell, the number of integration points per direction is the following: | | HEXA8 mesh cell | HEXA20 mesh cell | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | direction x or y | direction z | direction x or y | direction z | | matrix \hat{K} | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | | matrix \hat{A} | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | | matrix \hat{D} | 2 | 3 | 2 | 3 | | matrix \hat{M} | 2 | 4 | 2 | 4 | Four families of Gauss points have been defined. Each family corresponds to one of the matrices of the problem to be solved. On the segment [-1,1], the coordinates of the integration points and their weights are the following [bib7]: | Number of integration points | Coordinates | Weights | | --- | --- | --- | | 2 | \hat{M} | 1 | | 3 | 0 \pm \sqrt{3/5} | 8/9 5/9 | | 4 | \pm \sqrt{ rac{3-2\sqrt{6/5}}{7}} \pm \sqrt{ rac{3+2\sqrt{6/5}}{7}} |  rac{1}{2}+ rac{1}{6\sqrt{6/5}}  rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{6\sqrt{6/5}} | The weight of a Gauss point in the three-dimensional reference element is obtained by multiplying the three weights corresponding to each of the coordinates of the Gauss point. 5.4 Addition of the tension and torsion problems To complete the homogenised bending problem described previously, the tension problem and the torsion problem are added in an uncoupled manner (these problems do not involve the fluid). 5.4.1 Tension problem The homogenised tension problem is written in the following form: {\int}_{\Omega} rac{ES{\mu}^{2}}{∣Y∣} rac{\partial {s}_{z}}{\partial z} rac{\partial v}{\partial z}+{\int}_{\Omega} rac{{\mu}^{2}{ho}_{S}S}{∣Y∣} rac{{\partial}^{2}{s}_{z}}{\partial {t}^{2}}v=0 orall v\in V with V={H}^{1}([0,L]) \mu being the side ratio of the reference elementary cell, of area ∣Y∣ , with respect to the real periodicity cell of the heterogeneous medium. The reference finite element is a HEXA8 having as unknown the displacement at each node. The associated shape functions are {Q}_{1} . 5.4.2 Torsion problem The homogenised torsion problem is written in the following form: {\int}_{\Omega} rac{E{J}_{z}{\mu}^{2}}{2(1+
