inertia tensor I , torsion constant {J}_{z} ) are supplied directly under the factor keyword POUTRE of the command AFFE_CARA_ELEM. The characteristics of the homogenised coefficients and of the reference cell are supplied under the factor keyword POUTRE_FLUI of the command AFFE_CARA_ELEM. For the simple keywords, the correspondence is the following: B_T: {b}_{11} B_N: {b}_{22} B_TN: {b}_{12} A_FLUI: {Y}_{F} A_CELL: Y={Y}_{F}+{Y}_{S} COEF_ECHELLE: \mu The characteristics of the materials are supplied in the command DEFI_MATERIAU. For the tubes, the factor keyword ELAS is used to indicate Young's modulus (E: E ), Poisson's ratio (NU: 
u ) and the density (RHO: {ho}_{S} ). For the fluid, the factor keyword FLUIDE is used to indicate the density of the fluid (RHO: {ho}_{F} ). 6.2 Orientation of the axes of the beams The generators of the cylindrical mesh cells are obligatorily parallel to the axis of the beams and the bases of the mesh cells perpendicular to this same axis. During the construction of the mesh, one must ensure that the order of the nodes (local numbering) of each cylindrical mesh cell is correct: the nodes of the lower base then the nodes of the upper base (or vice versa). The direction of the axis of the beams is supplied under the factor keyword ORIENTATION of the command AFFE_CARA_ELEM. The following assumption has been made: the reference frame is the same as the principal inertia frame of the characteristic tube representing the homogenised medium. This means that in the equations [eq 3.1-3], the term {I}_{xy} is zero. 6.3 Modal analysis The finite element developed makes it possible to characterise the vibratory behaviour of a network of beams immersed in a fluid. It is interesting to determine the vibration frequencies of such a network in air and in water. To carry out a modal analysis in air ( {ho}_{F}=0 ), one must fix all the degrees of freedom corresponding to the fluid displacement potential \Phi , otherwise the stiffness matrix (and even the shifted matrix of the modal problem) is not invertible R5.01.01. To carry out a modal analysis in water ( {ho}_{F}
e 0 ), one must use in the command CALC_MODES with OPTION='CENTRE'. The shifted matrix (	ilde{K}-\sigma 	ilde{M}) is then invertible if \sigma is not an eigenvalue or if \sigma is different from zero. 7 Characterisation of the spectrum of the homogenised model 7.1 Heterogeneous model Let a square-pitch network of n beams clamped at their lower ends and whose upper ends move in the same way (uniform motion) (cf. figure [fig 7.1-a]). Only the bending motions are considered. Figure 7.1-a The vibration spectrum in air of this network has the following form. For each order of bending vibration mode, the modal structure consists of a double frequency corresponding to a mode in x and to a mode in y in which the whole of the upper part moves (all the beams have the same deformed shape) and of a frequency of multiplicity (2n-2) corresponding to modes in which the whole of the upper part of the beams is immobile and in which beams move in phase opposition. In the presence of fluid, the spectrum is modified. For each order of bending vibration mode, the 2n vibration frequencies are lower than the vibration frequencies obtained in air. The effect of the incompressible fluid is comparable to an added mass. One still has a double frequency corresponding to a mode in x and to a mode in y in which the whole of the upper part moves (all the beams have the same deformed shape). On the other hand, one obtains (n-1) different couples of double frequency (one in x and one in y ) corresponding to modes in which the whole of the upper part of the beams is immobile and in which beams move in phase opposition. Figure 7.1-b 7.2 Homogeneous model The heterogeneous medium has been replaced by a homogeneous medium. 7.2.1 Continuous problem Recent work, concerning a plane homogenisation problem of a network of masses retained by springs and immersed in a fluid, shows that the spectrum of the continuous homogeneous model consists of a continuous part and of two frequencies of infinite multiplicity [bib10]. The spectrum of the eigenfrequencies of the problem in water is also contained in a well-defined interval bounded above by the vibration frequency in air of a mass [bib5]. These results can be transposed for each bending order of the network of tubes. 7.2.2 Discretised problem Let the homogeneous domain be meshed by hexahedra. Let p be the number of generators parallel to the axis z of the network of beams. Figure 7.2.2-a One finds results analogous to those obtained for the heterogeneous model. It suffices to replace n by p . For a beam bending order, the number of frequencies corresponding to modes in which the beams do not all vibrate in the same direction depends on the discretisation used in the directions transverse to the axis of the beams. Depending on the finite element used (HEXA8 mesh cell or HEXA20 mesh cell), the distribution of the last (2p-2) frequencies is different. The first double frequency (the one corresponding to the mode in which the upper part moves) is the same for the two finite elements. Figure 7.2.2-b Overall, the homogeneous model makes it possible to obtain easily the vibration frequencies corresponding to modes in which all the beams vibrate in the same direction. The other modes obtained supply only a partial view of the spectrum. In the discretised spectrum, one can recover one or both frequencies of infinite multiplicity present in the spectrum of the continuous model. 8 Conclusion The use of the finite elements developed, associated with the homogenised model of a periodic bundle of tubes immersed in a fluid, makes it possible to characterise the overall vibratory motions (the whole structure moves in the same direction) of such a structure. 9 Bibliography 1. Sanchez-Palencia (1980), "Non homogeneous media and vibration theory", Springer Verlag. 2. “Asymptotic study of the dynamic behaviour of the fuel assemblies of a nuclear reactor” Siaka Berete, Doctoral thesis of the Université Paris VI, defended on 19 April 1991. 3.