e substantial range of space estimators existing in Code_Aster. For the first time, time indicators make it possible to quantify the error in the time discretisation. The prospects of this work are of several kinds: 1. Extend the scope of use of the HM estimators to the non-linear modellings; 2. Develop estimators for the THHM modellings in general; 3. Set up a procedure for cutting the time steps on the basis of the time estimators. For the moment, only the numerical values of the time estimators are supplied in STAT_NON_LINE, without being connected to a mechanism for adapting the time discretisation. 5 Bibliography 1. MEUNIER. A posteriori error analysis for the Hydro-Mechanical couplings and implementation in Code_Aster. Doctoral thesis of the ENPC, 2007. 1. MEUNIER. Thermo-Hydro-Mechanical modelling in Code_Aster : Equations and discretisation. Note HI-23/05/018/A. 1. CHAVANT. THHM modellings. General points and algorithms. Aster document R7.01.10-A 1. GRANET. THHM constitutive models. Aster document R7.01.11-C 1. AINSWORTH&J. T. ODEN. A posteriori error estimation in finite element analysis. Pure and Applied Mathematics. Wiley-Interscience, 2000. 1. MEUNIER. A posteriori error estimation for the steady Hydro-Mechanical problem. Note HI-23/05/032/A, 2005. 1. MEUNIER. Thermo-Hydro-Mechanical modelling in Code_Aster : Equations and discretisation. Note HI-23/05/018/A. 6 History of the versions of the document | Version Aster | Author(s) or contributor(s), organisation | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | | 9.4 | S.MEUNIER EDF-R&D / AMA | Initial text |

r5.03.03 Taking into account the plane-stress hypothesis in non-linear constitutive behaviours r5.03.03 Taking into account the plane-stress hypothesis in non-linear constitutive behaviours Summary: This document describes a general method for integrating non-linear constitutive models (elastoplastic, viscoplastic, damaging, ...) in plane stresses. This is carried out by a static condensation method due to R. de Borst. This method makes it possible to use the C_PLAN modelling, or the modellings COQUE_3D, DKT and TUYAU, for all the incremental constitutive models of (STAT/DYNA)_NON_LINE available in axisymmetric conditions or in plane strains. 1 Introduction One presents here a general method for integrating non-linear constitutive models (plasticity, viscoplasticity, damage) in plane stresses. If the chosen behaviour is not integrated analytically in plane stresses, the method of R. De Borst is activated automatically for the modellings C_PLAN, DKT, COQUE3D and TUYAU. 2 Difficulty of integrating non-linear constitutive behaviours in plane stresses The modelling C_PLAN (as well as the modellings COQUE_3D, DKT, TUYAU) assumes that the local stress state is plane, that is to say that {\sigma}_{zz}=0 , z representing the direction of the normal to the surface. The stress and strain tensors therefore have the following form (in C_PLAN): \varepsilon =( egin{array}{ccc}{\varepsilon}_{xx}& {\varepsilon}_{xy}& 0\ {\varepsilon}_{xy}& {\varepsilon}_{yy}& 0\ 0& 0& {\varepsilon}_{zz}\end{array}) \sigma =( egin{array}{ccc}{\sigma}_{xx}& {\sigma}_{xy}& 0\ {\sigma}_{xy}& {\sigma}_{yy}& 0\ 0& 0& {\sigma}_{zz}\end{array}) eq.2-1 Note: For the shells, one must add terms due to transverse shear ( {\sigma}_{\mathrm{xzn}}{\sigma}_{yz} ), but these are treated elastically and do not enter into the solution of the local behaviour. This hypothesis implies that the corresponding strain is a priori indeterminate (unlike the other two-dimensional modellings, in which one makes a hypothesis directly on {\varepsilon}_{zz} ). It can be determined only with the aid of the constitutive relation. Now, the condition {\sigma}_{zz}=0 is not innocuous for the integration of the behaviour, in which one computes a stress increment \Delta \sigma as a function of the strain increment \Delta \varepsilon supplied by the Newton algorithm. In the case of linear elasticity, taking this condition into account is simple and makes it possible to find: {\varepsilon}_{zz}=- rac{
u}{1-
u }({\varepsilon}_{xx}+{\varepsilon}_{yy}) But if the behaviour is non-linear, \Delta {\varepsilon}_{zz} cannot be computed solely from \Delta u and is not deduced simply from the other components of the strain tensor. Taking this hypothesis into account must then be done (when it is feasible) in a manner specific to each behaviour, and very often leads to additional solution difficulties: this is the case in particular for the von Mises behaviour with isotropic hardening R5.03.02. Because of this, many constitutive models are not available in plane stresses. The method presented here has the great advantage of requiring no particular development in the integration of the behaviour in order to satisfy the plane-stress hypothesis. It can be used as soon as the constitutive model is available in axisymmetric conditions or in plane strains. 3 Principle of the treatment of plane stresses by the De Borst method 3.1 Reminder of the Newton method In non-linear statics one is led to solve the following equation ([cf. R5.03.01]): R(u)={f}^{int}(u)-{f}^{	ext{ext}}(t)	o 0 , eq. 3.1-1 where {f}^{	ext{ext}} is the external force and {f}^{int} the internal force, defined as, {f}^{\int(u)}=\underset{V}{\int}{B}^{T}\sigma (\varepsilon (u))\mathrm{dV} . eq. 3.1-2 By using the finite-element method (FEM), the internal forces, {f}^{int} , are obtained from the stress field, which is itself determined, by a constitutive law, from the strain field, the discretised strain tensor, \varepsilon , being defined as: \varepsilon =Bu . According to the Newton method one solves the system in [eq. 3.1-1] with the following iterative process: 1. {R}^{(n)}=R({u}^{(n)}) 2. \Delta {u}^{(n+1)}=\underset{{K}^{{(n)}^{-1}}}{\underset{\underbrace{}}{-( rac{\partial {R}^{(n)}}{\partial {u}^{(n)}})}}{R}^{(n)}=-{K}^{{(n)}^{-1}}{R}^{(n)} 3. {u}^{(n+1)}={u}^{(n)}+\Delta {u}^{(n)} , repeat 1) until {R}^{(n)} 1. In addition to the computation of {\varepsilon}_{zz} at the local level, which leads to modifying the