n{array}{c}\Delta \hat{\epsilon}\ \Delta {\varepsilon}_{zz}\end{array}) , with \Delta {\sigma}_{zz}={\sigma}_{zz}^{(n+1)}-{\sigma}_{zz}^{(n)} , and one obtains: {\sigma}_{zz}^{(n+1)}\approx {\sigma}_{zz}^{(n)}+{D}_{{}_{21}}^{(n)}\Delta {\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n)}+{D}_{{}_{22}}^{(n)}\Delta {\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n)}	o 0 , eq 3.2-2 From [eq. 3.2-2] one can condense \Delta {\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n)} as, \Delta {\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n)}=- rac{{\sigma}_{zz}^{(n)}+{D}_{{}_{21}}^{(n)}\Delta {\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n)}}{{D}_{{}_{22}}^{(n)}} . eq 3.2-3 It is exactly the condensation in [eq 3.2-3] which allows us to use the 2D framework for the solution with the FEM. Finally, one seeks to correct the 2D components (denoted by the index 1), both for the stresses and for the tangent operator, so that the plane-stress condition is satisfied. The plane-stress algorithm is entirely local, applied within the framework of a global finite-element architecture corresponding to a 2D modelling equivalent to that of plane strain. One places oneself at a Gauss point, where one knows the value of the strain at iteration (n+1), {\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n+1)}={\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n)}+\Delta {\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n)} , and one must compute the values of the stress {\hat{\sigma}}^{(n+1)} and of the tangent operator, {\hat{D}}^{(n+1)} . Algorithm 1: 1. Update {\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n)} , {\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n+1)}={\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n)}- rac{{\sigma}_{zz}^{(n)}+{D}_{{}_{21}}^{(n)}\Delta {\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n)}}{{D}_{{}_{22}}^{(n)}} 1. Compute the intermediate stresses and the tangent operator {\sigma}^{(n+1)} , {D}^{(n+1)} by the 3D constitutive law, imposing {\varepsilon}_{xz}={\varepsilon}_{yz}=0 , {\sigma}^{(n+1)}=\sigma ({\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n+1)},{\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n+1)}) and {D}^{(n+1)}=D({\hat{\varepsilon}}^{(n+1)},{\varepsilon}_{zz}^{(n+1)}) 1. Compute the final stress using the intermediate correction of {	ilde{\varepsilon}}_{zz}^{(n+1)} , \Delta {	ilde{\varepsilon}}_{zz}^{(n+1)}=- rac{{\sig