r5.03.06 Rousselier model in large strains r5.03.06 Rousselier model in large strains Summary One presents here a variant of the Rousselier model which makes it possible to describe the plastic growth of cavities in a steel. The constitutive relation is elastoplastic with isotropic hardening, allows plastic changes of volume and is written in large strains. The latter rely on the theory proposed by Simo and Miehe, modified in order to facilitate the numerical integration of the constitutive law and to place the model back within the framework of generalised standard materials. This model is available in the command STAT_NON_LINE through the keyword RELATION:’ROUSSELIER’ under the factor keyword COMPORTEMENT and with the keyword DEFORMATION:’SIMO_MIEHE’. This model is implemented for the three-dimensional (3D), axisymmetric (AXIS) and plane-strain (D_PLAN) modellings. One presents the writing and the numerical treatment of this model. > Contents 1 Introduction The mechanisms at the origin of the ductile fracture of steels are associated with the development of cavities within the material. One generally distinguishes three phases: - nucleation: this is the initiation of the cavities, at sites which correspond preferentially to the defects of the material, - growth: this is the phase which corresponds to the development proper of the cavities, driven essentially by the plastic flow of the metallic matrix which surrounds these cavities, - coalescence: this is the phase which corresponds to the interaction of the cavities with one another in order to create macroscopic cracks. In what follows, we treat only the phases of growth and of coalescence. Rousselier [bib1] proposed a constitutive law capable of accounting for these two phases. With respect to this original formulation, Lorentz et al. [bib2] introduced several modifications bearing essentially on the treatment of large strains (multiplicative decomposition), the evolution of the porosity (a function of the total strain) and the expression of the flow rule at the singular point of the threshold surface. More precisely, the model is based on assumptions which introduce a microstructure made up of a cavity and of a rigid-plastic, hence isochoric, matrix. In this case, the porosity f , defined as the ratio between the volume of the cavity {V}^{C} and the total volume V of the representative elementary volume, is directly related to the macroscopic strain by: J=\detF= rac{1-{f}_{0}}{1-f}	ext{avec}f= rac{{V}^{c}}{V}\iff \dot{f}=(1-f)	ext{tr}D eq 1-1 where {f}_{0} denotes the initial porosity, F the transformation-gradient tensor, J the variation of volume and \mathrm{nitalicD} the strain rate. In order to construct the cavity-growth law, Rousselier drew inspiration from a phenomenological analysis which leads him to the following ingredients: - large plastic strains, - irreversible changes of volume, - isotropic hardening. These considerations lead him to write the plasticity criterion F in the following form: F(	au ,R)={	au}_{	ext{eq}}+{\sigma}_{1}Df\exp( rac{{	au}_{H}}{{\sigma}_{1}})-R(p)-{\sigma}_{y} eq 1-2 where 	au is the Kirchhoff stress, R the isotropic hardening, a function of the cumulative plastic strain p , and {\sigma}_{1} , D and {\sigma}_{y} material parameters. The presence in the plasticity criterion of the hydrostatic stress {	au}_{H} authorises plastic changes of volume. One also notes that this model does not include a specific damage variable because the only microstructural information retained is the porosity, directly linked to the macroscopic strain by equation [eq 1-1]. As for the treatment of large strains, one adopts the theory of Simo and Miehe but in a slightly modified formulation. The approximations introduced make it possible to make easier the numerical integration of the constitutive law but also to place the theory of Simo and Miehe back within the framework of generalised standard materials. Subsequently, one briefly gives a few notions of mechanics in large strains, then one recalls the theory of Simo and Miehe as well as the modifications introduced. One finally presents the constitutive relations of the Rousselier model and its numerical integration. 2 Notations One will denote by: | \mathrm{Id} | identity matrix | | --- | --- | | | | | 	ext{tr}A | trace of the tensor A | | | | | {A}^{T} | transpose of the tensor A | | | | | \detA | determinant of A | | | | | 	ilde{A} | deviatoric part of the tensor A defined by 	ilde{A}=A-( rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}A)	ext{Id} | | | | | {A}_{H} | hydrostatic part of the tensor A defined by {A}_{H}= rac{	ext{tr}A}{3} | | : | doubly contracted product: A:B=\sum_{i,j}{A}_{ij}{B}_{ij}=	ext{tr}({	ext{AB}}^{T}) | | | | | \otimes | tensor product: (A\otimes B{)}_{ijkl}={A}_{ij}{B}_{kl} | | {A}_{	ext{eq}} | von Mises equivalent value defined by {A}_{	ext{eq}}=\sqrt{ rac{3}{2}	ilde{A}:	ilde{A}} | | | | | {
