 strains, - this model leads, during its numerical integration, to an incrementally objective model (cf. [§3.2.3]) which makes it possible to obtain the exact solution in the presence of large rotations. Subsequently, one briefly recalls a few notions of mechanics in large strains. 3.2 Generalities on large strains 3.2.1 Kinematics Consider a solid subjected to large strains. Let {\Omega}_{0} be the domain occupied by the solid before deformation and \Omega (t) the domain occupied at the instant t by the deformed solid. Figure 3.2.1-a: Representation of the initial and deformed configurations In the initial configuration {\Omega}_{0} , the position of any particle of the solid is designated by X (Lagrangian description). After deformation, the position at the instant t of the particle which occupied the position X before deformation is given by the variable x (Eulerian description). The global motion of the solid is defined, with u the displacement, by: x=\hat{x}(X,t)=X+u eq 3.2.1-1 In order to define the change of metric in the neighbourhood of a point, one introduces the transformation-gradient tensor F : F= rac{\partial \hat{x}}{\partial X}=	ext{Id}+{
