r5.03.09 Non-linear 1D constitutive relations r5.03.09 Non-linear 1D constitutive relations Summary: This document describes the quantities computed by the operator STAT_NON_LINE which are necessary for the implementation of the quasi-static non-linear algorithm described in R5.03.01 in the case of unidimensional elastoplastic or viscoplastic constitutive behaviours. These behaviours, unless otherwise stated, are applicable to BARRE elements, to beam elements and multifibre beams (axial direction only) and to concrete reinforcement elements (modelling GRILLE). The constitutive behaviours described in this document are: - the Von Mises behaviour with linear isotropic hardening: VMIS_ISOT_LINE, and arbitrary VMIS_ISOT_TRAC, - the Von Mises behaviour with linear kinematic hardening: VMIS_CINE_LINE, - the Von Mises behaviour with linear hardening, non-symmetric in tension and compression, with restoration of the centre of the elastic domain: VMIS_ASYM_LINE. The latter was developed in order to model the action of the soil on Gas-Insulated Cables, - the viscoplastic behaviours with an irradiation effect: VISC_IRRA_LOG, GRAN_IRRA_LOG. - the MAZARS behaviour in its \mathrm{1D} version. The \mathrm{1D} version of the Mazars model makes it possible to account for the restoration of stiffness in the event of crack closure. - the behaviour for modelling the relaxation of prestressed cables. The solution is carried out, case by case, by an implicit integration method, unless otherwise stated; from the previous analysis time instant, one computes the stress field resulting from a strain increment, and the tangent behaviour which makes it possible to construct the tangent matrices. Finally, one describes a method, similar to the method due to R. de Borst R5.03.03, making it possible to use all the behaviours available in 3D in the 1D elements. 1 Use of the 1D constitutive relations 1.1 1D constitutive relations The relations treated in this document are: | VMIS_ISOT_LINE | Von Mises with symmetric linear isotropic hardening | | --- | --- | | VMIS_ISOT_TRAC | Von Mises with arbitrary isotropic hardening | | VMIS_CINE_LINE | Von Mises with symmetric linear kinematic hardening. | | ECRO_CINE_1D | Von Mises with symmetric linear kinematic hardening. | | GRILLE_ISOT_LINE | Von Mises with symmetric linear isotropic hardening | | GRILLE_CINE_LINE | Von Mises with symmetric linear kinematic hardening | | VMIS_ASYM_LINE | Von Mises with asymmetric linear hardening and restoration | | VISC_IRRA_LOG, GRAN_IRRA_LOG | Viscoplastic behaviour of the fuel assemblies: models derived from the REFLET and FLETAN tests | | MAZARS | MAZARS behaviour in its \mathrm{1D} version. | | RELAX_ACIER | Behaviour for modelling the relaxation of prestressed cables. | These (incremental) constitutive relations are given in the operator STAT_NON_LINE U4.51.03 under the factor keyword COMPORTEMENT, by the keyword RELATION U4.51.03. They are valid only in small strains. One describes, for each constitutive relation, the computation of the stress field from a given strain increment (cf. Newton algorithm R5.03.01), the computation of the nodal forces R and of the tangent matrix {K}_{i}^{n} . 1.2 General notation All the quantities evaluated at the previous time instant are indexed by - . The quantities evaluated at the time instant t+\Delta t are not indexed. The increments are denoted by \Delta . One thus has: Q=Q(t+\Delta t)={Q}^{-}(t)+\Delta Q={Q}^{-}+\Delta Q | \sigma | stress tensor (in 1D, one is interested only in the single non-zero uniaxial component). | | --- | --- | | 	ilde{\sigma} | deviator operator: 	ilde{{\sigma}_{ij}}={\sigma}_{ij}- rac{1}{3}{\sigma}_{kk}{\delta}_{ij} . | | (	ext{})\mathrm{eq} | Von Mises equivalent value, equal in 1D to the absolute value | | \Delta \varepsilon | strain increment. | | A | elasticity tensor, equal in 1D to Young's modulus E | | \lambda ,\mu ,E,K | coefficients of isotropic elasticity. | | \alpha | secant thermal expansion coefficient. | | T | temperature. | | {(	ext{})}_{+} | positive part. | | P | cumulative plastic strain | | {\varepsilon}^{p} | plastic strain | 1.3 Change of variables Whatever the type of finite element referring to a 1D constitutive law, one must carry out a change of variables in order to pass from the elementary quantities (forces, displacements) to the stresses and strains. 1.3.1 Computation of the strains (small strains) For each of the finite elements of Code_Aster , in STAT_NON_LINE, the global algorithm (Newton) supplies to the elementary routine, which integrates the behaviour, an increment of the displacement field. For the bar elements, one computes the strain (a single axial component) by: \varepsilon = rac{u(l)-u(0)}{l} , and the strain increment by: \Delta \varepsilon = rac{\Delta u(l)-\Delta u(0)}{l} , For the grid elements (modellings GRILLE and GRILLE_MEMBRANE), one computes the membrane strain as for the DKT shell elements. Simply, a single direction corresponds physically to the directions of the reinforcements. One is therefore in the presence of a 1D behaviour. Moreover, in small strains, for all the models described in this document, one writes for every time instant the partition of the strains in the form of an elastic contribution, of thermal expansion, and of plastic strain: \varepsilon (t)={\varepsilon}^{e}(t)+{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{th}}(t)+{\varepsilon}^{p}(t) with  egin{array}{}{\varepsilon}^{e}(t)={A}^{-1}(T(t))\sigma (t)= rac{1}{E(T)}\sigma (t)\ {\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{th}}(t)=\alpha (T(t))(T(t)-{T}_{\mathrm{ref}})\mathrm{Id}\end{array} 1.3.2 Computation of the generalised forces (integrated stresses) After integration of the 1D behaviour, one must integrate the stress component obtained, in order to supply to the global algorithm (Newton) a vector containing the generalised forces. For the bar elements, one computes the force (uniform in the element, assuming that the cross-section is constant) by: N=S\sigma , and the equivalent nodal force vector (as for the beam elements, R3.08.01) by: F=\left[ egin{array}{}-N\ N\end{array}ight] For the GRILLE elements, one computes the forces as for the DKT shell elements (membrane forces) by integration of the stresses through the thickness (a single layer and a single integration point). 2 Von Mises behaviour with linear isotropic hardening: VMIS_ISOT_LINE or VMIS_ISOT_TRAC 2.1 Equations of the model VMIS_ISOT_LINE They are the restriction of the 3D behaviour R5.03.02 to the uniaxial case: ( egin{array}{c} ar{\dot{{\varepsilon}^{p}}}= rac{3}{2} ar{\dot{p}} rac{	ilde{\sigma}}{{\sigma}_{\mathrm{eq}}}= ar{\dot{p}} rac{\sigma}{\mid \sigma \mid }\  rac{\sigma}{E}=\varepsilon -{\varepsilon}^{p}-{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{th}}\ {\sigma}_{\mathrm{eq}}-R(p)=\mid \sigma \mid -R(p)\le 0\ ( egin{array}{c} ar{\dot{p}}=0\mathrm{si}{\sigma}_{\mathrm{eq}}-R(p) R({p}^{-}) then one must solve:  egin{array}{}{\sigma}^{e}={\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma +E\Delta p rac{{\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma }{∣{\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma ∣}\ {\sigma}^{e}=(1+ rac{E\Delta p}{∣{\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma ∣})({\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma )\end{array} hence, taking the absolute value: ∣{\sigma}^{e}∣=(1+ rac{E\Delta p}{∣{\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma ∣})({\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma ) that is, using  egin{array}{}∣{\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma ∣=R({p}^{-}+\Delta p)\ ∣{\sigma}^{e}∣=R({p}^{-}+\Delta p)+E\Delta p\end{array} . One therefore deduces: - in the case of a linear hardening: \Delta p= rac{∣{\sigma}^{e}∣-({\sigma}_{y}+{\mathrm{Hp}}^{-})}{E+H} - and in the case of an arbitrary hardening, the curve R(p) being refined piecewise, one solves directly the equation in \Delta p : in the same way E\Delta p+R({p}^{-}+\Delta p)=∣{\sigma}^{e}∣ as in 3D R5.03.02. Let us note in passing that:  rac{{\sigma}^{e}}{∣{\sigma}^{e}∣}= rac{\sigma}{R(p)} then \sigma =({\sigma}^{-}+\Delta \sigma )= rac{{\sigma}^{e}}{∣{\sigma}^{e}∣}R(p)= rac{{\sigma}^{e}}{1+ rac{E\Delta p}{R(p)}} Moreover, the option FULL_MECA makes it possible to compute the tangent matrix {K}_{i}^{n} at each iteration. The tangent operator which serves to construct it is computed directly on the previous discretised system. One obtains directly: if ∣{\sigma}^{e}∣>R({p}^{-})  rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }={E}_{T} otherwise  rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }=E Note: The option RIGI_MECA_TANG, which makes it possible to compute the tangent matrix {K}_{i}^{0} used in the prediction phase of the Newton algorithm, takes into account the plasticity indicator at the previous time instant: > if \chi =1 rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }={E}_{T} if \chi =0 rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }=E 2.3 Internal variables The constitutive relation VMIS_ISOT_LINE produces two internal variables: p and \chi 3 Von Mises behaviour, 1D linear kinematic hardening: VMIS_CINE_LINE 3.1 Equation of the model VMIS_CINE_LINE For reasons of performance the relation is written in 1D. They are the restriction of the 3D behaviour (R5.03.02 and R5.03.16) to the uniaxial case. The 3D behaviour is written: \sigma =K(\varepsilon -{\varepsilon}^{p}-{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{th}}) with K the elasticity operator X=C{\varepsilon}^{p} F(\sigma ,R,X)={(	ilde{\sigma}-X)}_{\mathrm{eq}}-{\sigma}_{y} with {A}_{\mathrm{eq}}=\sqrt{ rac{3}{2}	ilde{A}\cdot 	ilde{A}} \dot{{\varepsilon}^{p}}=\dot{p} rac{\partial F}{\partial \sigma }= rac{3}{2}\dot{p rac{	ilde{\sigma}-X}{{(	ilde{\sigma}-X)}_{\mathrm{eq}}}} \lbrace  egin{array}{c}\mathrm{si}F R({p}^{-})  rac{\delta \partial }{\delta \varepsilon }={E}_{T} otherwise  rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }=E The option RIGI_MECA_TANG, which makes it possible to compute the tangent matrix {K}_{i}^{0} used in the prediction phase of the Newton algorithm, is obtained with the aid of the plasticity indicator {\chi }^{-} of the previous time instant: - if {\chi }^{-}=1 then  rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }={E}_{T} - if {\chi }^{-}=0 then  rac{\delta \sigma }{\delta \varepsilon }=E 3.3 Internal variables The constitutive relation VMIS_CINE_LINE produces two internal variables: X and \chi 4 Von Mises behaviour, 1D linear kinematic hardening: VMIS_CINE_GC 4.1 Equation of the model VMIS_CINE_GC For reasons of performance the relation is also written in 1D for use with finite elements of multifibre-beam type. The equations come from the restriction of the 3D behaviour (R5.03.02 and R5.03.16) to the uniaxial case. The equations of the model are the same as those of § 3.1 . The material data are those supplied under the factor keyword ECRO_LINE of the operator DEFI_MATERIAU [U4.43.01]: /ECRO_LINE=_F( > ♦D_SIGM_EPSI = {E}_{T} [Real] > ♦SY = {\sigma}_{y} [Real] > ◊SIGM_LIM =sigmlim [Real] > ◊EPSI_LIM =epsilim [Real] ) The operands sigm_lim and espi_lim make it possible to define the bounds which correspond to the serviceability and ultimate limit states, classically used during a civil-engineering study. ◊SIGM_LIM=sigmlim Definition of the limit stress. ◊EPSI_LIM=epslim Definition of the limit strain. These bounds are obligatory when one uses the behaviour VMIS_CINE_GC (Cf. U4.51.11 Non-linear behaviours, U4.42.07 DEFI_MATER_GC). In the other cases they are not taken into account. 4.2 Integration of the relation VMIS_CINE_GC The integration method is identical to that presented in § 3.2 . 4.3 Internal variables The supported modelling is 1D; the number of internal variables is 6. - \mathit{V1} : This variable represents the stress divided by the limit stress sigmlim. - \mathit{V2} : This variable represents the total strain divided by the limit strain epslim. - \mathit{V3} : Kinematic hardening: XCINXX. In 1D only a scalar is necessary. - \mathit{V4} : Plastic indicator: INDIPLAS. Indicates whether the material has exceeded the elastic criterion. - \mathit{V5} : non-recoverable dissipation: DISSIP. During seismic analyses it may be useful to the user to know the non-recoverable dissipated energy. The variable dissip represents the cumulated non-recoverable energy. The increme