e formulation adopted is based on that proposed by J. Lemaitre: \sigma =E.{\epsilon}^{e} \epsilon ={\epsilon}^{e}+{\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}} {{\dot{\epsilon}}^{\mathit{an}}=⟨ rac{\sigma –R\left({\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}}ight)}{{f}_{\mathit{prg}}.k}⟩}^{n} with R\left({\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}}ight)= rac{{f}_{\mathit{prg}}.c.{\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}}}{{\left(1+{\left(b.{\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}}ight)}^{\mathit{nr}}ight)}^{ rac{1}{\mathit{nr}}}} [eq9.1-1] The constitutive law is 1D, and available only for modellings of bar type, which are used to model the prestressing cables in the operator DEFI_CABLE_BP. In order to take into account the influence of the temperature on the relaxation, all the coefficients of the law may be functions of the temperature. Remarque: The parameters c , b , n and \mathit{nr} are dimensionless, and therefore independent of the units used during the study. k is made dimensionless with respect to {f}_{\mathit{prg}} , hence with respect to the stresses, but not with respect to time. Indeed {\dot{\epsilon}}^{\mathit{an}} is homogeneous to {[s]}^{-1} , if the unit of time is the second. Therefore, if one knows k for a strain rate in one unit of time, it is necessary to convert its value with respect to the unit of time used during the study. 8.2 Internal variables Two internal variables: - V1 : the cumulative anelastic strain: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{an}} . - V2 : storage of the tangent stiffness of the behaviour. 8.3 Explicit integration The integration is carried out by a Runge–Kutta method of order five with variable steps. 8.4 Identification of the parameters Several solutions are possible: - An analytical piece of work makes it possible to determine relations between the coefficients present in the regulatory formulae and that of the constitutive law: tangent at the beginning of the relaxation, asymptote for an “infinite” time, … - Simulation, with the aid of the regulatory formulae, of several “relaxation tests”. For a given type of cable, which corresponds to a set of parameters (k1, k2, {ho}_{1000} , {f}_{\mathit{prg}} ). Different values of \mu will make it possible to obtain a bundle of “tests” on which an identification of the parameters can be carried out. - A bibliographical study in order to recover the experimental and theoretical work carried out on the relaxation of prestressing cables, so as to use the tests directly and to identify the parameters. 8.5 Result of an identification This identification was carried out with respect to the simulation of a relaxation test obtained with the regulatory formulae. One places oneself in the case {ho}_{1000}=2.5	ext{\%} ( k1=6.0E-03 , k2=1.1 ) with {f}_{\mathit{prg}}=1800\mathit{MPa} , E=190000\mathit{MPa} . The initial strain corresponds to a loading level of \mu =0.75 . The simulation as well as the identification are carried out over [0h, 4000h]. The figures below compare the result of the identification with the regulatory curve. Figure 9.5-a: Evolution of the stress in the course of time: reference and code_aster. Figure 9.5-b: Zoom on the first 10 hours, of the evolution of the stress in the course of time. The observations with respect to the identification: - Very good correspondence between the identified law and the regulatory law. - Very good identification of the slope at the origin and of the asymptote. The law proposed is therefore capable of describing correctly the relaxation of a prestressing cable, if its relaxation follows the regulatory curves. 8.6 Use in code_aster The definition of the material parameters is carried out in the usual way in the command DEFi_materiau, keyword RELAX_ACIER; all the parameters may depend on the temperature. | {f}_{\mathit{prg}} | Stress at failure of the cable. This quantity is optional, because it can also be defined in the materials BPEL_ACIER or ETCC_ACIER and in this case {f}_{\mathit{prg}} is a constant. The value/function given under RELAX_ACIER_CABL is priority. | | --- | --- | | ECOU_K ECOU_N | Corr