r5.03.15 Viscoplastic behaviour with CHABOCHE damage r5.03.15 Viscoplastic behaviour with CHABOCHE damage Summary: The viscoplastic model coupled to the isotropic damage of Chaboche (originally developed in order to predict successfully the lifetime and the cracking of the blades of modern turbojets) is used for analyses predicting the time to failure of structures at high temperatures. This model is implemented in Code_Aster under the name VENDOCHAB; the rate equations are integrated numerically by an explicit Runge-Kutta scheme of order 2 with automatic subdivision into local sub-steps according to an estimate of the integration error (embedded Runge-Kutta method, see R5.03.14), or by an implicit integration method R5.03.01. The tests SSNV126 and SSNV183 validate the integration of this model. The validation document V6.04.126 supplies the analytical solution for an isothermal uniaxial creep test. > Contents 1 Introduction The finite-element analyses carried out in the framework of the studies on severe accidents of nuclear reactors have highlighted the need to use damage models in order to predict the failure of a structure such as the vessel subjected to the severe and complex thermal conditions (high temperatures going as far as melting, strong thermal gradients in space or in time, etc.) that the corium would impose on it [bib1]. The major interest of this choice lies in the fact that the value of the damage variable at rupture (or at cracking) can be regarded as an intrinsic parameter of the material which is accessible, although this is difficult and delicate, by physical measurements (ultrasonics, X diffraction, etc.). The rupture criterion with damage theory is then more "physical" than the maximum-strain criteria sometimes used in viscoplastic analyses without damage, or the uncoupled damage criteria (cumulative rule of the time actually spent under certain conditions (\sigma ,T) divided by the time to failure for those same conditions). The model implemented in Code_Aster is a model of viscoplastic constitutive behaviour with multiplicative viscosity hardening coupled to isotropic damage (model due to Chaboche [bib2]). Nota Bene: A detailed description of the capabilities of the model, a methodology for the identification of the parameters and the values of these parameters for steel 22MoNiCr37 will be found in reference [bib3]. 2 Formulation of the model 2.1 Theoretical framework In this sub-chapter, the specificity of the law VENDOCHAB (that is to say the damage) with respect to the usual viscoplastic models is emphasised. For further details, the reader is referred to [bib2]. Damage theory describes the evolution of the phenomena between the virgin state and the initiation of the macroscopic crack in a material by means of a continuous variable (scalar or tensorial) describing the progressive deterioration of this material. This idea, due to Kachanov, who was the first to use it to model rupture by creep of metals under uniaxial loading, was taken up in France in the 1970s by Lemaitre and Chaboche. The evolution of the material from its virgin state to its damaged state is not always easy to distinguish from the strain phenomenon that most often accompanies it, and is due to several different mechanisms, of which creep is one. Viscoplastic creep damage corresponds to the intergranular decohesions accompanying the viscoplastic strains for metals at medium and high temperatures. In order to define what this damage variable is, consider the area S of one of the faces of a volume element \Omega located by its outward normal 	ext{n} . On this section, the microcracks and the cavities that constitute the damage leave traces of various shapes. Let 	ilde{S} be the effective resisting area and {S}_{D} the total area of all the traces. One has: {S}_{D}=S-	ilde{S} and the damage variable is defined by: {D}_{n}= rac{{S}_{D}}{S} {D}_{n} is the measure of the local damage relative to the direction n . From a physical point of view, the damage variable {D}_{n} is therefore the relative area of the cracks and cavities cut by the plane normal to the direction \overrightarrow{n} . From a mathematical point of view, by letting S tend to 0, the variable {D}_{\overrightarrow{n}} is the surface density of the discontinuities of the matter in the plane normal to n . {D}_{n}=0 corresponds to the undamaged virgin state. {D}_{n}=1 corresponds to the volume element broken into two parts along a plane normal to n . The isotropy assumption implies that the cracks and cavities are uniformly distributed in orientation at a point of the material. In this case, the damage variable becomes a scalar which no longer depends on the orientation and is denoted D . One has: D={D}_{n} orall n Only the isotropic damage variable will be considered here. The global mechanical measurements (modification of the elasticity, plasticity or viscoplasticity characteristics) are easier to interpret in terms of a damage variable thanks to the notion of effective stress introduced by Rabotnov. The effective stress represents the stress related to the section that effectively resists the forces. In the case of isotropic damage, it is written: 	ilde{\sigma}= rac{\sigma}{(1-D)} And one has: 1. 	ilde{\sigma}=\sigma for a virgin material 2. 	ilde{\sigma}	o +\infty at the moment of rupture The principle of equivalence in strain implies that any strain behaviour, unidimensional or three-dimensional, of a damaged material is expressed by the constitutive laws of the virgin material in which the usual stress is replaced by the effective stress. Two types of variables are distinguished in order to characterise the medium: The observable (measurable) variables: - the temperature T - the total strain \underline{\underline{\varepsilon}} which is decomposed as indicated below: \underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}={\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{e}+{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{vp}}+{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{th}} The internal variables: - the viscoplastic strain {\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{vp}} - the isotropic hardening variable r - the isotropic damage variable D Let \Psi =\Psi (\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}},{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{vp}},T,r,D) , the state potential; the state laws describing this potential are: \lbrace  egin{array}{}\underline{\underline{\sigma}}=ho  rac{\partial \Psi }{\partial \underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}\ R=-ho  rac{\partial \Psi }{\partial r}\ s=-ho  rac{\partial \Psi }{\partial T}\ Y=-ho  rac{\partial \Psi }{\partial D}\end{array} According to the normality law, one has, with \Phi , the dissipation potential: \lbrace  egin{array}{}{\underline{\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}}^{	ext{vp}}= rac{\partial \Phi }{\partial \underline{\underline{\sigma}}}\ \dot{r}= rac{\partial \Phi }{\partial R}\ \dot{D}= rac{\partial \Phi }{\partial Y}\end{array} The modelling of the hardening and of the damage of the material is done by means of internal (or hidden) variables. In the case of the model VENDOCHAB, the internal variables introduced in Code_Aster are: - {\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{vp}} : tensor of the inelastic strains - p : cumulative plastic strain - r : viscosity hardening variable - D : scalar variable of isotropic damage 2.2 Equations of the model The equations of the model are then written: \lbrace  egin{array}{}\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}={\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{e}+{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{th}}+{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{	ext{vp}}\ \underline{\underline{\sigma}}=(1-D)\underline{\underline{\underline{\underline{\Lambda}}}}{\underline{\underline{\varepsilon}}}^{e}\ {\underline{\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}}^{	ext{vp}}= rac{3}{2}\dot{p} rac{{\underline{\underline{\sigma}}}^{'}}{{\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}}\ \dot{p}= rac{\dot{r}}{(1-D)}\ \dot{r}={\langle  rac{{\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}-{\sigma}_{y}(1-D)}{(1-D)K{r}^{}}angle }^{N}\ \dot{D}={\