r5.03.21 Elasto(visco)plastic modelling with isotropic hardening in large strains r5.03.21 Elasto(visco)plastic modelling with isotropic hardening in large strains Summary One describes here a thermoelastoplastic constitutive relation with isotropic hardening written in large strains and proposed by Simo and Miehe. This model is available in the command STAT_NON_LINE through the keyword RELATION:’VMIS_ISOT_TRAC’, ‘VMIS_ISOT_PUIS’ or ’VMIS_ISOT_LINE’ under the factor keyword COMPORTEMENT and with the keyword DEFORMATION:’SIMO_MIEHE’. A viscous version of this model is also proposed: ’VISC_ISOT_TRAC’ and ’VISC_ISOT_LINE’. This model is implemented for the three-dimensional (3D), axisymmetric (AXIS) and plane-strain (D_PLAN) modellings. One presents the writing and the numerical treatment of this law, as well as the associated variational formulation. It is an Eulerian variational formulation, with updating of the geometry, which takes into account the constitutive stiffness and the geometric stiffness. 1 Introduction We present here a thermoelastoplastic constitutive law written in large strains proposed by SIMO J.C. and MIEHE C. [bib1] which tends, in small strains, towards the elastoplastic constitutive model with isotropic hardening and von Mises criterion, described in R5.03.02. The kinematic choices make it possible, as with the simple updating available via the keyword PETIT_REAC, to treat large displacements and large strains but also large rotations in an exact manner. The specific features of this model are the following: - just as in small strains, one assumes the existence of a relaxed configuration, that is locally stress-free, which makes it possible to decompose the total strain into a thermoelastic part and a plastic part, - the decomposition of this strain into thermoelastic and plastic parts is no longer additive as in small strains (or for the large-strain models written in strain rates with for example a Jaumann derivative) but multiplicative, - the elastic strains are measured in the current (deformed) configuration whereas the plastic strains are measured in the initial configuration, - as in small strains, the stresses depend only on the thermoelastic strains, - the plastic strains take place at constant volume. The variation of volume is then due only to the thermoelastic strains, - this model leads, during its numerical integration, to an incrementally objective model (cf. [§3.3]) which makes it possible to obtain the exact solution in the presence of large rotations. A viscous version of this model is also available (hyperbolic-sine law as in the case of the Rousselier model ROUSS_VISC, cf. R5.03.07). In what follows, one briefly recalls a few notions of mechanics in large strains, then one presents the constitutive relations of the model and its numerical integration in order to treat the equilibrium equations. One proposes an Eulerian variational formulation, with updating of the geometry. In this respect, one expresses the work of the internal forces and its variation (with a view to a solution by the Newton method) for the continuous problem, which supply respectively, after discretisation by finite elements, the internal-force vector and the tangent matrix. Note : One will find in [bib2] or [bib3] a thorough presentation of large strains. This document is extracted from [bib4] where one gives a more detailed presentation of the elastoplastic model, of its numerical integration and where one gives a few validation examples. 2 Notations One will denote by: | \mathrm{Id} | identity matrix | | --- | --- | | | | | 	ext{tr}A | trace of the tensor A | | | | | {A}^{T} | transpose of the tensor A | | | | | \detA | determinant of A | | | | | \langle Xangle | positive part of X | | | | | 	ilde{A} | deviatoric part of the tensor A defined by 	ilde{A}=A-( rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}A)	ext{Id} | | | | | : | doubly contracted product: A:B=\sum_{i,j}{A}_{ij}{B}_{ij}=	ext{tr}({	ext{AB}}^{T}) | | | | | \otimes | tensor produc