ion coefficient), - TRACTION for ‘VMIS_ISOT_TRAC’ and ‘VISC_ISOT_TRAC’ (one gives the rational tensile curve), - ECRO_PUIS for ‘VMIS_ISOT_PUIS’ (one gives the parameters of the power law), - ECRO_LINE for ‘VMIS_ISOT_LINE’ and ‘VISC_ISOT_LINE’ (one gives the elastic limit and the hardening slope), - VISC_SINH for ‘VISC_ISOT_TRAC’ and ‘VISC_ISOT_LINE’ (one gives the three viscosity coefficients). Note : The user must make sure that the “experimental” tensile curve used, either directly or in order to deduce from it the hardening slope, is indeed given in the plane rational stress \sigma =F/S - logarithmic strain \ln(1+\Delta l/{l}_{0}) where {l}_{0} is the initial length of the gauge length of the specimen, \Delta l the change in length after deformation, F the applied force and S the current area . One will note that \sigma =F/S= rac{F}{{S}_{0}} rac{{l}_{0}}{l} rac{1}{J} whence 	au =J\sigma = rac{F}{{S}_{0}} rac{{l}_{0}}{l} . In general, it is indeed the quantity  rac{F}{{S}_{0}} rac{{l}_{0}}{l} which is measured by experimentalists and this gives directly the Kirchhoff stress used in the model of Simo and Miehe. 4.5 Stresses and internal variables The stresses are the Cauchy stresses , computed therefore on the current configuration (six components in 3D, four in 2D). The internal variables produced in Code_Aster are: - \mathit{V1} , the cumulated plastic strain p , - \mathit{V2} the plasticity indicator (0 if the last computed increment is elastic, 1 otherwise). - \mathit{V3} to \mathit{V8} : the opposite of the elastic strain { ar{b}}^{e} Note : If the user wishes possibly to recover strains in the post-processing of the analysis, the Green-Lagrange strains E , which represent a measure of the strains in large strains (options EPSG_ELGA or EPSG_ELNO ), must be plotted. The classical linearised strains \varepsilon measure strains under the small-strain assumption and have no meaning in large strains. 4.6 Range of use The choice of a kinematics DEFORMATION: ‘PETIT_REAC’ also makes it possible to treat a thermo-elastoplastic constitutive law with isotropic hardening and von Mises criterion in large strains. The law is written in small strains and large strains are taken into account by updating the geometry. Between the law presented here (SIMO_MIEHE ) and PETIT_REAC, - there is no difference if the strains are small - there is no difference if the strains are large but the rotations small - there are differences if the rotations are large. In particular, the solution obtained with the kinematics PETIT_REAC may depart noticeably from the exact solution in the presence of large rotations, and this whatever the size of the time steps chosen by the user, unlike the kinematics SIMO_MIEHE. 4.7 Integration of the constitutive law In the case of an incremental behaviour, factor keyword COMPORTEMENT, knowing the stress {\sigma}^{-} , the cumulated plastic strain {p}^{-} , the trace divided by three of the elastic strain tensor  rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}{ ar{b}}^{e-} , the displacements {u}^{-} and \Delta u and the temperatures {T}^{-} and T , one seeks to determine (\sigma ,p, rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}{ ar{b}}^{e}) . The displacements being known, the gradients of the transformation from {\Omega}_{0} to {\Omega}^{-} , denoted {F}^{-} , and from {\Omega}^{-} to \Omega , denoted \Delta F , are known. The implicit discretisation of the law gives: F=\Delta F{F}^{-} J=\detF  ar{F}={J}^{-1/3}F { ar{b}}^{e}= ar{F}{G}^{p}{ ar{F}}^{T} J\sigma =	au 	ilde{	au}=\mu { ar{	ilde{b}}}^{e}  rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}	au = rac{1}{2}K({J}^{2}-1)- rac{3}{2}K\alpha (T-{T}_{	ext{ref}})(J+ rac{1}{J}) f={	au}_{	ext{eq}}-R({p}^{-}+\Delta p)-{\sigma}_{y}  ar{F}({G}^{p}-{G}^{p-}){ ar{F}}^{T}=-	ext{tr}{ ar{b}}^{e} rac{	ilde{	au}}{{	au}_{	ext{eq}}}\Delta p whence { ar{b}}^{e}= ar{F}{G}^{p-}{ ar{F}}^{T}-	ext{tr}{ ar{b}}^{e} rac{	ilde{	au}}{{	au}_{	ext{eq}}}\Delta p In the plastic case: \Delta p\ge 0,f\le 0	ext{et}f\Delta p=0 In the viscous case: \langle {	au}_{	ext{eq}}-R({p}^{-}+\Delta p)