r5.03.23 Elastoplastic constitutive behaviour under irradiation of metals: application to reactor-vessel internals r5.03.23 Elastoplastic constitutive behaviour under irradiation of metals: application to reactor-vessel internals Summary: This document presents the writing of a constitutive law under irradiation for the stainless steels 304 and 316, the materials of which the internal structures of the vessels of nuclear reactors are made. The formalism of the model is identical for the two materials; only the parameters differ from one material to the other. The model takes into account, besides thermoelasticity, plasticity, irradiation creep, as well as a possible swelling under neutron flux. The implementation of the model, as well as its limits, is detailed here. This constitutive law, although developed for needs specific to EDF, can be used to account for the behaviour of any material presenting characteristics of plasticity, irradiation creep and swelling. 1 Introduction The purpose of this document is to describe the development and the numerical implementation of a constitutive law under irradiation for the materials constituting the internal structure of the vessels of nuclear reactors: the cold-worked steels 316 and 316L for the bolting, as well as the solution-annealed steels 304 and 304L for the baffles, the reinforcements and the core shroud. This constitutive law is formally the same for the two types of steel; only the parameters vary from one material to the other. The model developed must be capable of accounting for (besides thermoelasticity) the plasticity induced by the tightening of the bolts, the irradiation creep well known for metallic materials, and a possible swelling induced by irradiation. The effects of irradiation creep and of swelling are distinguished by the fact that the strain occurs at constant volume for creep, which is not the case for swelling. The various phenomena cited above are listed exhaustively in the document [ref] and will therefore not be the subject of an additional description in what follows. The present document is organised as follows: the constitutive law chosen to describe the response of the solution-annealed steels 304/304L and of the cold-worked steels 316/316L is first set out, as well as the equations allowing its implementation in the form of an implicit integration. Each mechanism is detailed in a general way. The dependence of the material parameters on the command variables, which are the irradiation and the temperature, is made explicit. The numerical values of these parameters are set out in the note EDF R&D HT-26/05/045/A. One then presents the limits of the model and its application in Code_Aster. It should be noted that the formalism presented here, although implemented in the framework of a very specific application, can be used to describe the behaviour of any type of material presenting characteristics of plasticity, irradiation creep and swelling. In that case, it will be incumbent on the user of Code_Aster to obtain the material coefficients necessary for the use of the model. 2 Formulation of the constitutive law One proposes to employ an additive decomposition of the strains. The mechanical strain is then expressed as: > {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{m}={\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e}+{\underline{\varepsilon}}_{p}+{\underline{\varepsilon}}_{i}+{\underline{\varepsilon}}_{g} eq 2-1 The mechanical strain, {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{m} , is defined as the total strain minus the thermal strain: {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{m}=\underline{\varepsilon}-{\varepsilon}_{	ext{th}} . The other components are: the elastic strain {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e} , the plastic strain {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{p} , the irradiation-creep strain {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{i} and the swelling strain {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{g} . - The elastic strain is related to the stress by Hooke's law: \underline{\sigma}=\underline{\underline{E}}:{\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e} . The elasticity tensor \underline{\underline{E}} depends on the temperature T . - The plastic strain is given by a law of von Mises type. The yield surface is expressed as: > f={\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}-{\sigma}^{f} eq 2-2 where {\sigma}_{	ext{eq}} is the von Mises stress and {\sigma}^{f} the yield limit. The latter depends on the cumulative plastic strain, p , on the temperature T , and on the fluence \Phi . The plastic flow is given by the normality rule, so that: > {\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{p}=\dot{p}\underline{n} with \underline{n}= rac{3}{2} rac{\underline{s}}{{\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}} eq 2-3 where \underline{s} is the stress deviator. \dot{p} is given by the consistency condition f=0 and \dot{f}=0 . The irradiation-creep laws are expressed (for a test at constant stress and flux) [ref]: > \varepsilon =\max({A}_{i}\cdot \sigma \cdot \Phi -{A}_{0}) eq 2-4 The coefficient {A}_{0} expresses a threshold effect. In differential form, the previous law may be rewritten as: > \dot{\varepsilon}={A}_{i}\sigma \cdot \varphi if {\eta}_{i}>{A}_{0}/{A}_{i} with {\dot{\eta}}_{i}=\sigma \cdot \varphi eq 2-5 where \varphi is the flux ( \varphi =\dot{\Phi} ). One therefore introduces an additional state variable, {\eta}_{i} , in order to describe the threshold effect. This uniaxial law must be extended to the multiaxial case. Since irradiation creep takes place without change of volume, one will describe this mechanism by a viscoplastic model based on equipotentials given by the von Mises stress. One will also assume that the evolution of the variable {\eta}_{i} is governed by this same stress. One then obtains the following laws: > {\dot{\eta}}_{i}={\zeta}_{f}\cdot \sigma \cdot \varphi eq 2-6 > {\dot{p}}_{i}={A}_{i}\cdot \sigma \cdot \varphi if {\eta}_{i}>{\eta}_{i}^{s} otherwise {\dot{p}}_{i}=0 eq 2-7 > {\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{i}={\dot{p}}_{i}\underline{n} eq 2-8 where {p}_{i} is the equivalent irradiation-creep strain. {A}_{i} and {\eta}_{i}^{s} are coefficients of the model which may depend on the temperature (one may envisage a dependence with respect to the fluence, but the model will be more complex to identify). The function {\zeta}_{f} makes it possible to introduce a dependence with respect to the temperature in the evolution law of the variable {\eta}_{i} (see equation ). One will note that the model thus formulated will be able to operate for variable temperatures and fluxes. Note no. 1: One will note that irradiation “creep” is entirely driven by the fluence and not by time. In the case where a thermal-creep mechanism were added, time would of course play a role explicitly . Note no. 2: In order to ensure that the threshold {\eta}_{i}^{s} is crossed correctly (from a numerical point of view), it is necessary to define an error criterion. This criterion "TOLER_ET" corresponds to the % of overshoot of the threshold that one allows during the numerical integration. The irradiation-creep strain begins as soon as the threshold {\eta}_{i}^{s} is crossed, but one forces Code_Aster to respect a reasonable evolution of the variable {\eta}_{i} in the vicinity of the threshold {\eta}_{i}^{s} . If during the analysis the criterion is not respected, Code_Aster subdivides the time steps, provided that the subdivision of the time steps is authorised. Swelling may be described by laws of the type: >  rac{\Delta V}{{V}_{0}}={F}_{g}(\Phi ) eq 2-9 where \Delta V is the variation of volume and {V}_{0} the initial volume. By differentiating this equation and assuming that the variation of volume remains small ( \Delta V\ll {V}_{0} ) one obtains: >  rac{\dot{V}}{V}= rac{{	ext{dF}}_{g}}{d\Phi }\varphi eq 2-10 This rate of variation is identified with the trace of the swelling rate tensor: \dot{V}/V=\mathrm{trace}({\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{g}) . One assumes here that the swelling takes place in an isotropic manner and therefore that {\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{g} may be expressed as: > {\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{g}=\dot{g}\underline{1} eq 2-11 where \underline{1} is the unit tensor. One therefore has \dot{V}/V=3\dot{g} > That is: \dot{g}={A}_{g}\varphi eq 2-12 with {A}_{g}= rac{1}{3}{	ext{dF}}_{g}/d\Phi . {A}_{g} is a new material parameter. It depends on the temperature and on the fluence. In a problem with variable temperature, it is therefore important to use the equation and not the equation (the latter being implicitly written for a constant temperature). A possible coupling between the stress state and swelling is neglected. The definition of the model rests on five internal variables: p , {\eta}_{i} , {p}_{i} , g and {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e} . The temperature and the fluence are regarded as parameters imposed for a given analysis. These values come from the neutronics and thermal analyses. The equations of the model are recalled in the table . | Internal variables | Evolution equations | | --- | --- | | p | consistency \dot{p} | | {\eta}_{i} | {\dot{\eta}}_{i}={\zeta}_{f}\cdot {\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}\cdot \varphi | | {p}_{i} | {\dot{p}}_{i}={A}_{i}\cdot \sigma \cdot \varphi if {\eta}_{i}>{\eta}_{i}^{s} otherwise {\dot{p}}_{i}={A}_{i}\cdot \sigma \cdot \varphi {\dot{p}}_{i}=0 | | g | \dot{g}={A}_{g}\varphi | | {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e} | {\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{e}={\underline{\dot{\varepsilon}}}_{m}-\dot{p}\cdot \underline{n}-{\dot{p}}_{i}\cdot \underline{n}-\dot{g}\cdot \underline{1} | Table 2.1: The equations of the model. 3 Implementation: implicit integration Implicit integration consists in finding the increments of the internal variables \Delta {V}_{i}=(\Delta P,\Delta {\eta}_{i},\Delta {P}_{i},\Delta g,\Delta {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e}) over a discrete time increment \Delta t [ref]. The discretisation of the equations of the table leads to the following system of non-linear equations: | {\underline{R}}_{e}=\Delta {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{e}+\Delta p\underline{n}+\Delta {p}_{i}\underline{n}+\Delta g\underline{1}-\Delta {\underline{\varepsilon}}_{m}=0 | | --- | | {R}_{p}=