r5.03.36 Constitutive law KICHENIN_NL combining elastoplasticity and non-linear viscoelasticity r5.03.36 Constitutive law KICHENIN_NL combining elastoplasticity and non-linear viscoelasticity Summary: This document describes the KICHENIN_NL constitutive model, intended to describe the mechanical response of polyethylene. It combines an elastoplastic branch with linear kinematic hardening and a non-linear Maxwell viscoelastic branch. A large-strain modelling is available via the GDEF_LOG kinematics. By way of preamble, let us make clear that this documentation is based on the note [Lorentz, 2022]. It retains from it only the main elements concerning the formulation and the numerical integration of the model. The interested reader is referred to this note and its references for further details where appropriate. 1 Scope 1.1 Purpose The constitutive law KICHENIN_NL combines in parallel an elastoplastic model with linear kinematic hardening and a Maxwell viscoelastic model whose dashpot is non-linear, of Norton type. A rheological illustration of it is given below. It is originally dedicated to the description of polyethylene but can no doubt be applied to other materials. This constitutive model is compatible with a small-perturbation kinematics (PETIT) or a large-strain kinematics (GDEF_LOG). Finally, this model describes the stress response as an explicit function of the strain and of the internal variables, and not as an evolution law in incremental stress. The stress does not contribute to defining the mechanical state of the system; it is a consequence of it (in particular, the stress does not enter into the definition of an initial state of the system). 1.2 Material parameters The constitutive relation is based on nine parameters, characteristic of the material considered: - Young's modulus {E}^{p} and Poisson's ratio {
u}^{p} of the elastoplastic branch, defined under the factor keyword ELAS(E, NU) of the command DEFI_MATERIAU; - The elastic limit {\sigma}^{c} and the Prager constant C (without the corrective factor 2/3), defined under the factor keyword KICHENIN_NL (SIGC and PRAGER) of the command DEFI_MATERIAU; - Young's modulus {E}^{v} and Poisson's ratio {
u}^{v} of the viscoelastic branch, defined under the factor keyword KICHENIN_NL (E_VISC and NU_VISC) of the command DEFI_MATERIAU; - the viscosity coefficient \eta , Poisson's ratio {
u}^{d} and the non-linearity exponent n\le 1 of the dashpot, defined under the factor keyword KICHENIN_NL (ETA_AMOR, NU_AMOR and N_AMOR) of the command DEFI_MATERIAU. These parameters may depend on the temperature (factor keywords _FO). The constitutive law is expressed in mechanical strains, that is to say that it is compatible with the usual shrinkage terms, in particular the thermal strain. It is important to note that the stiffness defined under the factor keyword ELAS does not constitute the whole of the (instantaneous) stiffness of the material, but only that relating to the elastoplastic branch. 1.3 Internal variables The constitutive law is based on twelve physical internal variables: the components of the plastic strain {\epsilon}^{p} (at the slider of the elastoplastic branch) and of the viscous strain {\epsilon}^{v} (at the dashpot of the viscoelastic branch). They are completed by the accumulated plastic strain and the accumulated viscous strain. | EPSPEQ | V1 | Accumulated plastic strain | | --- | --- | --- | | EPSPXX - EPSPYZ | V2- V7 | Components of the plastic strain | | EPSVEQ | V8 | Accumulated viscous strain | | EPVXX - EPVYZ | V9- V14 | Components of the viscous strain | 2 Continuous model In accordance with the rheological presentation, the stress \sigma is the sum of two contributions: that coming from the elastoplastic branch {\sigma}^{p} and that coming from the viscoelastic branch {\sigma}^{v} : \sigma ={\sigma}^{p}+{\sigma}^{v} 2.1 Elastoplastic branch This is a classical plasticity model with a von Mises threshold and linear kinematic hardening. The (mechanical) strain \epsilon is decomposed additively into a plastic part {\epsilon}^{p} and an elastic part \epsilon -{\epsilon}^{p} , the latter being related to the stress of the elastoplastic branch by the following elastic relation: {\sigma}^{p}={C}^{p}:(\epsilon -{\epsilon}^{p})=3{K}^{p}\mathit{sph}(\epsilon -{\epsilon}^{p})S+2{\mu}^{p}\mathit{dev}(\epsilon -{\epsilon}^{p})	ext{};	ext{}3{