r5.03.52 1 r5.03.52 Contact elements derived from a continuous hybrid formulation 1 r5.03.52 Contact elements derived from a continuous hybrid formulation Summary: This document describes the manner in which frictional contact elements are derived from a continuous hybrid formulation of contact problems between solids (2D or 3D) in large transformations and specifies the solution strategy used. An original formulation, called the stabilised Lagrangian formulation, is used, which makes it possible to recover the classical cases of the literature (Lagrangian, augmented Lagrangian, penalisation) by a judicious choice of its parameters. This formulation is available in the command DEFI_CONTACT under the name 'CONTINUE'. 2 r5.03.52 Contents 3 r5.03.52 List of figures 1 Introduction There exists, for the treatment of the frictional-contact problem, a significant gap between the so-called “discrete” formulations and the so-called “continuous” formulation. Implementations in analysis codes are based on models such as those developed by Hallquist [], Alart and Curnier [], Laursen and Simo ([]). The two essential objects of this document are, on the one hand, the description of the derivation of contact elements from a mixed variational formulation of a contact problem between three-dimensional solids, deformable and undergoing large transformations, and, on the other hand, the detail of the solution strategy. This document progressively constructs the frictional-contact problem implemented in Code_Aster under the name of “continuous method for frictional contact”, which can be activated via the keyword FORMULATION='CONTINUE' in DEFI_CONTACT, initially proposed by Ben-Dhia and Zarroug []. An abundant bibliography will be found at the end of the document. In particular, the four theses defended on the subject by M.Zarroug [], C.Zammali [], M.Torkhani [] and D.Kudawoo []. 2 Kinematics 2.1 Kinematics of the solids One considers two deformable solids {B}^{i}(i=1,2) in frictional 