arity comes from the fact that one does not know a priori which will be the pairs of points in contact. This lack of knowledge also entails that of the local frame (normal and tangent plane) at each potential contact point. This non-linearity is particularly pronounced for problems with warped contact surfaces (that is to say non-planar) and/or potentially subjected to large transformations (large relative slips) . In practice, a single correction is often sufficient if the contact surfaces undergo little geometric variation (small displacements). It is important to understand what geometric updating implies: it is, at each iteration, an entirely new frictional-contact problem which one solves, and in which one must start again from the determination of the pairs of paired points. This is why the geometry loop is the outermost. The pairing non-linearity also entails lack of knowledge of the local frame. Now the current local frame (the normal n and the two tangents {t}_{1} and {t}_{2} in 3D, in the deformed configuration) is present several times in the writing of the Signorini–Coulomb equations. If one takes the case of the normal, this means that it is written, in the course of the displacement {u}^{1} : n( ar{x})=n( ar{p}+{u}^{1}( ar{p})) The normal is therefore non-linear in displacement. This non-linearity may be differentiated in the equations, or one may make the assumption that the normal remains constant in the course of the loop on the geometry: n( ar{x})\approx n( ar{p}+{u}_{j}^{1}( ar{p})) In our case, one makes the assumption that the normal (and the tangents) remains constant during the loop on the geometry. The number of iterations of the loop is determined automatically by the code but one may fix it by NB_ITER_GEOM. It should be noted that there is always at least one pairing at the beginning of the time step, even if NB_ITER_GEOM=0. 5.3.2 Loop on the friction threshold The threshold non-linearity (Coulomb friction) comes from the fact that the friction threshold depends on the normal density of the contact force {\lambda}_{n} , which is itself an unknown of the problem. The fixed-point loop on the friction threshold {\lambda}_{s} amounts to treating the frictional-contact problem with a fixed friction threshold, which amounts at each iteration to treating a Tresca problem . This strategy is very common for solving Coulomb friction, and is justified theoretically: by fixing the friction threshold, one may derive an associated convex potential for the Coulomb law, which makes it possible to relate the displacement unknowns to their associated dual variables, the friction forces. 5.3.3 Loop on the contact statuses The last non-linearity concerns the lack of knowledge of the effective contact state. We have adopted a method derived from the active-constraint method implemented in Code_Aster for discrete contact (see R5.03.50). By default, the initial status of the contact nodes is non-contacting in Code_Aster . Furthermore {\lambda}_{s} is initialised to zero (unless one uses SEUIL_INIT). This amounts to beginning by solving a problem without contact at the first iteration, then with contact but without friction, and finally to activating friction with a fixed friction threshold {\lambda}_{s} (Tresca problem). One may nevertheless start from an initially contacting state by using the option CONTACT_INIT. In this case, it is possible to solve hypostatic problems with the continuous formulation. This is an important advantage of the continuous formulation of contact with respect to the discrete formulations, which do not support a mechanical problem which is “held” only by the contact. Moreover, the “slide” method makes it possible to simulate bilateral contact by assuming that the nodes which come into contact will remain so throughout the transient (functionality which can be activated by the keyword GLISSIERE). 5.4 Linearisations of the terms The non-linear algebraic system () will be solved by linearisation of 