ollowing points in common: - The writing of the PVW for the equilibrium of the structure, using a weak formulation of the equilibrium equation; - A vector field of unknowns: that of the primal variables representing either the displacements or the velocities (in dynamics, in the case of the theta-scheme); - The geometry is represented by a regular triangulation (classical polynomial finite elements); - An unknown scalar field representing the Lagrange multiplier of contact \lambda ; - An unknown vector field representing the Lagrange semi-multiplier of friction \Lambda ; - The Signorini contact model is applied in a weak manner, with an augmented Lagrangian formulation; - The Coulomb friction model is applied in a weak manner, with an augmented Lagrangian formulation; - Discontinuous sign fields, for contact and for friction, have been introduced into the formulation. In this chapter, we shall introduce all the ingredients necessary for the construction of the discretised model, then, in the following chapter, we shall see the manner of solving the problem. Important: henceforth, we shall denote by \lambda and no longer by {\lambda}_{n} the contact pressure in order to lighten the notations. The friction pressure is always denoted \Lambda . 6.1 Theoretical elements 6.1.1 Tessellation of the domain The domains {\Omega}^{i} are approximated by polygonal domains {\Omega}_{h}^{i} . With each domain {\Omega}^{i} is associated a family of triangulations {T}_{{h}^{i}} with the parameter {h}^{i} which is the mesh size of the domain {\Omega}^{i} . Denoting by {K}_{j}^{i} the {\mathit{NE}}^{i} elements of each triangulation, we may write: {\Omega}_{h}^{i}=\underset{j=1}{\overset{{\mathit{NE}}^{i}}{\cup}}{K}_{j}^{i} For the discretisation of the contact boundary {\Gamma}_{c}^{i} , we are faced with a first important assumption. Indeed, this boundary may be discretised in two manners: - One takes the geometric trace of the mesh of the underlying domain {\Omega}^{i} ; - One creates a new independent mesh, with its own discretisation size. In practice, the mesh of the boundary is almost always built on the first assumption, because otherwise this would imply meshing work difficult to carry out, particularly in 3D. One will therefore always use the geometric trace of the mesh of the domain {\Omega}^{i} as the mesh for the contact boundary. The trace of {T}_{{h}^{i}} forms a mesh {T}_{{h}^{i}}^{\Gamma} of {\Gamma}_{c}^{i} . The discretised boundary {\Gamma}_{c,h}^{i} is composed of {\mathit{NE}}_{c}^{i} elements {k}_{j}^{i} : {\Gamma}_{c,h}^{i}=\underset{j=1}{\overset{{\mathit{NE}}_{c}^{i}}{\cup}}{k}_{j}^{i} 6.1.2 Discrete approximation spaces The discrete version of the space {H}^{1}({\Omega}^{i}) , on the domain {\Omega}^{i} , which one will denote {H}_{h}^{i,q} , is such that: {H}_{h}^{i,q}=\left\lbrace {u}^{i}\in {(C({ ar{\Omega}}^{i}))}^{d};{u}_{\mid {K}_{j}^{i}}^{i}\in {({P}_{q}({K}_{j}^{i}))}^{d}	ext{pour}1\le j\le {\mathit{NE}}^{i}ightbrace with {\mathit{NE}}^{i} the number of elements coming from the triangulation (), d the dimension of the problem, C({ ar{\Omega}}^{i}) the space of the continuous functions on the closure of {\Omega}^{i} and {P}_{q}({K}_{j}^{i}) the space of polynomials of degree less than or equal to q on the domain. One has denoted by {\mathit{CA}}^{i} the space containing the kinematically admissible functions. The discrete version of this space, denoted {\mathit{CA}}_{h}^{i,q} , is constructed as (): {\mathit{CA}}_{h}^{i,q}=\left\lbrace {u}^{i}\in {(C({ ar{\Omega}}^{i}))}^{d};{u}_{\mid {K}_{j}^{i}}^{i}\in {({P}_{q}({K}_{j}^{i}))}^{d}	ext{pour}1\le j\le {\mathit{NE}}^{i}	ext{et}{u}_{\mid {\Gamma}_{u}^{i}}^{i}=0ightbrace We shall write the discrete version of the space of the traces of functions on the boundary: {\mathit{CA}}_{h}^{\gamma ,i}=\left\lbrace \mathit{trace}({u}^{i})	ext{sur}{\Gamma}_{c}^{i};{u}^{i}\in {\mathit{CA}}_{h}^{i,q}ightbrace Henceforth, one will forget the notation q for indicating the order of the polynomials. 6.1.3 The Inf-Sup condition or LBB condition The mixed character of the formulation imposes a dependence between the finite-element spaces of the contact and friction multipliers and the finite-element space of the displacements. This choice falls under the compatibility condition (or Inf-Sup condition or LBB condition of Ladyzenskaya–Babuška–Brezzi). A mechanical problem generally reduces to solving a variational equation of the type: 	ext{Trouver}u\in U	ext{tel que} orall {u}^{	ext{ }}\in U \lbrace  egin{array}{cc}a(u,{u}^{	ext{ }})=\langle l,{u}^{	ext{ }}angle & 	ext{dans}U\ u=g& 	ext{sur}\partial U\end{array} where U is a Hilbert space. It is often more convenient to rewrite this problem in a so-called mixed form, with two unknowns u\in U and \lambda \in M : 	ext{Trouver}u\in U	ext{et}\lambda \in M	ext{tel que} orall {u}^{	ext{ }}\in U	ext{et} orall {\lambda}^{	ext{ }}\in M \lbrace  egin{array}{cc}a(u,{u}^{	ext{ }})+b({u}^{	ext{ }},\lambda )=\langle l,{u}^{	ext{ }}angle & 	ext{dans}U\ b({u}^{	ext{ }},\lambda )=\langle g,{\lambda}^{	ext{ }}angle & 	ext{sur}\partial U\end{array} where M is also a Hilbert space, dual of U through the bilinear form b . The problem is that the formulation given by () is not equivalent to the formulation given by (). Thus, in this very general framework, the equivalence of the two formulations, as well as a necessary and sufficient condition of existence and uniqueness (in addition, of course, to the classical assumptions on the ellipticity and the coercivity of the bilinear form a ) of the solution of (), is given by the so-called condition of Ladyzenskaya , Babuška and Brezzi (LBB): \exists \alpha \in ℝ>0,\underset{{\lambda}^{	ext{ }}\in M}{\inf}\underset{{u}^{	ext{ }}\in U}{\sup} rac{\mid b({u}^{	ext{ }},{\lambda}^{	ext{ }})\mid }{{\parallel {\lambda}^{	ext{ }}\parallel }_{M}.{\parallel {u}^{	ext{ }}\parallel }_{U}}\ge \alpha >0 This condition indicates how one must choose the space M , so that the formul