e_Aster , this choice means that there is a risk of having convergence difficulties as soon as the contact interfaces are warped, the relative slip is significant, or the difference in discretisation is important between the two surfaces. 6.1.4 Sign fields The choice of the approximation spaces (of polynomial finite-element type) for the displacement field, the contact Lagrange-multiplier field and the field of the friction semi-multipliers is not sufficient to construct the discrete problem. There remains the case of the sign fields (for contact, friction, the velocity field, etc.) which are naturally in infinite spaces. One uses a collocation method to approximate these sign fields. One therefore chooses a finite collection of points of the discretised contact surface {\Gamma}_{c,h}^{i} : {({p}_{j}^{i})}_{1\le j\le {n}_{c}^{i}}\in {\Gamma}_{c,h}^{i} A sign field S will therefore be approximated by {S}_{h} , which is the following discrete sum: {S}_{h}=\sum_{j=1}^{{n}_{c}}{\omega}_{j}S({p}_{j}) Warning! The number of collocation points {n}_{c}^{i} is not necessarily the same as the number of points coming from the finite-element discretisation of the contact/friction multipliers (which rests, one recalls, on the geometric trace of the discretised volume). 6.1.5 Numerical integration To evaluate the integrals numerically, it is appropriate to choose a suitable numerical quadrature scheme. One bases oneself for this on the integration of the discontinuous terms (sign fields) which “guide” the best scheme to adopt. The question therefore shifts towards the choice of the optimal collocation points in order to obtain accurate contact forces. Satisfaction of the Inf-Sup condition has been addressed in the framework of the choice of the approximation functions of the contact and friction multiplier fields. Still with a view to satisfying this condition, it has been said that it was strongly linked to the accuracy of the numerical integration which one is capable of obtaining. The phenomenon is understood when one draws the analogy with the methods of numerical under-integration sometimes employed to solve problems of numerical locking due to too great a richness of the approximation, by then “lowering” the degree of interpolation of the fields by under-integrating. The reasoning may therefore easily be reversed: if the numerical integration “distorts” (by enriching or by impoverishing) the polynomial interpolation of the multiplier fields, one risks leaving the stability domain of the LBB condition. | | | --- | | Figure 7 : Numerical integration and compatibility | This is the case, notably in the event of incompatibility. Let us imagine that the elementary integration area on the slave surface covers several elements of the master surface. Since the test functions have as non-zero support a finite element, the fact of integrating numerically, by a Gauss quadrature, a non-polynomial function, but identically zero on part of the integration area, is clearly not exact, because the quadrature is quite inappropriate (see ). Yet this is what one does when one tries to integrate a functional field of a master element on a much more extended slave element (non-compatible). By reversing the choice of the surfaces, the result is clearly better ( on the right), but still without a guarantee of having a satisfactory integration. In what follows, we shall describe the method adopted for Code_Aster , based on a subdivision of the element which serves only for the integration . This method, which consists in refining the integration surface, limits the numerical integration error, and above all has the advantage of being extremely simple to implement. Several avenues have been explored in an attempt to minimise these problems of incompatibilities between the surfaces. The one which has been retained in Code_Aster is to propose different numerical integration schemes, usable according to the needs. There are several types of schemes: - Numerical integration at the nodes; - Numerical integration at the Gauss points; - Numerical integration by the Simpson method with 3, 5 or 9 integration points; - Integration by the Newton–Cotes method with 4, 5 or 10 integration points; Simpson integration makes it possible to improve the results in the event of mesh incompatibility between the two contact surfaces, thanks to the subdivision of the integration elements which we have already mentioned above. Newton–Cotes integration makes it possible to integrate exactly polynomials of high order (greater than three). It proves very useful when the interpolation functions used on one of the two contact surfaces are of order greater than one ( \mathit{P1}-\mathit{P2} , \mathit{P2}-\mathit{P2} …). Moreover, the Newton–Cotes quadratures also allow (like those of Simpson) a subdivision of the integration element, and therefore apply in the event of mesh incompatibility. However, one will take care not to believe that a systematic use of a richer quadrature (Simpson, Newton–Cotes) is always a good choice. First because, in most cases, the quadrature at the nodes proves in practice sufficient ( \mathit{P1}-\mathit{P1} with sufficiently fine meshes). Next because the richer a quadrature is (in number of integration points), the greater the volume of the analysis, and one will very quickly realise that, for example, the use of the Newton–Cotes quadrature quickly becomes prohibitive. There is therefore no systematic rule a priori as to the choice of a quadrature, apart from the few indications which we have given: one must take into account the specificity of the model and of the mechanical problem studied, and make a compromise between the accuracy desired for the solution and the analysis time required. 6.2 Contact elements After having discretised the mixed variational formulation and after having given the numerical integration schemes, all that remains for us is to set up finite elements for frictional contact. The idea of using finite elements is not immediate. For example, in the discrete methods of frictional contact R5.03.50, the kinematic contact conditions are imposed without using finite elements and by intervening directly on the algebraic system to be solved. Such a strategy has the drawback of making the programming and the architecture completely dependent on the formulation, which prevents contact from benefiting from improvements (architecture, performance) which would be made in the rest of the finite-element code. Conversely, we shall see that developing frictional-contact finite elements implies leaving the standard scheme of finite elements and that it is a sub-optimal strategy from the point of view of performance. To determine the stiffness matrix (and the right-hand side) of the discretised system, one must compute the elementary terms of the finite elements before carrying out their assembly. This elementary (classical) level is not sufficient for the treatment of our frictional-contact problem. Several incompatibilities come to make the problem non-standard in the sense of finite elements. 6.2.1 Geometric incompatibility One already notes a geometric incompatibility () between the discretisations of the two facing contact surfaces, a slave element being able for example to overlap several master elements. | | | --- | | Figure 8 : Geometric incompatibility | 6.2.2 Incompatibility of the statuses The discontinuous character of the contact/friction states entails an incompatibility of the statuses (). Within one and the same contact element, several frictional-contact states may coexist; | | | --- | | Figure 9 : Incompatibility of the statuses | This incompatibility is easily settled because one makes the assumption that the sign field (thus indicating the contact and friction status) is discretised by collocation and that the collocation points have been chosen to be those of the numerical integration. Which means that the contact status is not carried by a contact element, but by an integration point of a contact element. 6.2.3 Construction of the contact elements It remains to construct these contact elements. We shall illustrate the principle on a 2D case. | | | --- | | Figure 10 : 2D example of pairing between two incompatible surfaces | On the figure , we represent a 2D example of geometrically incompatible surfaces. The crosses represent the integration points and here are the pairings and the contact statuses: | Integration point | Slave element | Paired master element | Status | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | a | {e}_{1}-{e}_{2} | {m}_{2}-{m}_{3} | Sticking | | b | {e}_{1}-{e}_{2} | {m}_{2}-{m}_{3} | Sliding | | c | {e}_{2}-{e}_{3} | {m}_{3}-{m}_{4} | Sliding | | d | {e}_{2}-{e}_{3} | {m}_{4}-{m}_{5} | No contact | | e | {e}_{3}-{e}_{4} | {m}_{4}-{m}_{5} | No contact | | f | {e}_{3}-{e}_{4} | none | No contact | Table 6.2.3-1 There are therefore three contact elements which one represents on the figure . Each element is geometrically defined by the slave and master nodes. The status is represented by the collocation point which acts as an integration point for each contact element. | | | --- | | Figure 11 : Creation of the three contact elements | We have placed the collocation points (a,b,c,d) as Gauss points, but their location will of course depend on the integration scheme. Each contact element therefore represents an elementary matrix or vector on the degrees of freedom carried by the slave nodes and by the master nodes. In 3D, the largest surface mesh cell is the QUAD9 (nine-node quadrilateral), which makes 2	imes 9	imes 3=54 degrees of freedom for the displacements. In the case of contact with friction in 3D, one adds on the slave surface three additional degrees of freedom per node: the Lagrange multiplier of the contact multiplier and the two Lagrange multipliers of the friction semi-multiplier, which gives 9	imes 3=27 additional degrees of freedom. Therefore, in 3D, for the contact element with friction, the maximum size of the elementary matrix is 81 by 81 degrees of freedom. In practice the method is the following: - Creation of the contact element on the master and slave nodes; - For each contact element, loop over the collocation points; - For each collocation point, computation of the elementary matrix (or vector). It is because of the last point that the creation of the finite element is sub-optimal. Indeed, one calls the computation of the elementary matrix for each collocation point. Yet, for the classical finite elements, the loop over the integration points is inside the elementary computation. We therefore see that the use of a collocation scheme makes it possible for different contact states to coexist within the same element. However, there remains a problem of compatibility between the FE approximation of the shape functions and the integration scheme. Let us take the example of a segment (figure ). | | | --- | | Figure 12 : FE/collocation incompatibility on a segment | On this segment, the displacement is approximated by a linear shape function and it equals 0 at the left-hand node and it is greater than zero at the right-hand node. From the point of view of contact, this means that a point situated in the middle of the segment will necessarily separate. Yet the integration scheme by collocation does not necessarily indicate this state. There is a decoupling between the polynomial approximation of the displacements and the discretisation by collocation of the sign fields. Which means that the collocation method is perfect for zones with contact states constant on the element but that it is much less relevant if the contact state varies. Such a situation generates oscillations in the transition zones. These oscillations cannot be reduced by using more collocation points but by refining the mesh in this zone. Notes: - The frictional-contact elements having no geometric meaning, they are not conforming, with no impact on the quality of the result: they are infinitely flattened when there is contact, they may be very distorted and have “strange” connectivities in the case of the joining between surfaces of different degrees; - The fact of using the integration scheme at the nodes does not make the contact elements more efficient from the point of view of their use, since the principle described above is generic for all the integration schemes; - There is no procedure dedicated to perfectly compatible meshes. Two meshes chosen to be compatible will not give faster results than two incompatible meshes; - The fact of having to construct late finite elements results in a re-factorisation of the global matrix at each Newton iteration, since the topology of the matrix will be modified. 6.3 Preparation of the discretisation 6.3.1 Discretisation in time The (pseudo)-temporal discretisation will be indicated by the index k . For example, the displacement {u}_{k} at the current time step is written as the sum of the cumulated displacement {u}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}} since the beginning of the Newton process of the current time step and of the displacement increment of the current Newton iteration \Delta u which one is seeking: {u}_{k}={u}_{k-1}+{u}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}}+\Delta u This iterative writing is also valid for the other nodal unknowns (field of the Lagrange multipliers): {\lambda}_{k}={\lambda}_{k-1}+{\lambda}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}}+\Delta \lambda And: {\Lambda}_{k}={\Lambda}_{k-1}+{\Lambda}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}}+\Delta \Lambda In Code_Aster , one has chosen to work in total quantity (and not incremental) for the Lagrange multipliers; they are therefore initialised to zero at each beginning of a time step and one therefore has: {\lambda}_{k-1}=0	o {\lambda}_{k}={\lambda}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}}+\Delta \lambda And: {\Lambda}_{k-1}=0	o {\Lambda}_{k}={\Lambda}_{k}^{\mathit{cum}}+\Delta \Lambda One will use a notation which separates, at the current time step k , the known quantities {\hat{u}}_{k} and the unknown quantities \Delta u : {u}_{k}={\hat{u}}