ed variational formulation, one wrote that there was coupling between the displacements and the friction semi-multipliers (matrices \left[{A}_{u}^{\Lambda}ight] and \left[{A}_{\Lambda}^{u}ight] ), which means that the non-slip condition will give {u}_{x}=0 , whereas this latter condition is already imposed in the global system (the matrix [B] of the boundary conditions). One therefore imposes twice the relation {u}_{x}=0 . This situation leads to a zero-pivot error during the inversion of the tangent stiffness matrix. | | | --- | | Figure 13 : Plate in frictional contact on a rigid plane | 7.1.2 Choice of the approximation space The solution retained is always to give precedence to the boundary conditions over the sticking-sliding conditions. For this, it suffices to decouple the friction multipliers and the displacements for the component of the node concerned by the redundancy (to take up our example, one will decouple {u}_{x} and {\Lambda}_{x} at the nodes A and C ). This amounts to modifying the approximation space of the friction-multiplier field by using a function which equals zero for the appropriate degree of freedom. This operation is illustrated on the figure in the 2D case where the redundancy condition is at the point A . One takes {H}_{h} as the approximation space of the friction semi-multiplier {\mu}_{h} : {H}_{h}=\left\lbrace {\mu}_{h}\in {	ext{CA}}_{h};{\mu}_{h}(A)=0ightbrace This space is conforming from the point of view of the LBB condition. In practice, one simply puts zeros at the appropriate locations of the matrices \left[{A}_{e}^{\Lambda}ight] and \left[{A}_{\Lambda}^{e}ight] . The option SANS_GROUP_NO_FR/SANS_NOEUD_FR of Code_Aster makes it possible to remove the contribution to the friction matrix of a set of nodes, while keeping the boundary conditions and the unilateral contact conditions at these nodes. | | | --- | | Figure 14 : Modification of the approximation spaces for the friction/boundary-condition redundancy | In 2D, one and only one slip direction exists. The removal of the sticking condition for the problematic points is therefore sufficient to eliminate any redundancy. It is done by decoupling the friction semi-multiplier associated with a constraint from the displacement unknowns. In 3D on the other hand, the sliding or the sticking occurring in the tangent plane, one may wish to favour a particular slip direction (for example perpendicular to a restraint). For this, the user supplies under the keyword DIRE_EXCL_FROT the direction to be excluded. This direction allows the construction of a local frame which facilitates the decoupling of a single friction semi-multiplier. If no direction is supplied then this amounts to not treating friction on the nodes concerned (decoupling of the two multipliers). One acts only on the slave-displacement/friction-multiplier coupling because the risk of redundancy will appear only in the case where the boundary conditions are imposed on the slave surface, owing to the fact that the Lagrange multipliers (contact and friction) are discretised on the slave surface. 8 Cycling during the Newton process 8.1 The problem The use of a Newton algorithm on non-differentiable quantities sometimes brings about phenomena of numerical cycling. In a rigorous manner, one may use the Newton algorithm on quasi-differentiable quantities (in the classical sense); in this instance, one will speak here of B-differentiability [], that is to say that the points of non-differentiability belong to a space of zero measure. In practice, with the numerical approximations introduced by the discretisation and the passage to a floating-point algebra, it is “highly unlikely” that one will encounter these points of non-differentiability, but cycling problems are not excluded, particularly in the case where contact/friction couples with other non-linearities. A cycling corresponds to the crossing of a point of non-differentiability during the Newton process (that is to say from one Newton iteration to the next). In [], one distinguishes three kinds of them (see figure ): - Cycling on the contact status: a point of the mesh passes alternately from a case of contact to a case of non-contact; - Cycling on the friction status: a point of the mesh passes alternately from a case of sliding to a case of sticking; - Cycling on the slip threshold: a point of the mesh passes alternately from a case of forward slip to a case of backward slip; From a more heuristic point of view, one also shows that cycles of order 2 often cause non-convergence. Figure 15: The different cyclings of contact/friction 8.2 Detection The three kinds of cycle are detected automatically. 8.3 Treatment 8.3.1 Automatic treatment of cycling There is the possibility of automatically treating cycles of contact/separation, sticking/sliding and forward-slip/backward-slip type (keyword ADAPTATION in DEFI_CONTACT). For the keyword ADAPTATION='CYCLAGE', one constructs a basis of the generalised Jacobian over two successive iterations: one uses this basis to modify the current contact and friction matrices. This manipulation serves for contact/separation cycling. The cases of forward-slip/backward-slip cycling are treated so that the point is taken as sticking as soon as the norm of its slip is almost zero. In the case where the user requests a cycling-type treatment, one carries out an automatic switch of the non-converged points into penalisation with an automatic control of the penalisation coefficient (if the initial algorithm was the augmented Lagrangian). The variational formulation in ALGO_CONT='STANDARD' is thus modified. Indeed, in the expression of the initial variational formulation. Denoting by {G}_{	ext{c},k}^{\mathit{STD}} the weak contact force linked to ALGO_CONT='STANDARD' for the set of points which pose no problems, {G}_{	ext{c},k}^{\mathit{PENA}} for the points which have switched, one has: \lbrace  egin{array}{cc}\sum_{i=1}^{2}\left[{G}_{int,k}^{i}-{G}_{	ext{ext},k}^{i}ight]-{G}_{	ext{c},k}^{\mathit{STD}}+{G}_{	ext{c},k}-{G}_{	ext{f},k}=0& \stackrel{	ext{Discrétisation}}{	o }\left\lbrace {L}_{u,k}ightbrace =0\ {	ilde{G}}_{	ext{c},k}^{\mathit{STD}}=0& \stackrel{	ext{Discrétisation}}{	o }\left\lbrace {L}_{c,k}^{\mathit{STD}}ightbrace =0\ {	ilde{G}}_{	ext{c},k}^{\mathit{PENA}}=0& \stackrel{	ext{Discrétisation}}{	o }\left\lbrace {L}_{c,k}^{\mathit{PENA}}ightbrace =0\ {	ilde{G}}_{	ext{f},k}=0& \stackrel{	ext{Discrétisation}}{	o }\left\lbrace {L}_{f,k}^{\mathit{PENA}}ightbrace =0\end{array} Finally, in cycling mode, in order to reinforce robustness, one does not wait for the Newton convergence which ensures that the pressures and the gaps are indeed in the cone of the admissible values {\mathrm{ℝ}}^{-} . For the keyword ADAPTATION='ADAPT_COEF', there are two cases: - In the augmented Lagrangian (ALGO_CONT='STANDARD' and ALGO_FROT='STANDARD'), one will modify the augmentation coefficient in order to “break” the cycle between the sticking status and the sliding status at the same integration point. This modification of the coefficient is safe because it does not change the results, but only the rate of convergence. This mode serves to manage sticking/sliding cycling. For this, the following algorithm is employed: - For a given augmentation coefficient, one computes the sticking/sliding status at the previous cycle and at the current cycle of the point detected as “cyclic”: is one close to the point of discontinuity (tolerance fixed at 5%)? - If yes, one iteratively modifies the augmentation coefficient in order to leave this critical zone while not approaching it from the other side; - If no, one changes nothing; - One propagates the adapted coefficient onto the other points according to a specific algorithm (see § 8.3.2 ); - In the stabilised Lagrangian (ALGO_CONT='PENALISATION'), one will modify the regularisation coefficients in penalisation in order to detect automatically the cases of singular matrices and to treat automatically the maximum penetration computed in the course of the iterations: see § 8.3.3 . Notes on sticking/sliding cycling: - Owing to the scaling of the projection of the friction Lagrangian (see § 3.2.4 ), the tolerance given (here 5% on either side of the point of discontinuity) does not depend on the units of the problem; - The cycling-detection procedure has exhibited a point which varies between the sliding status and the sticking status. The strategy retained here consists in considering the cases where one of the statuses is close to the point of discontinuity. If this is not the case (cycling between two zones far from the discontinuity or between two zones close to the discontinuity), the algorithm does nothing. - The algorithm for adaptation of the augmentation coefficient is a simple loop which, in a first stage, increases the augmentation coefficient by multiplying it by two, and if one fails, starts off in the other direction by dividing the coefficient by two. The coefficient is chosen in the interval [{10}^{-8}{,10}^{+8}] . 8.3.2 Algorithm for propagation of the augmentation coefficient, sticking/sliding case The previous algorithm is effective only if one sets up a procedure which will propagate (or not) the optimal coefficient found on a cyclic point. Indeed, in practice, such an optimal coefficient has a strong chance of also being so for other points. This method thus makes it possible to anticipate the risks of cycling of the points which are not yet in contact and makes the algorithm much more efficient. For this, during the coefficient-adaptation procedure (algorithm of § 8.3.1 ), one stores the maximum and minimum values found over the whole contact zone and one applies the following algorithm: - Loop over the contact points - With the maximum coefficient on the contact zone, one evaluates on all the points whether or not one is close to the point of discontinuity; one then has four situations: 1. This new coefficient does not modify the situation of the point on the friction graph with respect to the old coefficient→ one propagates the new coefficient; 2. This coefficient modifies the situation of the point on the friction graph but the old situation was far from the discontinuity→ one does not propagate the new coefficient; 3. This coefficient modifies the situation of the point on the friction graph, the old situation was close to the discontinuity and the new position moves away from the discontinuity→ one propagates the new coefficient; 4. This coefficient modifies the situation of the point on the friction graph, the old situation was close to the discontinuity and the new position changes status (crossing of the discontinuity)→ one does not propagate the new coefficient; - With the minimum coefficient on the contact zone, one evaluates on all the points whether or not one is close to the point of discontinuity; one evaluates the same situations as in the previous case; - End of the loop over the contact points 8.3.3 Algorithm for initialisation of the penalisation coefficient We shall detail the use of the keyword ADAPTATION='ADAPT_COEF' in the case of penalised contact. In certain analyses with the penalisation method on contact, the matrix is singular from the first iteration of the first time step. This singularity is due to a poor choice of the initial penalisation coefficients. To detect this situation numerically and to treat it, one relies on the assembled matrix before the first factorisation. Indeed, one notes that this situation appears if the largest value on the diagonal (apart from the Lagrange terms due to the boundary conditions) is very far from the smallest value on this same diagonal. The profile of the assembled matrix looking at the diagonal is: K=\left( egin{array}{ccc}	ilde{K}& \mathrm{..}& {B}_{\mathit{lagr}}\ \mathrm{..}& {K}_{\mathit{pena}}& \mathrm{..}\ {B}_{\mathit{lagr}}& \mathrm{..}& \mathrm{..}\end{array}ight) With 	ilde{K} the global stiffness matrix reduced to the d