em in three manners: - Use a more robust algorithm such as the partial Newton or the incomplete Newton (see keywords ALGO_RESO_ ). In this latter case, another type of cycling may appear: it is the flip-flop, a cycling of contact type but of order 15; - Modify the augmentation parameters (keywords COEF_FROT and COEF_CONT). Here, there are no pre-established rules on the direction of the modification (decrease or increase the value) and on its amplitude. Nevertheless, it should be observed that the augmentation parameter {ho}_{t} is very sensitive. - Modify one's model. Either by refining/coarsening the mesh (warning! refinement may amplify the cycling phenomena), or by degrading the model (passage to an elastic material, or even a rigid one). 9 Functionalities and verification This documentation describes the modelling of frictional contact by the so-called “continuous” method; for its use, one may refer to the usage documentation of the command DEFI_CONTACT and to the modelling manual U2.04.04. There exist some sixty test cases validating the different functionalities linked to frictional contact, among which: - The Hertz contact test case: see ssnv104; - The frictional-pad test case: see ssnv128; - The test cases of the NAFEMS benchmark on contact/friction: see ssna122, ssnp154, ssnp155, ssnp156 and ssnp157; - The test case “shallow ironing”, see ssnp153; 10 Bibliography Alart P., Curnier A. A mixed formulation for frictional contact problems prone to Newton like solution methods. Comput. Methods. Appl. Mech. Engrg., vol.9, pp.353-375, 1991. Hallquist J.O., Goudreau G.L., Benson D.J. Sliding interfaces with contact-impact in large-scale langrangian computations. Comput. Methods. Appl. Mech. Engrg., vol.51, pp.107-137, 1985. Laursen T.A., Simo J.C. A continuum-based finite element formulation for the implicit solution of multibodies, large deformation frictional contact problems. Int. J. Num. Meth. Engng., vol.36, pp.3451-3485, 1993. Ben-Dhia H., Zarroug M. Hybrid