r5.04.01 Non-local modelling with gradients of internal variables GRAD_VARI r5.04.01 Non-local modelling with gradients of internal variables GRAD_VARI Summary The non-local modelling with a gradient of internal variables entitled GRAD_VARI in Code_Aster is presented here. This modelling stems from the work of E. Lorentz [Bib 1]. The algorithm making it possible to solve the equilibrium and regularisation equations has however been modified relative to the initial version of the model. The non-local modellings of type GRAD_VARI are available in 3D (3D_GRAD_VARI), axisymmetric (AXIS_GRAD_VARI) and plane strain (D_PLAN_GRAD_VARI). Unlike the former version, the use of GRAD_VARI is relatively simple, since it is sufficient to specify the modelling X_GRAD_VARI in AFFE_MODELE, to specify a characteristic length under the keyword NON_LOCAL in DEFI_MATERIAU, and to check that the constitutive law one wishes to use is indeed available in a non-local version. The formulation and the numerical treatment of this model are presented. 1 Reminder of the theory of gradient models The gradient models presented here were developed by E. Lorentz [bib1] in order to be able to describe the behaviour of materials subjected to strong gradients of the mechanical fields which appear in the damaged zones or else in the neighbourhood of geometric singularities. Indeed, in the case of strong gradients, the behaviour of a material point is no longer independent of its surroundings but depends on the behaviour of its neighbourhood, whence the introduction of gradients into the models. From a numerical point of view, the analysis of a structure with a classical local damage law shows that the damaged zone always localises on a single layer of finite elements and therefore that the response of the structure depends on the mesh adopted: gradient models remedy this problem. In what follows, a brief reminder of this theory is given. 1.1 Construction of gradient models This formulation is restricted to generalised standard materials [Bib 2]. Models with a gradient of internal variables consist in introducing the gradient of internal variables into a generalised standard formulation [Bib 2]. Let a be an internal variable and A its associated thermodynamic force, and let \Delta (\dot{a}) be the dissipation potential. If one considers that \Delta also depends on the gradient of \dot{a} , \Delta =\Delta (\dot{a},
