nd \lambda , causing spurious oscillations. 3 Available modellings These various elements are used in three types of modelling: | 2D analysis in plane strain: | D_PLAN_GRAD_VARI | | --- | --- | | 2D axisymmetric analysis: | AXIS_GRAD_VARI | | 3D analysis: | 3D_GRAD_VARI | The plane-stress mode is not yet available. 4 Constitutive laws available with the GRAD_VARI modellings The constitutive laws available in their non-local version with a gradient of internal variables are the following: | ENDO_SCALAIRE | Regularised isotropic brittle elastic constitutive law (cf. R5.03.25) | | --- | --- | | ENDO_ISOT_BETON | Isotropic and unilateral brittle elastic constitutive law for concrete (cf. R5.07.04) | | VMIS_ISOT_NL | Elastoplastic constitutive law with non-linear isotropic hardening (cf. R5.03.33) | 5 Advice / procedure for the implementation of a new constitutive law with gradients of internal variables As was seen in section 1.4, the implementation of a new constitutive law with a gradient of internal variables, starting from a local law, is relatively simple, provided that the local law respects the framework of generalised standard materials and, more particularly, that the evolution criterion of the internal variables involves a threshold function convex with respect to the thermodynamic forces associated with the internal variables (the thermodynamic force associated with the variable \alpha is defined as follows: {F}^{a}=- rac{\partial W}{\partial a} where W is the free energy of the structure) and that a normal flow law is adopted in order to compute the evolution of the internal variables. The table below summarises the steps for passing from a local constitutive law to a non-local constitutive law: | Type | Local model | Non-local model | | --- | --- | --- | | Thermodynamic force | {F}^{{a}_{	ext{local}}} | {F}^{{a}_{	ext{non}	ext{local}}}={F}^{{a}_{	ext{local}}}+\lambda +r\alpha -	ext{ra} | | Threshold function / criterion | f\left[{F}^{{a}_{	ext{local}}}ight