r6.02.01 About Gauss-type decomposition methods r6.02.01 About Gauss-type decomposition methods Summary: This document presents a few aspects related to the GAUSS decomposition method. After a rapid presentation, we recall the main advantages and disadvantages related to this direct method. Then, we detail the implementation of the LDLT algorithm implemented in Code_Aster . GAUSS (1777 - 1855) is at the origin of all the direct methods for the numerical solution of linear systems. Let him be thanked here. > Contents 1 Generalities on Gauss-type methods 1.1 Presentation of the method. Starting from the observation that it is easy to solve the system \mathrm{A.x}=b when A is a lower or upper triangular matrix, one seeks to decompose the initial full matrix by a factorisation into triangular matrices. The basic principle is to seek a regular matrix P , called permutation matrix, such that the product \mathrm{P.A} is triangular, then to solve \mathrm{P.A.x}=\mathrm{P.b} Note: In practice, P is determined by products of elementary permutation matrices P={P}^{(k)}\mathrm{...}{P}^{(1)}. The matrices {P}^{(i)} depend on the variant chosen, but one never computes the matrix P explicitly but only \mathrm{P.A} and \mathrm{P.b} The matrix A having been factorised in the general form L.U (L lower triangular matrix, U upper triangular matrix), we are led to solve the two linear systems: \lbrace  egin{array}{c}L.	ext{y = b}\ U.	ext{x = y}\end{array} Note: In the method called GAUSS elimination , one carries out simultaneously the factorisation of A and the solution of \mathrm{L.y}=b The following algorithm carries out the GAUSS elimination and the solution of \mathrm{L.y}=b at step (p+1) we have | {a}_{ij}^{(p+1)}={a}_{ij}^{(p)}-{a}_{ij}^{(p)}.{({a}_{	ext{pp}}^{(p)})}^{-1}.{a}_{	ext{pj}}^{(p)} | for p+1\le i\le n p+1\le j\le n+1 | | --- | --- | | {a}_{ij}^{(p+1)}={a}_{ij}^{(p)} | for 1\le i\le p 1\le j\le n+1 | | {a}_{ij}^{(p+1)}=0. | for p+1\le i\le n 1\le j\le p | Note: In this writing of the GAUSS elimination algorithm, the right-hand side b is considered as an additional column of the matrix which is then treated as an n x (n+1) matrix. 1.2 Notion of pivot The previous implementation of the GAUSS elimination algorithm implicitly assumes that the diagonal terms called pivots are not zero in the course of the calculation. Case of zero pivots : A regular matrix may have a zero pivot. example: the matrix ( egin{array}{cc}0& 1\ 2& 1\end{array}) To remedy this drawback, one may use a pivoting strategy - complete pivoting : this strategy consists in choosing as pivot the largest term in the remaining block then in carrying out a permutation of row and of column. One then has the system \mathrm{P.A.Q}({Q}^{T}x)=\mathrm{P.b} where P is the row-permutation matrix and Q that of the columns. The solution found is then y={Q}^{T}x, and it is therefore necessary to keep the permutation matrix Q in order to obtain the sought solution x=\mathrm{Q.y} - partial pivoting : the pivot is sought as being the term of maximum value, among the terms not yet treated, in the current column (the k-th at step k) then one carries out a row permutation. 1.3 Stability of the method | Definition: | A numerical method for solving a linear system is said to be mathematically stable when 'whatever the regular matrix A, the algorithm succeeds'. | | --- | --- | | | | | Theorem: | The GAUSS method with a pivoting strategy is mathematically stable for any regular matrix. | | | | | Corollary: | If in the course of a GAUSS factorisation with pivoting a zero pivot is detected then the matrix is singular and this system does not have a unique solution. | | | | | Theorem: | The GAUSS method (without pivoting) is stable for real positive-definite matrices. | For more details, one will consult the basic books which are [bib13] [bib14] [bib6]. 1.4 Uniqueness of the decomposition | Proposition: | The GAUSS decomposition is not unique, but if one specifies the diagonal of L or of U then one has uniqueness of the decomposition. | | --- | --- | 1.5 A variant: the CROUT factorisation The CROUT factorisation method [1] is the same algorithm, which requires the same number of operations and performs the same fill-in of the matrix but the calculations are carried out in a different way. We place ourselves in the case where the matrix is factorisable, which is always the case up to a permutation of the rows and of the columns as soon as the matrix is regular: one therefore has A=LU Then one proceeds by identification \lbrace  egin{array}{c}	ext{pour}i\le {	ext{j a}}_{ij}={u}_{ij}+\sum_{	ext{k=1}}^{	ext{i-1}}{l}_{	ext{ik}}.{u}_{	ext{kj}}\ 	ext{pour}i>{	ext{j a}}_{ij}=\sum_{	ext{k=1}}^{j}{l}_{	ext{ik}}.{u}_{	ext{kj}}	ext{}\end{array} ( {l}_{ij} and {u}_{ij} are the elements of L and U ) whence the values of uij and lij as a function of aij | {u}_{\mathrm{1j}}={a}_{\mathrm{1j}} | j=1,\dots ,n | | --- | --- | | {l}_{	ext{i1}}= | i=1,\dots ,n | | {u}_{ij}={a}_{ij}-\sum_{	ext{k=1}}^{	ext{i-1}}{l}_{	ext{ik}}.{u}_{	ext{kj}} | i\le j | | {l}_{ij}= rac{1}{{u}_{jj}}({a}_{ij}-\sum_{	ext{k=1}}^{	ext{j-1}}{l}_{	ext{ik}}.{u}_{	ext{kj}}) | i>j | Note: The order of the calculations is not arbitrary, one must know the l_ik located on the left and the u_kj above each term to be computed. One then sees that at the k-th step, one carries over onto the k-th row all the previous contributions leaving unchanged the rows k+1 to n. This CROUT variant is also called GAUSS elimination by columns (or active columm), and favours the scalar-product operation. 1.6 Case of symmetric matrices Proposition: The GAUSS decomposition respects symmetry. It suffices to note that at each step the terms {a}_{ij} and {a}_{ji} receive the same contribution. Indeed: | | by the induction hypothesis, one assumes the matrix symmetric at step k and from then on one has:  egin{array}{}{a}_{ij}^{(k+1)}={a}_{ij}^{(k)}-{a}_{	ext{ik}}^{(k)}{a}_{	ext{kj}}^{(k)}{	ext{/a}}_{kk}^{(k)}\ {a}_{ji}^{(k+1)}={a}_{ji}^{