r7.01.01 Constitutive relations BETON_GRANGER and BETON_GRANGER_V for the basic creep of concrete r7.01.01 Constitutive relations BETON_GRANGER and BETON_GRANGER_V for the basic creep of concrete Summary: This document presents the “Granger” basic-creep model, which is a way of modelling the basic creep of concrete. The writing and the numerical treatment of the model are also detailed there. In Code_Aster, the model is declined into two constitutive laws: the complete version BETON_GRANGER_V and a simplified version, named BETON_GRANGER, without the effects of hygrometry and of ageing. 1 Introduction Within the framework of studies of the long-term behaviour of concrete structures, a predominant part of the strains measured on the structure concerns the delayed strains that appear in the concrete during its life. They comprise the shrinkages at early age, the desiccation shrinkage, the basic creep and the drying creep. The model presented here is dedicated to the modelling of the delayed strain associated with basic creep. Basic creep is, in addition to drying creep, the part of the creep of concrete that would be observed during a test without exchange of water with the exterior. Experimentally, concrete in basic creep presents an ageing viscous behaviour. The creep strain observed is proportional to the loading stress, and depends on the temperature and on the hygrometry. The longitudinal strain is accompanied, as in elasticity, by a transverse strain of opposite sign. The model chosen is inspired by the one proposed by L. Granger [bib1]. It is a model of viscoelastic type that takes into account the effect of ageing and of hygrometry. The effect of temperature is not modelled. A brief reminder of linear viscoelastic models is first given, and the model proper is then presented, as well as its numerical integration in code_aster . In code_aster , two versions are available: BETON_GRANGER_V, the complete model, and BETON_GRANGER, which does not take ageing into account. 2 Reminder of the creep behaviour of a viscoelastic material The experimental creep curve represents the evolution, as a function of time, of the strain of a material subjected to a constant unidimensional stress \sigma . La déformation de fluage :math: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}} is, as opposed to the instantaneous strain, the part of the strain that evolves with time. If a material has a linear viscoelastic behaviour, the creep strain of a specimen subjected to a constant load \sigma constante est, par définition, proportionnelle à \sigma . The creep strain (1D) from the loading time {t}_{c} may be written: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}}(t)=\sigma J(t,{t}_{c}) The function J(t,{t}_{c}) is an increasing function of \left(t-{t}_{c}ight) and zero for \left(t-{t}_{c}ight) negative. If the material is non-ageing, the creep function at a given time instant depends only on the time \left(t-{t}_{c}ight) elapsed since the loading time instant [bib3]: J(t,{t}_{c})=f(t-{t}_{c}) The creep strain is written: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}}(t)=\sigma f(t-{t}_{c}) For an ageing viscoelastic material, the experimental creep curve varies for two different loading times. From the modelling point of view, the creep function J(t,{t}_{c}) no longer varies only as a function of the time elapsed from the loading time instant; it depends independently on the time t and on the loading time instant {t}_{c} . 2.1 Boltzmann superposition principle Relation () is valid only for a constant loading. For a non-constant loading history, the Boltzmann superposition principle may be applied, by virtue of the linear dependence on the stress. If the loading history \sigma (t) is decomposed into n load increments, one has: \sigma (t)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}H(t-{t}_{\mathit{ci}})\Delta {\sigma}_{i} Where H is the Heaviside function. The expression of the creep strain may then be written: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}}(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}J(t,{t}_{\mathit{ci}})\Delta {\sigma}_{i} Passing to the limit, for an infinitely small loading increment: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}}(t)=\underset{{t}_{c}=0}{\overset{t}{\int}}J(t,{t}_{c}) rac{\partial \sigma }{\partial {t}_{c}}\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}d{t}_{c} For a non-ageing linear viscoelastic material one has: {\epsilon}^{\mathit{fl}}(t)=\underset{{t}_{c}=0}{\overset{t}{\int}}f(t-{t}_{c}) rac{\partial \sigma }{\partial {t}_{c}}d{t}_{c}=f\ast  rac{\partial \sigma }{\partial t} where 	ext{ } represents the convolution product. 3 Presentation of the Granger basic-creep model 3.1 Experimental properties of the basic creep of concrete under uniaxial loading Basic-creep tests on specimens bring out the following properties: - in a stress range lower than about 50% of the failure strength, the basic creep is proportional to the stress; - the basic creep of a specimen at hygrometry {h}_{\mathit{ext}} is almost proportional to {h}_{\mathit{ext}} . The basic creep of a dry concrete is almost zero and it is maximal for a concrete saturated with water; - when the temperature T increases, there is an acceleration of the creep; - basic creep is a strongly ageing phenomenon: the strain depends not only on the time elapsed since the application of the load, but also on the loading time instant; in other words, the rheological behaviour of the material at the loading time instant depends on its “age”. In the case of concrete, the age is the time elapsed since casting. It is chosen to model the basic creep of concrete with a linear viscoelastic model, to which the dependence of the creep on hygrometry will be added, as well as ageing [bib1]. The effect of temperature, modelled in [bib1], will not be taken into account here. 3.2 Modelling of non-ageing creep by a generalised Kelvin model It can be shown that any non-ageing linear viscoelastic body can be modelled by a series arrangement of Kelvin chains, called a generalised Kelvin model. The creep function can then be put in the form: J(t,{t}_{c})=f(t-{t}_{c})=\sum_{s=1}^{r}{J}_{s}\left(1-\exp\left(- rac{t-{t}_{c}}{{	au}_{s}}ight)ight) Where {	au}_{s} and {J}_{s} are, respectively, the retardation time and the compliance of each Kelvin chain s . These are positive coefficients identified on the experimental creep curves. The choice has been made in Code_Aster to limit oneself to eight chains ( r=8 ), which in practice is sufficient to reproduce satisfactorily the experimental creep curves of concrete. It is very difficult to determine both the {J}_{s} and the {	au}_{s} as soon as the number of Kelvin series exceeds two, because there are too many possible solutions. A choice is therefore generally made a priori on the {	au}_{s} , and the {J}_{s} are then determined by linear regression. The retardation times are chosen so as to have {	au}_{s}={	au}_{1}\cdot {10}^{s-1} . The generalised Kelvin viscoelastic model is enriched in order to take into account the effect of hygrometry. To this end, an equivalent stress is defined. Then, the creep function () of the generalised Kelvin model is modified in order to take into account the effect of ageing. 3.3 Effect of ageing Physically, ageing in concrete is associated with hydration at early age and with other phenomena such as polymerisation for older concrete. The effect of ageing is modelled by multiplying the coefficients {J}_{s} by an ageing function k\left(a\left({t}_{c}ight)ight)=k\left({t}_{c}ight) depending on the age a of the material at the loading time {t}_{c} . The creep function becomes: J(t,{t}_{c})=k({t}_{c})\sum_{s=1}^{8}{J}_{s}\left(1-\exp\left(- rac{t-{t}_{c}}{{	au}_{s}}ight)ight) The choice is made to use the same ageing function for all the Kelvin chains. In this way, the contribution of ageing (which depends on {t}_{c} ) has been decoupled, in the creep function (), from the non-ageing contribution (in t-{t}_{c} ). In code_aster, the ageing function is a user datum. A possible modelling for taking into account the ageing associated with hydration is that of the CEB [bib2]: \lbrace  egin{array}{cc}k(a)= rac{{