r7.01.14 Constitutive law CAM_CLAY r7.01.14 Constitutive law CAM_CLAY Summary: The Cam-Clay model is one of the best known and most widely used elastoplastic models in soil mechanics. It is specially adapted to clayey materials. There are several types of Cam-Clay models; the one presented here is the most common and is called modified Cam-Clay. This model is characterised by hardenable loading surfaces in the form of ellipses in the diagram of the first two stress invariants. Inside these reversibility surfaces, the material is non-linear elastic. There exists, moreover, at one point of each ellipse, a critical state characterised by a zero volume variation. The set of these points constitutes a straight line separating the dilatancy and contractancy zones of the material as well as the zones of negative and positive hardening. The hardening is governed by a single scalar variable and the normal flow rule is adopted. 1 Notations \sigma denotes the effective-stress tensor in small perturbations, defined as the difference between the total stresses and the water pressure in the case of saturated soils, written in the form of the following vector:  egin{split}\left(  egin{array}{c}{\sigma}_{11}\ {\sigma}_{22}\ {\sigma}_{33}\ \sqrt{2}{\sigma}_{12}\ \sqrt{2}{\sigma}_{23}\ \sqrt{2}{\sigma}_{31}\end{array} ight)\end{split} One writes: | \underline{\underline{P=- rac{1}{3}	ext{tr}(\sigma )}} | confining stress | | --- | --- | | s=\sigma +	ext{PI} | stress deviator | | {I}_{2}= rac{1}{2}	ext{tr}(s.s) | second invariant of the stresses | | Q={\sigma}_{	ext{eq}}=\sqrt{{\mathrm{3I}}_{2}} | equivalent stress | | \epsilon = rac{1}{2}(
abla u+{
abla}^{T}u) | total strain | | \varepsilon ={\varepsilon}_{e}+{\varepsilon}_{p}+{\varepsilon}_{	ext{th}} | partition of the strains (elastic, plastic, thermal) | | \underline{\underline{{\varepsilon}_{v}=-	ext{tr}(\varepsilon )+3\alpha (T-{T}_{0})}} | total volumetric strain | | {\varepsilon}_{V}^{p}=-	ext{tr}({\varepsilon}^{p}) | volumetric plastic strain | | 	ilde{\varepsilon}=\varepsilon + rac{1}{3}{\varepsilon}_{v}I | strain deviator | | {	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{e}=	ilde{\varepsilon}-{	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{p} | elastic strain deviator | | {	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{p}={\varepsilon}^{p}+ rac{1}{3}{\varepsilon}_{v}^{p}I | deviatoric plastic strain | | {\varepsilon}_{	ext{eq}}^{e}=\sqrt{ rac{2}{3}	ext{tr}({	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{e}.{	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{e})} | equivalent elastic strain | | {\varepsilon}_{	ext{eq}}^{p}=\sqrt{ rac{2}{3}	ext{tr}({	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{p}.{	ilde{\varepsilon}}^{p})} | equivalent plastic strain | e void ratio of the material (ratio of the pore volume to the volume of the solid grains) {e}_{0} initial void ratio \phi porosity (ratio of the pore volume to the total volume) \kappa swelling coefficient (elastic slope in a hydrostatic compression test) M slope of the critical-state line {k}_{0}= rac{(1+{e}_{0})}{\kappa} {P}_{	ext{cr}} internal variable of the model, critical pressure equal to half the consolidation pressure {P}_{	ext{cons}} \lambda compressibility coefficient (plastic slope in a hydrostatic compression test) k= rac{(1+{e}_{0})}{(\lambda -\kappa )} \mu elastic shear coefficient (Lamé coefficient) f loading surface \Lambda plastic multiplier {I}^{d} unit tensor of order 2 whose current term is {\delta}_{ij} {I}_{4}^{d} unit tensor of order 4 whose current term is {\delta}_{ijkl} 2 Introduction The model described here is the so-called modified Cam-Clay model. The initial Cam-Clay model was developed by the Cambridge school of soil mechanics in the 1960s. It predicted deviatoric strains that were too large under a low deviatoric loading, and it was modified by Burland and Roscoe in 1968 [bib1]. 2.1 Phenomenology of the behaviour of soils Poroplastic materials such as certain clays are characterised by the following behaviours: - the high porosity of these materials causes irreversible strains under hydrostatic loading corresponding to a significant reduction in the porosity. This purely contracting mechanism is sometimes called “collapse”, - under deviatoric loading, these materials show a contracting phase followed by a phase in which the material deforms at constant plastic volume or dilates. For both types of loading, the energy locked in the material evolves as a function of the number of contacts between the grains. For a hydrostatic loading, the number of contacts increases, as does the locked energy; one therefore has positive hardening. For a deviatoric loading, the material may deform without volume variation at a constant number of intergranular contacts. Moreover, one may observe in the tests strain localisations accompanied by a strong dilatancy. In these zones, the number of grains in contact decreasing, there is a decrease in the locked energy and therefore softening. These behaviours are brought out essentially by axisymmetric triaxial tests. These observations lead one to postulate that there exists a plastic threshold whose evolution is governed by two mechanisms: one purely contracting, associated with the hydrostatic stress, and a deviatoric mechanism governed by the internal friction, taking place at constant volume and possibly dilating as localisation is approached. The whole interest of the Cam Clay model lies in its ability to describe these phenomena with a minimum of ingredients and in particular a single loading surface and a hardening associated with a single scalar variable. 2.2 Behaviour under hydrostatic compression During a hydrostatic compression test, soils present a void ratio which decreases logarithmically with the hydrostatic pressure exerted (cf. [Figure 2.2-a]). {e}_{0} being the initial void ratio under the initial loading. Up to a pressure {P}_{	ext{cons}}^{0} called the consolidation pressure, the behaviour is reversible; the slope \kappa of the diagram (e,	ext{Ln}P) is called the elastic swelling coefficient. {P}_{	ext{cons}}^{0} corresponds to the maximum pressure that the material has undergone during its history. Beyond this preconsolidation, the diagram presents a new slope \lambda (compressibility coefficient), more marked, and the appearance of irreversible strains. {P}_{	ext{cons}}^{0} therefore corresponds to an evolving elastoplastic threshold. Figure 2.2-a: Hydrostatic loading–unloading test Note: The diagram above corresponds to a set of measurements in which the effective stress is stabilised. Indeed, in the process of consolidation of soils, it is the water contained in the pores which first takes up the hydrostatic pressure with very little strain, before flowing out and allowing the skeleton to deform. After consolidation of the material and stabilisation of the water pressure, the effective stress (total stress minus water pressure) is stabilised and plotted on the graph. The constitutive relations in saturated porous media are generally expressed with the effective stresses according to the Terzaghi assumption. 2.3 Behaviour under deviatoric loading Axisymmetric triaxial tests make it possible to control both the deviatoric component Q and the spherical component P of the loading. Depending on the ratio of these two components, one observes a purely dilating plastic behaviour (  rac{Q}{P-{P}_{	ext{trac}}}>M ) or a contracting one (  rac{Q}{P-{P}_{	ext{trac}}} {P}_{	ext{cr}} the material is contracting ( {\dot{\epsilon}}_{v}^{p}>0 ) and {P}_{	ext{cr}} is increasing (hardening). 3.3 Elastic law and hardening law One makes the assumption of the decoupling of the elastic law into a hydrostatic part and a deviatoric part and the additional assumption that the shear modulus is constant. One therefore conside