AM_CLAY model, the non-linear elastic law makes a hydrostatic stress appear for a zero volumetric strain [eq 3.3-3]. The user adopts one of the following two choices: - Give the material parameter {K}_{	ext{cam}} , which represents an initial compressibility, a positive value. The analysis may be carried out with a state of zero initial stresses. - Give the material parameter {K}_{	ext{cam}} , which represents an initial compressibility, a zero value. Initialise the stress state according to one of the two different ways: - Carry out a linear elastic analysis by assigning boundary conditions such that the stress field in the structure is a uniform hydrostatic compression. One extracts from this analysis the stress field at the Gauss points. This stress field is considered as the initial state of the hydrostatic stress necessary to the law CAM_CLAY in the STAT_NON_LINE analysis using the CAM_CLAY model. - Use the operator CREA_CHAMP in order to create, with the operation 'AFFE', a hydrostatic stress field at the Gauss points; the stress in this case is given with a negative sign (convention of Aster for compressions) and constitutes the initial state in the following STAT_NON_LINE. 7.2 Examples of results obtained on triaxial tests The following figures show triaxial loading paths with evolutions of the axial strain as a function of the deviator Q . They come from numerical analyses carried out with the CAM_CLAY model implanted in Code_Aster . These tests were carried out using a modelling of KIT_HM type in undrained condition (this condition easily allows us to load in a purely deviatoric manner, the hydrostatic part of the loading being taken up by the water pressure). The shapes of the curves obtained numerically with Code_Aster are entirely comparable to the schematic curves presented in the paper by Charlez [bib2]. In the first test, the material is normally consolidated, that is to say that the starting hydrostatic pressure is equal to the consolidation pressure (in this case 6.{10}^{5} Pa). The (positive) hardening begins from the start of the deviatoric phase, without a preliminary elastic phase. The hardening continues up to a perfectly plastic plateau when one reaches the critical point ( Q=\mathit{MP} ). As for the three other tests, the deviatoric phase begins for a value of the mean effective stress lower than the consolidation pressure; the material is for this reason overconsolidated. In the case where P is greater than {P}_{	ext{cr}} equal to 3.{10}^{5}\mathit{Pa} , the specific point of the loading intersects the loading surface before the critical line. There will therefore be three specific phases: an elastic phase, a contracting plastic phase, then a perfectly plastic phase. In the case where P={P}_{	ext{cr}} , the behaviour is perfectly plastic just after the elastic phase. In the case where P is lower than {P}_{	ext{cr}} , the point representative of the loading intersects the critical line before the loading surface, which it reaches during a purely elastic path. In this configuration, the behaviour is softening and dilating and the locked energy decreases. The point representative of the loading then joins the critical state, where the material will enter perfect plasticity. The CAM_CLAY behaviour cannot produce a continuously contracting/dilating behaviour. The point representative of the loading is obliged to pass through the critical state, where the set of the hardening parameters (plastic volumetric strain, critical pressure, locked energy) become stationary [bib2]. 8 Appendix: Implicit tangent operator. Option FULL_MECA We present in this appendix elements of the computation of the consistent tangent operator. 8.1 General case 8.1.1 Treatment of the deviatoric part One considers here that the variation of the loading is purely deviatoric (\mathrm{\delta P}=0) . The increment of the deviatoric stress is written in the form: {\mathrm{\Delta s}}_{ij}=\mathrm{2\mu }(\Delta {	ilde{\epsilon}}_{ij}-\Delta