r7.01.15 Constitutive law of LAIGLE r7.01.15 Constitutive law of LAIGLE Summary: The rheological model of Laigle makes it possible to analyse the mechanical behaviour of rocks. The development of this constitutive model was initiated following the difficulty of correctly capturing the response of the rock mass during the excavation of an underground cavity, with the aim: - of defining the need for, and the nature of, any supports to be installed; - of determining the extent of the ground around a structure that is influenced by the excavation. The implementation of this elastoplastic model has been focused principally on the simulation of the post-peak behaviour of the rock. From this standpoint it is assumed that there is no hardening of the rock prior to its failure. This is expressed by a linear elastic behaviour up to the strength peak (the rock may nevertheless be damaged while the material is not yet at failure). The plasticity criterion defined is of generalised Hoek and Brown type and accounts for the influence of the stress level on the shear strength. The softening of the material is associated with a progressive decrease in the cohesion and friction-angle properties, accompanied by a change in volume. It is driven by the cumulative plastic deviatoric strain, taken as the sole hardening variable. In order to facilitate the integration of this model into Code_Aster , the law initially developed in the formalism of the principal stresses was rewritten with stress invariants on a basis of the Cambou‑Jafari-Sidoroff (CJS) model. The numerical formulation is implicit with respect to the criterion and explicit with respect to the flow direction. The sign convention used for the formulation of the equations, within the framework of this note, is that of continuum mechanics. 1 Notations 1.1 General points \sigma denotes the effective-stress tensor in small perturbations, written in the form of the following vector: ( egin{array}{}{\sigma}_{11}\ {\sigma}_{22}\ {\si