u | Poisson's ratio | | {\sigma}_{	ext{p1}} | Intersection of the intermediate criterion and of the peak criterion | | {\sigma}_{	ext{p2}} | Intersection of the intermediate criterion and of the ultimate criterion | | | Atmospheric pressure | 2 Introduction The purpose of this note is to present the rheological model for analysing the mechanical behaviour of rocks, adapted to the simulation of underground structures, introduced into Code_Aster and developed by the CIH [bib1]. The aim of this model is that it should be able to be implemented rapidly and industrially, in order to answer the principal questions that the engineer asks when analysing and designing an underground cavity. The rheological law must for this purpose remain relatively simple, both in the identification of the parameters and in its implementation and in the interpretation of the results. 2.1 Phenomenology of the behaviour of soils One particularity of a rock, compared with a soil, is that its mechanical behaviour is, over a wide stress range, driven by the cohesion. This cohesion is associated with a cementation of the medium, induced over the geological history of the rock mass, and is essentially epitaxial in nature. Conversely, the strength of a soil is governed more particularly by the friction and/or dilatancy term. The cohesion, of essentially capillary origin, then has an influence only for very low states of confining stress. This distinction between a soil and a rock is important because it guides the choice and the basic assumptions of the constitutive model. The principal rheological phenomena associated with this context are the following: - In the domain of small strains, the response of a rock, in particular under low states of confinement, may be likened to a linear elastic behaviour, weakly dependent on the stress state. Non-linearities of the behaviour are liable to appear prior to the strength peak, in the case of soft rocks, for a stress level of the order of 70 to 80% of the maximum value. This threshold decreases with the increase in the mean pressure, until it almost vanishes when the overconsolidation stress is reached (cap-model). Under very low confining stresses, representative of those prevailing in the vicinity of underground structures, these non-linearities are generally weak, all the more so as the cementation is significant, and therefore the level of overconsolidation of the rock is high. - The dilatancy (increase in volume) is initiated when the non-linearities appear on the stress–strain curve. This dilatancy increases until localisation occurs within the specimen. At that moment, the dilatancy rate (or the dilatancy angle \psi ) is maximum, and thereafter decreases progressively and vanishes at very large strains. - The strength peak is reached for stresses describing a failure criterion, generally curved in the Mohr plane or in the plane of the major and minor principal stresses. The assumption of a linear Mohr–Coulomb criterion is therefore only a simplifying assumption, which tends, for low confining stresses, to overestimate the cohesion of the medium. - Once the maximum strength has been reached, the strength of the rock decreases. This post-peak softening is all the more rapid and significant (in intensity) as the confining stress is low. This decrease is linked to a more or less localised damage of the rock, depending on the confinement level. Whatever this stress, beyond the peak, the rock can no longer be considered as continuous . Its behaviour is then driven by the conditions of strain and of strength at the level of the strain-localisation zone. - The appearance of one or more kinematic discontinuities within the rock is associated with a loss of cohesion. The post-peak behaviour is then governed by the conditions of friction and of dilatancy along the discontinuity planes or within a strain-localisation band. It follows from this reasoning that, for very large strains, the behaviour of the rock, likened to a “structure”, is purely frictional, and is characterised by an ultimate friction angle \phi . This angle is an intrinsic datum of the material, a function of the minerals constituting the rock. It therefore does not depend directly on the cohesion conditions, and above all it may be considered as independent of the dimensions of the specimen. - When the behaviour becomes purely frictional, it is associated with no volumetric strain. The dilatancy has therefore vanished, and no longer exists at large strains. - The evolution between the peak strength and the critical state corresponding to large strains is more or less progressive depending on the state of the applied stresses. For a zero state of confinement (uniaxial compression), the behaviour is driven solely by the cohesion, and failure results in an immediate and sudden loss of all strength. The softening will be more progressive as the confining stress increases, until it becomes non-existent beyond a certain confining stress that bounds the ductile and brittle behavioural domains. 2.2 Context of the study and simplifying assumptions of the model The wish to develop a model that is easy to implement necessarily entails simplifications, arising from a compromise between the objectives sought, the conditions of use of the model (quality of the input data, time and cost available…) and the means implemented to carry out these developments. These compromises are essentially the following: - A linear elastic behaviour up to the strength peak. This amounts to assuming that there is no hardening of the rock prior to its failure. - Only a shear-failure criterion is retained . This means that if the rock is crushed in an isotropic manner, the behaviour remains elastic, and that there is no damage and no hardening of the material under this type of path. During the excavation phases of an underground structure with the installation of a light support, the mean pressure in the rock mass situated nearby can only decrease (or remain constant in the ideal case of a circular cavity subjected to an isotropic loading, for a linear elastic behaviour). Plastification under isotropic stress, which one may find on a Cap-Model or on a Cam-Clay type law, did not appear indispensable to us in view of the objectives sought, and in the case of an isothermal and short-term loading. During the development of this model, we deliberately focused on the study and the simulation of the post-peak behaviour of the rock. In this behavioural domain, the strength of the material is assumed to be driven, as a function of the stress state and of the level of damage of the rock, by the cohesion, the dilatancy or the friction. The cohesion defines the strength of the material so long as the latter remains continuous. It is active up to the strength peak, and has only a slight influence on the softening behaviour, unless the cohesion is representative of a ductile “glue” (case of soils injected with silicate gel,…). As the cohesion deteriorates through damage, the dilatancy increases, until it reaches its maximum value at the loss of continuity of the medium. At that moment, under the effect of the shearing of the induced discontinuity, this dilatancy degrades progressively and slowly. The rheology of the rock then evolves towards a purely frictional behaviour. 3 The continuum model 3.1 Elastic behaviour The elastic behaviour is driven by a linear law, with a constant modulus independent of the stress state. The 2 parameters characterising this behaviour are the elasticity modulus E and Poisson's ratio 
