r7.01.16 Integration of the associated (DRUCK_PRAGER) and non-associated (DRUCK_PRAG_N_A) elastoplastic Drucker-Prager mechanical constitutive behaviours, and post-processing r7.01.16 Integration of the associated (DRUCK_PRAGER) and non-associated (DRUCK_PRAG_N_A) elastoplastic Drucker-Prager mechanical constitutive behaviours, and post-processing Summary: This document describes the principles of several developments concerning the elastoplastic Drucker-Prager constitutive law in the associated version (DRUCK_PRAGER) and the non-associated version (DRUCK_PRAG_N_A). One is interested first in the integration of the law itself then, this law being a softening law, in a Rice localisation indicator and finally in the sensitivity computation by direct differentiation for this law. For the integration of the law, an implicit scheme is used. Contents 1 Introduction The Drucker-Prager law makes it possible to model, in an elementary way, the elastoplastic constitutive behaviour of concrete or of certain soils. Compared with Von-Mises plasticity with isotropic hardening, the difference lies in the presence of a term in \mathrm{Tr}(\sigma ) in the formulation of the threshold and of a non-zero spherical component of the plastic strain tensor. In Code_Aster, the law exists in the associated version (DRUCK_PRAGER) and the non-associated version (DRUCK_PRAG_N_A), which is better suited to certain soils because it makes it possible to take dilatancy into account more accurately. This note gathers the theoretical aspects of several developments carried out in the code around this law: its integration according to a time-implicit scheme, a Rice localisation indicator and the sensitivity computation by direct differentiation. The material is assumed to be isotropic. The Rice indicator and the sensitivity computation do not work under the plane-stress assumption. The theory and the developments have been carried out for three types of hardening function: linear, parabolic and exponential (DRUCK_PRAG_N_A only). In the linear and parabolic cases, this function is in every case constant beyond an “ultimate” cumulative plastic strain. 2 Integration of the Drucker-Prager constitutive law 2.1 Notation Mechanical stresses are counted positive in tension, strains positive in extension. | u | displacements of the skeleton, of components {u}_{x},{u}_{y},{u}_{z} | | --- | --- | | \varepsilon = rac{1}{2}(
