terials loaded in the inelastic domain. To study the instabilities linked to localisation one distinguishes, on the one hand, the classes of materials with time-dependent constitutive behaviour and, on the other hand, those that do not depend on time. For materials with time-independent constitutive behaviour, the approach commonly used is the so-called bifurcation method (it is this method that is of interest in this note). It consists in analysing the losses of uniqueness of the rate problem. For materials with time-dependent constitutive behaviour, uniqueness of the rate problem is often guaranteed, and this does not prevent the observation of instabilities during their deformation. For these materials, one must then resort to other approaches. The most commonly used is the perturbation approach. This approach will not be treated in this note, but for further information consult the notes , . Rudnicki and Rice have shown that the study of strain localisation in rock mechanics falls within the framework of bifurcation theory. The latter is based on the notion of unstable equilibrium. Rice considers that the bifurcation point marks the end of the stable regime. The onset of localisation is associated with a rheological instability of the system, and this instability corresponds locally to the loss of ellipticity of the equations which govern the continuous incremental equilibrium in rates. Rice thus proposes a criterion known as “localisation bifurcation”, which makes it possible to detect the state from which the solution of the mathematical equations which govern the boundary-value problem under consideration and the evolution of the mechanical system under study (equations, equilibrium, constitutive law) lose their uniqueness. This theory makes it possible to compute the initiation state of localisation, which is perceived as the limit of validity of classical finite-element analyses. 3.2 Theoretical approach 3.2.1 Statement of the rate problem One considers a s