r7.01.18 Modified HOEK_BROWN constitutive law r7.01.18 Modified HOEK_BROWN constitutive law Summary: This document describes a constitutive law for rocks. The yield criterion is a parabolic criterion of Hoek-Brown type. It is written in principal stresses. The evolution of the plastic strains is of «non-associated» type, formulated from a Drucker-Prager criterion whose friction angle evolves with yielding. This law can be used in pure mechanics as well as in coupled thermo-hydro-mechanical modelling. In thermo-hydro-mechanical modelling, it can be used in effective stresses or in total stresses: - In the first case, it is the effective stresses that are required to satisfy the Hoek and Brown criterion. The plastic strains are computed using the Drucker surface and the friction angle in the space of effective stresses - In the second case, it is the total stresses that are required to satisfy the Hoek and Brown criterion. The plastic strains are computed using the Drucker surface and the friction angle in the space of total stresses 1 Introduction This note presents a mechanical constitutive model for rocks. To represent the mechanical behaviour of rocks the user of Code_Aster can use the Drucker-Prager law DRUCK_PRAGER, the LAIGLE law or the HOEK_BROWN law presented here. The Drucker-Prager law is the simplest and the furthest from the real behaviour of rocks. The Laigle law is the most faithful to the physics of the phenomena. Thus the law presented in this note is intermediate between these two laws, in terms of complexity as well as in terms of representation of reality. It uses formulations that are fairly classical in the field of geomechanics. This law can be used in modellings of mechanics alone or in modellings of THM type. In thermo-hydro-mechanical modelling, it can be used in effective stresses or in total stresses: - In the first case, it is the effective stresses that are required to satisfy the Hoek and Brown criterion. The plastic strains are computed using the Drucker surface and the friction angle in the space of effective stresses - In the second case, it is the total stresses that are required to satisfy the Hoek and Brown criterion. The plastic strains are computed using the Drucker surface and the friction angle in the space of total stresses One will find in reference [1] elements useful for the understanding of this law The formulation is of non-associated plasticity type: - The elastic domain is defined by a criterion of Hoek-Brown type, whose parameters evolve with the hardening parameter - The hardening parameter is a combination of the plastic shear strain and of the plastic volumetric strain - The plastic strains derive from a Drucker-Prager criterion whose friction angle evolves with yielding. 1.1 Characteristics of the model The model simulates the short-term mechanical behaviour of rocks in 4 phases, described in reference [1]: 1. Elastic phase characterised by a constant Young's modulus and a constant Poisson's ratio 2. Elastoplastic phase with positive hardening which simulates the initiation of a form of damage and its progression towards failure of the rock. This phase is modelled by a plasticity criterion of Hoek-Brown type. This criterion evolves as a function of the major irreversible strain. The evolution of the irreversible strain is determined by a plastic flow potential expressed by a function of Drucker-Prager type. 3. Elastoplastic phase with negative hardening which represents the post-failure behaviour of rocks. The failure criterion is of Hoek-Brown type. The strain is determined by a non-associated plastic flow potential of Drucker-Prager type. 4. Residual strength phase characterised by a modified Hoek-Brown type function. 2 Notation 2.1 General points Strains are counted positive in extension and stresses are positive for tensile states. | Notation | Description | | --- | --- | | {I}_{1}=	ext{tr}(\sigma ) | First invariant of the stresses | | s=\sigma - rac{	ext{tr}(\sigma )}{3