r7.01.34 SUSHI finite-volume schemes for the modelling of miscible unsaturated flows r7.01.34 SUSHI finite-volume schemes for the modelling of miscible unsaturated flows Summary: This note briefly presents the finite-volume schemes developed in Code_Aster. A brief reminder of the constitutive equations concerned is given, then the schemes used are presented. > Contents 1 Introduction We present here the SUSHI finite-volume schemes developed for the modelling of miscible two-phase (unsaturated) flows in a porous medium, miscible meaning here that the components can exist in several phases. These schemes concern the family of the THM modellings. They are intended only for pure hydraulics (neither mechanics nor thermal analysis), that is, if one takes up the classical nomenclature described in R7.01.11, the KIT_HH. The SUSHI finite-volume schemes (Scheme Using Stabilization and Hybrid Interfaces) were developed by Ophélie Angelini in the framework of her thesis [1] in order to treat in particular problems of steep fronts on arbitrary meshes. This scheme is inspired by the Hybrid Finite Volume (HVF) scheme introduced by R. Eymard et al. [4]. At the origin of this work there was an abundant literature indicating that these schemes, ensuring by definition a good representation of the flux, were better suited to this type of hyperbolic equations applied to strongly heterogeneous domains. Implementing and having such schemes available therefore quickly seemed reasonable, if only in order to compare oneself with the members of the community of flows in porous media [2]. These schemes do not claim to replace the finite elements but propose a robust alternative. We shall present here only briefly the physical model of two-phase flows detailed in the documentation R7.01.11. This documentation therefore essentially presents the finite-volume spatial discretisation schemes implemented on these models in Code_Aster. For further details, the reader is referred to the thesis of Ophélie Angélini [1] 2 Presentation of the problem: Assumptions, Notations The whole of the hydraulic physical model is presented in detail in R7.01.11. We content ourselves here with recalling the main variables and assumptions as well as the equations treated. The formalism comes essentially from the works of Coussy [5]. The routines managing the constitutive laws are not affected by the use of the finite-volume scheme. 2.1 Modelling framework These developments concern only the models with 2 phases (liquid gas) and 2 miscible components (for example water and hydrogen). We therefore place ourselves in the framework of the _HH2 modellings (cf. U2.04.05). More precisely one will speak here of the HH2SUDA modelling (cf. section 4). It is recalled that the 2 hydraulic constitutive laws that can be used are then: - LIQU_AD_GAZ_VAPE: 2 components per phase - LIQU_AD_GAZ: 2 components in the liquid phase, only one in the gas phase (vapour neglected) In what follows only the complete model with 2 components, 2 phases will be discussed. The case without vapour simply amounts to cancelling the terms concerning it. 2.2 Notations We assume that the pores of the solid are occupied by two constituents denoted w (for water) and h (for hydrogen), each coexisting in two phases at most, one liquid denoted l and the other gaseous denoted g . The quantities (grandeur) A associated with the phase p ( p=l,g ) of the component c will be denoted: {X}_{p}^{c} . Concretely, this gives: - {A}_{l}^{w} : quantity (grandeur) A for liquid water, - {A}_{g}^{w} : quantity (grandeur) A for water vapour, - {A}_{l}^{h} : quantity (grandeur) A for the component h dissolved in the liquid, - {A}_{g}^{h} : quantity (grandeur) A for the component h in gaseous form (for example dry hydrogen). The general assumptions made are the following: - anisotropic constitutive behaviour, - the gases are perfect gases, - ideal mixture of perfect gases (total pressure = sum of the partial pressures), - thermodynamic equilibrium between the phases of one and the same constituent. The various notations are made explicit below. 2.2.1 State variables The variables are: - the pressures of each constituent {P}_{p}^{c} - the temperature of the medium T . These various variables are not totally independent. Indeed, if one considers a single constituent, the thermodynamic equilibrium between its phases imposes a relation between the vapour pressure and the liquid pressure of this constituent. Finally, there is only one independent pressure per constituent, just as there is only one mass-conservation equation. The number of independent pressures is therefore equal to the number of independent constituents. The choice of these pressures is free (combinations of the pressures of the constituents) provided that the pressures chosen, associated with the temperature, form a system of independent variables. We have chosen — and in order to be homogeneous with the finite-element formulation — as independent variables descriptive of the medium: - the total pressure of the gas {P}_{	ext{g}}={P}_{g}^{w}+{P}_{g}^{h} , (Dalton's law) - The total pressure of the liquid {P}_{l}={P}_{l}^{w}+{P}_{l}^{h} - the capillary pressure {P}_{c}={P}_{	ext{g}}-{P}_{l}={P}_{g}^{w}+{P}_{g}^{h}-({P}_{l}^{w}+{P}_{l}^{h}) . 2.2.2 Characteristic quantities (grandeur) of the solid phase One denotes: The porosity: \phi , The intrinsic permeability tensor: k 2.2.3 Characteristic quantities (grandeur) of the fluids One denotes: - The density of the phase p : {ho}_{	ext{p}} , {ho}_{	ext{p}}={ho}_{p}^{w}+{ho}_{p}^{h} - The viscosity of the phase p : {\mu}_{	ext{p}} - The saturation of the phase p : , {S}_{l}+{S}_{	ext{g}}=1 which is a decreasing function of the capillary pressure. One therefore has {S}_{l}=f({P}_{c}) . - The relative permeability of the phase p : {k}_{	ext{rp}} function of the saturation - The hydraulic conductivity of the phase p : {\lambda}_{	ext{p}}^{H} such that: {\lambda}_{	ext{p}}^{H}= rac{k{k}_{\mathrm