abla u))=f(x) . Its approximation on a set \Omega \subset {ℝ}^{n} is a piecewise-constant function on a partition of \Omega into control volumes denoted in what follows by K . The principle of the finite-volume method is to integrate the equation over each of these control volumes. Using Green's formula, the equations are written: {\int}_{\partial K}\Theta (X,u,
abla u).{n}_{K}d\gamma ={\int}_{K}f(x)\mathrm{dx}, orall K with {n}_{K} the unit outward normal of the control volume K . One then sees that applying a finite-volume scheme consists in approximating fluxes on boundaries. For a transient equation of the type:  rac{\partial u}{\partial t}+div(\Theta (X,u,
abla u))=f(x) , one will logically have a formulation of the type {\int}_{K}u\mathrm{dx}+{\int}_{\partial K}\Theta (X,u,
abla u).{n}_{K}d\gamma ={\int}_{K}f(x)\mathrm{dx}, orall K In summary, a finite-volume scheme is defined by: - The choice of the control volume K , which may be identical to the mesh cell, or else a construction (of Voronoi type for example). The approximate solution given by the finite-volume type schemes will be piecewise constant on this partition. - The position of the approximation nodes of the unknowns for each volume. - The manner of approximating the flux on an edge of the control volume: they are approximated by the integral formulation of the equations and the application of Green's formula on the boundary of the control volume. During the approximation, the fluxes must be conservative and consistent. Conservativity is expressed by the continuity of the fluxes at the interface, by definition ensured by the finite volumes (the flux is written explicitly). Consistency is reached when the difference between the real flux and its approximation tends to 0. There exists a very large number of schemes according to the way of computing this flux and the consistency required. The writing of the flux can therefore relate nodes that do not necessarily belong to the same element. They may be related for example by a common interface. One therefore sees that in this case, two nodes are related, no longer when they belong to the same element as in finite elements, but when they belong to two neighbours (related by a common face). In order to implement most of these schemes one must therefore have recourse to the topological notion of neighbourhood, which constitutes an important difference with respect to finite elements. 3.2 The SUSHI finite volumes applied to miscible two-phase flows Two-phase flows in a porous medium are governed by the system of equations: \lbrace  egin{array}{}\dot{{m}_{l}^{w}}+{\dot{m}}_{g}^{w}+	ext{Div}({F}_{l}^{w}+{F}_{g}^{w})=0\ \dot{{m}_{l}^{h}}+{\dot{m}}_{g}^{h}+	ext{Div}({F}_{l}^{h}+{F}_{g}^{h})=0\ {P}_{l}(x,t)=	ilde{{P}_{l}}\mathrm{sur}\par