pstream scheme, which upwinds this term according to the direction of the flow of the phase in the current mesh cell or in the neighbouring mesh cell through the edge considered, - the causal scheme, which will upwind this term differently when it is a factor of a pressure term or a factor of a gravity term. The upwinding will nevertheless be done in the current mesh cell or in the neighbouring mesh cell, - the scheme with variable Péclet number, which makes it possible to decompose the convective part of the flux into a linear combination between the upwinded flux of the upstream scheme and the centred flux. Thus the parameter of this combination can be adjusted locally on each edge according to the diffusion introduced by the capillary pressure. We shall present below the formulation retained in Code_Aster. Initially 3 formulations were implemented in Code_Aster: two of them will upwind the mobilities by the upstream scheme, either on the edge of the current mesh cell or on the neighbouring mesh cell through the edge, and the third formulation will use mobilities centred in the current mesh cell. We have decided to retain a single formulation (for reasons of stability and of computation time): the edge-upwinded formulation. Formulation: Edge-Upwinded Finite-Volume scheme (VFDA) The interest of this formulation is both to keep the notion of upwinding, while keeping a small stencil of the Jacobian matrix. For this purpose, we continue to write the continuity of the total mass fluxes but we upwind the mobilities on the edges of the current mesh cell. We therefore consider the continuity of the following quantities: {\int}_{\partial K}\left[-k{k}_{l}^{w}
