r7.02.08 Computation of the stress intensity factors by extrapolation of the displacement field r7.02.08 Computation of the stress intensity factors by extrapolation of the displacement field Summary: One describes here a method for computing \mathit{K1} , \mathit{K2} and \mathit{K3} in 2D (plane and axisymmetric) and in 3D by extrapolation of the displacement jumps on the crack lips. It can be used by means of the command POST_K1_K2_K3, both for a meshed crack (classical finite elements) and for a non-meshed crack (enriched finite elements: X-FEM method). If the crack is meshed, it must necessarily be plane; if the crack is not meshed (X-FEM method), it may be non-plane (but sufficiently regular). In both cases, the method is applicable only for linear elastic, homogeneous and isotropic materials. The method used is theoretically less accurate than the computation from the bilinear form of the energy release rate and of the singular displacements [R7.02.01 and R7.02.05] (operator CALC_G). It nevertheless makes it possible to obtain easily relatively reliable values of the stress intensity factors. The comparison of the different computation methods is useful for estimating the accuracy of the results obtained. The accuracy of the results of the method of extrapolation of the displacement jumps is distinctly improved if the mesh is quadratic. For a meshed crack, it is recommended to use so-called “Barsoum” elements at the crack front (elements whose mid-side nodes are located at the quarter of the edges). For a non-meshed crack, it is recommended to enrich several layers of elements around the crack front. 1 Statement of the problem The method for computing the stress intensity factors by extrapolation of the displacement is based on the asymptotic expansion of the displacement field at the crack front. In 2D, in an elastic, linear, isotropic and homogeneous medium, the stress and displacement fields are known analytically for the opening modes of the crack (characterised by \mathit{K1} ), of in-plane sliding ( \mathit{K2} ) and of anti-plane sliding ( \mathit{K3} ), cf. and . In the general case in 3D, one can show that the asymptotic behaviour of the displacements and of the stresses is the sum of the solutions corresponding to modes 1 and 2 (in plane strains) and to mode 3 (anti-plane), and of four other particular solutions, but which are more regular than the preceding ones. In all cases, the singularity is therefore the same and one can write the following relations in the plane normal to the crack front, at a point M :  egin{array}{c}{K}_{1}(M)=\underset{r	o 0}{\lim}( rac{E}{8(1-{
