s the following: Loop over these nodes: If {r}_{i}^{\sup}=\parallel {	ext{MP}}_{i}^{\sup}\parallel 	ext{dmax} : extraction of the displacement at {P}_{i}^{\sup} If {r}_{i}^{\inf}=\parallel {	ext{MP}}_{i}^{\inf}\parallel 	ext{dmax} : extraction of the displacement at {P}_{i}^{\inf} > Computation of the displacement jump in the three directions Loop over the nodes of the crack front (current point: M ) Definition of the plane \Gamma normal to the crack and to the crack front, at the point M (plane of normal 	ext{t} ) Identification of the nodes of the two lips which belong to \Gamma : {P}_{i}^{\sup} and {P}_{i}^{\inf} Extrapolation of the displacement jump Three extrapolation methods are programmed. They are illustrated in this paragraph for a meshed crack (quadratic mesh), with or without elements of “Barsoum” type. The Barsoum elements are such that the non-vertex nodes on the sides of the quadratic elements touching the crack front are moved to the quarter of the side. They make it possible to capture better the singularity of the stress field at the crack front | Classical finite element (M being a node of the crack front) | Finite element of Barsoum type | | --- | --- | - Method 1: one computes the square of the jump of the displacement field and one divides it by r . Different values of {K}^{2} are obtained (up to a multiplicative factor) by extrapolation to r=0 of the straight-line segments thus obtained. If the solution were perfect (analytical asymptotic field everywhere), one should obtain a straight line. In reality, one obtains almost a straight line with a mesh of “Barsoum” type, and a non-straight curve otherwise: - Method 2: one plots the square of the jump of the displacement field as a function of r . The approximations of K are (still up to a multiplicative factor) equal to the square root of the slope of the segments connecting the origin to the different points of the curve. - Method 3: one identifies the stress intensity factor K from the displacement jump [U] by a least-squares method. The fitting is carried out on a segment of length \mathit{dmax} , where \mathit{dmax} is the parameter fixed in the operand ABSC_CURV_MAXI of the operator POST_K1_K2_K3 or, in the case of a meshed crack, if ABSC_CURV_MAXI is not given, \mathit{dmax} is equal to four times the maximum size of the mesh cells connected to the nodes of the front: K minimises J(k)= rac{1}{2}\underset{0}{\overset{	ext{dmax}}{\int}}{([U(r)]-k\sqrt{r})}^{2}\mathrm{dr} Hence the explicit formula for computing K : K= rac{2}{{r}_{m}^{2}}\underset{0}{\overset{	ext{dmax}}{\int}}[U(r)]\sqrt{r}\mathrm{dr}= rac{1}{{r}_{m}^{2}}\sum_{i=0}^{\mathrm{nbno}-1}({r}_{i+1}-{r}_{i})({[U]}_{i+1}\sqrt{{r}_{i+1}}-{[U]}_{i}\sqrt{{r}_{i}}) where \mathit{nbno} is the number of nodes on the fitting segment [0,\mathit{dmax}] . One notes that in this expression K is, for a fixed \mathit{dmax} , a linear form of the displacement field. 3 Accuracy of the proposed methods The method of extrapolation of the displacement jumps has been validated on tests whose analytical solutions are known. One presents below certain results, in 2D and in 3D, for a meshed or non-meshed crack. One also compares the results with the theoretically more accurate method based on the computation of the energy release rate and on the singular functions (operator CALC_G: theta method). 3.1 Test SSLP313: 2D C_PLAN (meshed crack) This is an inclined crack in an infinite medium subjected to a uniform stress field in one direction (analytical reference solution in plane stresses, exact in an infinite medium). The crack opens in mixed mode ( \mathit{K1} and \mathit{K2} ) V3.02.313. For the test, the crack is meshed in a fairly large plate. The quadratic mesh is very fine. The results are the following: Reference solution (analytical solution) | \mathit{K1} | \mathit{K2} | G | | --- | --- | --- | | 3.58E+06 | 2.69E+06 | 1.00E+02 | Computation with the theta method (CALC_G) | | \mathit{K1} | \mathit{K2} | G | | 