ar with respect to the crack, the values of K and of G computed vary along the crack front. For the comparison below, one retains the value corresponding to a particular point chosen arbitrarily (middle of the crack front represented). The mesh is linear and relatively coarse . In the X-FEM method, the user can choose the zone over which the elements around the crack front are enriched with the asymptotic displacements (keywords RAYON_ENRI and NB_COUCHES of DEFI_FISS_XFEM). This enrichment aims to improve the accuracy of the computation. One compares here the results obtained with an enrichment limited to the elements containing the crack front only and with an enrichment over four layers of elements around the crack front. Computation with the theta method (CALC_G – default smoothing of type LEGENDRE of degree 5) | | G | | --- | --- | | CALC_G with enrichment over one layer | 11.42 | | Deviation / ref. | -1.4 % | | CALC_G with enrichment over four layers | 11.61 | | Deviation / ref. | 0.2 % | POST_K1_K2_K3: enrichment over a single layer | method | {\mathit{K1}}_{max} | {\mathit{K1}}_{min} | {G}_{max} | {G}_{min} | Deviation {G}_{max} / ref. | Deviation {G}_{min} / ref. | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 1.65E+06 | 1.43E+06 | 12.4 | 9.34 | 6,99% | –19,41% | | 2 | 1.52E+06 | 1.44E+06 | 10.5 | 9.45 | –9,41% | –18,46% | | 3 | 1.47E+06 | 9.81 | –15,35% | | | | POST_K1_K2_K3: enrichment over four layers | method | {\mathit{K1}}_{max} | {\mathit{K1}}_{min} | {G}_{max} | {G}_{min} | Deviation {G}_{max} / ref. | Deviation {G}_{min} / ref. | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 1.58E+06 | 1.58E+06 | 11.3 | 11.3 | –2,51% | –2,51% | | 2 | 1.55E+06 | 1.47E+06 | 10.9 | 9.88 | –5,95% | –14,65% | | 3 | 1.51E+06 | 10.4 | –10,26% | | | | On this test, one observes that it is indispensable to enrich over several layers of elements around the crack front in order to have satisfactory results. Note that the mesh used here is linear and relatively coarse: with a finer mesh, the results are significantly improved. A convergence study on a similar case is presented in . With an enrichment over four layers, method 1 is the one which leads to the most accurate results. The maximum curvilinear abscissa corresponds, in both cases, to the distance of about four elements. The theta method is for its part less sensitive here to the enrichment parameter. 4 Conclusion The results obtained with the method of extrapolation of the displacement are on the whole satisfactory, with less than 5% error on G , especially if the elements of the crack front are of Barsoum type (meshed crack case) or if several layers of elements are enriched around the front (non-meshed crack case, X-FEM method). In both cases, it is a matter of capturing as well as possible the asymptotic behaviour of the displacement. One must indeed note that the asymptotic expression of the displacements is valid only for r tending to 0. This is why one must take care not to choose too large an extrapolation domain (distance \mathit{dmax} of the operator POST_K1_K2_K3 of the order of 4 to 5 elements). On the tests presented for a meshed crack, method 1 gives the most accurate and the most stable results, whether in 2D or in 3D, if there are Barsoum elements. If the mesh does not contain Barsoum elements, one then advises using the results of method 3. For a non-meshed crack, method 1 also seems the most accurate. On a study for which one does not know a reference solution, it is possible to estimate a posteriori the quality of the computation. Indeed, POST_K1_K2_K3 systematically supplies, for the first two methods, the maximum values and the minimum values (over the set of the points computed) of the stress intensity factors, as well as the value of G recomputed by the Irwin formula. Method 3 for its part supplies only a single value for each stress intensity factor. This method is a weighted average of the stress intensity factors extrapolated at each node. A result may be considered satisfactory if the 5 values thus supplied (min and max of methods 1 and 2, and method 3) are close. One can also recommend comparing the results obtained with this method with those coming from the computation of the energy release rate and of the singular functions (operator CALC_G). 5 Bibliography H.D.BUI: “Brittle fracture mechanics” - Masson, 1978. 1. LEMAITRE, J.L.CHABOCHE: “Mechanics of solid materials” - Dunod, 1996. J.B. LEBLOND: "Mechanics of brittle and ductile fracture"– Lavoisier, 2003. R.S. BARSOUM: "Triangular quarter-point elements as elastic and perfectly-plastic crack tip elements"– Int. J. for Numerical Methods in Engineerig, vol. 11, 85-98, 1977. 1. GENIAUT: “Convergence in fracture mechanics: validation of the classical and enriched finite elements in Code_Aster” – EDF R&D note H-T64-2008-0047, 2008. 6 Description of the versions of the document | Index | Version Aster | Author(s) or contributor(s), organisation | Description of the modifications | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | A | 5 | J.M.Proix EDF/R&D/AMA | Initial text | | B | 7.4 | E.Galenne EDF/R&D/AMA | Least-squares method 7.2.24 | | C | 9.4 | E.Galenne EDF/R&D/AMA | Non-plane crack with XFEM 9.2.8 |

r7.02.14 Elements with an internal discontinuity, behaviour CZM_EXP and piloting of the loading r7.02.14 Elements with an internal discontinuity, behaviour CZM_EXP and piloting of the loading Summary: Relying on an energetic formulation set out briefly in a first part, we present a numerical model of initiation and propagation of two-dimensional cracks. It relies on a finite element with an internal displacement discontinuity, close to the models known under the name “embedded discontinuity finite element” in the literature. For this model, we detail the constitutive law used (of exponential Cohesive Zone Model type: keyword CZM_EXP), the properties of the finite element as well as the solution of the problem of minimisation of the total energy. We furthermore describe a technique of load piloting making it possible to follow possible instabilities in the global response of the structure. These elements can be used in 2D plane or axisymmetric conditions only with the modellings PLAN_ELDI and AXIS_ELDI. They are validated on the test cases ssnp128a (documentation V6.03.128) and ssna115a (documentation V6.01.115). Notes : - This documentation is largely inspired by the thesis work [6]. The interested reader may refer to it in order to have a more complete view of the link of this model with the energetic approach “Francfort Marigo” as well as a certain number of numerical results. - The choice of grouping in the same documentation the presentation of a finite element and of a constitutive law is linked to the specificity of the model and is intended to facilitate its understanding. > Contents 1 Energetic model of cracking The aim of this part is to set the general framework of a model of crack initiation and propagation based on a principle of energy minimisation. This model is inspired by those developed in the literature under the name “Cohesive Force Models” in the sense that one seeks to take into account a residual interaction between the faces of the crack. However, the major difference of our approach is the formulation of the problem in a broader framework by adopting the energetic point of view introduced by Francfort and Marigo [3]. The idea consists in taking into account the process of energy dissipation in the course of cracking by means of an energy defined on a surface of discontinuity and depending on the displacement jump across the latter. We shall content ourselves here with posing the problem in its form continuous in space and semi-discretised in time without seeking to make explicit the form of the surface energy. The following part will be devoted to the implementation of the finite element with an internal discontinuity relying on this energetic formulation. 1.1 General principle One considers an elastic structure defined by the domain \Omega and a crack defined as a surface of discontinuity denoted \Gamma across which the displacement u admits a jump \delta : \delta ={〚u〛}_{\Gamma} One defines the total energy {E}_{T} of this structure as the sum of its elastic energy denoted \Phi , of a surface energy denoted \Psi and of {W}^{	ext{ext}} the potential of the external forces: {E}_{T}=\Phi +\Psi -{W}^{	ext{ext}} One writes \Phi ={\int}_{\Omega}\phi .d\Omega and \Psi ={\int}_{\Gamma}\psi .d\Gamma , where \phi and \psi denote respectively the elastic energy density and the surface energy density. The total energy is a function of the displacement u and of the displacement jump \delta ; the minimisation problem is written: 	ext{Chercher}({u}^{	ext{ }},{\delta}^{	ext{ }})	ext{minimum local de l'énergie totale}{E}_{T}(u,\delta ) Let us recall that one carries out a search for a local minimum because the total energy is not bounded from below in the presence of external forces. Let us now see what hypotheses one can formulate on the surface energy in order to take into account the condition of non-interpenetration of the crack faces and according to whether one considers cracking as a reversible process or not. 1.2 Reversible model Let us present a first simple model, but not very realistic from a physical point of view at the macroscopic scale, in which one assumes that the cracking of a material is a reversible process. One considers that the total reclosing of the faces of a crack makes it possible to recover a sound material. The surface energy can be written as the sum of a function of the Euclidean norm of the displacement jump and of an indicator function accounting for the condition of non-interpenetration of the faces: \Psi (\delta )={\Psi}_{\mathrm{rev}}(\parallel \delta \parallel )+{I}_{{ℝ}^{+}}({\delta}_{n}) with {\delta}_{n}=\delta \cdot n where n is the normal to the discontinuity and {I}_{{ℝ}^{+}}({\delta}_{n}) is the indicator function: {I}_{{ℝ}^{+}}({\delta}_{n})=\lbrace  egin{array}{cc}+\infty & 	ext{si}{\delta}_{n} 0\end{array} One can equivalently express this evolution law by means of a threshold function f defined by: f(\kappa ,\delta )=\mathrm{\parallel }\delta \mathrm{\parallel }-\kappa The evolution law then takes the form of a classical consistency condition: f\le 0,\dot{\kappa}\ge 0,\mathrm{f.}\kappa =0 One defines the surface energy \Psi as a function of the displacement jump and of the variable \kappa : \Psi (\delta ,\kappa )=H(\parallel \delta \parallel -\kappa ).{\Psi}_{\mathrm{dis}}(\parallel \delta \parallel )+\left[1-H(\parallel \delta \parallel -\kappa )ight].{\Psi}_{\mathrm{lin}}(\parallel \delta \parallel ,\kappa )+{I}_{{ℝ}^{+}}({\delta}_{n}) with H the Heaviside function defined by: H(x)=\lbrace  egin{array}{ccc}1& 	ext{si}& x\ge 0\ 0& 	ext{si}& x \kappa \end{array} This is the interface law which we shall adopt. 1.4 Time discretisation Let us now present the time discretisation. Let us first specify that only quasi-static evolutions are envisaged; time is therefore parametrised by loading increments. By carrying out a semi-discretisation in time, one defines the surface energy \Psi at a time instant i as a function of the displacement jump and of {\kappa}^{i-1} , the internal variable at the time instant i-1 : \Psi (\delta ,{\kappa}^{i-1})=H(\parallel \delta \parallel -{\kappa}^{i-1}).{\Psi}_{\mathrm{dis}}(\parallel \delta \parallel )+\left[1-H(\parallel \delta \parallel -{\kappa}^{i-1})ight].{\Psi}_{\mathrm{lin}}(\parallel \delta \parallel ,{\kappa}^{i-1})+{I}_{{ℝ}^{+}}({\delta}_{n}) The minimisation problem is then written at the time instant : \underset{u,\delta }{\min}{E}_{T}(u,\delta ;{\kappa}^{i-1}) The internal variable at the time instant is updated once the jump at the time instant is known: {\kappa}^{i}=\max(\parallel \delta \parallel ,{\kappa}^{i-1}) 2 Model with an internal discontinuity This model makes it possible to take into account the initiation and the propagation of cracks in a structure for a given direction. It is based on particular finite elements which one calls elements with an internal discontinuity (from the English embedded discontinuity finite elements). The main idea rests on the introduction of a discontinuity included in the element and of an internal threshold variable governing the dissipative process as well as the irreversible character of cracking. Moreover, the displacement jump will be considered constant per element, which will facilitate the numerical solution. One will be able to adopt a static-condensation technique in which the computation of the jump during the minimisation will be done at the element level. The discontinuity element has been developed in order to do without the regularisation of the energy at zero (penalised adhesion) of the law CZM_EXP_REG developed for the joint element (see documentation R3.06.09 for the element and R7.02.11 for the law). Indeed, the latter is detrimental for models based on a principle of minimum energy since it leads to cancelling the derivative of the surface energy density at zero, that is the critical stress of the model. In such a situation, the initiation of a crack occurs as soon as the loading is applied, however small it may be (let us note however that this does not prevent one from obtaining interesting results with such a law, as soon as one fixes the cracking path). In order to take the discontinuity into account one carries out an enrichment of the displacements and of the strains which makes it possible to ensure their compatibility. In this part one will first present the constitutive law which governs the discontinuity element. One will be led to define a stress vector in the element which will be used during the minimisation of the energy. One will then detail the properties of the finite element and then the numerical solution of the energy-minimisation problem. 2.1 Constitutive law of the discontinuity element: CZM_EXP The discontinuity elements have a mixed behaviour: elastic and dissipative. The total energy of the element is written as the sum of an elastic energy and of a surface energy of CZM type. The following parts will be devoted to the presentation of the latter as well as of the stress which derives from it. 2.1.1 Total energy in the discontinuity element One chooses the total energy {E}_{T} as the sum of an elastic energy defined on the element denoted {\Omega}_{e} and of a surface energy defined on the discontinuity of the element denoted {\Gamma}_{e} : {E}_{T}(U,\delta ;\kappa )=\Phi (U,\delta )+\Psi (\delta ,\kappa ) where \Phi corresponds to the elastic energy. Let us note that the latter depends on the nodal displacement U and on the displacement jump constant (in the element), which one will see induces an additional strain. The surface energy \Psi depends on the displacement jump and on \kappa , an internal variable making it possible to treat the irreversibility of cracking. Note : The cracking path is known a priori ; the normal to the discontinuity will therefore be fixed by the orientation of the elements in the mesh (see figure ). 2.1.1.1 Elastic energy In order to define the elastic energy we shall need to introduce right away