r7.02.15 Modelling of cracks with hydro-mechanical coupling in a saturated porous medium r7.02.15 Modelling of cracks with hydro-mechanical coupling in a saturated porous medium Summary: A new joint element is proposed, making it possible to model in 2D discontinuities with hydromechanical coupling. This element is used to mesh a crack or a crack path in a mesh of classical volumetric THM elements in the case of a saturated porous material. It makes it possible to model the behaviour of a hydraulic joint or of a crack under fluid pressure. The constitutive equations of the mechanics and of the hydraulics in the crack are presented here, together with the corresponding constitutive laws. The discretisation adopted and the internal force vector and the tangent operator introduced in the operator STAT_NON_LINE are also detailed. 1 Introduction A model of a discontinuity in a saturated porous medium with hydro-mechanical coupling is presented here, which accounts for the following coupled phenomena: - the preferential flow of the interstitial liquid in the joint, which depends on the crack opening, - the fluid exchanges between the joint and the porous medium, - the deformation of the rock mass and the crack opening under the action of the crack pressure, - the propagation of the crack. Depending on the constitutive law chosen, this model makes it possible to model the propagation of the crack or simply the behaviour of a hydraulic joint that is already completely open. This joint element is based on the formulation of the joint elements already present in Code_Aster in classical mechanics. Hydraulic degrees of freedom are added in order to take account of the flow in the crack and of the fluid exchanges with the porous rock mass. Propagation is represented by a cohesive-zone model and one uses the regularised cohesive laws of the mechanical joint elements. In order to model the behaviour of completely open hydraulic joints, the Bandis constitutive law is introduced. The crack path is discretised by these joint elements, while the rest of the structure is meshed by volumetric THM elements already existing in Code_Aster . The hydraulic and mechanical conservation equations along the discontinuity, the corresponding constitutive laws, and the numerical integration of these equations are described here. This work falls within the framework of the GNR MoMas (programme TB5, Excavation Damage Zone). 2 Presentation of the problem 2.1 Hypotheses One considers a porous medium, denoted \Omega at the current time instant and whose boundary is denoted \partial \Omega . This volume is separated into two parts {\Omega}^{+} and {\Omega}^{-} by an interface \Gamma . All the quantities in {\Omega}^{+} (resp. {\Omega}^{-} ) are indexed by a + (resp. by a -). One restricts oneself to the study of a saturated porous medium in two dimensions. 2.2 Notation 2.2.1 Mechanical quantities The displacement field in the rock mass and the displacement discontinuity across the crack are denoted respectively u=( egin{array}{c}{u}_{x}\ {u}_{y}\end{array}) and 〚u〛=( egin{array}{c}〚{u}_{x}〛\ 〚{u}_{y}〛\end{array}) . The total stress and the effective stress are denoted respectively \sigma and \sigma ' in the rock mass and 	au and 	au ' on the interface. 2.2.2 Hydraulic quantities The pore pressure of the interstitial fluid is denoted {p}^{+} and {p}^{-} in the rock masses surrounding the crack. The fluid pressure in the crack is denoted p . The pressure gradient in the rock masses is denoted 
abla {p}^{+} or 
abla {p}^{-} . The longitudinal gradient of the crack pressure is denoted {
abla}_{l}p and is defined by {
abla}_{l}p=
abla p- rac{\partial p}{\partial n}n where n is the normal to the interface. The fluid mass supplies are denoted m in the rock mass (unit: {\mathit{M.L}}^{-3} ) . In the crack, they are denoted w (unit: {\mathit{M.L}}^{-2} ) and are integrated over the thickness \varepsilon of the crack: The hydraulic mass flux in the rock mass is denoted M (unit: {\mathit{M.T}}^{-1}.{L}^{-2} ) . In the crack, the hydraulic mass flow rate is denoted W (unit: {\mathit{M.T}}^{-1}.{L}^{-1} ). \varepsilon is the crack opening and is therefore related to the normal displacement jump by \varepsilon ={\varepsilon}_{0}+〚u〛.n Where {\varepsilon}_{0} is the initial thickness. 3 Continuous equations 3.1 Mechanics 3.1.1 Constitutive equations The formulation of the cohesive-zone models and their numerical integration are presented in the reference documentation R7.02.11. A very brief reminder is given here. One seeks the displacement field u at equilibrium by minimisation of the energy E(u)={\int}_{\Omega /\Gamma }\phi (\varepsilon (u))d\Omega -{W}^{	ext{ext}}(u)+{\int}_{\Gamma}\Psi (〚u〛)d\Gamma where \phi is the volumetric density of mechanical energy, {W}^{	ext{ext}} is the work of the external forces and \Psi is the surface energy density. The datum of \Psi characterises the mechanical behaviour of the interface. It is expressed by a cohesive law which relates the cohesive force 	au exerted on the crack faces to the displacement jump by: 	au = rac{\partial \Psi }{\partial 〚u〛} Furthermore, one makes the effective-stress hypothesis. In the rock mass, the total stress tensor is decomposed into: \sigma =\sigma '+{\sigma}_{p}I where \sigma ' denotes the effective stress and {\sigma}_{p} the hydraulic stress. On the discontinuity, the total stress vector is decomposed into: 	au =	au '+{	au}_{p}n where 	au ' denotes the effective stress. 3.1.2 Constitutive laws 3.1.2.1 Effective stresses In the rock mass, one makes the hypothesis of Biot effective stresses for saturated media, by introducing the hydraulic stress {\sigma}_{p} as a function of the pressure p (in this instance {p}^{+} or {p}^{-} in the rock mass) such that: {\sigma}_{p}=-bp where b is the Biot coefficient of the material. On the discontinuity, within the framework of a cohesive-zone model, one distinguishes three zones: - an open zone in which the total stress on the faces is equal to pn ; - a transition zone between the open medium and the sound medium. In this zone, one observes the appearance of microcracking and of plasticity. One thus makes the hypothesis of plastic incompressibility of the matrix, and it is the Terzaghi effective stress which controls the opening of the zone. The total stress is then written 	au =	au '-pn - a sound zone in which there is adhesion and non-interpenetration of the faces. So long as the value of the stress remains below the critical stress, the opening remains zero. One therefore makes the hypothesis, consistently with the behaviour of these three zones, that {	au}_{p}=-p 3.1.2.2 Cohesive laws The model presented here is compatible with the cohesive laws whose energy is regularised at zero: - CZM_LIN_REG, - CZM_EXP_REG. These laws make it possible to take into account: - the non-interpenetration of the crack faces by penalisation; - crack propagation by a softening law; - the irreversibility of the cracking. The material parameters of the interface are the critical stress at fracture {\sigma}_{c} and the fracture energy {G}_{c} . The numerical parameters are the energy regularisation parameter PENA_ADHERENCE and the interpenetration penalisation parameter PENA_CONTACT. 3.1.2.3 Bandis law In this case, the joint model does not take crack propagation into account. The joint separates two rock masses while being open all along its length, and is simply provided with a law which relates the opening to the effective stress. In the case of the empirical Bandis law, the relation between the normal effective stress 	au {'}_{n} and the normal crack closure U={U}_{\max