r7.02.18 Hydromechanical element coupled with XFEM r7.02.18 Hydromechanical element coupled with XFEM Summary: In this document we present the formulation of a new type of finite element. It is a hydromechanical element crossed by a discontinuity (an interface or a crack) whose design rests on the use of the extended finite-element method in the formulation of the discretised equations of the poro-mechanical model for a porous medium saturated with fluid. 1 Introduction The formulation of the coupled HM-XFEM model is based on the equations of the joint model R7.02.15 as well as on those of the THHM constitutive model R7.01.11 in the saturated case. Classically, the joint model is used to model in 2D the behaviour of a hydraulic joint or of a discontinuity in the presence of a fluid flow within it, thus generating a fluid pressure. The joint model makes it possible to take into account: - the preferential flow of the fluid in the discontinuity, conditioned by the opening of the latter, - the exchange of fluid between the porous medium and the discontinuity crossing it, - the propagation of the discontinuity, - the deformation of the porous medium induced by the fluid pressure. Within the framework of the classical finite-element method, the use of such a model presents a major drawback. Indeed, it is necessary to represent the discontinuity explicitly in the mesh and to make the lips of the latter coincide with the edges of the elements constituting the mesh. This implies that, in the course of its evolution, one must have recourse to elaborate projection algorithms for the updating of its geometry. This step can prove very costly in computation time for complex geometries. In order to free oneself from this constraint linked to the mesh, we envisage the introduction of a new hydromechanical (HM) element coupled with the extended finite-element method (XFEM) [1,2,3]. This method, based on the partition-of-unity principle [4], ensures greater flexibility for modellings involving more or less complex geometries. Indeed, with this method, the discontinuity is no longer represented physically in the mesh but symbolically, by enriching the approximated fields with supplementary degrees of freedom. For more details on the extended finite-element method in Code_Aster , the user may refer to the documentation R7.02.12 (mechanical case only). In the literature certain authors have already envisaged the coupling of the XFEM method with the coupled poro-mechanical HM model. This is the case of [5] in the case of the dynamic analysis of porous media in unsaturated conditions. The extension to the THM case for a saturated medium crossed by an impermeable interface is envisaged in [6]. Other authors such as [7] have proposed a model able to take into account the singularity of the pressure field at the tip of the discontinuity, by adapting the expression of the singular functions for the discontinuity tip. However, the models developed by these authors take into account neither the exchange phenomena which may exist between the surrounding medium (called the rock mass in the remainder of this document) and the discontinuity, nor the propagation of the latter within the porous medium. This point will be taken into account in the model developed in the present documentation by the introduction of the regularised cohesive laws into the weak formulation of the mechanical equilibrium equation (see § 3.1.2 ). From a practical point of view, the principal difficulty in the formulation of the HM-XFEM element is the construction of the various approximation spaces of the mechanical and hydraulic quantities. Respect of the LBB stability condition is essential in order to obtain a unique and convergent solution [8,9]. The violation of this condition (that is, not choosing the right degree of interpolation of the various approximated fields, see § 5.2.1 ) leads to oscillations of the solution. The coexistence of the HM-XFEM elements and of the classical HM elements (those to which the documentations R7.01.11 and R7.01.10 refer) is also a delicate point, in particular as regards the distribution of the degrees of freedom at the various nodes (vertices or mid-sides) of each type of element (see § 5.2.1 ). In the remainder of this documentation we shall recall the framework of study of the problem, then, in a second stage, the fundamental equations of the poro-mechanical model involved in the formulation of the HM-XFEM model. Finally we shall proceed to the discretisation of the variational forms of the equilibrium equations, both in time (by means of a 	heta -scheme) and in space (by means of the XFEM method). The presentation of the HM-XFEM model and its validation have been the subject of a scientific publication [15]. 2 Presentation of the problem 2.1 Definition of the domain of study Let \Omega \subset {\mathrm{ℝ}}^{d} with d\in \lbrace 2,3brace be a domain crossed entirely by a crack or a permeable interface. Let n be the outward normal to the boundary \partial \Omega of the domain, and {n}_{c} that of the interface {\Gamma}_{c} . It is possible to decompose: - the boundary of the domain \Omega into \partial \Omega ={\Gamma}_{u}\cup {\Gamma}_{t}\cup {\Gamma}_{p}\cup {\Gamma}_{F} where the boundary conditions (of Dirichlet and Neumann type) are imposed for the hydraulics (on {\Gamma}_{p} and {\Gamma}_{F} ) and for the mechanics (on {\Gamma}_{u} and {\Gamma}_{t} ), - the interface into {\Gamma}_{c}={\Gamma}_{f}\cup {\Gamma}_{1}\cup {\Gamma}_{2} where {\Gamma}_{1} and {\Gamma}_{2} represent the lips of the discontinuity. One imposes flux conditions on {\Gamma}_{1} , {\Gamma}_{2} and {\Gamma}_{f} and cohesive surface forces on {\Gamma}_{1} and {\Gamma}_{2} . The gives a symbolic representation of the conditions imposed on the boundary of the domain. Figure 2.1-1: Imposition of the boundary conditions 2.2 Hypotheses and notation One considers a porous medium saturated with liquid (in general water). The associated coupling law in Code_Aster is therefore LIQU_SATU (for more details refer to the user's guide of the THM model U2.04.05). On the other hand there exists a longitudinal flow of fluid at the level of the interface. Assuming that the latter is permeable, the fluid exchanges take place between the rock mass (the part of \Omega which is not the interface) and the interface. They are represented on the . Figure 2.2-1: Orientation of the exchanges between the rock mass and the discontinuity {q}_{1} and {q}_{2} are the fluxes due to the exchanges between the rock mass and the interface, and are expressed in {\mathit{kg.m}}^{	ext{-2}}.{s}^{	ext{-1}} . These fluxes come from the interface and are directed respectively towards the upper and lower parts of the rock mass at the level of the lips {\Gamma}_{1} and {\Gamma}_{2} of the interface. They are directed from the discontinuity towards the rock mass. The pressure field at the level of the rock mass is denoted p and that at the level of the interface is denoted {p}_{f} (field induced by the fluid circulating at the level of the interface). The displacement field is denoted u and the displacement jump at the level of the interface is denoted ⟦u⟧ . Let {P}_{1} be a point of {\Gamma}_{1} and {P}_{2} a point of {\Gamma}_{2} and {n}_{c}={n}_{c}^{1} the outward normal to {\Gamma}_{1} and {n}_{c}^{2} the outward normal to {\Gamma}_{2} . The normal displacement jump (taken negative or zero in opening and positive in interpenetration of the lips) is therefore defined in the following way: ⟦u⟧.{n}_{c}=(u({P}_{1})-u({P}_{2})).{n}_{c}⩽0 On the , one indicates the conventions adopted for taking the displacement jump into account at the level of the interface. Figure 2.2-2: Definition of the displacement jump at the level of the discontinuity For the rock mass, as for the interface, the effective-stress hypothesis is taken into consideration. Thus: - the total stress in the rock mass is denoted \sigma , - the total stress (linked to the cohesive forces) at the level of the interface is denoted {t}_{c} . The small-perturbation hypothesis is adopted. On the other hand one considers that the mechanical and hydraulic quantities are isotropic. 3 Constitutive equations of the HM-XFEM model 3.1 Equations for the mechanics 3.1.1 Equilibrium equation (case of the rock mass) In the case of the rock mass the equation of conservation of momentum (taking the volume forces into account) can be put in the form: 	ext{Div}(\sigma )+r{F}^{m}=0 with: - \sigma ={\sigma}^{'}-\mathit{bp}1 (under the effective-stress hypothesis) where b is the Biot coefficient, - r the homogenised density such that in the saturated case r={r}_{0}+{m}_{w} where {r}_{0} is the homogenised density in the reference configuration and {m}_{w} the mass supplies of water, - r{F}^{m} represent the volume forces acting on \Omega (in practice these are the gravity forces). 3.1.2 Cohesive-zone model (case of the interface) In order to take into account the propagation of the discontinuity, the irreversibility of the fracturing and the non-interpenetration of the lips of the discontinuity, one chooses to model the behaviour of the interface or of the crack by means of a cohesive law. For more information concerning the establishment of these laws, the user may refer to the documentation R7.02.11. As far as we are concerned, at the level of the discontinuity, it is possible to distinguish 3 zones: - a completely open zone, at the level of which the total stress on the lips of the discontinuity is equal to {p}_{f}{n}_{c} on {\Gamma}_{2} and equal to -{p}_{f}{n}_{c} on {\Gamma}_{1} . In this zone the value of the stress is due principally to the fluid circulating there, - a cohesive zone (or Fracture Processing Zone (FPZ)) whose opening depends on the value of the total stress, which is then equal to {t}_{c}={t}_{c}^{'}-{p}_{f}n , n being the outward normal to the surface concerned {\Gamma}_{1} or {\Gamma}_{2} . Beyond a certain opening {w}_{c} , the stress corresponds to that of the preceding zone, - and a sound adherent zone at the level of which the lips of the discontinuity are in contact and do not interpenetrate. Figure 3.1.2-1: Distribution of the stresses at the level of the discontinuity On the one locates the various stress zones associated with the cohesive-zone model. The crack front is then naturally localised at the boundary between the cohesive zone and the adherent zone. The constitutive law used for the cohesive laws takes the form of a softening relation between the cohesive stress {t}_{c}' and the displacement jump ⟦u⟧ at the level of the lips of the discontinuity. Thus one sets: {t}_{c}'\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}} rac{\partial \psi }{\partial ⟦u⟧} with \psi the surface energy, whose expression depends on the cohesive law used. The cohesive law adopted for the hydromechanical XFEM elements is the law CZM_LIN_MIX detailed in R7.02.19. It is a non-regularised cohesive law (therefore with an infinite initial slope). {\sigma}_{c} is the critical stress from which the damage begins in the cohesive zone. {G}_{c}= rac{{\sigma}_{c}{w}_{c}}{2} corresponds to the quantity of energy to be supplied in order to break completely a unit of surface. Figure 3.1.2-2: Mixed cohesive law. 3.1.3 Boundary conditions for the mechanics The writing of the boundary conditions for the mechanics on the boundary of the domain \Omega and at the level of the interface is written: | Boundary conditions for the mechanics | | --- | | u=0 on {\Gamma}_{u} (imposed displacements) \sigma \cdot n=t on {\Gamma}_{t} (imposed surface forces) \sigma \cdot {n}_{c}^{1}={t}_{c}^{1} on {\Gamma}_{1} (imposed cohesive surface forces) \sigma \cdot {n}_{c}^{2}={t}_{c}^{2} on {\Gamma}_{2} (imposed cohesive surface forces) | 3.2 Equations for the hydraulics 3.2.1 Mass conservation equation (case of the rock mass) In the case of the rock mass the mass conservation equation is put in the form:  rac{\partial {m}_{w}}{\partial