We consider that every element of the mesh crossed by the interface is of HM-XFEM type. This element is surrounded by non-enriched HM elements. These are those used classically for the HM modellings. The Figure represents the HM-XFEM element with a QUAD8 mesh cell. | | | --- | Figure 5.2.1-1: Representation of the HM-XFEM element with QUAD8 On each of the figures presented previously, the lists at each node of the element contain the degrees of freedom (dofs) associated with each category of nodes (vertex nodes or mid-side nodes). The degrees of freedom: - a and b are associated respectively with the classical part and the enriched part of the approximation of the displacement field {u}^{h} , - c and d are associated respectively with the classical part and the enriched part of the approximation of the pressure field in the rock mass {p}^{h} , - {p}_{f} are associated with the approximation of the pressure field {p}_{f}^{h} at the level of the interface, - {q}_{1} and {q}_{2} are associated with the approximation of the hydraulic Lagrange-multiplier fields {q}_{1}^{h} and {q}_{2}^{h} . - \lambda , \mu and w are respectively associated with the approximation of the Lagrange multipliers {\lambda}^{h} and {\mu}^{h} and of the displacement jump {w}^{h} . The hydromechanical elements present in Code_Aster use a “mixed interpolation”, in order to reduce the oscillations of the numerical solution [14]. The displacement field is thus interpolated quadratically whereas the pore-pressure field is interpolated linearly. The degrees of freedom c and d are therefore carried only by the vertex nodes whereas the degrees of freedom a and b are carried both by the mid-side nodes and the vertex nodes. As regards the fields associated with the discontinuity: {p}_{f}^{h} , {q}_{1}^{h} , {q}_{2}^{h} , {\lambda}^{h} , {\mu}^{h} and {w}^{h} , the degrees of freedom are carried only by the vertex nodes. In addition, their approximation space is reduced (see § 5.2.2 ) in order to respect the LBB stability condition [8,9]. This condition indeed imposes a hierarchy between the approximation spaces, failing which one observes the appearance of oscillations and the non-uniqueness of the solution of the coupled problem. Furthermore it is important to note that the HM-XFEM elements which share vertex nodes with the classical HM elements must undergo an additional treatment. One must indeed set to zero the enriched degrees of freedom (for the pressure fields in the rock mass and the displacement fields) but also set to zero the degrees of freedom {p}_{f} , {q}_{1} , {q}_{2} , \lambda , \mu and w at the level of the vertex nodes in common between these two types of element. The elimination procedure used is that described in § 4.4 of the documentation R5.03.54. On the one summarises the problematic points associated with the coexistence between the HM-XFEM elements and the classical HM elements. Figure 5.2.1-1: Coexistence of the HM-XFEM element with the classical HM element 5.2.2 Reduced space for the discretisation of the fields associated with the discontinuity For a detailed description of the discretisation of the contact unknowns (and a fortiori of all the unknowns associated with the discontinuity), the reader may refer to the documentation R5.03.54, §5. In short, the initial components of the multiplier are defined on the vertex nodes K of the intersected parent elements (see fig.). The implementation of such contact elements is detailed in R5.03.54, §4. One then imposes equality relations between certain of these initial components in order to arrive at a smaller number {N}_{\lambda} of effectively independent degrees of freedom. These relations are carried by certain intersected edges V , called vital edges: a truly independent degree of freedom I is shared by a group of nodes of K (see fig.), which produces an extended contact shape function {\psi}_{I}:=\sum_{i\in I}{N}_{i} (see fig.). The algorithm for selecting such vital edges, and therefore for constructing the reduced space, is detailed in the documentation R5.03.54, §6. The multiplier field is then obtained by interpolation on the parent elements and the discrete multiplier is the trace of this field on the interface: {M}_{h}:=\left\lbrace \sum_{I}{\mu}_{I}{\psi}_{I}{	ext{|}}_{\Gamma},{\mu}_{I}\in {\mathrm{ℝ}}^{d}ightbrace Figure 5.2.2-1: Mesh non-conforming to the crack and reduced space for the interface In our case, the fields associated with the discontinuity are not only the Lagrange multipliers \lambda ,\mu et le saut de déplacement :math: w useful for the cohesive law CZM_LIN_MIX but also the hydraulic fields associated with the discontinuity: the fluid pressure in the discontinuity {p}_{f} , and the fluxes {q}_{1} and {q}_{2} . These fields are all discretised on the reduced space {M}_{h} . 5.2.3 Approximations of the fields with XFEM The approximation of the fields present can therefore be put in the form (the test fields used in the weak forms of the equilibrium equations are approximated in the same manner): - for the displacement field: {u}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nn}}{a}_{i}{\mathrm{\phi }}_{i}(x)\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}+\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}\sum_{j=1}^{\mathit{nne}}{b}_{j}{\mathrm{\phi }}_{j}(x)	ilde{H}(\mathit{lsn}(x)) - for the pressure field in the rock mass: {p}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nns}}{c}_{i}{\psi}_{i}(x)\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}+\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}\sum_{j=1}^{\mathit{nnse}}{d}_{j}{\psi}_{j}(x)	ilde{H}(\mathit{lsn}(x)) - for the pressure field in the interface: {p}_{f}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nnc}}{({p}_{f})}_{i}	ilde{{\psi}_{i}}(x) - for the hydraulic Lagrange-multiplier fields: {q}_{1}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nnc}}{({q}_{1})}_{i}	ilde{{\psi}_{i}}(x) {q}_{2}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nnc}}{({q}_{2})}_{i}	ilde{{\psi}_{i}}(x) - for the Lagrange-multiplier fields and the displacement jump useful for the cohesive-zone model: {\lambda}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nnc}}{\lambda}_{i}	ilde{{\psi}_{i}}(x) {\m