approximation of the displacement field is inappropriate here, but entirely conceivable as regards the enrichment of the approximation of the pressure field in the rock mass as suggested by [7]. 5.3 Extension to the multi-crack case 5.3.1 Representation of the multi-crack HM-XFEM element and associated degrees of freedom In Code_Aster , the XFEM elements can support up to 4 cracks per element (see R7.02.12). It is also possible to have connected cracks. These functionalities are in particular available for the HM-XFEM elements. At each additional crack, one adds a set of enriched degrees of freedom for the displacement and pore-pressure field as well as a set of degrees of freedom associated with the additional discontinuity. On the Figure , one represents an HM-XFEM QUAD8 element intersected by two interfaces (not connected on the left and connected on the right) as well as the degrees of freedom carried by each of the nodes. | | | | --- | --- | Figure 5.3.1-1: Representation of an HM-XFEM QUAD8 element intersected by two discontinuities that are not connected (on the left) and connected (on the right) 5.3.2 Approximations of the fields with XFEM in the multi-crack case In the case where several cracks intersect the same mesh cell, for each node, a correspondence is established between each interface and the enriched and interface degrees of freedom (see R7.02.12). Thus the approximation of the fields present can be put in the following form: - for the displacement field: {u}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nn}}{a}_{i}{\mathrm{\phi }}_{i}(x)\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}+\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}\sum_{\mathit{ifiss}=1}^{\mathit{nfiss}}\sum_{j=1}^{\mathit{nne}}{b}_{j}^{\alpha (\mathit{ifiss},j)}{\mathrm{\phi }}_{j}(x)	ilde{H}({\mathit{lsn}}_{\mathit{ifiss}}(x)) - for the pressure field in the rock mass: {p}^{h}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{\mathit{nns}}{c}_{i}{\psi}_{i}(x)\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}+\phantom{ule{0.5em}{0ex}}\sum_{\mathit{ifiss}}^{\mathit{nfiss}}\sum_{j=1}^{\mathit{nnse}}