r7.02.23 Interface constitutive law for dams CZM_FROT_MIX r7.02.23 Interface constitutive law for dams CZM_FROT_MIX Summary: This document describes the cohesive constitutive law CZM_FROT_MIX, modelling the opening and the slip of an interface according to Coulomb's friction law. CZM_FROT_MIX aims to cover the same phenomenology as JOINT_MECA_FROT for the modelling of the mechanical behaviour of dams, but relying on the interface finite element and a mixed augmented-Lagrangian formulation (see R3.06.13). 1 Introduction The modelling of the mechanical integrity of dams has relied for nearly twenty years on the development of cohesive constitutive laws. These laws relate the displacement jump across a potential discontinuity surface, defined beforehand, to the associated stress vector (otherwise called cohesive forces). The laws currently available — JOINT_MECA_RUPT, JOINT_MECA_FROT and JOINT_MECA_ENDO — thus aim to represent different mechanisms of failure and/or of slip at the principal joints of the dam: concrete–rock interface, concreting joints, or monolith joints R7.01.25. Historically, these laws rest on the joint finite elements (modellings _JOINT), which rely on a direct formulation in displacement, simple to implement R3.06.09. Nevertheless, it has been observed on several occasions that this direct formulation may lack robustness, leading to convergence difficulties, in particular when very stiff penalisation parameters are used or in the presence of geometric singularities in the vicinity of the joints. In parallel, the interface finite elements have also been developed (modellings _INTERFACE). These rest on a mixed formulation , whose degrees of freedom are both the displacements and the cohesive forces (at spatial convergence) R3.06.13. This mixed formulation, on which a wide set of models dedicated to the modelling of failure has been built, proves particularly well suited to modelling with exactness the adherence conditions prior to cracking R7.02.11. The following table presents, in addition, a brief comparison of the two types of finite element according to several criteria. | Comparison criterion | Joint element | Interface element | | --- | --- | --- | | Formulation | Primal (penalised) | Mixed | | Degrees of freedom | Displacements | Displacements and cohesive forces | | Modelling of the adherence | Approximate | Exact | | Hydromechanical extension | Yes (existing) | No (to be developed) | | Robustness / Performance | Poor conditioning, difficulty of convergence, oscillations of the stresses | LBB condition satisfied (with quadratic elements), stabilisation possible in the case of softening | | Sensitivity | Solutions dependent on the penalisation parameters | Solutions convergent with mesh refinement | In order to increase the robustness (and the performance) of the cohesive laws used for the modelling of the mechanical behaviour of dams, the aim is now to make available the phenomenological elements currently covered by JOINT_MECA_RUPT, JOINT_MECA_FROT and JOINT_MECA_ENDO by means of equivalent laws relying on the mixed formulation of the interface elements. In this direction, this document presents in a first stage an equivalent of JOINT_MECA_FROT, named CZM_FROT_MIX. 2 Phenomenology and use 2.1 Expected behaviours of CZM_FROT_MIX The cohesive law CZM_FROT_MIX, as indicated in the introduction, aims to cover the same phenomenology as JOINT_MECA_FROT: - In mode I (tension), the evolution of the normal component ⟦u_n⟧ of the displacement jump at the interface is always reversible. The normal component T_n of the stress vector evolves in proportion to ⟦u_n⟧ , with a normal stiffness K_n , until the maximum tensile strength threshold is reached, equal to c/\mu , where c denotes the cohesion and \mu the friction coefficient. - In mode II (shear), the evolution of the tangential component ⟦u_t⟧ of the displacement jump at the interface is reversible so long as the tangential component T_t of the stress vector remains within the open interval (-c+\mu T_n; c-\mu T_n) . In this domain, T_t evolves linearly with ⟦u_t⟧ , according to a tangential stiffness K_t . For T_t = \pm (c-\mu T_n) , the evolution becomes irreversible when the loading continues, which leads to a plastic slip \dot{\delta}^p . The following figure presents these two loading modes. In the plane (T_n, T_t) , they lie in the reversibility domain defined by the Coulomb cone f(\vector{T})=
ormEucl{\vector{T}_t}+\mu T_n-c\leq 0 . Behaviour of CZM_FROT_MIX: (a) response in mode I, (b) response in mode II, (c) loading paths and reversibility domain in 2D. Note Unlike JOINT_MECA_FROT, treating the case of infinitely large stiffnesses K_n and K_t (perfect adherence) is made possible with CZM_FROT_MIX because of the mixed formulation on which the law relies. The associated responses in modes I and II are represented by dashed lines on the preceding figure, respectively in (a) and (b). 2.2 Parameters of the model The parameters of the model are six in number: | Name | Symbol | Interval | Status | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | RIGI_NOR | K_n | 	ensFour{R}_+^ | Optional | | RIGI_TAN | K_t | 	ensFour{R}_+^ | Optional | | COHESION | c | 	ensFour{R}_+ | Mandatory | | COEF_FROT | \mu | 	ensFour{R}_+ | Mandatory | | PENA_LAGR_ABSO | r | 	ensFour{R}_+^ | Mandatory | | ADHE | -- | {ELAS, PARFAITE} | Mandatory | | RIGI_REGU_VISC | k | 	ensFour{R}_+ | Optional | | TAU_REGU_VISC | 	au | 	ensFour{R}_+ | Optional | The values of K_n and K_t must be supplied when the adherence is linear elastic (ADHE = ELAS). On the other hand, when the adherence is perfect (ADHE = PARFAITE), neither K_n nor K_t enters into the solution of the equations of CZM_FROT_MIX. r denotes the augmentation coefficient of the augmented Lagrangian of the mixed formulation R3.06.13. Its value is strictly positive, and its effect disappears with mesh refinement. Although a precise estimate of r for all analysis configurations is delicate, it may be proposed to take r\sim 10-1000	imes(K_n+K_t) in the case of elastic adherence, and r\sim K_{mesh} in the case of perfect adherence, where K_{mesh} denotes an “average” stiffness of a mesh cell adjacent to the interface. Note The role of the parameters RIGI_REGU_VISC TAU_REGU_VISC is set out in section r7.02.23-regularisation_visqueuse. 2.3 Internal variables The internal variables stored are the following eleven: | Name | Number | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | | SAUT_PLAS_1 | V1 | First component of the plastic slip | | SAUT_PLAS_2 | V2 | Second component of the plastic slip (V2 is set to zero in 2D) | | SAUT_N | V3 | Opening | | SAUT_T1 | V4 | First component of the slip | | SAUT_T2 | V5 | Second component of the slip (V5 is set to zero in 2D) | | INDIPLAS | V6 | Plastic slip indicator (0 if reversible regime, 1 if plastic regime) | | INDIOUV | V7 | Opening indicator (0 if \delta_n\leq 0 , 1 otherwise) | | SAUT_VISC_N | V8 | Opening of the dashpot of the Maxwell chain | | SAUT_VISC_T1 | V9 | First component of the slip of the dashpot of the Maxwell chain | | SAUT_VISC_T2 | V10 | Second component of the slip of the dashpot of the Maxwell chain (V10 is set to zero in 2D) | | SIGMVISC | V11 | Distance between the current stress vector and that in the absence of viscous regularisation | Note Only V1 and V2 (and V8, V9 and V10 if viscous regularisation) are “true” internal variables, in the sense that they are necessary for the solution of the discretised constitutive equations of CZM_FROT_MIX. The other variables are intended solely for the post-processing of the results. 3 Equations of the model This paragraph presents the constitutive equations of CZM_FROT_MIX. In order to make them easier to read, they are first expressed in their direct formulation, before being introduced in their mixed formulation. To lighten the presentation further, the equations are given only in the case of a linear elastic adherence, for which the stiffnesses K_n and K_t appear explicitly. The case of a perfect adherence is then obtained naturally by considering the limit K_n, K_t ightarrow +\infty in the mixed formulation (which the reader is invited to check). 3.1 Primal formulation Let ⟦\vector{u}⟧ be the displacement jump seen by the volume , and \vector{\delta}^p the plastic slip. The relation between the stress vector \vector{T} and the displacement jump is written \vector{T} = \min\left\{K_n ⟦u_n⟧,  rac{c}{\mu}ight\}\vector{n} + K_t\left(