ession tests for which the endurance limit is {d}_{0}({d}_{0} {	au}_{0}	ext{dommage}\end{array}ightbrace 	ext{pour une courbe de Wöhler définie en cisaillement,}\ \left\lbrace  egin{array}{c}{\sigma}^{\ast }\le {d}_{0}	ext{pas}	ext{de}	ext{dommage}\ {\sigma}^{\ast }>{d}_{0}	ext{dommage}\end{array}ightbrace 	ext{pour une courbe de Wöhler définie en traction-compression}.\end{array} It therefore seems to us possible to take:  egin{array}{}{\sigma}^{\ast }={R}_{	ext{crit}}+{	au}_{0}	ext{pour une courbe de Wöhler en cisaillement}(	ext{ce qui est assez rare}),\ {\sigma}^{\ast }=({R}_{	ext{crit}}+{	au}_{0})\ast ({d}_{0}/{	au}_{0})	ext{pour une courbe de Wöhler en traction-compression}.\end{array} In a general way, the user can take {\sigma}^{\ast }=({R}_{	ext{crit}}+{	au}_{0})\ast 	ext{corr} where 	ext{corr} is a correction coefficient introduced by the user. By default, this coefficient \mathrm{corr} is taken equal to ({d}_{0}/{	au}_{0}) (case of the Wöhler curve introduced in tension-compression). Note : In the literature, one does not find a presentation of an approach for using a criterion in order to compute a damage value. One knows nevertheless that certain industrial users employ such an approach, but without knowing the form adopted. The approach implemented in Code_Aster is proposed by the AMA department. 5 Conclusion In this note are set out the various methods of damage computation available either in the operator POST_FATIGUE or in the operator CALC_FATIGUE, or in both commands simultaneously. One can classify these methods into two large classes: - estimation of the damage at large numbers of cycles, - estimation of the damage in plastic low-cycle fatigue. In the first class of problems, one finds the Wöhler method, based on uniaxial tests, and which applies to stress loadings. One also finds in this class the Crossland criterion, which is an empirical criterion relying on macroscopic quantities, and the Dang Van Papadopoulos criterion, which is based on microscopic phenomena. The two criteria are addressed to stress loadings which may be uniaxial or multiaxial but periodic. In the second class of problems, one finds the Manson-Coffin method and the Taheri methods, which apply to strain loadings. All of the methods based on uniaxial tests (Wöhler method, Manson-Coffin method and Taheri methods) are available in both operators POST_FATIGUE and CALC_FATIGUE. The criteria, for their part, are available only in POST_FATIGUE. 6 Bibliography 1. BATHIAS, J.P. BAILON: Fatigue of materials and of structures. Collection Université de Compiègne - PUM les Presses de l'Université de Montréal MALOINE S.A. Publisher Paris. 1. FOURNIER: Fatigue algorithm: comparison of different methods of counting stress cycles - Note HP/169/88/44. 3. Fatigue under variable-amplitude loadings: Rainflow method of cycle counting. AFNOR standard A 03-406 November 1993. 4. RCC_M. Edition January 1983. 1. VATIN: Specification of version 2 of the software POSTDAM - NoteHP/14/94/017/A. 1. WAECKEL: Estimation of high-cycle fatigue - NoteHP/62/94/128/A. 1. LEMAITRE: Unified formulation of damage-evolution laws. CR Académie des Sciences, Paris, T.305, series II, 1987. 1. BALLARD, DANG VAN KY, H. MAITOURNAM: Fatigue computation of metallic parts. Course notes Collège Polytechnique (5, 6 and 7 February 1996). 1. LORENTZ: Implementation of the fatigue criteria. Creation of a post-processor for Systus (GDF). 1. Papadopoulos: High-cycle fatigue of metals. A new approach. Thesis of Ioannis V. PAPADOPOULOS 1987. 1. DANG VAN, B. GRIVEAU, O. MENAGE: On a new Multiaxial Fatigue Limit Criterior theory and applications. Mechanical Engineering Publications, London 1989. 1. TAHERI: A low cycle fatigue dammage cumulation rule for non proportional loading. Note HI-74/94/082/0. 1. TAHERI: Bibliography on high-cycle multiaxial fatigue, HI-74/94/086/0. 14. Ph. SERMAGE: Multiaxial thermal fatigue at variable temperature, doctoral thesis ENS-Cachan, Dec. 1998. 1. PETRY, G. LIN