u2.06.42 Implementation of a modal updating procedure u2.06.42 Implementation of a modal updating procedure Summary: In this document, modal updating techniques are presented. Modal updating consists in exploiting the eigenmodes of the structure in order to obtain a numerical model which reflects as well as possible the dynamic behaviour of the structure under study. 1 Introduction Modal updating consists in adjusting the parameters of the numerical model from the knowledge of the eigenmodes identified experimentally on the real structure. Using optimisation techniques, one tries to find the numerical model for which the associated eigenmodes are close to the eigenmodes identified experimentally. Three modal updating techniques are presented in this document. The first two are dedicated to the conservative system and the third is dedicated to the dissipative system. 2 Modal updating of a conservative system Two modal updating techniques are presented. The first exploits the sensitivity of the eigenmodes and the second exploits the diagonality of the generalised matrices. 2.1 Exploitation of the discrepancy between modal shapes and the discrepancy between natural frequencies One tries to find the parameters of the numerical model such that the computed modal shape restricted to the observation points is collinear with the modal shape obtained experimentally and such that the associated natural frequency is equal to the natural frequency identified experimentally. To this end, one computes the normalised scalar product between the measured eigenmode shape and the computed eigenmode shape restricted to the degrees of freedom associated with the measurement points. This computation corresponds to the computation of the MAC (Modal Assurance Criterion). If one denotes respectively by ({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}},{f}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}}) and ({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}},{f}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}}) the computed eigenmodes and the identified eigenmodes, one has: > \mathit{MAC}({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}},{y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}})= rac{{({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}}^{T}{y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}})}^{2}}{({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}}^{T}{y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}})({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}}^{T}{y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}})} One defines the vectors V and {F}_{r} such that: > V=\left( egin{array}{c}⋮\ \mathit{MAC}({y}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}},{y}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}})-1\ ⋮\end{array}ight) > {F}_{r}=\left( egin{array}{c}⋮\ {f}_{{i}_{\mathit{num}}}-{f}_{{i}_{\mathit{mes}}}\ ⋮\end{array}ight) The functional to be minimised is formulated as follows: > ϵ={V}^{T}{W}_{\mathit{MAC}}V+{F}_{r}^{T}{W}_{\mathit{freq}}{F}_{r} One must of course evaluate the discrepancy between two analogous modes. Thus, this technique is not suited to a structure in which the modal density is high. This procedure is used by MACR_RECAL (option DYNAMIQUE) in the test case sdls121a V2.03.121. 2.2 Exploitation of the orthogonality of the measured eigenmodes From the modal shapes recorded at the observation points, one carries out an expansion onto the support numerical model. One then tries to recover the parameters of the numerical model so that the generalised mass and stiffness matrices relative to the modes identified experimentally are diagonal and so that the discrepancy between the measured natural circular frequency and the computed natural circular frequency is minimal. One estimates the natural circular frequency (or more exactly the square of the circular frequency) by computing the ratio between the generalised stiffness and the generalised mass of the identified mode. This technique requires neither a pairing between the experimental mode and the numerical mode, nor a modal analysis. It is therefore suited to structures in which the modal density is high. It nevertheless requires the expansion of the identified modes onto the numerical model. The expansion of the i-th identified mode onto the numerical model can be done in the following manner: - One chooses an expansion basis composed of modal shapes computed with the support numerical model: > Y=[{y}_{1}\dots {y}_{n}] - One then computes the coordinates {\eta}_{i} of the identified modal shape {{\Phi}_{i}}^{\mathit{mes}} on the basis Y , restricted to the observation points. These coordinates can be obtained by a least-squares type minimisation. > \epsilon ={\left({{\Phi}_{i}}^{\mathit{mes}}-Y{\eta}_{i}ight)}^{T}{W}_{i}\left({{\Phi}_{i}}^{\mathit{mes}}-Y{\eta}_{i}ight) - One then carries out an expansion of the identified shape onto the degrees of freedom of the numerical model: {\Phi}_{i}=Y{\eta}_{i} The following step consists in computing the normalised generalised matrices: > {\mathit{MAC}}_{W}(i,j)= rac{{({{\Phi}_{i}}^{T}W{\Phi}_{j})}^{2}}{({{\Phi}_{i}}^{T}W{\Phi}_{i})({{\Phi}_{j}}^{T}W{\Phi}_{j})} If the weighting matrix W is equal to the mass matrix M or to the stiffness matrix K , {\mathit{MAC}}_{W} becomes a diagonal matrix. It is then a matter of finding the terms of the matrices K and M which minimise at the same time: > {\mathit{MAC}}_{K}(i,j) for i
