he drawback is that one is obliged to carry out a pairing between the measured mode and the analogous computed mode at each iteration of the updating procedure. This technique is therefore not appropriate when the modal density is high. The advantage of the second category lies in the fact that one carries out neither a modal analysis on the numerical model in the course of the analysis iterations nor a pairing between the computed mode and the measured mode. The drawback is that one is obliged to carry out an expansion of the measurement onto the numerical model. This technique is therefore not appropriate when the number of measurement points is small. A second updating iteration after updating of the support numerical model may be necessary in order to refine the results. Indeed, updating the support model makes it possible to improve the quality of the modal expansion. Overall, one can draw the following conclusion: - If the modal density is high, exploitation of the properties of the modal matrices generally gives better results. This nevertheless requires a good distribution of the observation points in order to be able to carry out a better expansion of the modal shape. - If the modes of the structure are isolated modes, and if one is limited in the number of observation points, then the sensitivity-type technique is much more appropriate for the adjustment of the initial numerical model. 5 Bibliography 1. U4.73.02: Macro-command MACR_RECAL 2. U4.52.15: Operator MAC_MODES 1. R4.03.06: Updating algorithm 2. H-T61-2008-04006-FR, I. NISTOR, Updating in dynamics with code_aster: theoretical synthesis and development specifications 3. V2.03.121: SDLS121: Updating of the parameters of a dynamic model by modal analysis

u2.08.08 Use of the Method of Manufactured Solutions for software verification u2.08.08 Use of the Method of Manufactured Solutions for software verification Summary: This document presents the use of the Method of Manufactured Solutions for the software validation of Code_Aster . The principle of this method is detailed on a simple example of thermal diffusion. This example allows us to specify the interest of this approach and, in particular, to present the validation procedure which can be implemented. Finally, the software implementation of the Method of Manufactured Solutions, based on the use of symbolic computation, is detailed. 1 Introduction The verification of a scientific software consists in making sure of the absence of error in the programming of the modelling implemented. A classical and very reliable approach consists in comparing the result of the software with that of an analytical solution. Unfortunately, for complex and/or non-linear problems, analytical solutions become very difficult to obtain. It is in this context that the Method of Manufactured Solutions is particularly interesting. The Method of Manufactured Solutions is a method for the verification of scientific software. It is a systematic method for obtaining analytical solutions for problems which may be very complex and non-linear. Its principle is simple: one gives oneself explicitly the solution sought in the form of an analytical expression and, from this solution, one constructs the data (constraints, loadings) necessary to obtain this solution. It is interesting to note that this method is very commonly used in fluid mechanics. Thus the “American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics” (AIAA) included it in its software quality-assurance standards in 1998 [bib1]. This practice is much rarer in solid mechanics; thus the “American Society of Mechanical Engineers” (ASME) integrated it into its standard only in 2006 [bib2], that is to say much more recently. This is not linked to any particular difficulty, extensive studies in solid mechanics having been carried out thanks to this method [bib3]. In the remainder of this document, we shall set out the principle of this method on a simple example, then the applicable validation procedure. We shall then detail the software implementation in Code_Aster . 2 General principle and use for verification 2.1 The Method of Manufactured Solutions in thermal analysis The principle of this method is very simple; we shall illustrate it on an example of linear 2D thermal analysis. First of all, we recall the equations of a linear thermal problem. In the domain \Omega , one seeks the temperature field T(x,y) such that: \lbrace  egin{array}{c}-\lambda \Delta T=s	ext{dans}\Omega \ T={T}_{d}	ext{sur}\partial {\Omega}_{d}\ \lambda \overrightarrow{
abla}T\cdot \overrightarrow{n}={q}_{n}	ext{sur}\partial {\Omega}_{n}\end{array} where {T}_{d} and {q}_{n} are respectively the temperature and the flux imposed on parts of the boundary. The steps of the Method of Manufactured Solutions are the following: - One chooses an arbitrary 2D study domain \Omega comprising the boundaries \partial {\Omega}_{d} with Dirichlet conditions and \partial {\Omega}_{n} with Neumann conditions. By “choose”, one means “choose the shape of the domain”. Thus we choose the shape of the figure . - One chooses an arbitrary solution, say - By substituting the previous expression back into , one deduces the expressions: - of the source term; s(x,y)=-\lambda \Delta T=-\lambda (6x+6y) - of the Dirichlet term; {T}_{d}(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3} - of the Neumann term: {q}_{d}(x,y)=\lambda \overrightarrow{
abla}T(x,y)\cdot \overrightarrow{n} If one wants to impose this condition on the edge \partial {\Omega}_{n} , then one knows the expression of the normal \overrightarrow{n} , namely \overrightarrow{n}={[1,0]}^{T} . One therefore obtains: {q}_{d}(x,y)=\lambda \overrightarrow{
abla}T(x,y)\cdot \overrightarrow{n}=3\lambda {x}^{2} At this stage, one has the information necessary for the modelling of the problem studied. It is nevertheless essential to make the following remark: in order to obtain the solution sought, one must indeed assign boundary conditions on the whole boundary \partial \Omega of the study domain. At one's choice, following a partition of the boundary, this may be a mixture of Dirichlet and Neumann conditions. The obligation to impose conditions on the whole boundary is due in particular to the fact that an absence of boundary condition is implicitly a zero Neumann condition. Now the equation shows that one does not have the choice of the value of this condition. In the present example, we choose to impose a Neumann condition on \partial {\Omega}_{n} and Dirichlet conditions on \partial {\Omega}_{d} , \partial {\Omega}_{d}^{1} and \partial {\Omega}_{d}^{2} . Drawing 1: Domain under study Once the previous equations have been obtained, one moves on to the modelling phase. One begins by choosing a finite-element discretisation {V}_{h} (linear, quadratic). One models, in one's preferred finite-element software, the domain {\Omega}_{h} to which one applies the loadings {s}_{h}(x,y) , {T}_{\mathrm{dh}}(x,y) and {q}_{\mathrm{dh}}(x,y) . After solution, one obtains the approximate solution {T}_{h}(x,y) . 2.2 Verification techniques Thanks to the MMS, various verifications can be carried out. 2.2.1 Choosing a reference solution in the approximation space T\in {V}_{h} This is the simplest verification because one must then obtain T(x,y)={T}_{h}(x,y) . Note that, because of the numerical solution, one will not have perfect equality but the difference must be of the order of machine precision 	ext{~}1.E-15 . In concrete terms, if one uses linear elements, one will then have to choose a linear solution. 2.2.2 Choosing a reference solution outside the approximation space T
