u4.42.06 Operator DEFI_GLRC u4.42.06 Operator DEFI_GLRC 1 Purpose The operator DEFI_GLRC makes it possible to define the parameters of the reinforced-concrete constitutive models GLRC_DAMAGE [R7.01.31] and GLRC_DM [R7.01.32]. It makes it possible to determine the homogenised characteristics of the reinforced concrete from the properties of the concrete and of several types of reinforcement (passive reinforcement, prestressing cables, metal liner). In this command, one specifies the physical properties (elastic coefficients, elastic limits) and geometric properties (section and positions of the steel) of the reinforced concrete. On output, one has a “material” concept, which one can then assign to the different plate mesh cells with the command AFFE_MATERIAU. It is important to note that, before calling upon DEFI_GLRC, it is necessary to use DEFI_MATERIAU or DEFI_MATER_GC in order to specify all of the material parameters concerning the steel and concrete components. Produces a data structure of type mater. 2 Syntax mater = DEFI_GLRC( ◇ reuse = , ◇ RELATION = / "GLRC_DAMAGE" (par défaut), / "GLRC_DM", ◇ ALPHA = float, ◇ INFO = / 1 (par défaut), / 2, # Si: equal_to("RELATION", 'GLRC_DM') ◆ BETON = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ EPAIS = float, ), ◆ NAPPE = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ OMY = float, ◆ OMX = float, ◆ RY = float, ◆ RX = float, ), ◇ RHO = float, ◇ AMOR_ALPHA = float, ◇ AMOR_BETA = float, ◇ AMOR_HYST = float, ◆ PENTE = _F( ◇ TRACTION = / "PLAS_ACIER", / "RIGI_ACIER" (par défaut), / "UTIL", # Si: equal_to("TRACTION", 'UTIL') ◇ EPSI_MEMB = float (défaut: 0.0), ◇ FLEXION = / "PLAS_ACIER", / "RIGI_ACIER", / "RIGI_INIT" (par défaut), / "UTIL", # Si: equal_to("FLEXION", 'UTIL') ◆ KAPPA_FLEX = float, ), ◇ CISAIL = / "NON" (par défaut), / "OUI", # Si: equal_to("RELATION", 'GLRC_DAMAGE') ◇ CISAIL_NL = _F( ◆ BTD1 = float, ◆ BTD2 = float, ◆ TSD = float, ), ◆ BETON = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ EPAIS = float, ◆ GAMMA = float, ◆ QP1 = float, ◆ QP2 = float, ◆ C1N1 = float, ◆ C1N2 = float, ◆ C1N3 = float, ◆ C2N1 = float, ◆ C2N2 = float, ◆ C2N3 = float, ◆ C1M1 = float, ◆ C1M2 = float, ◆ C1M3 = float, ◆ C2M1 = float, ◆ C2M2 = float, ◆ C2M3 = float, ◇ & OMT = float, & EAT = float, ◇ & BT1 = float, & BT2 = float, ◇ & MP1X = float, & MP1Y = float, & MP2X = float, & MP2Y = float, ◇ & MP1X_FO = fonction, & MP1Y_FO = fonction, & MP2X_FO = fonction, & MP2Y_FO = fonction, ), ◆ NAPPE = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ OMX = float, ◆ OMY = float, ◆ RX = float, ◆ RY = float, ), ◇ CABLE_PREC = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ OMX = float, ◆ OMY = float, ◆ RX = float, ◆ RY = float, ◆ PREX = float, ◆ PREY = float, ), ◇ LINER = _F( ◆ MATER = mater, ◆ OML = float, ◆ RLR = float, ), ) ◆ : obligatoire ◇ : optionnel ⟐ : présent par défaut & : ensemble / : un seul parmi | : plusieurs choix possibles 3 General description of the reinforced-concrete shell In this paragraph one describes the geometry of the shell considered. Figure 3-a : Current section of the reinforced-concrete shell. The basic section of a reinforced-concrete slab () is composed of: - the concrete shell - the passive reinforcement - and, in the case of GLRC_DAMAGE, the section may in addition contain: - prestressing cables - a metal liner The liner is a steel plate placed on the inner skin of the containment, ensuring in particular leak-tightness in the event of an accidental leak. The prestressing makes it possible to compress the concrete of the civil-engineering structure. This prestressing is applied with the help of steel prestressing cables put under tension. 4 Operands RELATION = GLRC_DM One will consult the documentation of the model GLRC_DM R7.01.32. 4.1 Keyword BETON The factor keyword BETON makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the concrete. 4.1.1 Operand MATER MATER = mat_beton Defines the name of the material, produced obligatorily by DEFI_MATER_GC/BETON_GLRC, used for the concrete. This operand makes it possible to check that the parameters associated with the constitutive behaviours of the concrete do indeed exist in the material. 4.1.2 Operand EPAIS EPAIS = ep Defines the thickness of the concrete plate. One checks that \mathit{ep}\ge 0 . Note: The value of this thickness must be identical to that given in AFFE_CARA_ELEM for the shell elements using the material mat_beton (defined by DEFI_GLRC). 4.2 Keyword NAPPE The factor keyword NAPPE makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the passive reinforcement. This keyword can be defined only once. Indeed, under the hypothesis of isotropy in elasticity of the constitutive law GLRC_DM, all the reinforcements are necessarily identical and equidistant from the neutral fibre. 4.2.1 Operand MATER MATER = mat_acier Defines the name of the material, produced obligatorily by DEFI_MATE_GC/ACIER, used for the passive reinforcement. This operand makes it possible to recover the material parameters used for the passive reinforcement (Young's modulus {E}_{a} , Poisson's ratio {
u}_{a} and elastic limit {\sigma}_{\mathrm{ya}} ). 4.2.2 Operands OMX and OMY OMX = Wx OMY = Wy Define the steel sections {\Omega}_{x} and {\Omega}_{y} of either of the two reinforcement layers given along the directions x and y (in {m}^{2}/m per linear unit if the thickness is given in m ). One recalls that the formulation of the model GLRC_DM imposes that the two reinforcement layers be identical. One checks that {\Omega}_{x}>0 and {\Omega}_{x}={\Omega}_{y} . With the two reinforcement layers in the reinforced-concrete section, one will therefore have a total reinforcement ratio equal to 2{\Omega}_{x}=2{\Omega}_{y} . 4.2.3 Operands RX and RY RX = rx RY = ry Define the dimensionless position of a reinforcement layer with respect to the thickness of the concrete shell, given in the directions x and y ( -1\le \mathit{rx}\le 1 , -1\le \mathit{ry}\le 1 , ). One checks that \mathit{rx}=\mathit{ry} . Figure 4.2.3-a : D efinition of the dimensionless position of the reinforcement layers. 4.3 Operand RHO RHO = rho Optional operand allowing the user to define the equivalent density of the reinforced-concrete slab. In the case where the operand is not defined, the density is computed in the following way: {ho}_{\mathit{eq}}={ho}_{b}+ rac{{ho}_{a}}{h}({\Omega}_{x}^{\sup}+{\Omega}_{x}^{\inf}+{\Omega}_{y}^{\sup}+{\Omega}_{y}^{\inf}) Where {ho}_{a} designates the density of the steel and is recovered in the concept mat_acier supplied by the operand MATER of the keyword NAPPE. Where {ho}_{b} designates the density of the concrete and is recovered in the concept mat_beton supplied by the operand MATER of the keyword BETON . Where h is the thickness supplied by the keyword EPAIS. 4.4 Operands AMOR_ALPHA, AMOR_BETA and AMOR_HYST AMOR_ALPHA = amor_alpha AMOR_BETA = amor_beta AMOR_HYST = amor_hyst Optional operand allowing the user to define the coefficients \alpha and  eta which serve to build the Rayleigh damping matrix, and \eta for the hysteretic damping. C=\alpha K+ eta M One will refer to the documents on the modelling of mechanical damping U2.06.03 and R5.05.04. If the operands are not specified in the command, they take the values defined in the concept mat_beton supplied by the operand MATER of the keyword BETON. 4.5 Factor keyword PENTE 4.5.1 Operand TRACTION ◊ TRACTION = / RIGI_ACIER [DEFAUT] / PLAS_ACIER / UTIL The keyword TRACTION makes it possible to define the method of computation of the post-elastic slope. Indeed, it is possible to carry out this computation according to three methods named RIGI_ACIER, PLAS_ACIER and UTIL. These three computations of slopes make it possible to set up three different fitting methods according to the material properties specified for tension. In the case where the elastic limit of the steels is not known, the fitting methods RIGI_ACIER, i.e. post-elastic slope equal to the slope of stiffness recovery of the steels, and UTIL, i.e. post-elastic slope intersecting the slope of stiffness recovery of the steels at a maximum strain whose value is imposed by the user, are accessible (this method is not suited to maximum strains smaller than the trough of the reference curve, see ). In the case where the elastic limit of the steels is known, it is possible to use the fitting method at the plasticity limit of the steels (PLAS_ACIER). The different fitting methods are illustrated by the figures which follow. Figure 4.5.1-a: Tension curve (GLRC_DM vs Reference) Fitting PENTE = RIGI_ACIER Figure 4.5.1-b: Tension curve (GLRC_DM vs Reference) Fitting PENTE = UTIL Figure 4.5.1-c: Tension curve (GLRC_DM vs Reference) Fitting PENTE = PLAS_ACIER In the case of the fitting at the maximum strain (TRACTION=UTIL), it is necessary to specify the maximum membrane strain (EPSI_MEMB). 4.5.2 Operand FLEXION ◊ FLEXION = / RIGI_INIT [DEFAUT] / RIGI_ACIER / PLAS_ACIER / UTIL The keyword FLEXION makes it possible to define the method of computation of the post-elastic slope. Indeed, it is possible to carry out this computation according to two methods named RIGI_INIT, RIGI_ACIER, PLAS_ACIER and UTIL. The options RIGI_ACIER and PLAS_ACIER correspond to the parameter settings set out for bending in 4.5.1, transposed to the case of bending. In the case of the option RIGI_INIT, the bilinear approximation of the bending response of the section is determined in the following way (cf. R7.01.32): - the elasticity threshold is fixed for a value M which departs by 5% from the theoretical curve; - the second slope is defined so that the bilinear law is tangent to the theoretical curve. Figure 4.5.2-a: Bending curve (GLRC_DM vs Reference) Fitting PENTE = RIGI_INIT In the case of the option UTIL, the bilinear approximation of the bending response of the section is determined so as to reduce the relative area with respect to the theoretical response of the section up to the curvature kf (KAPPA_FLEX). (cf. R7.01.32) Figure 4.5.2-b: Bending curve (GLRC_DM vs Reference) Fitting PENTE = UTIL 4.5.3 Operand EPSI_MEMB EPSI_MEMB = em Defines the value of the maximum membrane strain in the case TRACTION=UTIL. 4.5.4 Operand KAPPA_FLEX KAPPA_FLEX = kf Defines the value of the maximum bending curvature (the inverse of a length) in the case FLEXION=UTIL. 4.6 Keyword CISAIL The simple keyword CISAIL makes it possible to define whether the homogenised elastic parameters are those computed by homogenisation for a standard application of the constitutive model (CISAIL=NON), or those computed for a particular application in order to respect the fact that, when one is in pure in-plane shear, the stiffness of the steels does not come into play in the elastic constitutive behaviour (CISAIL=OUI). 4.7 Keyword INFO With INFO = 2, one obtains the printout in RESULTAT format of the list of the homogenised parameters used as input to the constitutive model GLRC_DM: elasticity, thresholds and damaging constitutive behaviour. 4.8 Example of use One may consult the example of use reported in the test SSNS106A, in tension–compression, and in the test SSNS106B, in alternating bending, cf. V6.05.106. It can be used in order to check, on the case to be studied, the consequences in terms of response, for elementary loadings in alternating statics, of the choice of the parameters and of the identification methods. 5 Operands RELATION = GLRC_DAMAGE One will consult the documentation of the model GLRC_DAMAGE R7.01.31. 5.1 Keyword BETON The factor keyword BETON makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the concrete. 5.1.1 Operand MATER MATER = mat_beton Defines the name of the material produced by DEFI_MATERIAU used for the concrete. This operand makes it possible to check that the parameters associated with the constitutive behaviours chosen under the keywords ECOULEMENT, ECRO_ISOT, ECRO_CINE and ELAS do indeed exist in the material. 5.1.2 Operand EPAIS EPAIS = ep Defines the thickness of the concrete plate. One checks that \mathit{ep}\ge 0 . Note: The value of this thickness must be identical to that given in AFFE_CARA_ELEM for the shell elements using the material mat_beton (defined by DEFI_GLRC). 5.1.3 Operand GAMMA GAMMA = gamma Defines the damage parameter which characterises the slope of the moment–curvature curve during the cracking of the concrete (figure 2). gamma can be considered as being the ratio of the slope during cracking to the elastic slope. If gamma>0 , the slope is positive. If gamma \gamma = rac{{p}_{f}}{{p}_{\mathrm{élas}}} with: - \gamma : GAMMA - {p}_{\mathrm{élas}} : elastic slope - {p}_{f} : slope during cracking Figure 5.1.3-a : Moment–curvature curve of the constitutive behaviour of a reinforced-concrete plate in bending. 5.1.4 Operands QP1 and QP2 QP1 = qp1 QP2 = qp2 Define slope ratios for a positive or negative bending. The ratio is assumed to be the ratio of the slope of the curvature–moment curve after cracking to the elastic slope. They are used only for the computation of the damage: {Q}_{P}= rac{{p}_{2}}{{p}_{\mathrm{élas}}} With: - {Q}_{p} : ratio of the slopes - {p}_{\mathrm{élas}} : elastic slope - {p}_{2} : slope after cracking One checks that 0 {C}_{i}{M}_{j}= rac{{p}_{\mathrm{élas}}{p}_{p}}{{p}_{\mathrm{élas}}-{p}_{p}} with: - {p}_{\mathrm{élas}} : elastic slope - {p}_{p} : plastic slope One checks that {C}_{i}{M}_{j}\ge 0 . 5.1.7 Operands BT1/BT2 and EAT/OMT BT1 = bt1 BT2 = bt2 EAT = eat OMT = omt In the case where the finite elements support the computation of the shear forces, these operands serve to define the elastic stiffness matrix of transverse shear. The shear forces V are related to the distortions \gamma by: V=\left[ egin{array}{cc}\mathit{BT1}& 0\ 0& \mathit{BT2}\end{array}ight]:\gamma If the user specifies the Young's modulus of the transverse steels EAT as well as the section of transverse steel per linear metre OMT, then one deduces the coefficients of the stiffness matrix by the following relation: {\mathit{bt}}_{i}= rac{5}{6} rac{\mathit{ep}}{2}( rac{\mathit{eb}}{1+\mathit{nub}}+\mathit{eat}	imes \mathit{omt}) The user cannot specify at the same time BT1, BT2 and the parameters EAT, OMT. One checks that these operands are strictly positive reals. 5.1.8 Operands MP1X/MP1Y/MP2X/MP2Y and MP1X_FO/MP1Y_FO/MP2X_FO/MP2Y_FO MP1X = mp1x MP1Y = mp1y MP2X = mp2x MP2Y = mp2y MP1X_FO = mp1x_fo MP1Y_FO = mp1y_fo MP2X_FO = mp2x_fo MP2Y_FO = mp2y_fo Define the limit plastic moments of the generalised Johansen criterion used in the constitutive model GLRC_DAMA. They can be defined either by constant values or by functions. It is not possible to mix functions and constants. Moreover, as soon as one of the operands is specified, it is mandatory to specify all of them. When they are not specified, they are computed automatically. 5.2 Keyword ARMA The factor keyword ARMA makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the passive reinforcement. 5.2.1 Operand MATER MATER= mat_acier Defines the name of the material produced by DEFI_MATERIAU used for the passive reinforcement. This operand makes it possible to recover the material parameters used for the passive reinforcement (Young's modulus {E}_{a} , Poisson's ratio {
u}_{a} and elastic limit {\sigma}_{\mathrm{ya}} ). 5.2.2 Operands OMX and OMY OMX = Wxa OMY = Wya Define the steel sections of a reinforcement layer given along the directions x and y (in {m}^{2}/m per linear unit, the thickness then being given in m ). One checks that \mathit{Wxa}\ge 0 and \mathit{Wya}\ge 0 . 5.2.3 Operands RX and RY RX = rxa RY = rya Define the dimensionless position of a reinforcement layer with respect to the thickness of the concrete shell, given in the directions x and y ( -1\le \mathit{rxa}\le 1 , -1\le \mathit{rya}\le 1 , figure 3). Figure 5.2.3-a : D efinition of the dimensionless position of the reinforcement layers. 5.3 Keyword CABLE_PREC The factor keyword CABLE_PREC makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the prestressing cables as well as the prestressing force used. 5.3.1 Operand MATER MATER = mat_cable Defines the name of the material produced by DEFI_MATERIAU used for the prestressing cables. This operand makes it possible to recover the material parameters used for the prestressing cables (Young's modulus {E}_{p} , Poisson's ratio {
u}_{p} and elastic limit {\sigma}_{\mathrm{yp}} ). 5.3.2 Operands OMX and OMY OMX = Wxp OMY = Wyp Define the steel sections of a layer of prestressing cables given along the directions x and y (in {m}^{2}/m per linear unit, the thickness then being given in m ). One checks that \mathit{Wxp}\ge 0 and \mathit{Wyp}\ge 0 . 5.3.3 Operands RX and RY RX = rxp RY = ryp Define the dimensionless position of a layer of prestressing cables with respect to the thickness of the concrete shell, given in the directions x and y ( -1\le \mathit{rxp}\le 1 , -1\le \mathit{ryp}\le 1 ). 5.3.4 Operands PREX and PREY PREX = precx, PREY = precy, Define the prestressing forces (in newtons) in the directions x and y (they must normally be negative, because one applies a compressive force). 5.4 Keyword LINER The factor keyword LINER makes it possible to define the geometric and material characteristics of the metal liner. 5.4.1 Operand MATER MATER = mat_liner Defines the name of the material produced by DEFI_MATERIAU used for the metal liner. This operand makes it possible to recover the material parameters used for the metal liner (Young's modulus {E}_{l} , Poisson's ratio {
u}_{l} and elastic limit {\sigma}_{\mathrm{yl}} ). 5.4.2 Operand OML OML = Wl Defines the thickness of the liner (in metres, according to the choice made for the other dimensioned parameters). One checks that \mathit{Wl}\ge 0 . 5.4.3 Operand RLR RLR = rlr, Defines the dimensionless position of the liner with respect to the thickness of the concrete shell (in practice, \mathit{rlr}=-1 or \mathit{rlr}=1 , because the metal liner is placed on the lower or upper face of the concrete shell). 5.5 Keyword ALPHA This keyword makes it possible to define a “mean” (and isotropic) thermal expansion coefficient for the shell element. 5.6 Keyword INFO Printout in RESULTAT format of the list of the homogenised parameters used as input to the constitutive model GLRC_DAMAGE. 6 Example of use The following example is taken from the test SDNS106A: > MAT = DEFI_GLRC( RELATION = GLRC_DAMAGE, BETON = _F( MATER = MAT_B, EPAIS = EP, GAMMA = 0.0, QP1 = 0.15, QP2 = 0.15, C1N1 = 87.3E6,C1N2 = 87.3E6,C1N3 = 87.3E6, C2N1 = 87.3E6,C2N2 = 87.3E6,C2N3 = 87.3E6, C1M1 = 14.8E6,C1M2 = 14.8E6,C1M3 = 14.8E6, C2M1 = 14.8E6,C2M2 = 14.8E6,C2M3 = 14.8E6,), NAPPE = ( _F(MATER = MAT_A1, OMX = 5.65E-4, OMY = 5.65E-4, RX = 0.95, RY = 0.95,), _F(MATER = MAT_A1, OMX = 5.65E-4, OMY = 5.65E-4, RX = -0.95, RY = -0.95,),), LINER = _F( MATER = MAT_A2, OML = 6.E-3, RLR = -1.,), CABLE_PREC = _F( MATER = MAT_A2, OMX = 4.56E-3, OMY = 1.35E-2, RX = 0.0, RY = 0.0, PREX = -3.0E6, PREY = -3.0E6,), INFO = 2,); Note: In this example, one uses 3 different materials: MAT_B (concrete), MAT_A1 (passive reinforcement) and MAT_A2 (metal liner and prestressing cables). Before defining the parameters of DEFI_GLRC, it is mandatory to use DEFI_MATERIAU in order to specify all the parameters concerning these materials: MAT_B=DEFI_MATERIAU( ELAS = _F( E = 30000.E6, NU = 0.2, RHO = 2500.0,), BETON_ECRO_LINE = _F( D_SIGM_EPSI = 0.0, SYT = 5E6, SYC = -35.E6,),); > MAT_A1=DEFI_MATERIAU( ELAS = _F( E = 2.E11, NU = 0.0,), ECRO_LINE = _F( D_SIGM_EPSI = 0.0, SY = 3.E9,),); > MAT_A2=DEFI_MATERIAU( ELAS = _F( E = 2.E11, NU = 0.3,), ECRO_LINE = _F( D_SIGM_EPSI = 0.0, SY = 5.E8,),); Although the homogenisation formulae used in DEFI_GLRC exploit only the threshold values SY for ECRO_LINE and SYT, SYC for BETON_ECRO_LINE of DEFI_MATERIAU, one is obliged also to specify the values D_SIGM_EPSI as indicated above, since these are mandatory keywords.

u4.42.07 Operator DEFI_MATER_GC u4.42.07 Operator DEFI_MATER_GC 1 Purpose The operator DEFI_MATER_GC makes it possible to define the material parameters used for civil-engineering studies. The aim is to help the user to define the material parameters from more physical quantities (grandeurs). In this command, one supplies the physical properties (elastic coefficients, elastic limits, characteristic strengths, ...), and as output one has a material concept, which one can then assign to the various mesh cells with the command AFFE_MATERIAU. Produces a data structure of type mater. 2 Syntax mater = DEFI_MATER_GC( ◆ / MAZARS = _F( ◆ CODIFICATION = / "ESSAI", / "BAEL91", / "EC2", # Si: equal_to("CODIFICATION", 'BAEL91') ◆ UNITE_CONTRAINTE = / "MPa", / "Pa", ◆ FCJ = float, # Si: equal_to("CODIFICATION", 'EC2') ◆ UNITE_CONTRAINTE = / "MPa", / "Pa", ◆ CLASSE = / "C12/15", / "C16/20", / "C20/25", / "C25/30", / "C30/37", / "C35/45", / "C40/50", / "C45/55", / "C50/60", / "C55/67", / "C60/75", / "C70/85", / "C80/95", / "C90/105", # Si: equal_to("CODIFICATION", 'ESSAI') # Si: exists('EPST0') ◇ EPSC0 = float, ◆ FCJ = float, ◆ EIJ = float, ◆ EPSI_C = float, ◆ FTJ = float, ◇ NU = float, ◇ / EPSD0 = float, / EPST0 = float, ◇ K = float, ◇ AC = float, ◇ BC = float, ◇ AT = float, ◇ BT = float, ◇ SIGM_LIM = float, ◇ EPSI_LIM = float, ), / ACIER = _F( ◆ E = float, ◆ SY = float, ◇ NU = float, ◇ D_SIGM_EPSI = float, ◇ SIGM_LIM = float, ◇ EPSI_LIM = float, ), / ENDO_FISS_EXP = _F( ◆ E = float, ◆ NU = float, ◆ FT = float, ◆ FC = float, ◆ GF = float, ◆ / P = float, / G_INIT = float, ◇ / Q = float, / Q_REL = float, ◆ LARG_BANDE = float, ◇ REST_RIGI_FC = float (défaut: 0.9), ◇ COEF_RIGI_MINI = float, ), / ENDO_LOCA_EXP = _F( ◆ E = float, ◆ NU = float, ◆ FT = float, ◆ FC = float, ◆ GF = float, ◇ P = float, ◆ DIST_FISSURE = float, ◇ REST_RIGI_FC = float (défaut: 0.95), ), / ENDO_LOCA_TC = _F( ◆ E = float, ◆ NU = float, ◆ FT = float, ◆ FC = float, ◆ SIG0 = float, ◆ GF = float, ◆ P = float, ◆ DIST_FISSURE = float, ◇ REST_RIGI_FC = float (défaut: 0.99), ◇ COEF_REDU_SEUIL = float (défaut: 0.95), ◇ TAU_REGU_VISC = float, ), / BETON_GLRC = _F( ◆ CODIFICATION = / "ESSAI", / "EC2", # Si: equal_to("CODIFICATION", 'EC2') ◆ UNITE_CONTRAINTE = / "MPa", / "Pa", ◆ CLASSE = / "C12/15", / "C16/20", / "C20/25", / "C25/30", / "C30/37", / "C35/45", / "C40/50", / "C45/55", / "C50/60", / "C55/67", / "C60/75", / "C70/85", / "C80/95", / "C90/105", # Si: equal_to("CODIFICATION", 'ESSAI') ◆ FCJ = float, ◆ EIJ = float, ◆ EPSI_C = float, ◆ FTJ = float, ◇ NU = float (défaut: 0.2), ), ◇ INFO = / 1 (par défaut), / 2, ◇ RHO = float, ◇ ALPHA = float, ◇ AMOR_ALPHA = float, ◇ AMOR_BETA = float, ◇ AMOR_HYST = float, ) ◆ : obligatoire ◇ : optionnel ⟐ : présent par défaut & : ensemble / : un seul parmi | : plusieurs choix possibles 3 Command DEFI_MATER_GC 3.1 Purpose of the command The purpose of the command DEFI_MATER_GC is to simplify the input of material coefficients for a civil-engineering application, with the following laws: - Mazars concrete law, - concrete law for GLRC_DM, - ecro_line steel law, - non-local damage law for concrete ENDO_FISS_EXP, - local damage law for concrete ENDO_LOCA_EXP. In this command, one supplies the physical properties (elastic coefficients, elastic limits, characteristic strengths, ...), and as output one has a "material" concept, which one can then assign to the various mesh cells with the command AFFE_MATERIAU. These output "material" concepts may also be used by the operator DEFI_GLRC, for the reinforced-concrete cases for GLRC_DM and for ECRO_LINE steel. 3.2 Operand INFO Makes it possible to have, in the message file, the echo of the command DEFI_MATERIAU used by DEFI_MATER_GC when defining the material. The command DEFI_MATER_GC always prints the material parameters which are passed to the command DEFI_MATERIAU (see the section "example of use"). 3.3 Other operands The operands RHO, ALPHA, AMOR_ALPHA, AMOR_BETA, AMOR_HYST correspond to, and have the same meaning as, those found under the factor keyword ELAS of the command DEFI_MATERIAU [U4.43.01]. 4 Factor keyword MAZARS The Mazars constitutive model is an elastic damageable constitutive model making it possible to describe the softening behaviour of concrete. It distinguishes the behaviour in tension and in compression. 4.1 Operand CODIFICATION The command DEFI_MATER_GC can use rules of good practice, or recommendations drawn from the codifications, in order to determine the parameters of the constitutive law MAZARS. ♦ CODIFICATION = ’BAEL91’|’EC2’|’ESSAI’ - ’BAEL91’: indicates that the parameters used in the definition of the materials are taken from the BAEL1991 codification. - ’EC2’: indicates that the parameters used in the definition of the materials are taken from the EUROCODE2 codification. - ’ESSAI’: indicates that the parameters used in the definition of the materials come from tests. For example, according to BAEL 1991, for the concrete material, given its characteristic compressive strength fcj, its tensile strength may be estimated by: \mathit{FTJ}=0.6+0.06\mathit{FCJ} where FTJ and FCJ are in \mathit{MPa} . 4.1.1 Case where CODIFICATION=’BAEL91’ The parameters used in the definition of the materials are taken from the BAEL1991 codification. ♦ UNITE_CONTRAINTE Stress unit of the problem [ \mathit{MPa} | \mathit{Pa} ]: megapascal or pascal. Supplying this operand is mandatory, because the formulae used below require one to know whether the material parameters are in \mathit{Pa} or \mathit{MPa} . The material parameters which are returned by the command are in the same system of units. UNITE_CONTRAINTE must therefore be compatible with the units of the study. - \mathit{Pa} : the material parameters have the dimension of \mathit{Pa} . - \mathit{MPa} : the material parameters have the dimension of \mathit{MPa} . ♦ FCJ Peak stress in compression, in \mathit{Pa} or \mathit{MPa} , according to the value of UNITE_CONTRAINTE. 4.1.2 Case where CODIFICATION=’EC2’ ♦ UNITE_CONTRAINTE Stress unit of the problem \mathit{MPa} or \mathit{Pa} : megapascal or pascal. Supplying this operand is mandatory, because it is necessary to return the material parameters in the system of units used to carry out the study. ♦ CLASSE The compressive strength of the concrete is designated by strength classes related to the characteristic strength measured on a cylinder or on a cube, in accordance with EN 206-1. The classes available in the operator are all those defined in EUROCODE 2: ’C12/15’ ’C16/20’ ’C20/25’ ’C25/30’ ’C30/37’ ’C35/45’ ’C40/50’ ’C45/55’ ’C50/60’ ’C55/67’ ’C60/75’ ’C70/85’ ’C80/95’ ’C90/105’ 4.1.3 Case where CODIFICATION=’ESSAI’ In this case, the user must supply all the characteristics required in order to determine the parameters of the MAZARS constitutive law. The user must give these characteristics in a system of units compatible with the study. The mandatory parameters: ♦ FCJ: Peak stress in compression. ♦ EIJ: Young's modulus. ♦ EPSI_C: Peak strain in compression. ♦ FTJ: Peak stress in tension. The optional parameters, presented in the table, are those found under the factor keyword MAZARS of the command DEFI_MATERIAU U4.43.01. | ePSD0 | Damage threshold in strain | | --- | --- | | EPST0 EPSC0 | Damage threshold in tension and in compression. | | ac, BC | Coefficients making it possible to set the shape of the post-peak curve in compression. | | at, bT | Coefficients making it possible to set the shape of the post-peak curve in tension. | | K | Correction parameter for shear. | Table 4.1.3-a : Material parameters of the MAZARS law. ◊ NU: Poisson's ratio. ◊ SIGM_LIM: Limit stress. ◊ EPSI_LIM: Limit strain. 4.2 Operation 4.2.1 Parameters of the MAZARS law The parameters of the law are determined using the following characteristics: FCJ, EIJ, EPSI_C, FTJ. The formulae are: {\epsilon}_{0}={f}_{\mathit{tj}}/{E}_{ij}  eta =1.10 {B}_{T}={E}_{ij}/{f}_{\mathit{tj}} {A}_{T}=0.70 {B}_{c}= rac{1}{{\epsilon}_{c}
