rd is used to define the concrete material which will be supplied to the keyword BETON/MATER of DEFI_GLRC/RELATION=’GLRC_DM’. 5.1 Operand CODIFICATION This operand works in the same way as that of the keyword MAZARS. ♦ CODIFICATION= ’EC2’|’ESSAI’ - ’EC2’: indicates that the parameters used in the definition of the materials are taken from the EUROCODE 2 codification. - ’ESSAI’: indicates that the parameters used in the definition of the materials come from tests. 5.1.1 Case where CODIFICATION=’EC2’ ♦ UNITE_CONTRAINTE Stress unit of the problem \mathit{MPa} or \mathit{Pa} : megapascal or pascal. Supplying this operand is mandatory, because it is necessary to return the material parameters in the system of units used to carry out the study. ♦ CLASSE The compressive strength of the concrete is designated by strength classes related to the characteristic strength measured on a cylinder or on a cube, in accordance with EN206-1. The classes available in the operator are all those defined in EUROCODE2: ’C12/15’’C16/20’’C20/25’’C25/30’’C30/37’’C35/45’’C40/50’ ’C45/55’’C50/60’’C55/67’’C60/75’’C70/85’’C80/95’’C90/105’ 5.1.2 Case where CODIFICATION=’ESSAI’ In this case, the user must supply the following parameters, which will be arranged into a "material" concept so as to be in the format expected by DEFI_GLRC. The user must give these characteristics in a system of units compatible with the study. ♦ FCJ: Peak stress in compression. ♦ EIJ: Young's modulus. ♦ EPSI_C: Peak strain in compression. ♦ FTJ: Peak stress in tension. ◊ NU: Poisson's ratio. (0.2 by default) 6 Factor keyword ACIER The constitutive model is elastoplastic with linear kinematic hardening. 6.1 Operands The operands are those found under the factor keywords ELAS and ecro_line of the command DEFI_MATERIAU U4.43.01. ♦ E: Young's modulus, elastic slope of the curve \sigma =f(\epsilon ) ♦ SY: Elastic limit. ◊ NU: Poisson's ratio ◊ D_SIGM_EPSI: Slope of the hardening on the curve \sigma =f(\epsilon ) . (cf. ecro_line of the command DEFI_MATERIAU). ◊ SIGM_LIM: Limit stress. ◊ EPSI_LIM: Limit strain. 6.2 Operation Two operands are mandatory: E, SY. If optional operands are supplied, they are taken into account by the command and the values will not be modified. It is therefore necessary to ensure the consistency of the quantities (grandeurs) supplied. In the case where optional operands are not supplied, the command determines the missing quantities (grandeurs) by the following formulae: 
u =0.3 \mathit{dsde}=E/10000.0 {\sigma}_{\mathit{ELS}}={\sigma}_{y}/1.1 {\epsilon}_{\mathit{ELU}}=10.0‰ 6.3 Examples of use To define a steel with an elastic limit of 400.0\mathit{MPa} , the command is used as follows: ACIER = DEFI_MATER_GC( ACIER =_F(E= 2.0E+11, SY= 400.0E+06, ) , ) The command prints in the message file all the values which are used to define the material. == Parameters of the law ECRO_LINE == Elasticity part: E = 2.000E+11, NU = 3.00000000E-01, Non-linear part: SY = 4.00000000E+08, EPSI_LIM = 1.00000000E-02, D_SIGM_EPSI = 2.00000000E+07, SIGM_LIM = 3.63636364E+08, For information: EPSI_ELAS = 2.00000000E-03, 7 Factor keyword ENDO_FISS_EXP The constitutive model ENDO_FISS_EXP is a non-local elastic damageable constitutive model (available in the GRAD_VARI modelling). It makes it possible to describe the softening behaviour of concrete. In particular, it distinguishes the behaviour in tension and in compression, partially restores the stiffness in compression, and describes well the states of shear or of biaxial tension. When the characteristic length tends towards zero, it approaches a cohesive model, which explains why one supplies parameters characteristic of a cohesive law rather than of a volumetric damage model. 7.1 Operands | E | Young's modulus (Pa) | | --- | --- | | NU | Poisson's ratio | | FT | Damage threshold in uniaxial tension (Pa) | | FC | Damage threshold in compression (Pa) | | GF | Fracture energy (N/m) | | P | Principal hardening parameter of the asymptotic cohesive law | | G_INIT | Partial fracture energy resulting from the initial slope | | Q | Secondary hardening parameter of the asymptotic cohesive law | | Q_REL | Secondary hardening parameter (between 0 and 1) | | LARG_BANDE | Width of the localisation band (m) | | REST_RIGI_FC | Stiffness-restoration coefficient (0 = no restoration) | | COEF_RIGI_MINI | Threshold for switching to the frozen secant tangent matrix | Table 7.1-a: Material parameters of the law ENDO_FISS_EXP 7.2 Operation Some characteristics do not require further explanation: Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio, the damage thresholds in tension and in compression, the fracture energy. They are usual characteristics for the modelling of the damage of concrete. As regards the parameters P and Q, which characterise the normalised response of the asymptotic cohesive model (the stress is normalised by FT, the opening by GF/FT), several ways of supplying them are made available. One may directly supply P (greater than 1), or the effect of P on the initial slope of the cohesive response via the datum of the corresponding fracture energy G_INIT, if the cohesive-softening model were linear with the initial slope as its slope. This way of characterising the initial slope is used by some authors, notably within the framework of a bilinear cohesive law (one then speaks of Gf , with a lower-case letter, as opposed to GF , with an upper-case letter, which denotes the whole of the fracture energy). For Q, one may directly supply its value (zero by default), but it is constrained to remain between 0 and a maximum value which depends on P. To simplify the supplying of Q, the possibility is offered of supplying Q relative to this maximum value via the keyword Q_REL (with a value between 0 and 1, therefore). As regards stiffness restoration, the model ENDO_FISS_EXP introduces a regularisation parameter \gamma which smooths the jump in stiffness between tension and compression. Rather than supplying directly the value of \gamma , which is not necessarily very meaningful, one prefers to indicate via REST_RIGI_FC the level of restored stiffness relative to the initial stiffness for a strain (in compression) equal to FC/E. This value therefore lies between 0 (no stiffness restoration) and 1 (strictly less than 1, total restoration of stiffness not being possible with the chosen regularisation function). A value of 0.9 is proposed by default. Finally, one introduces the width of the localisation band, which is supposed to reflect a cohesive crack. It is twice the parameter D (which, for its part, measures the half-width of the band in 1D, as described in the theoretical reference of the model). As for the parameter COEF_RIGI_MINI, it is the one introduced in DEFI_MATERIAU U4.43.01. When the residual stiffness normalised by E is lower than COEF_RIGI_MINI, one substitutes for the tangent matrix the secant matrix corresponding to this stiffness, which limits the problems linked to possible totally destroyed zones (i.e. with no residual stiffness). This parameter has no effect on the physics of the model; it acts only on the convergence properties of the Newton algorithm. By default, this function is not activated (COEF_RIGI_MINI = 0), which seems to suit most cases very well. In general, one refers to the reference documentation of the model R5.03.28 for more detailed explanations of the meaning of the various parameters of the model. 8 Factor keyword ENDO_LOCA_EXP The constitutive model ENDO_LOCA_EXP is a local elastic damageable constitutive model. It makes it possible to describe the softening behaviour of concrete. In particular, it distinguishes the behaviour in tension and in compression, partially restores the stiffness in compression, and describes well the states of shear or of biaxial tension. Owing to its local character, it is intended for a homogenised representation of structural cracks, which explains the introduction of an inter-crack distance which sets the size of the zones containing at most one crack. At the scale of this zone, its response is close to that of ENDO_FISS_EXP which, for its part, would describe the detail of the crack. Some parameters are therefore common with those of ENDO_FISS_EXP. For further information, one may refer to the document R7.01.42 and to the references cited therein. 8.1 Operands | E | Young's modulus (Pa) | | --- | --- | | NU | Poisson's ratio | | FT | Strength in uniaxial tension (Pa) | | FC | Strength in uniaxial compression (Pa) | | GF | Fracture energy (N/m) | | P | Hardening parameter | | DIST_FISSURE | Mean inter-crack distance (m) | | REST_RIGI_FC | Stiffness-restoration coefficient (0 = no restoration) | Table 8.1-a: Material parameters of the law ENDO_LOCA_EXP 8.2 Operation Some characteristics do not require further explanation: Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio, the strengths in tension and in compression, the fracture energy. They are usual characteristics for the modelling of the damage of concrete. The parameter P influences the shape of the hardening, leading to a stress–strain response which is all the more curved the larger it is. It is analogous to the parameter P of the law ENDO_FISS_EXP. There is no other parameter characterising the hardening: relative to ENDO_FISS_EXP, the choice has been made to take Q=0. As regards stiffness restoration, the model ENDO_LOCA_EXP introduces a regularisation parameter \gamma which smooths the jump in stiffness between tension and compression. Rather than supplying directly the value of \gamma , which is not necessarily very meaningful, one prefers to indicate via REST_RIGI_FC the level of restored stiffness relative to the initial stiffness for a strain (in compression) equal to FC/E. This value therefore lies between 0 (no stiffness restoration) and 1 (strictly less than 1, total restoration of stiffness not being possible with the chosen regularisation function). A value of 0.95 is proposed by default. Finally, one introduces DIST_FISSURE, the mean inter-crack distance, which depends in particular on the reinforcement in the concrete. In practice, only the ratio GF/DIST_FISSURE plays a role, but it has been preferred to keep the two parameters because each carries a specific physical meaning. For reasons of local stability of the model (no snap-back at the scale of the material point), there is an upper bound on the inter-crack distance that one can model. Compliance with this condition is checked during data setup. 9 Factor keyword ENDO_LOCA_TC The constitutive model ENDO_LOCA_TC R7.01.47 is a local elastic damageable constitutive model. It makes it possible to describe the softening behaviour of concrete. In particular, it distinguishes the behaviour in tension and in compression, partially restores the stiffness in compression, and describes well the states of shear or of biaxial tension. It takes up the fundamentals of the models ENDO_ISOT_BETON R7.01.04 and ENDO_LOCA_EXP R7.01.42 and enriches them by introducing: - a rationalised damage threshold, with a view to optimising numerical robustness; - an effect of the damage in compression, so as to bound the stresses there, without however seeking to model the post-peak phase. The threshold stress in compression tends monotonically towards a limit, the compressive strength. A local model such as ENDO_LOCA_TC must be used in situations of diffuse damage which may appear owing to the presence of reinforcement in the concrete, or because of an imposed-strain loading as in thermal analysis. The scope of use of ENDO_LOCA_TC is therefore that of distributed cracks (or a network of cracks) for which one can construct a mean behaviour per RVE (Representative Elementary Volume). 9.1 Operands | E | Young's modulus (Pa) | | --- | --- | | NU | Poisson's ratio | | FT | Strength in uniaxial tension (Pa) | | FC | Strength in uniaxial compression (mean) (Pa) | | GF | Fracture energy (N/m) | | COEF_ECRO_TRAC | Hardening parameter | | SIGM_COMP_SEUIL | Threshold stress for initiation in compression | | DIST_FISSURE | Mean inter-crack distance (m) | | TAU_REGU_VISC | Characteristic time of viscosity | | CODIFICATION | ESSAI for measured parameters, or FIB_MODEL_CODE in order to compute the parameters with the FIB model | Table 8.1-a: Material parameters of the law ENDO_LOCA_TC 9.2 Operation Some characteristics do not require further explanation: Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio, the strengths in tension and in compression, the threshold stress for initiation in compression, the fracture energy. They are usual characteristics for the modelling of the damage of concrete, identified by means of a uniaxial tensile test and a uniaxial compressive test. The parameter COEF_ECRO_TRAC influences the shape of the hardening, leading to a stress–strain response which is all the more curved the larger it is. A value of COEF_ECRO_TRAC of -0.2 leads to quasi-linear (negative) hardening, whereas values between 1 and 4 are more representative of the hollowing of the tensile curves. In the absence of a tensile curve with which to identify this parameter, a recommended value is 1.5. Finally, one introduces DIST_FISSURE (denoted in the reference documentation L_{F} ) the mean inter-crack distance, which depends in particular on the reinforcement in the concrete. In practice, only the ratio GF/DIST_FISSURE (ENER_TRAC_RUPT_N) plays a role but it has been preferred to keep the two parameters because each carries a specific physical meaning. For reasons of local stability of the model (no snap-back at the scale of the material point), there is an upper bound on the inter-crack distance that one can model. Compliance with this condition is checked during data setup. It is possible to use the keyword CODIFICATION with the option "FIB_MODEL_CODE" in order to identify the parameters of the model using equations taken from the fib model. However, the value of COEF_ECRO_TRAC may also be set to 1.5, following Peterson's proposal based on a bilinear post-peak curve. The option "Essai" makes it possible to supply the values of all the parameters. The user may also use "FIB_MODEL_CODE" and supply the values of the parameters that are known.

u4.81.43 Operator CALC_PRESSION u4.81.43 Operator CALC_PRESSION 1 Purpose Complete a result by computing a nodal field corresponding to the density of the normal and tangential interface forces on a defined zone. The amplitude of these forces is stored in the produced field PRES_NOEU on the quantity (grandeur) PRES_R with the component names PRES and CISA. The vectors of the tangential force are stored in the components VX, VY and VZ. This field is computed from the Cauchy stress tensor and the field of normals to the surface considered. The computed contact pressure then corresponds to: p=	ranspose{
ormal} 	ensTwo{\sigma} 
ormal where 
ormal is the normal to the surface considered and 	ensTwo{\sigma} the Cauchy stress tensor. This field can be useful for accessing the stress state at the interface in various mechanical problems (contact, fracture mechanics …). The tangential force is deduced by projecting the contact force onto the tangential plane: \vector{s} = (	ensTwo{\sigma} - p 	ensTwoUnit).
ormal The tangential force will be useful for justifying the mesh refinement and the analysis model. In the case of contact with friction, the ratio between the adhesion force and the contact pressure must not exceed the friction coefficient. In the case of frictionless contact, the tangential force is expected to be zero. At present, the tangential force density computed by the command has been validated only in 2D (cf. V6.03.154). In 3D, the analysis is not released because no verification tests are available. 2 Syntax / evol_elas / evol_noli = CALC_PRESSION( ◆ reuse = , ◆ RESULTAT = evol_elas / evol_noli, ◆ GROUP_MA = list[grma], ◆ / INST = list[float], / TOUT_ORDRE = "OUI" (ou non renseigné), ◇ GEOMETRIE = / "DEFORMEE" (par défaut), / "INITIALE", ◇ CRITERE = / "ABSOLU", / "RELATIF" (par défaut), # Si: (equal_to("CRITERE", 'RELATIF')) ◇ PRECISION = float (défaut: 1e-06), # Si: (equal_to("CRITERE", 'ABSOLU')) ◆ PRECISION = float, ◇ INFO = / 1 (par défaut), / 2, ) ◆ : obligatoire ◇ : optionnel ⟐ : présent par défaut & : ensemble / : un seul parmi | : plusieurs choix possibles 3 Operands 3.1 Operand RESULTAT ♦ reuse = resu The result data structure to be enriched is supplied. It comes from the solution of the mechanical problem considered. 3.2 Operand GROUP_MA ♦ GROUP_MA = l_grma The list of mesh-cell groups defining the interfaces considered is supplied. In the case of facets embedded in the volume, the user can, by means of the command MODI_MAILLAGE / ORIE_PEAU_3D / GROUP_MA_VOLU or MODI_MAILLAGE / ORIE_PEAU_2D / GROUP_MA_SURF, reorient the normal correctly. 3.3 Operands TOUT_ORDRE/INST ♦ TOUT_ORDRE = 'OUI', This keyword makes it possible to compute the pressure field for all order numbers. ♦ INST = l_inst, A list of the analysis time instants considered for the post-processing is supplied. 3.4 Operands PRECISION / CRITERE The use of these two keywords is described in document U4.71.00. 3.5 Operands GEOMETRIE ♦ GEOMETRIE = / 'DEFORMEE', [DEFAUT] / 'INITIALE' The configuration on which the normal field is computed is supplied; this keyword is mandatory. 3.6 Operand INFO ◊ INFO = /1, [DEFAUT] /2, Printout level.

v1.01.320 PLEXU05 – Prestressed plate of reinforced concrete under uniform pressure with GLRC_DAMAGE law v1.01.320 PLEXU05 – Prestressed plate of reinforced concrete under uniform pressure with GLRC_DAMAGE law Summary: The purpose of this test is to validate the connection of a voltage calculation of a prestress cable in Code_Aster with a fast dynamic calculation in Europlexus via the macro-command of Code_Aster CALC_EUROPLEXUS, and in the presence of the constitutive law GLRC_DAMAGE. Specifically, it validates the following points: - the correct transmission of information from the GLRC_DAMAGE law from Code_Aster to Europlexus via the CALC_EUROPLEXUS macro-command, - the consideration of kinematic relationships from DEFI_CABLE_BP in the presence of GLRC_DAMAGE law. 1 Description 1.1 Geometry The concrete plate is made up of a square of L=0,9m edge length and e=0,6m thickness. The four tops of the plate are named {A}_{1} , {A}_{2} , {A}_{3} and {A}_{4} . A cable, located on the [{A}_{3}{A}_{4}] segment, crosses the plate horizontally, without eccentricity in the thickness. L-section of the right section of the cable is {S}_{a}={1.10}^{-4}{m}^{2} . Figure 1.1-1: Géométrie 1.2 Material properties The plate is made of reinforced concrete and the steel cable. The properties of reinforced concrete are the result of homogenization of the properties of the concrete and the properties of the reinforcements thanks to the DEFI_GLRC control. | Material | Concrete | Weapons | Cable steel | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Young's modulus | {E}_{b}=3,57{.10}^{10}\mathit{Pa} | {E}_{a}={\mathrm{2.1.10}}^{11}\mathit{Pa} | {E}_{a}={\mathrm{2.1.10}}^{11}\mathit{Pa} | | Poisson's ratio | {
u}_{b}=0.2 | {
u}_{a}=0 | {
u}_{a}=0 | | Density | {m}_{b}=2500\mathit{kg}/{m}^{3} | {m}_{a}=7500\mathit{kg}/{m}^{3} | {m}_{a}=7500\mathit{kg}/{m}^{3} | Table 1.2-1: Propriétés des matériaux. 1.3 Boundary conditions and loads [{A}_{1}{A}_{2}] and [{A}_{1}{A}_{3}] segments are blocked according to Y direction and X direction respectively. The [{A}_{2}{A}_{4}] segment is blocked according to Z direction and rotates around the three axes. The load is applied in two steps. A first quasistatic calculation allows the cable to be prestressed at a T=2,0{.10}^{5}N voltage. Then a uniform pressure is applied positively oriented in the -Z direction on the entire surface of the plate. Its maximum amplitude is {P}_{max}=0,15\mathit{MPa} , and it is associated with a ramp ranging from 0 to 1 between {t}_{\mathit{initial}}=\mathrm{0s} and {t}_{\mathit{final}}=\mathrm{0.004s} moments. 2 reference solution This is a non-regression test for the calculation performed with the DYNA_NON_LINE command. The DYNA_NON_LINE command is then used as a reference to the Europlexus calculation using the CALC_EUROPLEXUS macro-command. 3 Modelling A 3.1 Modelling characteristics The reinforced concrete plate uses Q4GG Modelling while the cable is in BARRE Modelling. 3.2 Mesh characteristics The reinforced concrete plate is composed of 72 elements T3GG, while the cable is modeled by 6 elements BARRE. 3.3 Tested quantities and results The Z direction of the {A}_{1} top is tested. A first calculation with DYNA_NON_LINE is used by a non-regression test, which is used as a reference for the Europlexus calculation using the CALC_EUROPLEXUS macro-command. | Identification | Reference type | Reference value | Tolerance | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | DYNA_NON_LINE- Point {A}_{1} - \mathit{DZ} | 'NON_REGRESSION' | -4,3214499919696. 10-4 | 1.10-6 % | | CALC_EUROPLEXUS- Point {A}_{1} - \mathit{DZ} | 'AUTRE_ASTER' | -4,3214499919696. 10-4 | 0,5 % | Table 3.3-1: Grandeurs testées 4 Executive summary The calculation done with Europlexus via CALC_EUROPLEXUS took into account the different parameters of the GLRC_DAMAGE law. The prestress calculation of the cable validated this functionality when the concrete is modelled by the constitutive law GLRC_DAMAGE.

v2.02.118 SDLL118 - Axial fluid-elastic excitation beam v2.02.118 SDLL118 - Axial fluid-elastic excitation beam Summary: A PVC tube is considered to be centrally located in a cylindrical circular-section chamber and subjected to the action of water axial flow. This physical configuration corresponds to the experimental device of Tanaka et al. [bib1] which is used to measure changes in frequency and damping reduced from the first mode of the tube to the average flow speed. The purpose of this test case is to validate the MEFISTEAU R4.07.04 model resorption to calculate the modal characteristics of a wired structure under confined axial flow, taking into account fluid-elastic excitation. The specific features to be tested are: - DEFI_FLUI_STRU U4.25.01 operator: definition of parameters for the consideration of fluid-elastic coupling, in the case of a configuration of the "xial flow tube beam" type (factor keyword FAISCEAU_AXIAL), - CALC_FLUI_STRU U47.66.02 operator: calculation of the evolution of modal reduced frequencies and dampings according to the average flow speed, by implementing the MEFISTEAU model. The numerical results of the Tanaka et al. device simulation are validated by comparison with the experimental results. Given the relatively large uncertainties on the experimental values, the reference results for the non-regression of the code are those obtained numerically when test case was returned. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry The tube in question is a hollow cylinder with the following dimensions: | length | L=1m | | --- | --- | | outer diameter | {\phi }_{	ext{ext}}=13\mathrm{mm} | | inner diameter | {\phi }_{int}=8,8\mathrm{mm} | The tube is placed in the centre of a cylindrical enclosure with circular cross section. The inner diameter of the enclosure is d=5\mathrm{cm} . The roughness of the tube walls is \varepsilon ={10}^{–5}m . 1.2 Material properties The physical characteristics of the PVC material constituting the tube are as follows: | Young's