u}_{\mathrm{eau}}=1,1.10–6{m}^{2}/s | 1.3 Boundary conditions and loads Both ends of the tube are connected to fixed supports by two metal rods. The relative flexibility of these rods frees the degrees of freedom of tube ends from rotation. Therefore, it can be estimated that the tube supports are of the rotulated-rotulated type, with the metal rods introducing at each end an additional stiffness of rotation. In addition, these rods allow an axial stress to be applied to the tube, which can thus be prestressed in traction or compression. In practice, two configurations are studied: - non-prestressed tube: no effort is applied. This configuration corresponds to Modelling A of test case, - prestressed compression tube by applying an axial stress of 40N at one end. This configuration corresponds to the Modelling of test case B. 1.4 Bibliographic reference 1. TANAKA, K. FUJITA, A. HOTTA and N. KONO: 2 reference solution The experimental measurements performed on the Tanaka et al. device provide the reference values for model validation. The two graphs below, representing the changes in frequency and damping reduced from the first double bending mode to the average flow speed, allow comparison of the model results with the experimental results. Given the uncertainties over the measurements, the relative d-difference tolerance for model validation is quite broad. Therefore, experimental measurements cannot be used as reference values for test case, with a narrower tolerance required to guarantee the non-regression of the code. The reference values used are therefore those obtained numerically when test case is returned. 3 Modelling A 3.1 Modelling characteristics The tube is represented by elements straight beams Timoshenko (MECA_POU_D_T), supported by as many mesh cells segments as 2 nodes (SEG2). Two elements MECA_DIS_TR are added to the nodes ends of the tube, allowing to model metal rods by discreet rotation stiffeners. The elements beam shall be made of the character