rst two modes to guarantee the non-regression of the code. 3.4.1 Frequency of the first two beam bending modes - Comparison test with experience in the first mode: The relative tolerance of d-difference relative to the experimental value is 10%. | Mode number | Experimental value | Calculated value | Relative deviation | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 4,47 \mathrm{Hz} | 4,735 \mathrm{Hz} | 5.90% | 3.4.2 Reduced damping of the first two modes of beam bending - Comparison test with experience in the first mode: The relative tolerance of d-difference relative to the experimental value is 20%. | Mode number | Experimental value | Calculated value | Relative deviation | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 19 % | 22.6474% | +19,20 % | 3.5 Remarks The reference values are those obtained by Code_Aster when the test case is returned, which will allow the subsequent non-regression of the code to be verified during its evolution. 4 Modelling B 4.1 Modelling characteristics Model B is identical to model A (see paragraph [§3.1]), but this time a simplified beam representation is used. A mesh-cell group corresponding to one of the tubes is defined, which defines the only class of equivalence for all the tubes in the beam. The geometry of the beam is defined by giving the coordinates of the centres of the tubes in the orthogonal plane to the beam. It is then assumed, in the resolution of the fluid-structure coupling, that all the tubes have the same modal deformation, which is that of the tube defining the class of equivalence. The mode studied corresponds to a movement of the beam tubes as a whole. The reduced frequency and damping evolutions of the first two beam bending modes are calculated for average flow speeds ranging from 0 to \mathrm{10 }m/s by \mathrm{1 }m/s steps. An initial reduced damping of 12.3% is taken into account. Given the nature of the mode studied (the beam assembly mode), Modelling B should lead to results very similar to those of Modelling A. 4.2 Mesh characteristics The Mesh characteristics of this second model are the same as the model A, i.e.: 459 nodes used and 470 mesh cells type SEG2. The mesh file is in ASTER format. 4.3 Calculation steps The functions which l-on wish to validate are the same as those listed for Modelling A (see paragraph [§3.3]), but this time using a simplified beam representation. The parameters defining the fluid-elastic coupling of a simplified beam representation are defined with the operator DEFI_FLUI_STRU, factor keyword FAISCEAU_AXIAL. The CALC_FLUI_STRU operator shall perform the calculation of the changes in the modal frequency and dampings as a function of the mean flow speed by implementing the MEFISTEAU model based on a simplified beam representation. 4.4 Test values The tests are carried out on the reduced frequencies and damping of the first two modes of beam bending at the average flow speed of \mathrm{4 }m/s . The experimental measurements are only concerned with the characteristics of the first vibrating bending mode on the largest side of the enclosure. This mode is the first determined by the calculation. Two types of tests are carried out: - a comparison test with experimental values on the first mode, - a test of the first two modes to guarantee the non-regression of the code. 4.4.1 Frequency of the first two beam bending modes - Comparison test with experience in the first mode: The relative tolerance of d-difference relative to the experimental value is 10%. | Mode number | Experimental value | Calculated value | Relative deviation | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 4,47 \mathrm{Hz} | 4,735 \mathrm{Hz} | 5.90% | 4.4.2 Reduced damping of the first two modes of beam bending - Comparison test with experience in the first mode: The relative tolerance of d-difference relative to the experimental value is 20%. | Mode number | Experimental value | Calculated value | Relative deviation | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 1 | 19 % | 22.6474% | 19.20% | 5 Remarks The reference values are those obtained by Code_Aster when the test case is returned, which will allow the non-regression of the code to be verified later in its evolution. The results for reduced damping are very slightly different between modellings A and B. This is explained by the fact that Modelling B, which uses a simplified beam representation, assumes that all tubes have a strictly identical modal deformity: the common deformity is that of a tube chosen arbitrarily in the beam. Modelling A, which uses the complete beam representation, takes into account the infinitesimal variations of the deformity of one tube to another. Therefore, it is normal that very slight differences in the results appear. In addition, the low differences observed on the results indicate the value of using a simplified beam representation when having combined modes. The gain in calculation time is quite substantial.

