v3.02.105 SSLP105 - Excavation of a circular tunnel in a linear elastic massif v3.02.105 SSLP105 - Excavation of a circular tunnel in a linear elastic massif Summary: This test is an example of the implementation of a global methodology for the two-dimensional simulation of the digging and support of a circular gallery in an underground massif with Code_Aster . To validate the approach on the basis of a simple analytical solution, we have to make restrictive assumptions on the geometry of the problem, the behaviour of the materials (linear elastic) and the initial stress field (isotrope). The reference solution is given by the so-called "convergence-containment" method, which is the classic method for this type of 2D Modelling. For more details on the methodologies used, we will refer to the U2.04.06 documentation. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry It is a circular cross section tunnel, coated with a concrete ring, which is excavated in a massif of soil. Both materials are assumed to be linear elastic. 1.2 Material properties The materials are linear elastic. 1.2.1 Soil {E}_{s}=4\mathit{GPa} {
u}_{s}=0,3 1.2.2 Concrete {E}_{b}=20\mathit{GPa} {
u}_{b}=0,2 1.3 Initial conditions, boundary conditions and loads The stresses in the massif are initially assumed to be ({\sigma}_{xx}={\sigma}_{yy}={\sigma}_{zz}={\sigma}_{0}) isotropic. The method used to simulate excavation and the laying of the support is the "convergence-containment" method, as presented in [bib1] and [bib2], for example. The basic principle is that the nodal reactions generated at the edge of the future gallery by the initial state of stress are reduced. This operation is referred to as "disposal". When the deconfination has reached the value corresponding to the construction conditions which one wishes to model, the installation of the concrete support on the edge of the gallery is simulated. The boundary conditions and the loading are summarized in the following table. The phases correspond to those in the above diagram, the edges are composed with the nodes identified on the diagram of paragraph [§3.1] and in parentheses the names of the groups of mesh cell or node of the .comm file. | Bords | Phase 1 | Phase 2 | Phase 3 | Phase 4 | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | \mathit{N0N1} (no_bas1) | \mathrm{DY}=0 | \mathrm{DY}=0 | | | | \mathrm{N1N2} (bas_bet) | \mathrm{DY}=0 | \mathrm{DY}=0 | | \mathrm{DY}=0 | | \mathrm{N2N3} (no_bas2) | \mathrm{DY}=0 | \mathrm{DY}=0 | \mathrm{DY}=0 | \mathrm{DY}=0 | | \mathrm{N3N4} (right_no) | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathit{DX}=0 | | \mathrm{N4N5} (ma_top) | {\sigma}_{yy}=-5\mathit{MPa} | {\sigma}_{yy}=-5\mathit{MPa} | {\sigma}_{yy}=-5\mathit{MPa} | {\sigma}_{yy}=-5\mathit{MPa} | | \mathrm{N5N6} (no_left2) | \mathit{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | | \mathrm{N6N7} (no_left_bet) | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | | \mathrm{DX}=0 | | \mathrm{N7N0} (no_left1) | \mathrm{DX}=0 | \mathrm{DX}=0 | | | | \mathrm{N6N2} (board) | | | Nodal reactions corresponding to the de-undefined | | | \mathrm{N7N1} | | | | Free | 2 reference solution 2.1 Method of calculation 2.1.1 Behavior of the massif \lambda is the rate of de-confination, which represents the relative position of the tunnel section considered relative to the face. In the method of convergence - confinement, the excavated future terrain is replaced by an equivalent stress tensor, whose intensity is reduced via \lambda to simulate the digging and distance from the face. The solution of the problem is therefore similar to that of the infinitely thick tube charged by an internal pressure of (1-\lambda ){\sigma}_{0} intensity and by an external pressure of {\sigma}_{0} intensity (see [bib3] for the detailed calculations). Radial, orthoradial stresses and radial displacement to the tunnel wall in elastic medium subject to \lambda de-confination are as follows: \lbrace  egin{array}{c}{\sigma}_{R}=(1- rac{\lambda \cdot {R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}){\sigma}^{0}\ {\sigma}_{	heta}=(1+ rac{\lambda \cdot {R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}){\sigma}^{0}\ {U}_{R}=\lambda  rac{{R}^{2}}{r}\cdot  rac{{\sigma}^{0}}{\mathrm{2G}}\end{array} G is the shear module given by the following relationship: G= rac{E}{2(1+
u )} . 2.1.2 Support behaviour The support will oppose the natural convergence movement of the tunnel and thus apply artificial confinement to the rock. {K}_{s} the stiffness of the support, it is given by the following relationship if it is considered that the support is similar to a thin tube ( {
