v3.04.110 1 v3.04.110 SSLV110 - Elliptical fissure in infinite medium Figure 1.1-1: géométrie et fond de fissure elliptique 1 v3.04.110 SSLV110 - Elliptical fissure in infinite medium Summary: This is a static test for a three-dimensional problem. This test allows to calculate the local energy return rate on the crack bottom by the theta method (command CALC_G in FEM and CALC_G_XFEM in X-FEM). The radius of the integration crowns is variable along the crack, and the local energy return rate is calculated using two different methods (LEGENDRE and LINEAIRE/LAGRANGE). The integration crowns are defined in two ways: by the variable radius data (R_INF_FO and R_SUP_FO) and by the number of layers data (NB_COUCHE_INF and NB_COUCHE_SUP). The value of the test is the validation of the theta method in \mathrm{3D} and the following points: - comparison of results with an analytical solution, - stability of the results vis-à-vis the integration crowns, - comparison between two different methods for the calculation of local G , - 2 equivalent loads (distributed pressure and volume loading). - Calculation of \mathrm{K1} from G and formula d This test contains 3 different modellings (A,F,G). Modelling F tests the calculation of \mathrm{K1} for an unmesh crack (X-FEM method). It also compares errors made on the calculation of \mathit{K1} with the POST_K1_K2_K3 operator or the CALC_G_XFEM operator. The G Modelling validates the calculation of the equivalent stress intensity factor in the presence of cohesive zones (see R7.02.18 documentation), by the CALC_G_XFEM operator. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry It is an elliptical crack plunged into an supposed infinite medium. Only one eighth of a parallelepiped is modelled: 1.2 Material properties E=210\mathrm{000.MPa} 
