ing one quarter of the structure, i.e. a portion of 90° of the ellipse, is modelled. The mesh is composed of mesh cells HEXA8, evenly distributed along the X and Y axes and distributed in geometrically progressive along the Z axis so that in the Z=0 plane, the mesh cells are approximately 10\mathit{mm} side cubes. Symmetrical conditions are applied on the X=0 and Y=0 faces. The rigid mode along the Z axis is blocked by blocking the Z movement of the point located in (0,0,-1250\mathit{mm}) . Loading: Unit pressure distributed on both normal sides of the block: P=1\mathit{MPa} in Z=\pm 1250\mathit{mm} planes. 4.2 Mesh characteristics Number of nodes : 21000 Number of mesh cells and types: 13000 PENTA6 and 12500 HEXA8 (mesh linear) Figure 4.2-1: maillage initial, vue d'ensemble Figure 4.2-2: maillage initial, zoom dans le plan milieu Since this initial mesh is far too coarse for accurate calculation of stress intensity factors along the crack bottom, an automatic refinement procedure for mesh cells near the crack bottom is used, as recommended in the U2.05.02 documentation. The target size of the mesh cells desired is b/9 . This will induce 5 successive refinements. The mesh cells size of the mesh so refined is then h=0,39\mathit{mm} . The refined mesh (the one on which the mechanical calculation is performed) has as its characteristics: - 26484 nodes - 7720 TETRA4, 10650 PYRAM5 et 20080 HEXA8 This mesh induces 99 points along the crack bottom and takes into account the blocking conditions 118404 equations in the system to be solved Figure 4.2-3: maillage raffiné, zoom sur la zone proche de la fissure 4.3 Tested quantities and results The values tested are the stress intensity factors \mathrm{K1} along the crack bottom calculated either by CALC_G_XFEM or by POST_K1_K2_K3. For CALC_G_XFEM, the integration crown is 2h-4h . The default smoothing (Legendre) is used. For POST_K1_K2_K3, the maximum curvilinear abscisse is 4h . To reduce the calculation times of POST_K1_K2_K3, only 20 points are post-processed evenly distributed along the crack bottom. Note that the calculation time for the Legendre smoothing of CALC_G is insensitive to this number. The values are tested at A ( s=0 ) and B ( s=26,7 ) points. | Identification | Reference type | Reference value | Tolerance | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | CALC_G_ XFEM: \mathit{K1}(A) | 'ANALYTIQUE' | 0.000 | 2.0% | | CALC_G_ XFEM : \mathit{K1}(B) | 'ANALYTIQUE' | 4.068 | 2.0% | | POST_K1_K2_K3 : \mathit{K1}(A) | 'ANALYTIQUE' | 0.000 | 6.0% | | POST_K1_K2_K3 : \mathit{K1}(B) | 'ANALYTIQUE' | 4.068 | 6.0% | For CALC_G_XFEM, LAGRANGE smoothings do not allow easy to use results, so a LEGENDRE smoothing is the best option. 5 Modelling G 5.1 Modelling characteristics The geometry and loading are identical to the F Modelling: a quarter of the structure is modelled, a pressure is applied to the upper face. In this Modelling, the initial crack is meshed. Compared to the F Modelling, cohesive zones are introduced into the extension of the crack. This extension is represented by level-set, so that the discontinuity is taken into account by XFEM Modelling, as for F Modelling. The CZM_LIN_MIX cohesive law is introduced in this model XFEM by the DEFI_CONTACT command. Loading: Unit pressure distributed on the side of the block opposite the lip plane: P=1\mathrm{MPa} in the Z=1250\mathrm{mm} plane. The cohesive parameters are chosen so that this will open some cohesive elements near the initial crack bottom: - To avoid a complete break, but simply a decohesion close to the initial crack tip, we take {G}_{c}>{G}_{max} , with {G}_{max} the maximum local G on the long front, while keeping the same order of quantity for both values. In our case, {G}_{c}=2.5	imes {10}^{-4}{\mathit{N.mm}}^{-1} against {G}_{max}=7.2	imes {10}^{-5}{\mathit{N.mm}}^{-1} . - To observe a decohesion in the vicinity of the tip, the characteristic size of the {l}_{c}= rac{E{G}_{c}}{(1-{
