IQUE | 2,4 | | max(G\mathit{local}) - Discretization LINEAIRE, R_INF R_SUP | 11.59 | ANALYTIQUE | 2,5 | | min(G\mathit{local}) - Discretization LINEAIRE, R_INF R_SUP | 11.59 | ANALYTIQUE | 2.0 | 13 Summary of results The conclusions of this test case are as follows: - The definition and calculation of local G on closed crack bottoms is validated. In particular, the independence of local G from the angle for a crack and a load axisymmetric is verified. There is a difference of less than 	ext{2\%} on the entire crack bottom for a DISCRETISATION‘LINEAIRE. - The POST_K1_K2_K3 control, which allows the calculation of stress intensity factors by exploiting the leap of movement on the crack lips, is also validated. This method, less accurate than CALC_G/CALC_G_XFEM, allows the \mathrm{K1} and \mathrm{K3} values to be obtained here (with an appropriate mesh: nodes edge media touching the crack bottom moved to the quarter of these edges) from the \mathrm{K1} and \mathrm{K3} values within 	ext{2 \%} of the reference. Three methods of interpolation are used and give close results. Method 3 is interesting because it provides a single value of stress intensity factors and not a maximum value and a minimum value. The use of POST_K1_K2_K3 to study a nodes release crack is tested and gives satisfactory results. - The calculation of the bilinear form of G and CALC_K_G option of CALC_G_XEM is valid. - The 	ext{X-FEM} method allows to evaluate the stress intensity factors K on an uncracked mesh with an error below 	ext{20\%} with CALC_G_XFEM. With the POST_K1_K2_K3 operator, the accuracy increases considerably, to reach a difference at analytical solution of 	ext{2\%} . - The calculations are valid for elements 3D_INCO_UPG, 3D_INCO_UP, AXIS_INCO_UPG and AXIS_INCO_UP

v3.04.156 FORMA07 - Practical work of the Advanced Use Training: Circular crack in infinite medium v3.04.156 FORMA07 - Practical work of the Advanced Use Training: Circular crack in infinite medium Summary: This \mathrm{3D} test is in quasi-static form, and is part of the post-processings validation in linear elastic fracture mechanics. The structure with a circular crack is set to tensile. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry A cube subjected to a tensile force with a horizontal circular crack of a=2m radius is considered. A cube on the side of 10a will be considered. Figure 1.1-a : geometry 1.2 Material properties A homogeneous isotropic material with linear elasticity is considered to have the following characteristics: - Young's modulus E=\mathrm{200 000}\mathit{Mpa} , - Poisson's ratio 
u =0,3 . 1.3 Boundary conditions and loads The structure is in traction ( \sigma =1\mathit{MPa} ). 2 reference solution 2.1 Method used for reference solution For a circular a radius crack in an infinite medium, subject to uniform \sigma traction as normal in the lip plane, the local G(s) energy return rate is independent of the curvilinear abscisse along the s crack front and is expressed as follows[1]: G(s)= rac{(1-{
u}^{2})}{\pi E}4{\sigma}^{2}a The stress factor {K}_{I}(s) is given by the formula of Irwin: G(s)= rac{(1-{
u}^{2})}{E}{K}_{I}^{2} , i.e. {K}_{I}(s)= rac{2\sigma \sqrt{a}}{\sqrt{\pi}} 2.2 Baseline With the numerical values of the statement, we find: {K}_{I}=1,5957\mathit{MPa}.\sqrt{m} and G=11,59{\mathit{J.m}}^{-2} . 2.3 Bibliographical references 1. Tada, P. Paris, G. Irwin, The stress analysis of cracks manual, 3rd edition, 2000 3 Modelling A: FEM 3D 3.1 TP procedure 3.1.1 Mesh The structure's quadratic mesh is provided in the MED format: forma07a.mmed. Taking into account some symmetries of the problem, only a quarter of the structure is represented. One could only represent 1/{8}^{\mathit{ème}} of the structure. The mesh was generated with GIBI software, and tores are defined around the crack bottom: - radius of the smallest torus: 0,12m , - radius of the largest torus: 0,53m . FACE_SUP FACE_AV Figure 3.1.1-a : mesh FACE_INF FACE_LAT Nodes media of the elements edges affecting the crack bottom are moved to the quarter of these edges. 3.1.2 Creating command file without post-processing of the break | Launch Salome_Meca, then choose the AsterStudy module. Add the following steps to your case: | | --- | | Read a mesh ( LIRE_MAILLAGE ). Select the mesh forma07a.mmed and the MED format | | Modify a mesh ( MODI_MAILLAGE ). Choose the mesh previously read and select the rouse. Select the ORIE_PEAU_3D action to redirect normals to the outside of the mesh (elements FACE_AV groups, FACE_LAT, FACE_SUP, FACE_INF, LEV_INF and LEV_SUP). | | Assign finished element (AFFE_MODELE). Choose the mechanical phenomenon and 3D continuous media modelling (3D) | | Definition and allocation of material: Definition a material ( DEFI_MATERIAU ) and Assign a material ( AFFE_MATERIAU ) | | Definition of boundary conditions and loads: Assign mechanical load ( AFFE_CHAR_MECA ) : Symmetry on the 'FACE_LAT' (DOF force); Symmetry on the 'FACE_AV' (DOF force); Blocking of rigid modes (DOF force on the GROUP_NO 'D'); Application of traction on 'FACE_SUP' and 'FACE_INF' (PRES_REP) | | Elastic problem resolution: Static mechanical analysis ( MECA_STATIQUE ); | | For viewing with Paravis: Calculation of extrapolated stress field nodes (CALC_CHAMP, option 'CONTRAINTE' with field'SIGM_NOEU') Calculation of the equivalent stress field (CALC_CHAMP, option 'CRITERES' with field'SIEQ_NOEU') To do this, the concept from MECA_STATIQUE will be enriched by the same concept name. | | Print results in MED format: Results output(IMPR_RESU). | | View the travel and stress fields obtained in Paravis | 3.1.3 Post-processing for failure | To separate the calculation and the post-processing, you can add a new step (New Stage) to your study case (Case). | | --- | | Definition of the crack bottom ( DEFI_FOND_FISS ). Defining the crack bottom in DEFI_FOND_FISS from the LFF bottom mesh group and the LEV_INF and LEV_SUP lips. | | Calculation of G and K with CALC_G (OPTION=('G', 'K')). Complete the information on the THETA field: the description of the crack via the keywordFISURE the rays of the crown of the theta field(R_INF, R_SUP), to be defined according to the mesh used. Print the values of G (IMPR_TABLE). | | Calcul de K and G with POST_K1_K2_K3: Inform crack background Inform ABSC_CURV_MAXI parameter Print results in table(IMPR_TABLE) | | Trace the values of G and K1 from CALC_G and POST_K1_K2_K3 as a function of the curvilinear abscisse of the crack front (column 'ABSC_CURV') in a spreadsheet. | 3.1.4 Further: Study of the influence of 3D discretion In CALC_G, enter the DISCRETISATION keyword of the factor THETA keyword with the LEGENDRE keyword Compare values with those obtained previously. As a reminder, when DISCRETISATION is not specified, its default value is LINEAIRE. In a second step, add the keyword NB_POINT_FOND=5 from the factor THETA keyword to the LINEAIRE discretization and observe the values of G or K1 along the front. Also compare the calculation times of CALC_G with and without NB_POINT_FOND of the input. 3.2 Tested quantities and results | Identification | Reference | % tolerance | | --- | --- | --- | | {K}_{I} de POST_K1_K2_K3 | 1,5957 106 | 0,8% | 4 Modelling B: X-FEM 3D The problem considered is the same as that studied in Part II. However, unlike the previous part where the crack was meshed, this part considers a healthy mesh. The crack is therefore not meshed. For this, the X-FEM method is used. 4.1 TP procedure 4.1.1 Mesh refinement The objective of this first step is to use HOMARD to obtain a refined mesh from a relatively coarse healthy mesh. You can create the coarse mesh with Salomé (1⁄4 of the structure is modelled) or use the one provided: forma07b.mmed In order to achieve the refinement of the mesh, it is necessary to use a python loop. The command file of this part is therefore not graphically editable with AsterStudy. Therefore, we will use the text mode of AsterStudy for the first step of the calculation. | Start Salome-Meca and then choose AsterStudy. Go to the preferences (File → Preferences → AsterStudy) and uncheck External editor. Create a new step for your calculation (New Stage) that you can rename (Refining) if you want. Left-click on this new step and click Edit. A window will appear on the left and you can edit your command file. | | --- | | First, you will need to calculate the number of iterations required to get the refined mesh. First, you must import the math library: import math.Inquire the following information: Size of mesh cell in the crack insertion area h0 Size of mesh cell target hc (a/20.) Calculation of the number of iterations to reach hc: n=(ln(h0)+ln(hc))/ln(2.) nb_raff=E(n) (integer part of n: int(n)) nb_iter=nb_raff+1 Calculation of the final size of elements near the crack front: h=h0/2^nb_raff | | Initialize all vectors that will be used in the refinement loop: MA = [None] (nb_iter+1) (mesh) CHERR = [None] nb_iter (Error) FISS = [None] nb_iter (Fissure) | | Read mesh in MED format ( LIRE_MAILLAGE ): MA[0]= LIRE_MAILLAGE(UNITE=20, FORMAT='MED'); Select mesh forma07b.mmed that will be added to the Data Files tab under unit 20. | | Make the refinement loop: for i_raff in range(nb_raff): Definition of the crack FISS[i_raff]=DEFI_FISS_XFEM(mesh=MA[i_raff], TYPE_DISCONTINUITE='FISSURE', DEFI_FISS=_F(FORM_FISS='ELLIPSE', DEMI_GRAND_AXE=a, DEMI_PETIT_AXE=a, CENTRE=(0,0,0,), VECT_X=(1,0,0, 0,), VECT_Y=(0,1,0,),),); Calculation of the refinement criterion CHERR[i_raff]=RAFF_XFEM(FISSURE=FISS[i_raff],TYPE='ZONE',RAYON=r_raff CHERR[i_raff],CHERR[i_raff],XXXX_rf,XX_rf,XXX,XX_rf,X,XXX,X | | To view the new mesh, print it in MED format: IMPR_RESU(FORMAT='MED',UNITE=80,RESU=_F(mesh=MA[nb_raff])]; Choose mesh forma07b_raff.mmed that will be added to the Data Files tab under the 80 unit. | If you want to pass this step, you can also use the refined mesh provided directly: forma07b.41 In the meshes provided (see them in Salomé), the names of the groups of mesh cells are as follows: - node group: D, - group of faces: FACE_AV, FACE_LAT, FACE_SUP, FACE_INF, - group of volumes: CUBE. Figure 4.1.1-a : Groups defined on the mesh provided 4.1.2 Creating the command file without post-processing of the break 4.1.2.1 Reading the fine healthy mesh and defining the unenriched model | Launch Salome-Meca, then choose the AsterStudy module. Add the following steps to your case. | | --- | | Read a mesh ( LIRE_MAILLAGE ). Select the refined mesh you created and the MED format | | Modify a mesh ( MODI_MAILLAGE ). Choose the mesh previously read and select the rouse. Select the ORIE_PEAU_3D action to redirect normals to the outside of the mesh (elements FACE_AV groups, FACE_LAT, FACE_SUP, FACE_INF) | | Final element ( AFFE_MODELE ) . Choose the mechanical phenomenon and 3D continuous media modelling | 4.1.2.2 Definition of the crack and elements X-FEM | In the Fracture and Fatigue tab:Definition of the crack DEFI_FISS_XFEM . Preferably use the crack catalog (FORM_FISS='ELLIPSE') | | --- | | In the Fracture and Fatigue tab: Model modification to take into account elements X-FEM( MODI_MODELE_XFEM ) | 4.1.2.3 Definition of material, conditions and mechanical problem resolution | Definition and allocation of material: Definition a material ( DEFI_MATERIAU ) and Assign a material ( AFFE_MATERIAU ) | | --- | | Definition of boundary conditions and loads on the enriched model. Assign mechanical load ( AFFE_CHAR_MECA ) : Symmetry on the 'FACE_LAT' plane of symmetry DOF force ; Symmetry on the 'FACE_AV' plane of symmetry DOF force ; Rigid mode blocking DOF force on 'D' Tensile application on 'FACE_SUP' and 'FACE_INF' ( PRES_REP | | Elastic problem solving: Static mechanical analysis ( MECA_STATIQUE ) with enriched model | 4.1.2.4 Post-processing displacement and constraints with X-FEM and visualization with Paravis To separate the calculation and the post-processing, you can add a new step (New Stage) to your study case (Case). | In the tab Fracture and Fatigue : Creating a mesh of visualization ( POST_MAIL_XFEM ) | | --- | | In the Finish Element tab: Creating a template for viewing ( AFFE_MODELE ) on the mesh created for viewing | | In the Fracture and Fatigue tab: Creating a result field on the mesh visualization X-FEM ( POST_CHAM_XFEM ) | | For viewing with Paravis: Calculation of extrapolated stress field nodes (CALC_CHAMP, option 'CONTRAINTE' with field'SIGM_NOEU') Calculation of the equivalent stress field (CALC_CHAMP, option 'CRITERES' with field'SIEQ_NOEU') To do this, the concept from POST_CHAM_XFEM will be enriched by the same concept name. | | Printing results in MED format: Results output (IMPR_RESU). | 4.1.3 Added post-processing steps in mechanical rupture For the post-processing of the breakup you can also add a new step (New Stage) to your current study case. > For all the next steps, you will have to go to the Fracture and Fatigue tab. 1. G-calculate with CALC_G_ XFEM > Calculate the energy return rate G and stress intensity factors with CALC_G_XFEM (OPTION='CALC_K_G') and Legendre level 5 smoothing. Use the result of the static ca