v3.06.103 SSLA103 - Calculation of desiccation removal and endogenous removal on a cylinder v3.06.103 SSLA103 - Calculation of desiccation removal and endogenous removal on a cylinder Summary: This test case is designed to validate the calculation of desiccation removal and endogenous withdrawal. It also tests the possibility of making dependent the characteristics of hydration and drying materials (in the case of the Mazars model). It is a cylinder that undergoes a uniform drying and hydration. The temperature also varies. The cylinder is modeled by four elements quadrangles at 8 nodes for modellings A , C , E , F , H and by a element HEXA20 for B Modelling . For modellings: - A and B , the behavior is assumed to be elastic, which allows to validate the withdrawal calculation both with STAT_NON_LINE and with MECA_STATIQUE. - C and H enable the removal calculation to be validated with the law of MAZARS and MAZARS_UNIL (without damage activation). - E validates the removal calculation with ENDO_ISOT_BETON law (with NEWTON method and IMPLEX method). - F coupling ENDO_ISOT_BETON / BETON_UMLV_FP The results obtained by c ode_aster are identical to the reference analytical solution. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry Cylindrical test. 1.2 Material properties For modellings A and B , the material is assumed to be elastic and the material characteristics are constant to validate the calculation with MECA_STATIQUE, For C modelling, MAZARS law is used and some parameters depend on hydration and drying. E Modelling allows testing ENDO_ISOT_BETON law, using NEWTON and IMPLEX methods, and F Modelling ENDO_ISOT_BETON / BETON_UMLV_FP coupling, knowing that the material parameters of BETON_UMLV_FP law are chosen in such a way that we did not creep and therefore we find the behavior of ENDO_ISOT_BETON law. In both cases, the material characteristics are constant. Note that, given the loading (dilation, hydration and free drying), no damage is occurring: in all cases, the elastic solu