thit{mm}brace 2 reference solution The work of La Borderie & Pijaudier-Cabot [bib1] consisted of a field of tests of extractions carried out on fibers and reinforcements for reinforced concrete. The purpose of these tests was to study the influence of stress state in the matrix on the mechanical properties of the interface. Each specimen was a 80	imes 80	imes 80\mathit{mm} cube with a concrete paste of \mathit{Grade}\mathit{C30}/37 ( 16\mathrm{mm} gravier) with resistance at the time of testing (three days after casting and with a 4-hour heat treatment) of 14.5\mathrm{MPa} , an Young's modulus of 15500\mathit{MPa} and an Poisson's ratio of 0,17 . For the reinforcements, they used 8\mathrm{mm} high-adhesive bars of diameter, with a steel-beton contact length of 45\mathrm{mm} , plus 10\mathrm{mm} free at the edge of the concrete, which eliminated the singularities in the field of concrete stresses in the clamped surface of the cube. The test was performed with controlled displacements ( \dot{u}=8.3	imes 10E-3\mathrm{mm}/s ) and four containment levels: 0,5 , 10 and 15\mathrm{MPa} , constant during each experiment. 2.1 Bibliographical references - LA BORDERIE C. & PIJAUDIER-CABOT G. - Experimental study of the behaviour of reinforced materials: Experimental determination of constitutive laws of the fibre-matrix interface. Laboratory of Mechanical and Technology (LMT); ENS Cachan/CNRS/Université Paris 6; Contract I 70/1F 3146 with Electricity of France, 1994 3 Modelling A 3.1 Modelling characteristics This is a \mathrm{2D} axisymmetric Modelling, where you can identify 2 groups d Axisymmetric Modelling (keyword AXIS) for concrete and steel elements. Axisymmetric crack Modelling (keyword AXIS_JOINT) for the joint element. Concrete and steel are modeled with elements QUAD4. The interface is modelled with degenerate elements QUAD4 (nodes combined). Figure 4.1-a: axisymmetric test model 3.2 Mesh characteristics Number of nodes: 94 (with 20 nodes combined) Number of mesh cells and type: 11 QUAD4 for steel - 9 QUAD4 for the interface - 54 QUAD4 for concrete. 3.3 Tested quantities and results xy components of the element are tested for the tangential components of the local behavioral law in the interface, from the SIEF_ELGA stress field. The values are tested at the point of Gauss 2 of the element joined, at 4 time step different: at the beginning of loading, during the growth phase of damage, in the peak of maximum strength and after the peak of resistance of the restraint. Champ SIEF_ELGA component SITX | Identification | Reference | 	ext{\%} tolerance | | --- | --- | --- | | For a forced {U}_{\mathit{TT}}=0.2\mathit{mm} displacement | -7.20 E+00 | 5 | | For a forced {U}_{\mathit{TT}}=0.8\mathit{mm} displacement | -1.14 E+01 | 5 | | For a forced {U}_{\mathit{TT}}=1.0\mathit{mm} displacement | -1.26 E+01 | 5 | | For a forced {U}_{\mathit{TT}}=1.6\mathit{mm} displacement | -1.22 E+01 | 5 | 3.4 Evolution of damage To observe the consistency of the damage evolution in the different elements of restraint, the damage variable graph is constructed in relation to the displacement imposed. 4 Summary of the results With differences below 	ext{5.0 \%} compared to experimental results obtained by La Borderie & Pijaudier-Cabot [bib1], the axisymmetric simulation of the removal test is considered satisfactory. However, in order to test the stability of the constitutive law in combination with other laws (e.g. the MAZARS law for concrete), the size of the time step will have to be reduced by considering that other laws are normally made for small deformation formulations.

