v6.03.171 SSNP171 - Closing of a flexing crack v6.03.171 SSNP171 - Closing of a flexing crack Summary This test allows the XFEM method to be evaluated with contact when cracks are closed. A 2D bending test piece is considered, with a central crack. The bending of the test piece causes a partial closing of the crack. On the crack bottom remaining at the opening, the stress factor {K}_{I} is tested. The theory predicts that the contact factor corrects the {K}_{I} factor of about 8	ext{\%} . To gauge the influence of the contact when closing, seven modellings are envisaged: - Modelling A: 2D Modelling and linear elements, with interpenetration of crack lips. - Modelling B: 2D Modelling and linear elements, with contact between crack lips. - Modelization C: 2D Modelling and quadratic elements, with interpenetration of crack lips. - Modelization D: 2D Modelling and quadratic elements, with contact between crack lips. - Modelling E: 3D Modelling and linear elements, with interpenetration of crack lips. - Modelling F: 3D Modelling and linear elements, with contact between crack lips. - G Modelling: 3D Modelling and quadratic elements, with interpenetration of crack lips. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry The \mathrm{2D} structure is a rectangular test piece ( \mathit{LX}=10 , \mathit{LY}=20 ), with a straight and horizontal central crack []. The crack half length is constant ( a=1 ). The 3D structure corresponds to an extrusion in the Z direction of the 2D test piece. The test piece becomes parallelepipedic in dimensions: \mathit{LX}=10 , \mathit{LY}=20 , \mathit{LZ}=1 . The a=1 length crack in the plane (X,Y) is also extruded following Z. The nodes rated \mathit{NOEUD1} , \mathit{NOEUD2} on it are used to impose the boundary conditions, which are explained in paragraph [§ 1.3 ]. Figure 1.1-1: géométrie de la plaque fissurée 2D 1.2 Material properties Young's modulus: E={10}^{6}\mathit{Pa} Poisson's ratio: 
