v6.04.402 SSNV402 – constitutive law BETON_RAG verification: phenomena coupling v6.04.402 SSNV402 – constitutive law BETON_RAG verification: phenomena coupling Summary: These tests check the constitutive law BETON_RAG on a cube that is required by a temperature and drying load. The model's response is compared to solutions obtained by analytically resolving the equations that govern the R7.01.26 behavior. Several modellings are proposed: - Modelization a: the cube is free of deformation. - Modelization b: the cube is blocked on movement. 1 Reference problem 1.1 Geometry Geometry is a cube of 1m on the side. Geometry adopted 1.2 Mesh The mesh is made with 1 HEXA8. 1.3 Materials The material is of BETON_RAG type. The necessary characteristics are those related to chemical advancement phenomena as well as those related to RAG. You must not use the material characteristics of "BETON_RAG" of this test case to study. They are not physical, they are used for mathematical verification. The material parameters used in this test case are given in v6.04.402-paramsBRAG. 1.4 boundary conditions 1.4.1 For the free side deformation cube These boundary conditions are for Modelling a. The following movements are imposed on the faces: - BAS : DZ = 0 - GAUCHE : DY = 0 - ARRIERE : DX = 0 1.4.2 For the cube blocked in lateral deformations These boundary conditions are for Modelling a. The following movements are imposed on the faces: - BAS, HAUT : DZ = 0 - GAUCHE, DROITE : DY = 0 - ARRIERE, AVANT : DX = 0 1.5 Modelling All modellings are 3D. 2 Modelling A 2.1 Modelling characteristics This Modelling is done on a cube free of deformation. Loading is a temperature and drying that varies over time. The figure shows the evolution of the load over time. Evolution of temperature loading and drying. 2.2 reference solution The equations are from R7.01.26. 2.2.1 Chemical advance Chemical advancement follows the following law:  rac{\partial A}{\partial t}\phantom{ule{2em}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{2em