crete. On the other hand, there are large differences between the GLRC_DM and DHRC models and the EIB model curve on the Poisson displacement curve. This is explained by the fact that \mathrm{DY} displacements are calculated in the multilayer calculation (EIB) on concrete layers, but the stress applied is such that concrete reaches its tensile strength limit at around the 0.1s moment. 11 Modelling I 11.1 Modelling characteristics Pure flexion alternated. Figure 11.1-a: maillage et conditions aux limites Modelling: DKTG boundary conditions: - \mathrm{DRY}=0.0 on the {A}_{1}-{A}_{3} edge - \mathrm{DRY}={R}_{0}	imes f(t) on the {A}_{2}-{A}_{4} edge, where {R}_{0}=3.0	imes {10}^{-2} and f(t) is the amplitude of the cyclic load as a function of the parameter (pseudo-time) t . To verify the model, the following loading function shall be considered: Figure 11.1-b: fonction de chargement 11.2 Mesh characteristics Number of nodes: 9. Number of mesh cells: 8 TRIA3; 8 SEG2. 11.3 Modelling DHRC The modellings for high stress levels allows the validation of the DHRC model. This model is able to represent the relative slip between the rebar and the surrounding concrete during high stress. The H model then compares the results obtained for the DHRC model with those of the GLRC_DM and EIB models. The parameters chosen for GLRC_DM are then set according to the parameters identified by DHRC. 11.3.1 Parameter identification The DHRC model uses a large number of parameters from an automated identification performed prior to the structure calculation. This identification is based on a periodic representative elemental volume representing a section of the reinforced concrete plate, based on the periodicity of the reinforcement grid. Figure 11.3-a : maillag e of the plate section for the identification of DHRC parameters The mesh presented to it thus represents a section of the 10	imes 10	imes 10{\mathit{cm}}^{3} plate studied with steels of 10\mathit{mm} diameter corresponding to a rate 