DHRC models under high enough loads to ensure that the stiffness recovery of the steels is actually shown on the EIB reference. This test case takes the test case SSNS106B back up by adding the DHRC reference to compare the global GLRC_DM model with a model that takes into account more energy dissipation via the representation of internal sliding mechanisms at the steel-beton interface. On the moment-rotation curve, it is noted that the responses given by the modellings GLRC_DM and DHRC differ from the response given by the EIB multilayer model. Indeed, the modelling of bending behavior is greatly affected by the position of the steels in plate thickness and the consideration of an 0,4\mathit{cm} excentre in EIB Modelling is only approximate since the coating of the reinforcements and 0,1\mathit{cm} for a half-thickness of 0,5\mathit{cm} plate and 10\mathit{mm} diameter steels. The same observation can be made as in § 10.5 on \mathit{DRX} rotations this time, in fact, the rotations, for the multi-layer model are also calculated on the concrete and thus become null once the concrete has been destroyed. Furthermore, it is observed on the evolution diagrams of the internal damage variables, that positive bending leads to an evolution of the internal damage variable {d}_{2} corresponding to the damage in the lower half of the plate and that the negative bending leads to the evolution of the variable {d}_{1} ; for the two models considered. This is in agreement with the expected physics of the problem, the plate mainly damages in its tense part. 12 Modelling J 12.1 Modelling characteristics Traction Coupling - Compression and Flexion. Figure 12.1-a: maillage et conditions aux limites Modelling: DKTG Boundary conditions: Traction coupling – Compression and Flexion: - \mathrm{DX}=0.0 and \mathrm{DRY}=0.0 on the {A}_{1}-{A}_{3} edge - \mathrm{DX}={U}_{0}	imes f(t) and \mathrm{DRY}={R}_{0}	imes f(t) on the {A}_{2}-{A}_{4} edge, where {U}_{0}=1.	imes {10}^{-3} , {R}_{0}=3.	im