a number of these weaknesses. In summary, the roles of the tests are as follows: A : Only test the tensile/compression behaviour under the condition of uniformity (quasi \mathrm{1D} ). B : Only test the behaviour in cyclic bending under the condition of uniformity (quasi \mathrm{1D} ). We identify the bending parameters. C : Test behavior matching membrane and bending phenomena under the condition of uniformity (quasi \mathrm{1D} ). D :Test shear and distortion behaviour in the plane E :Test bending coupling and shearing in the plane. F :Test traction behavior – pure compression – with damaged elastoplastic "kit_dll" behavior (GLRC_DM + Von Bets). G :Test traction behaviour – pure compression – with damaged elastoplastic "kit_ddl" (GLRC_DM + Von Bets). H :Test the traction-compression behavior with significant stresses to evaluate GLRC_DM and DHRC I :Test the bending behavior alternately with significant stresses to evaluate GLRC_DM and DHRC J :Test the traction-flexion behaviour alternately with significant stresses to assess the coupled GLRC_DM and DHRC stress behaviour K : Test compression behavior - traction with ALPHA_C=100 for GLRC_DM L : Test pure shear and distortion behaviour in the plane with high stresses to evaluate GLRC_DM and DHRC M : Test bending and shear coupling behaviour in the plane with high stresses to evaluate GLRC_DM and DHRC N : Test the anticlastic bending behavior with high stresses to evaluate GLRC_DM and DHRC. O :Test a thermomechanical load of expansion. In most situations of modellings A to E, the displacements, forces and moments predicted by the GLRC_DM model are represented with a modest error (<10%) by reference to a multilayer model, which seems quite satisfactory for a model that is intended to represent the "global" behavior of a structure. The greater error is observed (~25%) in tests on the effect of Poisson in the damaging phase and when damage is activated in coupled bending membranes. The first defect is less important if one is more interested in dissipated energy in the system and less in displacements. The second defect is more embarrassing and shows well that an optimal "global" model should always be set in relation to the main stress that one wishes to model: the parameters of the model will be chosen accordingly. The main contribution to error is probably due to the anisotropy of the reinforced concrete not fully taken into account by the GLRC_DM model (see R7.01.32). The modellings H-I-J-L-M-N allows direct comparison between GLRC_DM and DHRC behavior models for a choice of coherent parameters: the value of accurately representing the position of steels in the plate thickness, of enriching the description of dissipation by the degradation of the restraint steel-beton. However, the overall responses of these two models remain fairly similar in all loading situations: the triggering thresholds for damage are concomitant, the generalized force-displacement curves are similar; with the DHRC model the terms resulting from Poisson effect coupling appear more realistic. Modelling O validates the use of these models in reinforced concrete building studies with thermal expansion loading. The two modellings F and Gs where the GLRC_DM kit and Von Mises elastoplasticity are evaluated have above all a demonstrative value of the possibilities offered.

