kness of the plate. It is verified that membrane correctors vary linearly in thickness. Evolution of the vertical component of the bending corrector through the thickness of the plate. It is verified that bending correctors vary quadraticly in thickness. Evolution of the component following x of the transverse shear corrector through the thickness of the plate. Evolution of the component following y of the transverse shear corrector through the thickness of the plate. When the Mindlin formulation is applied, it is verified that the transverse shear corrector is identically zero through the plate thickness. When the Touratier formulation is applied, it is verified that the transverse shear corrector is sinusoidally evolving through the plate thickness. 8.4 Evolution of transverse shear stresses through plate thickness The contribution of the stresses due to the deformation imposed and the corrector shall be summated to obtain the total stress through the plate subjected to a unit deformation in transverse shear. Evolution of \sigma_{xz} local shear stress \gamma_{xz} transverse shear through plate thickness. Evolution of \sigma_{yz} local shear stress \gamma_{yz} transverse shear through plate thickness. When the Mindlin formulation is applied, it is verified that the transverse shear stress is constant through the plate thickness. When the Touratier formulation is applied, it is verified that the transverse shear stress changes sinusoidally through the plate thickness. In addition, it is verified that the shear force obtained is equal for both formulations. 9 Modelling E 9.1 Modelling characteristics The cell is stratified in three layers and homogenized to TYPE_HOMO PLAQUE, PLAQUE_CT_MINDLIN & PLAQUE_CT_TOURATIER. 9.2 Test values The homogenized elastic coefficients calculated for the different transverse shear formulations are compared with their analytical value derived from u5.03.37. | Homogenized coefficients MEMB & CISA in N.m^{-1} FLEX in N.m | Reference | Ca