ce  egin{array}{cc} rac{{P}_{\mathit{lq}}(y,0)}{{F}_{0}}=1&  orall y ]; The choice of the finished elements type has a considerable influence on the solution obtained in the vicinity of the free surface around t=0 . In Code_Aster, the selective Modelling (HMS) eliminates these oscillations ( Figure 2.2-a ), unlike conventional Modelling (HM). However, obtaining the exact solution for hydraulic pressure {P}_{\mathit{lq}}(y,0) by HMS modelling results in a greater approximation (compared to HM Modelling, see the case of mesh end of Figure 2.2-b ) for the solution on vertical displacement {u}_{y}(y,0) . This approximation is all the more important as the mesh is more coarse around the free surface. Finally, we note that the displacement calculated by the Lagamine code (University of Liège) is perfectly accurate and independent of the finesse of the mesh, but with in return a solution in hydraulic pressure very oscillating [:ref: HT66-05-012-A ]. This is due to the type of finished elements used by Lagamine which are different from ours since it is a P2P2 with a 9PG/9PG quadrature. This element does not check the inf-sup condition and therefore cannot give a correct solution in hydraulic pressure . 2.3 Bibliographical references 1. L.MEIROVITCH: Analytic methods in vibration . McMillan Ed., 1967. 2. J.J.MARIGO, E.PLANCHAIS: Introduction to asymptotic methods. Application to linear thermal problems . Note EDF/DER/IMA/MMN HI-70/7563, 31/08/1992. Figure 2.2-a: Comparaison des solutions en contraintes effectives verticales (SIYY) et en pression hydraulique (SIP) obtenues au premier pas de calcul au voisinage de la surface libre (suivant une coupe verticale) pour différentes finesses de maillage et pour les modélisations sélective (HMS) et classique (HM) dans le cas du maillage «fin». Comparaison avec les solutions analytiques à t=0 . Figure 2.2-b: Comparaison du déplacement vertical de la surface libre en fonction du temps obtenu pour 3 maillages différents et Lagamine pour